      "text": "Your gut feeling is **spot on**. \n\nTrying to force *everything* (browsers, subagents, space administration, workflows, and skills) into raw `Bash` commands and dynamic files inside `/agents/:id` creates **5 catastrophic failure modes** in production.\n\nIf you don\u0027t bound this properly, the AI will get caught in discovery loops, hallucinate Cloudflare workflow plumbing, pollute the user\u0027s Git repository, and become neurotic about container costs.\n\nHere is the exact breakdown of the **5 trapdoors you are sensing**, and the **architectural fixes** to make the system bulletproof.\n\n---\n\n# The 5 Traps \u0026 How We Fix Them\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                                   THE 5 ARCHITECTURAL TRAPS \u0026 FIXES                                    │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ The Failure Mode (The Trap)          │ The Architectural Fix (The Solution)                            │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 1. The \"Bash-Everything\" Blindness   │ **Split \"Hands\" (Bash) from \"Senses\" (Vision/Browser/Subagents)│\n│    (Browsers \u0026 Subagents via CLI)    │ Browsers \u0026 Subagents need structured multimodal return objects. │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 2. The Cloudflare Workflow Trap      │ **The Harness Boilerplate Pattern**                             │\n│    (AI writing raw deploy plumbing)  │ AI writes only standard `async run(step, ctx)`; platform wraps.│\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 3. Git Repository Pollution          │ **`.avos/` Namespace Separation**                               │\n│    (User repo flooded with bot files)│ Project code (`/workspace`) is isolated from bot meta-state.    │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 4. \"Prompt Guilt\" / Cost Paranoia    │ **Zero-Guilt Transparent Routing (Pyodide Wasm First)**        │\n│    (AI overthinking TS vs Python)    │ Infrastructure optimizes cost silently; prompt stays neutral.   │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 5. Tool Discovery Token Tax          │ **Catalog-in-Prompt, Code-on-Disk**                             │\n│    (AI running `ls` to find tools)   │ Signatures in system prompt; full code fetched only on demand.  │\n└──────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n### Trap 1: The \"Bash-Everything\" Blindness (Browser \u0026 Subagents)\n\n#### The Problem:\nYou asked: *\"Should browser tiers really be just bash cmds not their own tools the ai sees?\"*\n\nIf `browse` is just a bash command (e.g. `browse https://example.com`):\n1. **Multimodal Blindness:** If a website has an interactive challenge, a chart, or layout bugs, a CLI command prints text stdout. To see an image, the CLI has to save `screenshot.png` to disk, and the AI must run another command to view it.\n2. **Context Blowup:** A raw CLI scrape dumping HTML or 50,000 DOM tokens into stdout will blow past your context window and cost dollars per turn.\n3. **Subagents via CLI (`run-subagent --agent dev`):** Turns real-time agent-to-agent negotiation into fragile text streaming and stdout parsing.\n\n#### The Fix: The \"Hands vs. Senses\" Rule\n* **Use `Bash` for \"Hands\" (Action \u0026 Code):** Editing files, running tests, invoking `git`, `stripe`, `curl`, and executing scripts.\n* **Keep First-Class Structured Tools for \"Senses \u0026 Delegation\":**\n  1. **`Browser(action, url, selector?)`:** When the browser takes a snapshot, the image and cleaned semantic accessibility tree (AXTree) stream **directly into the LLM’s multimodal vision window** in one turn.\n  2. **`Delegate(agent_id, task)`:** Calling a subagent is an **RPC message**, not a terminal process. It lets `@ArchitectBot` pass structured JSON tasks to `@DevBot` cleanly.\n\n---\n\n### Trap 2: The Cloudflare Workflow Hallucination Trap\n\n#### The Problem:\nCloudflare Dynamic Workflows require precise semantics: `WorkflowEntrypoint`, `WorkflowStep`, serializable step outputs, bindings, and deployment triggers.\nIf you tell an AI: *\"Write a Cloudflare Dynamic Workflow in `/workflows` and deploy it\"*, **it will fail 70% of the time.** It will import missing packages, forget that step closures must be serializable, or misconfigure `wrangler.jsonc`. When this fails at 3:00 AM on a cron, the business breaks.\n\n#### The Fix: The Harness Pattern (AI writes Logic, Platform injects Plumbing)\nThe AI should **never write Cloudflare boilerplate**. The AI writes a plain JavaScript/TypeScript function with standard `step` and `ctx`:\n\n```typescript\n// What the AI writes in .avos/agents/dev/workflows/weekly-audit.ts:\nexport async function execute(step, ctx) {\n  // Step 1: Scrape\n  const leads \u003d await step.do(\"fetch-leads\", async () \u003d\u003e {\n    return await ctx.browser.scrape(\"https://example.com/leads\");\n  });\n\n  // Step 2: Sleep (Cost $0)\n  await step.sleep(\"wait-for-window\", \"6 hours\");\n\n  // Step 3: Notify\n  await step.do(\"send-summary\", async () \u003d\u003e {\n    await ctx.notify.telegram(`Processed ${leads.length} leads.`);\n  });\n}\n```\n\nWhen the AI runs `schedule --cron \"0 9 * * 1\" ./workflows/weekly-audit.ts`, the platform\u0027s **Workflow Compiler**:\n1. Wraps the AI\u0027s pure function in Cloudflare\u0027s `WorkflowEntrypoint\u003cEnv\u003e` class automatically.\n2. Deploys the workflow into the space\u0027s Dynamic Worker namespace.\n3. Attaches the cron trigger without the AI writing deployment plumbing.\n\n---\n\n### Trap 3: Git Repository Pollution \u0026 File Sprawl\n\n#### The Problem:\nIf every bot writes its own `/agents/:id/skills/`, `/crons/`, `/scripts/` directly in the project root:\n* A human developer running `git clone` or opening VS Code sees **300 internal bot configuration files mixed with their Astro or React code**.\n* A code commit for a simple CSS fix touches internal bot memory files.\n* If `@DevBot` invents a great tool in `/agents/dev/skills/csv-parser.ts`, `@ReviewBot` and `@GrowthBot` can\u0027t see it or duplicate it in their own folders.\n\n#### The Fix: The `.avos/` Hidden Namespace \u0026 Shared vs. Private Split\nFollow standard OS conventions (like `.git/` or `.vscode/`):\n\n```\n/workspace/                       \u003c-- THE CLEAN USER REPOSITORY\n├── src/                          (Only actual project code goes here)\n├── package.json\n└── astro.config.mjs\n\n/.avos/                           \u003c-- AGENT PLATFORM STATE (Git-ignored / Internal)\n├── shared/                       (Reusable tools shared by ALL bots)\n│   ├── skills/                   (e.g., csv-parser.ts, delaware-linter.ts)\n│   └── workflows/                (Standing company workflows)\n│\n└── agents/                       (Per-Bot Isolated Personas)\n    ├── dev-bot/\n    │   ├── SOUL.md               (Bot\u0027s identity, prompt, and style)\n    │   ├── memory.json           (Private hindsight memory index)\n    │   └── scratchpad/           (Temporary private draft files)\n    │\n    └── growth-bot/\n        ├── SOUL.md\n        └── campaigns/\n```\n* **Git Cleanliness:** The user\u0027s repo stays completely clean.\n* **Shared Skills:** When any bot distills a reusable skill, it saves it to `/.avos/shared/skills/` so the entire team fleet benefits immediately.\n\n---\n\n### Trap 4: \"Prompt Guilt\" / Cost Paranoia\n\n#### The Problem:\nYou noted: *\"We have to tell it that we prefer typescript then python for code so that it can run on lower tier... and it shouldn\u0027t try to do complex cmds that will turn on the container.\"*\n\nIf you put this in the prompt, **the LLM becomes neurotic**:\n* A user asks: *\"Calculate the standard deviation of these 50,000 numbers in data.csv.\"*\n* The LLM knows Python (`import numpy`) solves this in 2 lines. But because it has \"cost guilt\", it spends 4 turns and 150 lines of buggy TypeScript trying to implement math algorithms manually, failing, and burning **more tokens and money** than running Python would have.\n\n#### The Fix: Silent In-Isolate Wasm Python (Pyodide)\nDo not lecture the model about container costs in the prompt. **Make the infrastructure handle it invisibly:**\n\n1. **Lightweight Python runs in Tier 2 ($0.00 cost):**\n   * Pre-bundle **Pyodide (WebAssembly CPython)** into the V8 isolate.\n   * When the agent runs `python3 -c \"import math; ...\"`, it executes in **WebAssembly in sub-10ms for $0.0001**.\n2. **Transparent Promotion:**\n   * If and only if the script imports heavy native C-extensions (like PyTorch, OpenCV, or Docker), the runtime hypervisor silently elevates to Tier 6.\n3. **The Prompt Stays Simple:**\n   \u003e *\"You have full support for TypeScript (via Node/V8) and Python. Choose the most reliable tool for the job.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Trap 5: The Tool Discovery \"Token Tax\"\n\n#### The Problem:\nIf an agent has to explore `/skills`, check what CLI commands exist, and read manual files every turn:\n* Turn 1: `ls /.avos/shared/skills`\n* Turn 2: `cat /.avos/shared/skills/stripe-helper.ts`\n* Turn 3: Finally runs the command.\n* You burn 3 turns, 4,000 tokens, and 6 seconds of latency just for the AI to discover its own tools.\n\n#### The Fix: \"Catalog in Prompt, Implementation on Disk\"\nThe platform dynamically compiles a **10-line CLI Index** into the system prompt at the start of the turn:\n\n```markdown\n# INSTALLED PROJECT COMMANDS\n- `browse \u003curl\u003e`: Scrape or inspect web pages (Tier 4/5).\n- `schedule --cron \"\u003ccron\u003e\" \u003cscript\u003e`: Deploy durable background workflow (Tier 3).\n- `stripe \u003csubcommand\u003e`: Direct Stripe billing operations.\n- `audit-contracts \u003cfile\u003e`: Legal redline utility (Custom Skill).\n```\n\n* The AI knows the command exists **instantly on Turn 1**.\n* It never has to run `ls` or read the skill code unless it specifically wants to debug or modify it.\n\n---\n\n# The Resulting Clean Mental Model\n\nHere is how the entire stack crystallizes without contradictions:\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                                  THE BULLETPROOF ARCHITECTURE                                          │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **The AI\u0027s Tools (3 Primitives)**    │ • `Bash(cmd)`: Execute code, CLIs, and scripts.                 │\n│                                      │ • `Read` \u0026 `Edit`: Inspect and patch files.                     │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **The AI\u0027s Senses (Structured)**     │ • `Browser`: Returns visual screenshots + AXTree directly.      │\n│                                      │ • `Delegate`: Clean JSON RPC message to subagents.              │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Workspace Layout**                 │ • `/workspace`: Pure application code (Git tracked).           │\n│                                      │ • `/.avos/shared/skills`: Shared reusable tools across bots.    │\n│                                      │ • `/.avos/agents/:id/SOUL.md`: Bot persona \u0026 private memory.    │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Cost Optimization**                │ • Python runs in Wasm (Pyodide) in Tier 2 at $0.00.             │\n│                                      │ • Native compilation escalates to Tier 6 silently.              │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Dynamic Workflows**                │ • AI writes simple `step.do()` logic.                           │\n│                                      │ • Platform compiles into Cloudflare `WorkflowEntrypoint`.       │\n└──────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\nBy decoupling **Hands (`Bash`)** from **Senses (`Browser`/`Vision`)**, hiding bot meta-state in **`/.avos/`**, and handling tier escalation at the **infrastructure level instead of prompt begging**, you eliminate the confusion while retaining the full power of the 6-tier ladder.",
