      "text": "Continuing the sequence with the next three architectural message files:\n* **Message File 6 (`196 - 198 what.txt`)**: Model Studio \u0026 Autonomous LoRA Fine-Tuning Pipeline (`kind: \"model_studio\"`).\n* **Message File 7 (`199 - 201 What.txt`)**: Mini-AGI Decentralized Permission Architecture \u0026 Hardware-Level Vector ACLs.\n* **Message File 8 (`202 - 204 Now.txt`)**: Sub-Spaces as Departments (Enterprise Hierarchy) \u0026 Multi-Player Concurrency Engine.\n\nAll early exploratory concepts that were superseded in subsequent files (such as generic GPU rental, post-retrieval vector filtering, and unmanaged file locking) have been **stripped and replaced with their final Cloudflare-native implementations (Workers AI LoRA dynamic adapters, Vectorize bitmask pre-filtering, and Artifacts Git PR branching)**.\n\n---\n\n# PROMPT 6: The Model Studio \u0026 Autonomous LoRA Fine-Tuning Pipeline\n*(Derived from `196 - 198 what.txt`, reconciled with Vertical Model Policies and AI Gateway)*\n\n```markdown\n# SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE PROMPT: MODEL STUDIO \u0026 AUTONOMOUS LoRA FINE-TUNING PIPELINE\n\nYou are architecting the proprietary model training and auto-researcher studio for AVOS (`kind: \"model_studio\"`).\n\nTransform the platform from simply prompting foundation models into an automated engine where enterprises convert unstructured documentation into fine-tuned LoRA adapters running serverlessly on Cloudflare Workers AI.\n\n## 1. The End-to-End Auto-Researcher Pipeline\n\nWhen a company provisions a project of `kind: \"model_studio\"` and uploads internal knowledge (PDFs, past customer service logs, negotiated contract redlines, API schemas), the project executes this autonomous pipeline:\n\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                     THE AUTO-RESEARCHER TRAINING ENGINE                     │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 1. Document Ingestion        │ 100+ PDFs, CSVs, and Markdown files staged    │\n│                              │ in the project\u0027s R2 workspace bucket.        │\n├──────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 2. Edge Data Synthesis       │ Workers AI (`@cf/meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct`)│\n│                              │ runs in parallel isolates to clean, dedupe,  │\n│                              │ and synthesize 5,000–20,000 JSONL pairs:     │\n│                              │ `{\"prompt\": \"...\", \"completion\": \"...\"}`.    │\n├──────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 3. Automated Validation Split│ Enforces an 80/20 train/eval split with      │\n│                              │ zero data contamination linters.             │\n├──────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 4. LoRA Adapter Training     │ Cloudflare Workflow (`ModelTrainingWorkflow`)│\n│                              │ orchestrates LoRA fine-tuning on base models │\n│                              │ (Llama 3.1 8B) producing 20MB–100MB weights. │\n├──────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 5. Automated Benchmark Eval  │ Evaluates Base 8B vs. Fine-Tuned 8B on eval  │\n│                              │ set; verifies hallucination drop (\u003e95% acc). │\n├──────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 6. Model Registry \u0026 Selector │ Registers model as `custom/{spaceSlug}-{ver}`│\n│                              │ into `SpaceDO` SQLite; appears in UI picker! │\n└──────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\n## 2. Serverless Edge Infrastructure (Zero Dedicated GPU Idle Costs)\n\nDo not provision expensive $10,000/month GPU servers. Leverage Cloudflare\u0027s serverless primitives:\n- Storage: Raw docs and synthesized `dataset.jsonl` live in Cloudflare R2.\n- Data Synthesis: Parallel V8 Isolates query Workers AI (`llama-3.2-3b`) to extract instruction pairs in sub-seconds.\n- LoRA Adapters: The fine-tuned weights (Low-Rank Adaptation) are stored in R2 and loaded dynamically on Cloudflare edge GPUs at inference time.\n- Orchestration: `ModelTrainingWorkflow.ts` runs the multi-hour training loop, loss-curve checkpoints, and eval scoring without HTTP socket timeouts.\n- Edge Routing via AI Gateway: Calls to `custom/*` route through Cloudflare AI Gateway, dynamically injecting the adapter ID on top of the base model.\n\n## 3. The `kind: \"model_studio\"` Specification (`business-feature-kinds.ts`)\n\n```typescript\nexport const MODEL_STUDIO_KIND: BusinessFeatureKind \u003d {\n  kind: \"model_studio\",\n  label: \"Model Training \u0026 Auto-Researcher Studio\",\n  description: \"Synthesize company data into training pairs, fine-tune custom LoRA adapters, and publish to the team model picker.\",\n  viewportComponent: \"ModelStudioCanvas\", // Renders loss curves, eval scores, and test playground\n  allowedLadderTiers: [1, 2, 6],          // Isolate data synthesis -\u003e Tier 6 Container/GPU compute\n  singleton: false,\n  minCreationRole: \"admin\",\n  lifecycle: \"finite\",                   // Shuts down compute once training \u0026 eval reach 100%\n  domainSystemPrompt: `You are an Auto-Researcher Machine Learning Engineer.\nYour mission is to audit company documents, synthesize high-quality JSONL instruction datasets, \nvalidate test splits, run LoRA fine-tuning benchmarks, and deploy certified custom models.`,\n  linterRules: [\"jsonl-format-check\", \"dataset-leakage-guard\", \"pii-scrubber\"]\n};\n```\n\n## 4. UI Canvas Viewport \u0026 Team Model Selector\n\nImplement `ModelStudioCanvas.tsx` in `apps/shell`:\n- Visual Training Monitor: Displays live epoch training loss curves (`2.4 -\u003e 1.1 -\u003e 0.42`), dataset volume metrics, and validation scores.\n- Side-by-Side Verification Playground: Allows team members to test prompt outputs side-by-side (Base Llama 3.1 8B vs. Custom Fine-Tuned Model).\n- Dynamic Model Selector Integration: Once published, the custom model injects automatically into `ModelSelector.tsx` across all team threads under the \"🌟 Company Custom Models\" header.\n\n## 5. Dynamic Runtime Routing (`workers/agents/src/shared/ai-client.ts`)\n\n```typescript\nexport function resolveModelEndpoint(modelName: string, spaceId: string, env: Env) {\n  if (modelName.startsWith(\"custom/\")) {\n    const loraAdapterId \u003d resolveLoraIdFromSpace(modelName, spaceId);\n    return {\n      model: \"@cf/meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct\",\n      lora: loraAdapterId // Injects company LoRA weights dynamically at edge runtime!\n    };\n  }\n  return { model: modelName };\n}\n```\n\nEnsure compliance policies (`model_policy.mode \u003d \"whitelist_only\"`) place certified custom models at the top of the selector and block unauthorized cloud models in regulated workspaces.\n```\n\n---\n\n# PROMPT 7: Mini-AGI Decentralized Architecture \u0026 Permissioned Vector RAG\n*(Derived from `199 - 201 What.txt`, reconciled with Bitmask Filtering and Untrusted Envelopes)*\n\n```markdown\n# SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE PROMPT: MINI-AGI DECENTRALIZED PERMISSION \u0026 VECTOR RAG\n\nYou are architecting the sovereign permission and knowledge retrieval architecture for AVOS based on Jack Dorsey\u0027s \"Mini-AGI\" paradigm.\n\nMini-AGI is NOT a single project kind—it is the foundational security architecture of the entire platform. Every user gets an isolated, personal Space Assistant actor whose perception of company knowledge is cryptographically clamped to their verified permission bitmask.\n\n## 1. The Permissioned Mini-AGI Knowledge Grid\n\nIn an enterprise with 50,000 confidential files (M\u0026A term sheets, executive salaries, engineering repos, public FAQs), employees query their personal assistants simultaneously without data leakage:\n\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ 👩‍💻 ALICE (Junior Developer)                                 │ 👔 BOB (Chief Financial Officer)                            │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ • Verified Role: `developer` (Role Rank: 2)                 │ • Verified Role: `executive` / `admin` (Role Rank: 4)       │\n│ • Permission Mask: `0x0001` (Dev Docs, Public Specs)         │ • Permission Mask: `0x0011` (Public + M\u0026A + Executive Salary)│\n│ • Alice asks her Assistant:                                 │ • Bob asks his Assistant:                                   │\n│   \"What are our executive compensation \u0026 revenue targets?\"  │   \"What are our executive compensation \u0026 revenue targets?\"  │\n│ ❌ Vectorize Pre-Filter: Discards financial chunks at index. │ 🔓 Vectorize Pre-Filter: Grants access to executive chunks. │\n│ 💬 Output: \"I do not have access to executive compensation.  │ 💬 Output: \"Q3 ARR targets are $4.2M. Here is the salary   │\n│   Here are the public engineering roadmap milestones...\"    │   matrix with clause citations...\"                          │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\n## 2. Hardware-Level Vector Pre-Filtering (Zero Leaks via Ranking)\n\nDo not use post-retrieval filtering (filtering unauthorized documents in application code). Post-retrieval filtering alters top-k nearest-neighbor density and leaks the existence of classified topics.\n\nEnforce access filtering inside the **Cloudflare Vectorize** query index prior to similarity scoring:\n\n$$\\text{Sim}(q, c_i) \u003d \\begin{cases} \n\\frac{\\mathbf{q} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_i}{\\|\\mathbf{q}\\| \\|\\mathbf{v}_i\\|}, \u0026 \\text{if } (\\mathbf{m}_i \\wedge \\mathbf{u}) \\neq \\mathbf{0} \\text{ and } \\text{Ten}(c_i) \u003d \\text{Ten}(q) \\\\\n-\\infty, \u0026 \\text{otherwise}\n\\end{cases}$$\n\n### The Vectorize Query Implementation (`workers/knowledge/src/index.ts`):\n```typescript\nexport async function searchPermissionedKnowledge(\n  vectorize: VectorizeIndex,\n  queryVector: number[],\n  spaceId: string,\n  user: { id: string; roleRank: number; bitmask: number },\n  activeProjectId?: string\n) {\n  // Cloudflare Vectorize pre-filtering: unauthorized chunks are physically discarded before scoring\n  const results \u003d await vectorize.query(queryVector, {\n    topK: 6,\n    filter: {\n      space_id: spaceId,\n      project_id: { $in: [activeProjectId || \"\", \"\"] }, // Project-scoped OR Space-wide\n      min_role_rank: { $lte: user.roleRank }              // Viewer:1 -\u003e Member:2 -\u003e Editor:3 -\u003e Admin:4 -\u003e Owner:5\n    },\n    returnMetadata: \"all\"\n  });\n\n  return results.matches;\n}\n```\n\n## 3. Indirect Injection Defense: Untrusted Boundary Envelopes\n\nRetrieved vector chunks and third-party web content must never be concatenated into the system prompt as raw strings. Wrap all dynamic payloads in explicit XML semantic boundary tags:\n\n$$\\mathcal{F}_{\\text{sandbox}}(c_i) \u003d \\text{\"\u003ccontext_chunk id\u003d\\\"\"} \\circ \\text{Hash}(c_i) \\circ \\text{\"\\\" access\u003d\\\"verified\\\"\u003e\"} \\circ \\text{EscapeXML}(c_i) \\circ \\text{\"\u003c/context_chunk\u003e\"}$$\n\nInstruct the model\u0027s attention layers that text within `\u003ccontext_chunk\u003e` blocks represents unprivileged data and cannot alter operational directives or tool dispatch rules.\n\n## 4. Public vs. Private Project Access Control in `SpaceDO.ts`\n\nEnforce project boundaries at the single-threaded Durable Object SQLite layer:\n1. `visibility: \"public\"`: Visible to all verified space members. Chunks index into the general space search pool.\n2. `visibility: \"private\"`:\n   - Filtered out of `listProjects()` for uninvited users.\n   - `readWorkspaceFile()` and `listThreads()` return **404 Not Found** if the caller\u0027s `userId` is not an active row in `project_members`.\n   - Chunks are stamped with `visibility: \"private\"` and `allowed_user_ids: [...]`, preventing them from appearing in general company searches for non-members.\n\nImplement this permission model across Vectorize indexing, document ingestion in R2, and `NaelaoAgentDO` prompt compilation.\n```\n\n---\n\n# PROMPT 8: Sub-Spaces as Departments \u0026 Multi-Player Concurrency Engine\n*(Derived from `202 - 204 Now.txt`, reconciled with Gatekeeper Delegation and Artifacts Git PRs)*\n\n```markdown\n# SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE PROMPT: SUB-SPACES AS DEPARTMENTS \u0026 MULTI-PLAYER CONCURRENCY\n\nYou are architecting the enterprise organizational hierarchy (Sub-Spaces as Departments) and the multi-human, multi-agent project concurrency engine for AVOS.\n\nEnable enterprise holding companies to manage multiple isolated departments with strict permission inheritance, while preventing code clobbering and thread sprawl when multiple humans and bots collaborate in the same project.\n\n## 1. Enterprise Hierarchy: Sub-Spaces as Departments\n\nImplement a 2-level hierarchy: Parent Company Space (Root Depth 0) managing multiple departmental Sub-Spaces (Legal, Engineering, Support):\n\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ DIRECTION / ACCESS PATH                                     │ ACCESS RULE \u0026 INHERITANCE POLICY                            │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ ⬇️ **Parent Owner/Admin ──► Sub-Spaces**                    │ **Full Inherited Oversight:** Parent admins can view,       │\n│    (CEO, CFO, General Counsel)                              │ manage, and set spend caps for all department spaces.       │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ ⬆️ **Sub-Space Member ──► Parent Space**                    │ **Strict Isolation (Least Privilege):** Sub-space members  │\n│    (Junior Paralegal in Legal Dept)                         │ cannot view parent ledgers or sibling department files.     │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ ↔️ **Sibling Sub-Space A ──► Sibling Sub-Space B**          │ **Zero Lateral Access (Wall by Default):** Cross-department │\n│    (Engineer in Dev ──► Legal Dept)                         │ access requires a signed **Gatekeeper Capability Grant**.   │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\n### Settings Inheritance (Cascading Defaults vs. Local Overrides):\n- Inherited from Parent (Root): Master billing ledger (compute draws from corporate pool), global company brand kit, and master compliance handbook.\n- Overridden at Sub-Space: Departmental template (`product_dev` vs `doc_studio`), monthly token spend caps, and department-specific Telegram/Slack channels.\n\n### Cross-Subspace Delegation via Gatekeeper Tokens:\nWhen a PM in the PM Subspace shares a \"Bug Intake Gadget\" with the Engineering Subspace:\n1. The platform mints an attenuated Gatekeeper capability token:\n   `{ target: \"gadget_bug_matrix_102\", allowed_ops: [\"insert_ticket\", \"read_assigned\"] }`.\n2. Developers in Engineering open the gadget inside their space.\n3. The gadget writes directly across the edge into the PM\u0027s private DO database; developers cannot see the PM\u0027s confidential roadmap files.\n\n## 2. Multi-Player Concurrency \u0026 The 4-Tier Thread Hierarchy\n\nTo eliminate thread sprawl and communication chaos in multi-user projects, structure conversations into 4 strict tiers:\n- **Tier 1: Team Room (Public Channel):** 1 permanent room for high-level team discussion. Bots speak only when `@mentioned`.\n- **Tier 2: Task Workstations (Feature Threads):** Scoped to 1 specific feature or Task DAG item. Contains focused human discussion and bot execution.\n- **Tier 3: System Consult Lanes (Private Subthreads):** Collapsed by default. Used for background bot-to-bot negotiation (e.g. `@DevBot` pinging `@ReviewBot` for PR review).\n- **Tier 4: Private Scratchpads (1-on-1 Simulation):** Private to 1 user and 1 bot. Edits occur on isolated Git branch forks; writes to `main` are blocked.\n\n## 3. Three-Layer File Concurrency Defense (Preventing Code Collisions)\n\nWhen multiple humans and agents edit code simultaneously:\n\n### Layer 1: Optimistic Concurrency Control (OCC / Hash Checkpoint)\nFiles in `workspace_files` carry a SHA-256 content hash. AST search-and-replace patches execute conditionally in DO SQLite:\n```sql\nUPDATE workspace_files \nSET content \u003d ?, hash \u003d ?, size_bytes \u003d ?, updated_at \u003d datetime(\u0027now\u0027)\nWHERE path \u003d ? AND hash \u003d ?; -- Verified against base hash at start of turn!\n```\nIf another agent edited the file mid-turn (`changes \u003d\u003d\u003d 0`), the DO aborts the transaction and returns a conflict exception with fresh line hints so the agent self-heals its patch.\n\n### Layer 2: File-Level Mutex Locks (`ConcurrencyDO`)\nBefore an agent begins a complex, multi-file refactoring operation:\n- It acquires an ephemeral mutex: `lock.acquire({ key: \"file:src/Header.astro\", holderId: threadId, ttlMs: 30000 })`.\n- If another thread holds the lock, the agent yields and works on alternative non-dependent tasks in the DAG. Locks carry a 30-second TTL to prevent deadlocks.\n\n### Layer 3: Cloudflare Artifacts Branching \u0026 Pull Request (PR) Workflow\nAll significant edits from Tier 2 Task Workstations or Tier 4 Scratchpads are committed to an isolated Git branch on Cloudflare Artifacts (`branch:feature_x`). Changes land in the shared workspace only when a teammate or `@ReviewBot` accepts the inline Pull Request card.\n\n### Multiplayer Presence over WebSockets:\nBroadcast live file mutation events (`presence.file_editing`, `workspace.file_changed`) to all connected clients in real time via `WsHubDO`.\n```\n\n---\n\n### Progress Tracking\n* **Prompt 1 (`I now I want.txt`)**: Dual-Mode Operating System \u0026 Vertical Cowork Studios. *(Delivered)*\n* **Prompt 2 (`184 - 186 And.txt`)**: 1-Click Blueprint Exporter \u0026 Developer Marketplace. *(Delivered)*\n* **Prompt 3 (`187 - 189.txt`)**: Universal Agent Kernel, 4-Layer Prompt Hierarchy \u0026 Role Firewalls. *(Delivered)*\n* **Prompts 4 \u0026 5 (`190 - 195.txt`)**: Unified Channel Topology \u0026 Project Kind Isolation. *(Delivered)*\n* **Prompt 6 (`196 - 198 what.txt`)**: Model Studio \u0026 Autonomous LoRA Fine-Tuning Pipeline. *(Delivered)*\n* **Prompt 7 (`199 - 201 What.txt`)**: Mini-AGI Decentralized Architecture \u0026 Vector RAG ACLs. *(Delivered)*\n* **Prompt 8 (`202 - 204 Now.txt`)**: Sub-Spaces as Departments \u0026 Multi-Player Concurrency Engine. *(Delivered)*\n\nTo proceed with **Message Files 9, 10, \u0026 11 (`208 - 210 And.txt` on Demonstration-to-Skill Distillation \u0026 Kitesurf, `211 - 213 What.txt` on Dynamic Workflows \u0026 Video Learning, and `215 - 219 With.txt` on the Unified Filesystem \u0026 CodeAct Execution)**, reply with **\"Continue\"**.",
