active bot management and resulting in a chat-heavy environment, which I can now see as higher-friction. The bandwidth is greater on Interface A, I think.\n\n\n**Evaluating Interface Benefits**\n\nI\u0027ve determined that the \"Project Agent\" architecture was fundamentally superior. While the Bot Roster provided a familiar mental model, the project agent approach was vastly better architecturally, functionally, and economically. A hybrid evolution might be ideal. The user may benefit from keeping the Project-First, Artifact-First, DAG-driven engine. I propose, in such a case, that bots be treated as background workers assigned to DAGs, never as an interface requirement that puts the human in a management role. This resolves the user\u0027s issue.\n\n\n**Contrasting the Paradigms**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on a point-by-point contrast between the Project Agent approach and the Bot Roster, which is further clarifying the differences. I\u0027ve noted a stark contrast in goal execution methods: the former\u0027s automated DAG creation versus the latter\u0027s manual, chat-based approach. State and context management are also radically different. Interface bandwidth, economic considerations, and security paradigms are all being examined. I\u0027m seeing that the \"Project Agent\" architecture is project-driven, and \"Context-Unified\", whereas the Bot Roster is task-delegation-driven, and chat-centric.\n\n\n",
