      "text": "**Considering Architectural Implications**\n\nI\u0027m wrestling with the architectural implications of integrating persistent, autonomous bots (like Hermes) with a UI framework. The challenge lies in harmonizing their independent operational nature with a cohesive UI structure. I\u0027m currently pondering the best approach to handle bot interactions within the UI without introducing tightly coupled dependencies. I\u0027m focusing on minimizing impact on the underlying UI.\n\n\n**Analyzing Thread Architectures**\n\nI\u0027m now diving into the implications of using a 4-tier threading system (or a refined alternative) to support these autonomous bots. I\u0027m exploring how to enable switching between different bots *within* a chat seamlessly. This includes addressing the Cron/background execution dilemma: determining if a Cron job should execute regardless of the currently selected bot or be tied to a specific bot or thread. I\u0027m also considering the implications of thread size on Cron jobs. My goal is a bulletproof architecture.\n\n\n**Exploring Bot Context Switching**\n\nI\u0027m now contemplating how to handle bot switching within a chat thread. I\u0027m considering two primary approaches: @mentions or an \"Active Operator\" selector within the composer. The core challenge is preserving context and thread history during the switch. Should the context be carried forward? What about thread history?\n\n\n**Defining Cron Execution**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on the best way to manage Cron jobs within this architecture. Should these jobs be tied to a specific thread, or remain project-universal with a bot ID? I\u0027ve determined that tying them to a thread presents challenges like context overflow and the disruption of a user\u0027s current interaction. My current thinking leans towards project-universal entities attributed to a specific agent ID. These jobs will spawn isolated execution runs.\n\n\n**Refining Cron Job Execution**\n\nI\u0027ve decided on the design for managing Cron jobs: They\u0027ll be project-universal and attributed to a specific bot ID, and will trigger isolated execution runs. The next hurdle is how they\u0027ll handle context. When a job runs, it will execute in a clean, ephemeral runner that does not load the entire chat history. Instead, the runner will load the task payload and relevant workspace files. A receipt card will be posted in the original thread indicating job completion.\n\n\n**Evaluating Bot Communication**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on bot communication styles within a thread. I\u0027m considering two methods: adopting the Slack-style @mentions or introducing an active persona switcher within the input dock. For Tier 1 and 2 (Team Room/Task Workstation), the conversation history is shared, but each bot maintains its own identity and private tools. In Tier 4 (Private Scratchpad), you converse one-on-one with the selected bot. I\u0027m also considering long chat thread context management, inspired by AVOS and its in-flight `compact_context` for milestone summarization.\n\n\n**Defining Context Retention**\n\nI\u0027ve been refining how to manage context, specifically in relation to background Cron jobs and long chat threads. I\u0027ve concluded that Cron jobs maintain pristine, small context and utilize a \"Receipt Card\" pattern to avoid thread bloat. I\u0027m now focusing on ensuring that any automatic AST/checkpoint squash summarizes milestone completions into the Project Journal and trims older turns, only retaining the summary and the most recent three turns.\n\n\n",
