      "text": "Here is the exact architectural breakdown of how to solve **both** problems:\n1. **The Multi-Mention \u0026 Autonomous Delegation Problem** (preventing two bots from waking up at once when one is just being talked *about*).\n2. **The Cross-Thread Knowledge Problem** (how Hermes and Grok Bot give bots persistent project-level learning without polluting fresh threads).\n\n---\n\n# PART 1: The Multi-Mention \u0026 Delegation Dilemma\n\n### The Scenario:\n\u003e 👩‍💻 **Alice:** *\"@ReviewBot, please audit @DevBot\u0027s idempotency patch for edge cases.\"*\n\nIf your system uses a naive regex like `/@(\\w+)/g`, both `@ReviewBot` and `@DevBot` match. Both Durable Objects wake up at the same millisecond:\n* `@DevBot` wakes up and replies: *\"I wrote that patch yesterday in `src/api/stripe.ts`!\"*\n* `@ReviewBot` wakes up and tries to audit the code at the same time.\n* **Result:** Total conversational chaos, double token spend, and race conditions.\n\n---\n\n### The Solution: Primary Turn-Holder vs. Referent Entity\n\nIn multi-agent operating systems (and human team platforms like Slack), mentions fall into two distinct syntactic categories:\n1. **The Direct Assignee (Turn-Holder):** The bot being commanded to act.\n2. **The Referent Entity:** A bot, user, or file being talked *about*.\n\n```\n                                  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n                                  │      INCOMING CHAT MESSAGE WITH MENTIONS     │\n                                  │ \"@ReviewBot, audit @DevBot\u0027s patch...\"       │\n                                  └──────────────────────┬───────────────────────┘\n                                                         │\n                                                         ▼\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ 1. INGRESS MENTION PARSER (`mention-router.ts`)                                                        │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ • Leading Mention Rule: First mention (`@ReviewBot`) ──► ASSIGNED PRIMARY TURN-HOLDER.                 │\n│ • Possessive / Prepositional Check: `@DevBot\u0027s` has trailing `\u0027s` or follows `from/by`                 │\n│   ──► CLASSIFIED AS CONTEXT REFERENT (Does NOT wake up!).                                              │\n│ • Turn Lock Minted: `thread_turn_lock` granted exclusively to `@ReviewBot`.                            │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                                                 │\n                                                 ▼\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ 2. PRIMARY BOT EXECUTES WITH DELEGATION CAPABILITY                                                     │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ `@ReviewBot` wakes up alone. It inspects the diff.                                                     │\n│ What if `@ReviewBot` finds a bug and needs `@DevBot` to fix it?                                        │\n│ It does NOT wait for Alice. It calls its built-in tool:                                                │\n│ `ctx.agent.delegate({ target: \"dev-bot\", task: \"Fix line 42 race condition\", branch: \"task_102\" })`    │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                                                 │\n                                                 ▼\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ 3. SEQUENTIAL WAKE-UP (Handoff Chain)                                                                  │\n│ `@ReviewBot` yields its turn lock. `@DevBot` wakes up in Round 2 with the exact task specification!   │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n#### The Mention Parsing Rule (`workers/agents/src/router/mention-router.ts`):\n```typescript\nexport function resolveTargetAgent(text: string, activeRoster: string[]): {\n  primaryAgentId: string;\n  referentAgentIds: string[];\n} {\n  // Extract all @mentions in order of appearance\n  const matches \u003d [...text.matchAll(/@([a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)(?:\u0027s)?/g)];\n  if (matches.length \u003d\u003d\u003d 0) return { primaryAgentId: \"default_assistant\", referentAgentIds: [] };\n\n  // First mention is ALWAYS the intended executor\n  const primaryName \u003d matches[0][1].toLowerCase();\n  \n  // Subsequent mentions are passed as contextual metadata (not woken up)\n  const referents \u003d matches.slice(1).map(m \u003d\u003e m[1].toLowerCase());\n\n  return {\n    primaryAgentId: resolveBotIdFromName(primaryName),\n    referentAgentIds: referents.map(resolveBotIdFromName)\n  };\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n# PART 2: How Hermes \u0026 Grok Bot Handle Memory Across Threads\n\nYou asked:\n\u003e *\"What about cross-thread knowledge? If the user created a new thread to have a fresh new chat with a different context, but uses the same bot, what if it takes context it learned from old threads? How does Grok Bot or Hermes Bot Mode do it?\"*\n\n### How Hermes Agent Does It:\nIn Nous Research\u0027s **Hermes Agent (`hermes-agent`)**:\n1. **Sessions are completely isolated from each other:** When you create a new session/chat with a bot, the raw conversation turns of previous sessions are **never injected into the context window**. A new chat starts with a clean `0-turn` context.\n2. **Profile Memory is separated from Chat Logs:**\n   Each bot has its own persistent profile directory:\n   ```\n   ~/.hermes/profiles/dev-bot/\n   ├── SOUL.md          \u003c-- Permanent personality \u0026 operating guidelines\n   ├── memory/\n   │   └── MEMORY.md    \u003c-- Distilled semantic facts (NOT raw chat logs)\n   └── sessions/        \u003c-- Archived chat transcripts (isolated per thread)\n   ```\n3. **The End-of-Turn Distillation Pass (Hindsight Memory):**\n   Hermes does not carry chat logs over. When a session achieves something, a background routine extracts **distilled atomic facts**:\n   * *Bad Memory (Carried Chat):* `\"Alice said she was unhappy with the Stripe fee, then Bob suggested BrainTree, then we decided on Stripe...\"` (Pollutes new threads).\n   * *Good Distilled Fact (Hermes Memory):* `\"Project uses Stripe API version 2026-08 with idempotency keys.\"`\n4. When a new thread starts, the bot loads its `SOUL.md` and only queries its `MEMORY.md` **via semantic vector search if relevant to the new prompt**.\n\n---\n\n### How Grok Bot Does It:\nxAI\u0027s **Grok Bot** follows a similar dual-plane architecture:\n1. **The Workspace Disk is Universal:** The files on disk (`/workspace`) are persistent across all threads. If a bot writes `src/api/stripe.ts` in Thread 1, the file is right there on disk for Thread 2.\n2. **The Conversational Slate is Ephemeral:** Each thread is a separate branch/session. Grok Bot starts fresh without historical prompt baggage.\n3. **Semantic Memory Graphs:** Grok Bot indexes past thread summaries in a background vector store. It does **not** dump past conversations into your new prompt. It only retrieves a past fact if your current prompt semantically triggers it.\n\n---\n\n# PART 3: The AVOS Hybrid Memory Solution\n\nIn AVOS, we implement the **Clean-Thread / Persistent-Knowledge Architecture**.\n\nA bot is a **persistent project entity**, but every thread has a **pristine context window**.\n\n```\n                                  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n                                  │           PROJECT: \"Core SaaS App\"           │\n                                  └──────────────────────┬───────────────────────┘\n                                                         │\n                         ┌───────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┐\n                         ▼                                                               ▼\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ 💬 THREAD #1: \"Stripe Webhook Bug\" (50 Messages Long)        │   │ 💬 THREAD #2: \"Write CSV Export Script\" (Brand New Thread)  │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤   ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ User and @DevBot spend 2 hours debugging Stripe signatures. │   │ User opens a fresh thread with @DevBot:                     │\n│ Context is full of compiler errors, stack traces, and logs. │   │ \"Write a script to export customer emails to CSV.\"          │\n└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘   └──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘\n                               │                                                                 │\n                               ▼ (Task Completes)                                                │\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐                                 │\n│ BACKGROUND DISTILLATION HOOK (Runs in V8 Isolate at $0.00)  │                                 │\n│ Extracts 1 atomic rule into Vectorize (`BOT_SKILL` scope):   │                                 │\n│ \"Rule #42: Always use crypto.subtle for webhook signatures.\"│                                 │\n└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘                                 │\n                               │                                                                 │\n                               ▼                                                                 │\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐                                 │\n│ 🧠 PERSISTENT BOT MEMORY BANK (Cloudflare Vectorize)         │                                 │\n│ Contains 15 learned technical rules for @DevBot.            │                                 │\n└──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘                                 │\n                               │                                                                 │\n                               │                                     (Query: \"Export to CSV\")    │\n                               │                                                                 ▼\n                               │                                  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n                               └─────────────────────────────────►│ SEMANTIC RELEVANCE FILTER (Cosine Similarity \u003e 0.82)        │\n                                                                  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n                                                                  │ • Stripe Webhook rule ──► Cosine Sim: 0.18 (DISCARDED!)     │\n                                                                  │ • CSV formatting rule ──► Cosine Sim: 0.89 (INJECTED!)      │\n                                                                  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n                                                                  │ 🚀 RESULT: Thread #2 stays 100% clean of Stripe pollution! │\n                                                                  │ @DevBot only knows the CSV rules relevant to this task!    │\n                                                                  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n### The 3 Rules of Cross-Thread Memory in AVOS\n\n#### Rule 1: Zero Raw Message Bleed Across Threads\n* `NaelaoAgentDO` **never** loads message transcripts from Thread 1 into Thread 2.\n* Thread 2\u0027s context contains **only**:\n  1. Universal Engine Directives (Claude Code rules).\n  2. The Bot’s `SOUL.md` (role, style, constraints).\n  3. The Project Brief from `PROJECT.md` (authoritative current architecture).\n  4. The User\u0027s new prompt in Thread 2.\n\n#### Rule 2: Learnings are Distilled into \"Rules\", Not Dialogues\nWhen a task finishes in any thread, a background Workers AI hook (`bge-small-en-v1.5`) runs this 1-second distillation:\n* Input: The last 10 messages of the completed task.\n* Prompt: *\"Extract any permanent architectural preferences or technical constraints learned. Ignore conversational small talk.\"*\n* Output: Pushes structured facts to Cloudflare Vectorize under `metadata: { scope: \"BOT_SKILL\", botId: \"dev-bot\" }`.\n\n#### Rule 3: Memory is Pulled via Semantic Relevance (Not Blindly Dumped)\nWhen Alice asks `@DevBot` to do something in a new thread:\n* The system embeds Alice\u0027s prompt.\n* It queries `@DevBot`\u0027s memory in Vectorize with a strict threshold: `similarity \u003e 0.80`.\n* If Alice asks: *\"Create a CSV script\"*, past facts about Stripe, Tailwind, or Redis have similarity scores under 0.30 and are **physically excluded from the prompt**.\n* If Alice asks: *\"How do we handle our Stripe checkout again?\"*, the similarity score hits 0.92, and the distilled rule is seamlessly injected into the context!\n\n---\n\n### Summary Checklist\n\n| Problem | How AVOS Resolves It |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Multiple `@mentions` in one message** | **First Mention \u003d Turn Holder.** Subsequent mentions (like `@DevBot\u0027s patch`) are treated as context references and do not wake up. |\n| **Bot-to-Bot Handoffs** | The primary bot uses `ctx.agent.delegate({ target: \"dev-bot\", task })` to pass work sequentially instead of both talking at once. |\n| **Cross-Thread Memory Pollution** | **Zero transcript carryover.** Thread 2 starts with a clean context window (\u003c 1,500 tokens). |\n| **Persistent Learning across Threads** | Past achievements are distilled into **atomic vector rules** in the bot\u0027s private memory bank. |\n| **Relevance Filtering** | In a new thread, old facts are retrieved **only if they semantically match the new task** (Stripe debugging never leaks into a CSV script). |",
