 space tools,  subagents etc we need to make skills and cmds for that too right. And the ai should know what order like if it wants to run a simple task it shouldnt try to do complex cmds that will turn on the container.\n\nI can\u0027t shake the feeling that this introduces a lot of problems what are they and how can we fix them\n\n### The 5 Traps \u0026 Production Fixes\n\nTrying to force *everything* (browsers, subagents, workflows, skills) into raw `Bash` commands and dynamic files inside `/agents/:id` creates 5 severe failure modes:\n\n```\n┌──────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ The Failure Mode (The Trap)          │ The Architectural Fix (The Solution)                            │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 1. The \"Bash-Everything\" Blindness   │ **Split \"Hands\" (Bash) from \"Senses\" (Vision/Browser/Delegate)**│\n│    (Browsers \u0026 Subagents via CLI)    │ Multimodal images \u0026 RPC cannot be cleanly piped through stdout. │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 2. The Cloudflare Workflow Trap      │ **The Harness Boilerplate Pattern**                             │\n│    (AI writing raw deploy plumbing)  │ AI writes only `execute(step, ctx)`; platform wraps Entrypoint. │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 3. Git Repository Pollution          │ **`/.avos/` Namespace Separation**                              │\n│    (User repo flooded with bot files)│ User code (`/workspace`) is isolated from bot meta-state.       │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 4. \"Prompt Guilt\" / Cost Paranoia    │ **Transparent Routing (Pyodide Wasm First)**                    │\n│    (AI overthinking TS vs Python)    │ Infrastructure optimizes cost silently; prompt stays neutral.   │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 5. Tool Discovery Token Tax          │ **Catalog-in-Prompt, Code-on-Disk**                             │\n│    (AI running `ls` to find tools)   │ Signatures in system prompt; code loaded only when executed.    │\n└──────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n### Deep Dive on the Solutions:\n\n#### 1. Senses vs. Hands: Why Browser \u0026 Delegate Must Be Distinct Tools\n* `Browser(action, url)` streams visual screenshot buffers and clean accessibility trees (AXTree) **directly into the LLM\u0027s multimodal vision window** in 1 turn. A CLI command would require saving PNGs to disk and running secondary commands to inspect them.\n* `Delegate(role, task)` is a **structured JSON-RPC message**, avoiding fragile text streaming over stdout.\n\n#### 2. The Cloudflare Workflow Harness Pattern\nNever ask an LLM to write `WorkflowEntrypoint\u003cEnv\u003e` or configure `wrangler.jsonc`.\nThe agent writes clean logic:\n```typescript\n// /.avos/shared/workflows/weekly-audit.ts\nexport async function execute(step: WorkflowStep, ctx: WorkflowContext) {\n  const data \u003d await step.do(\"scrape\", () \u003d\u003e ctx.browser.scrape(\"https://site.com\"));\n  await step.sleep(\"wait\", \"6 hours\"); // $0 compute during sleep\n  await step.do(\"notify\", () \u003d\u003e ctx.notify.telegram(\"Done\"));\n}\n```\nWhen called via `schedule --cron \"...\" script.ts`, the platform **Workflow Compiler** automatically wraps it in `WorkflowEntrypoint` and deploys it into the space\u0027s Dynamic Worker namespace.\n\n#### 3. Repository Cleanliness: `/workspace` vs. `/.avos/`\n* `/workspace/`: Tracked by Git Artifacts. Contains **pure application code** (`src/`, `package.json`).\n* `/.avos/`: Internal operating system state (Git-ignored). Contains shared skills (`/.avos/shared/skills/`), workflows, and bot definitions.\n\n#### 4. Removing Prompt Guilt: In-Isolate Python via Pyodide\nNever lecture the model about container costs in the prompt.\n* Pre-bundle Pyodide (Wasm CPython) in Tier 2. `python3 -c \"...\"` runs in Wasm in \u003c10ms for $0.00.\n* If and only if the script imports heavy native libraries (PyTorch, OpenCV), the runtime silently elevates to Tier 6 Containers.\n\n--- END OF FILE 327 - 329 The 5 Traps \u0026 Architectural Fixes.md ---\n```\n\n---\n\n### File 4: `330 - 333 Decoupled Crons, Receipt Cards \u0026 In-Chat Bot Switching.md`\n\n```markdown\n--- START OF FILE 330 - 333 Decoupled Crons, Receipt Cards \u0026 In-Chat Bot Switching.md ---\n\n330 - 333\nWhat if we have the same Hermes autonomous bots but the UI uses same old4 tier threads or something better less confusing but still autonomous bots. Where you can switch bots within a chat . Etc but how would say if one bot makes a Cron to wake up in a chat and do something and you change to another bot while in the chat then the cron bot wakes up should it do work when it\u0027s not selected or should it do and show as a card that it\u0027s a back ground Cron job by x etc and should crons be er bot of project universal with a bot id set to it that it\u0027s runn is not set to a thread it was set or should it live it the thread it was set to not confuse etc? But what if the thread is too big etc\n\n### 1. The Core Architecture: Crons are Project-Universal \u0026 Thread-Decoupled\n\nRunning autonomous crons directly inside a 50-message chat thread causes **context overflow** and **conversation hijacking**.\n\n**The Rules:**\n1. **Crons are Project-Universal:** Registered in `SpaceDO` SQLite (`project_scheduled_jobs`) with an attributed `owner_bot_id`.\n2. **Pristine Execution Context (\u003c2,000 tokens):** Wakes via Cloudflare Workflows with **zero chat history loaded**. It receives only the task payload and target workspace files.\n3. **Inline Receipt Cards:** Posts a 2-line expandable notification card to the thread timeline. It does **not** dump verbose logs into the conversation.\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ 📬 INLINE BACKGROUND JOB RECEIPT CARD (Rendered in Origin Thread or Team Room)                         │\n│ ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │\n│ │ ⏰ **Background Cron Run Completed** (by **@DevBot**)                              [ 09:01 UTC ]   │ │\n│ │ Task: *Weekly Dependency Security Audit*                                                           │ │\n│ │ Status: 🟢 **All 42 packages secure. 0 vulnerabilities found.**                                     │ │\n│ │ [ 🔍 View Full Output Logs ]                               [ 🌿 Inspect Commit `c_8a12` ]          │ │\n│ └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Thread Bloat Defense: \"What If the Thread Is Too Big?\"\n\n1. **Cron Context Decoupling:** Background jobs never inherit chat messages.\n2. **In-Flight Milestone Squashing (`compact_context`):**\n   * When a chat thread exceeds 25 turns (or 30,000 tokens), completed decisions squash into `PROJECT.md` / Project Journal.\n   * Old turns are archived to D1.\n   * Active prompt window retains only: `PROJECT.md` summary + last 4 turns.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Switching Bots Within the Same Chat Thread\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ 💬 THREAD #102: Stripe Billing Integration                                [ 🌿 branch:task_102 ] [ ⚙️ ]│\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 👩‍💻 **Alice:** \"@ReviewBot, please audit @DevBot\u0027s idempotency patch for edge cases.\"                   │\n│                                                                                                         │\n│ 🤖 **@ReviewBot:** \"Auditing `src/api/stripe.ts` diff:                                                  │\n│    - Idempotency table insertion is atomic. Verified 200 OK without double-crediting. Approved.\"        │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 📝 COMPOSER DOCK:                                                                                       │\n│ [ 🤖 Active Operator: @ReviewBot ▼ ]  [ 🔒 Personal Credentials: Off ]                                  │\n│ ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │\n│ │ Type a message, or use @DevBot, @ReviewBot to direct specific tasks...                              │ │\n│ └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n* **Attribution:** Every message in `SpaceDO` carries `sender_id: \"agent_dev_bot\"` or `sender_id: \"usr_alice\"`.\n* **Dropdown Pill:** The bottom composer shows `[ 🤖 Active: @DevBot ▼ ]` to set the default responder without typing `@` every turn.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Database Schema: `project_scheduled_jobs` (`SpaceDO` SQLite)\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS project_scheduled_jobs (\n  id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n  space_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n  project_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n  owner_bot_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n  origin_thread_id TEXT,\n  title TEXT NOT NULL,\n  cron_expression TEXT NOT NULL,\n  workflow_script_path TEXT NOT NULL,\n  payload_json TEXT DEFAULT \u0027{}\u0027,\n  status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT \u0027ACTIVE\u0027 CHECK (status IN (\u0027ACTIVE\u0027, \u0027PAUSED\u0027, \u0027COMPLETED\u0