      "text": "**Reflecting on Developer Burden**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on the burden placed on developers by the current approach. The proposed `CodingAgentStrategy` seems to require excessive low-level coding, involving custom AST parsers and typecheck runners. This level of detail might be overwhelming. I\u0027m reassessing if there\u0027s a way to abstract some of these complexities.\n\n\n**Re-Framing Agent Capabilities**\n\nI\u0027m now thinking about security implications stemming from arbitrary orchestration and deep system access, which seems risky. The focus has shifted toward generalizing the agent\u0027s role. It\u0027s becoming apparent that many tasks are actually background data processing, indexers, graphers, etc., not strictly coding endeavors. The micro-app approach, running in sandboxed environments, seems like an excellent fit for these operations. The key now is the universal agent execution loop, offering customizable steps, hooks, and pipelines, alongside secure Gadget integration for custom harnesses.\n\n\n**Re-imagining Gadget Integration**\n\nI\u0027ve got a much clearer picture of how Gadgets can be the workhorses. The previous concept was too complex. Now, I see them as sandboxed stateful entities that can run virtually anything. Their built-in security and MCP endpoint are a great fit. I\u0027m focusing on a declarative pipeline of steps and hooks, instead of custom loops.\n\n\n**Defining Agent Operations**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on defining agent operations. The core idea involves autonomous Gadgets as background indexers and graphers, functioning like micro-apps. They are isolated, subscribe to workspace changes, and update internal graphs using SQLite. This setup addresses both security concerns and reduces excessive coding, allowing for a diverse range of tasks in various domains, handled independently in sandboxed environments. The agent leverages the `/mcp` endpoint like any other tool, streamlining interactions.\n\n\n**Designing Declarative Workflows**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on how to enable workflow customization. Instead of asking developers to write code, I\u0027m exploring declarative JSON pipelines. These pipelines would define steps like context enrichment using MCP tools (e.g., `find_impact`), agent execution, and verification. The agent could inherently know how to use MCP tools based on attached gadgets, simplifying customization and reducing code complexity. Security is still a priority, with Gatekeepers acting as safeguards.\n\n\n**Refining Gadget Security**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on the security benefits of the Gadget-centric approach. Running Gadgets in V8 isolates, managed by Gatekeepers, is incredibly powerful. This means no unchecked access to the host filesystem, no direct exposure of API keys, and no arbitrary shell execution. This, along with developers being able to build Gadgets quickly using Preact/Hono templates, or even through AI prompts, simplifies the entire architecture. We\u0027ve avoided the previous pitfalls of a bloated framework with this focus on background indexers as autonomous gadgets.\n\n\n",
