      "text": "Your gut feeling is **100% right: giving a Dynamic Workflow subagent the entire operating system (`ctx` with threads, sessions, messages, ladder switching, etc.) is a massive architectural trap.**\n\nIf you force a subagent to manage its own threads, messages, and ladder promotions inside a Cloudflare Workflow, the architecture breaks in four critical ways:\n\n---\n\n### The 4 Fatal Flaws of Over-Engineering the Subagent\n\n1. **The Step Serialization Breakdown (Cloudflare Workflows Rule):**  \n   In Cloudflare Workflows, every value returned or passed across `step.do()` **must be JSON-serializable**. If `ctx` holds stateful Durable Object stubs, WebSocket connections, live file locks, or active thread handlers, Cloudflare Workflows will throw an instant serialization exception.\n2. **The \"Split-Brain\" Thread Chaos:**  \n   If an autonomous subagent running deep inside a background workflow can spawn new threads, post messages, and edit thread state independently, **who owns the conversation?** The user’s UI in `apps/shell` will be flooded with phantom threads that have no clear human owner or lifecycle.\n3. **The Workflow Latency Tax:**  \n   Cloudflare Workflows persist step state to disk between steps. If a subagent needs 3 quick reasoning turns to do a Repomix file selection, running that inside a Workflow step loop takes **15–20 seconds**. Running that exact same task as a plain function inside an in-memory V8 Isolate takes **800 milliseconds**.\n4. **Prompt Hallucination (Asking Too Much of the Creator Agent):**  \n   If you tell an agent: *\"Write a custom subagent that instantiates threads, handles message schemas, escalates execution tiers, and manages session history,\"* the LLM will hallucinate broken plumbing 80% of the time.\n\n---\n\n### How Palantir AIP \u0026 Cloudflare Agents SDK Actually Solve This\n\nNeither Palantir nor Cloudflare gives subagents their own operating systems:\n\n* **Palantir AIP Orchestrator:** Uses the **Ontology Pattern**. The orchestrator decomposes a high-level goal into discrete, typed *Operations* against the system. The subagent is an **anonymous task runner** that receives an input payload, calls 1 or 2 tools, and returns a structured output. It has zero knowledge of user sessions or UI.\n* **Cloudflare Agents SDK (`Agent\u003cEnv\u003e`):** The **Agent lives in the Durable Object** (handling state, memory, and WebSockets). The **Workflow is just a tool** that the Agent triggers when it needs to run a task that takes hours or needs to sleep (`step.sleep`).\n\n---\n\n# The Elegant Solution: Subagents as \"Functional Tasks,\" Not \"Virtual Citizens\"\n\nThe golden architectural rule for AVOS:  \n**Subagents are VERBS (ephemeral execution functions). Only the Parent Agent is a NOUN (the persistent entity that owns threads and talks to humans).**\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                                   THE ASYMMETRIC SUBAGENT TOPOLOGY                                     │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 🏛️ PARENT AGENT (`NaelaoAgentDO` in Durable Object)                                                    │\n│ • Owns the Human Thread, WebSockets, and UI Receipt Cards.                                            │\n│ • Decides: \"I need to run the Repomix Chunked Builder on this repository.\"                            │\n│ • Launches the Workflow: `ctx.workflows.start(\"repomix-builder\", { chunk: \"01\" })`                    │\n└───────────────────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                                                    │\n                                                    ▼\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ ⚡ TIER 3 CLOUDFLARE WORKFLOW: `repomix-builder.ts` (Clean, Pure Function)                             │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ • Receives a **Slimmed-Down `ctx`** (Only what it needs to execute the task).                          │\n│ • Has ZERO access to thread creation, message trees, or user sessions.                                │\n│ • Step 1: `step.do(\"map\", () \u003d\u003e ctx.cli.exec(\"repomix --compress\"))`                                  │\n│ • Step 2: `step.do(\"select\", () \u003d\u003e ctx.ai.selectFiles(map, chunk))`                                   │\n│ • Step 3: `step.do(\"implement\", () \u003d\u003e ctx.ai.generateCode(targetFiles, chunk))`                       │\n│ • Step 4: `step.do(\"commit\", () \u003d\u003e ctx.fs.writeAndCommit(files))`                                     │\n│ • Returns clean JSON: `{ success: true, commitSha: \"c_8a12\", filesChanged: 3 }`                        │\n└───────────────────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                                                    │\n                                                    ▼\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ 📬 PARENT AGENT POSTS INLINE RECEIPT CARD                                                              │\n│ • Parent receives the Workflow completion event over internal RPC.                                     │\n│ • Renders a 2-line expandable card in the origin thread: `[ ⏰ Repomix Chunk 01 Applied: c_8a12 ]`     │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n### What the Slimmed-Down `ctx` for Subagents Looks Like\n\nWhen a Dynamic Workflow subagent runs, it receives **`SubagentContext`**, which strips out all the bloated session and thread mechanics:\n\n```typescript\n// packages/types/src/subagent-context.ts\nexport 