      "text": "**Defining the System\u0027s Scope**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on defining the system\u0027s scope, specifically for AVOS. I\u0027m exploring how micro-apps and gadgets, using DO Facet SQLite databases, integrate into the overall structure. It\u0027s key to align this with the user\u0027s vision for Autonomous Ventures and the vertical coworking operating system.\n\n\n**Analyzing Workflow Tiers**\n\nI\u0027m now diving into the specifics of dynamic workflow tiering, considering long-running tasks and vertical cowork operations. I\u0027m brainstorming additional tiers beyond the initial suggestions of one-off tasks, scheduled tasks, project \"infects,\" and vertical cowork functions. I\u0027m also revisiting the micro-app tiers for better formalization, thinking of personal task-solving apps, canvas extensions, and background extensions.\n\n\n**Addressing Potential Risks**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on risk mitigation related to the multi-tiered workflows and micro-apps. I\u0027m actively considering edge cases, potential failure modes, and race conditions, especially regarding billing traps and runaway execution. Limits and quotas per subscription plan need careful consideration, alongside developer pricing structures. We need to decide if devs feel like owners or FDEs, and it\u0027s pivotal for our business model.\n\n\n**Formalizing Workflow Tiers**\n\nI\u0027m now formalizing the workflow tiers. I\u0027ve broken down \"Run-to-Completion\" tasks, like document ingestion or database migrations, into Tier 1. Tier 2 encompasses \"Cadenced Scheduled Rhythms\" using cron jobs or sweepers. \"Event-Driven Reactive Watchers,\" which include project sentinels triggered by webhooks or file modifications, constitute Tier 3. I\u0027m also now analyzing \"Long-Horizon Enterprise Daemons\" for M\u0026A processes, those are now designated Tier 4.\n\n\n**Refining Micro-App Structures**\n\nI\u0027m currently revising the micro-app structures with a sharper focus. I\u0027m aligning with the user\u0027s input, breaking them into three distinct tiers. Tier 1 encompasses personal, user-scoped gadgets with private SQLite databases. Tier 2 contains collaborative canvas extensions and interactive viewports, shared across project members. I\u0027m also planning Cloudflare primitives for approval workflows.\n\n\n**Expanding Micro-App Tiers**\n\nI\u0027m expanding the micro-app tiers, adding Tier 3: Background Intelligence Extensions. These headless daemons, like indexers and graphers, subscribe to file changes, building internal SQLite relational graphs. They also auto-expose stateless MCP v2 endpoints for agent interaction. I am also focusing on addressing potential pitfalls like runaway execution and state synchronization desync, with solutions like step timeouts, circuit breakers, and commit-based workflow design.\n\n\n**Addressing Storage Limitations**\n\nI am now tackling storage constraints within the micro-app environment. Specifically, I\u0027m focusing on \"Gadget Database Bloat,\" considering solutions like ephemeral Time-To-Live (TTL) values for Tier 1 personal gadgets and storage quota management enforced at the per-plan level, like a 50MB limit per DO Facet. I\u0027m also addressing the \"Dormant Secret Expiration\" problem in Tier 4 workflows, implementing a Universal Vault Layer to refresh OAuth tokens at the edge worker upon wake-up. I will introduce changes as a Git Branch + PR Proposal Card or through Atomic SQLite Hash Checks (OCC). Lastly, I\u0027m planning to allocate quotas for both default and add-on workflows, along with micro-apps per subscription plan.\n\n\n**Evaluating Subscription Tiers**\n\nI\u0027m now determining plan breakdowns, I\u0027ve broken them into Starter, Pro, and Enterprise tiers. Starter has limited workflows and storage, Pro allows more concurrent workflows and storage, and Enterprise offers unlimited capabilities with dedicated isolation.\n\n\n\n**Defining the Developer Pipeline**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on how developers transition from FDEs to Vertical SaaS owners. The vision is for devs to start with bespoke consulting, then export tested workflows as templates for recurring revenue. The key is allowing developers to charge up front and earn compounding MRR.\n\n\n",
