      "text": "Structuring **Dynamic Workflows** and **Micro-Apps (Gadgets)** into distinct tiers gives your platform a dual-engine architecture:\n* **Micro-Apps (Gadgets)** handle **State \u0026 Perception** (sub-millisecond relational storage, real-time UI viewports, and background indexers).\n* **Dynamic Workflows** handle **Time \u0026 Durability** (multi-hour batch runs, crons, sleeping state machines, and multi-day enterprise sagas).\n\nHere is the complete architectural blueprint detailing the workflow tiers, the formalized micro-app tiers, their failure modes and solutions, plan quotas, and the strategic answer to **\"Vertical SaaS Owner vs. Forward Deployed Engineer.\"**\n\n---\n\n# 1. The 4-Tier Dynamic Workflow Hierarchy (Long-Running Execution)\n\nJust as micro-apps have tiers, **Cloudflare Dynamic Workflows (`WorkflowEntrypoint`)** are structured into four operational tiers based on execution duration, trigger cadence, and state durability:\n\n```\n┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                                   THE 4-TIER DYNAMIC WORKFLOW HIERARCHY                                   │\n├────────────────────┬─────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┤\n│ Tier Level         │ Trigger \u0026 Cadence       │ Execution Lifecycle        │ Primary Purpose \u0026 Verticals   │\n├────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Tier 1: Job**    │ **On-Demand / One-Off** │ **Runs to Completion**     │ Heavy batch migrations, bulk  │\n│ **Runners**        │ (Manual or Agent-called)│ (10s – 15 minutes)         │ Parse 5 ingestion, PDF export │\n├────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Tier 2: Cadenced │ **Cron-Scheduled**      │ **Periodic Sweeper**       │ Daily 08:00 revenue audits,   │\n│ **Rhythms**        │ (`0 9 * * *`, weekly)   │ Wakes, executes, sleeps $0 │ nightly docket/court scrapers │\n├────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Tier 3: Sentinel │ **Event-Driven Webhook**│ **Reactive Sentry**        │ Port vessel arrivals, Stripe  │\n│ **Reactors**       │ (Inbound queues/events) │ Pauses for human review    │ charge disputes, lead intake  │\n├────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Tier 4: Multi-Day│ **Long-Horizon Sagas**  │ **Multi-Week Saga State**  │ M\u0026A Due Diligence audit,      │\n│ **Enterprise Sagas** (Multi-step approvals)  │ Days/weeks via `step.sleep`│ Subcontractor lien clearance  │\n└────────────────────┴─────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Mechanics for Each Workflow Tier:\n\n#### Workflow Tier 1: One-Off Job Runners (Batch Processing)\n* **What it is:** Triggered when an agent or user kicks off a heavy compute task that exceeds standard HTTP timeouts.\n* **Vertical Examples:**\n  * *Legal Cowork:* Ingesting a 500-page historical lease archive with Cohere Parse 5.\n  * *Finance Studio:* Running 10,000 Monte Carlo simulations on a port asset valuation.\n* **Lifecycle:** Runs sequentially through `step.do()`, serializes progress, writes output files to `/workspace`, and terminates.\n\n#### Workflow Tier 2: Cadenced Rhythms (Scheduled Maintenance \u0026 Crons)\n* **What it is:** Project-universal scheduled jobs that execute on exact cron expressions.\n* **Vertical Examples:**\n  * *Healthcare Cowork:* Waking daily at 10:00 AM to scan tomorrow’s appointments and dispatch confirmation calls via `@cloudflare/voice`.\n  * *Construction Studio:* Checking municipal zoning amendments every Monday at 06:00 AM.\n* **Lifecycle:** Wakes from dormant storage, executes in **pristine context (\u003c2,000 tokens)**, posts an expandable **Receipt Card** in the Team Room, and sleeps at **$0 compute cost**.\n\n#### Workflow Tier 3: Sentinel Reactors (Event-Driven Watchers \u0026 Elicitation)\n* **What it is:** A reactive background listener that wakes when external events hit Cloudflare Queues or webhooks.\n* **Vertical Examples:**\n  * *Logistics Cowork:* Wakes when a shipping line emits a `container.discharged` webhook, verifies the customs clearance status via `gadget_customs_matrix`, and alerts the broker.\n* **Lifecycle:** Can pause mid-execution using **MCP v2 Elicitation**, waiting days for human input (*\"Demurrage fee $500 detected. Approve payment?\"*) and resuming execution instantly upon webhook response.\n\n#### Workflow Tier 4: Multi-Day Enterprise Sagas (Cross-Subspace Orchestrators)\n* **What it is:** A resilient workflow spanning days or weeks, orchestrating multiple sub-spaces and human sign-offs.\n* **Vertical Examples:**\n  * *Corporate M\u0026A Deal Room:* A 14-day saga coordinating Legal, Accounting, and Board approval:\n    1. Day 1: Parse data room documents.\n    2. Day 3: Wait for General Counsel sign-off (`step.sleep`).\n    3. Day 7: Dispatch audit tasks to the Accounting Subspace via Gatekeeper capability tokens.\n    4. Day 14: Compile closing binders and execute fund transfers.\n\n---\n\n# 2. The Formalized 3-Tier Micro-App (Gadget) Hierarchy\n\nMicro-apps run as **Cloudflare OS Gadgets** inside sandboxed Dynamic Worker isolates with private DO Facet SQLite databases:\n\n```\n┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                                   THE 3-TIER MICRO-APP (GADGET) HIERARCHY                                 │\n├────────────────────┬─────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┤\n│ Tier Level         │ Boundary \u0026 Scoping      │ State \u0026 Database           │ Primary Purpose \u0026 Form Factor │\n├────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Tier 1: Personal │ **User-Private**        │ Private DO Facet SQLite    │ Ephemeral scratchpad tools,   │\n│ **Task Micro-Apps**│ (Only 1 human sees it)  │ Clamped storage (25MB)     │ Pomodoro timers, private notes│\n├────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Tier 2: Canvas   │ **Project-Bound**       │ Shared DO Facet SQLite     │ Interactive workspace UI      │\n│ **Extensions**     │ (Whole project team)    │ Real-time MCP-UI streaming │ (Redliner, Finance Grid, Comps│\n├────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Tier 3: Stateful │ **Headless / System**   │ Persistent Relational DB   │ Real-time symbol graphs, GL   │\n│ **Indexers/Graphs**│ (Auto-exposes `/mcp`)   │ High-throughput SQLite     │ ledgers, statute cross-refs   │\n└────────────────────┴─────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n# 3. Critical Failure Modes \u0026 How to Fix Them\n\nCombining multi-tiered workflows with stateful micro-apps creates **four major architectural traps** if not bounded properly:\n\n```\n┌──────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ The Failure Mode (The Trap)          │ The Architectural Fix (The Solution)                            │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 1. The Runaway Zombie Loop           │ **Execution Step Ceilings \u0026 Timeouts**                          │\n│    (Workflows looping forever)       │ Workflows enforce 50-step ceilings \u0026 per-step timeouts (5 mins).│\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 2. Concurrent State Clobbering       │ **The Git PR \u0026 OCC Checkpoint Rule**                            │\n│    (Workflows editing files live)    │ Workflows NEVER edit `/workspace` files live; they commit PRs!  │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 3. Gadget Storage Explosion          │ **Quota Caps \u0026 Ephemeral TTLs**                                 │\n│    (Thousands of dead SQLite DBs)    │ Tier 1 gadgets auto-delete after 14 days of silence; 50MB caps. │\n├──────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 4. Stale Token Expiration            │ **Outbound Worker Edge Refresh**                                │\n│    (Multi-day workflows wake with    │ Vault automatically refreshes OAuth tokens at the edge proxy    │\n│     expired OAuth tokens)            │ boundary upon wake-up.                                          │\n└──────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Deep Dive on the Solutions:\n\n#### Fix 1: Preventing Runaway Workflows\n* Every workflow execution carries an immutable step counter: `maxSteps \u003d 50`. If an autonomous workflow loops more than 50 times without terminating, the runtime forces a `FAILED_SAFETY_LIMIT` state and pings the space administrator on Telegram.\n* Every `step.do()` call has an enforced 5-minute timeout.\n\n#### Fix 2: Preventing Concurrent State Clobbering\n* **The Rule:** An automated background workflow or gadget is **never allowed to write directly to the active `main` workspace branch**.\n* If a Tier 2 nightly workflow updates client records or refactors code, it writes changes to an isolated branch: `branch:cron_update_20260905`.\n* It opens an inline **Pull Request Card** in the Team Room. The human reviews the diff and approves it with one click.\n\n#### Fix 3: Managing Gadget Database Bloat\n* Tier 1 personal gadgets carry an automatic **14-day idle TTL**. If unopened for 14 days, the DO Facet SQLite database is archived to cold R2 storage.\n* Tier 2 and Tier 3 gadgets have enforced SQLite database size limits (50MB on Starter, 250MB on Pro).\n\n#### Fix 4: Resolving Stale Credentials in Multi-Day Sagas\n* When a Tier 4 workflow sleeps for 7 days via `step.sleep(\"wait-step\", \"7 days\")`, any OAuth access tokens stored in memory are guaranteed to be dead when it wakes.\n* **The Solution:** The workflow script never holds raw tokens. It calls `ctx.vault.fetchWithAuth()`. The **Outbound Worker** automatically detects token expiration, calls the OAuth refresh endpoint in 50ms at the network boundary, and passes the fresh authorized request through.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Quotas \u0026 Limits per Subscription Plan\n\nTo ensure your platform maintains a **92%+ gross profit margin**, allocate workflows and micro-apps across your subscription tiers:\n\n```\n┌───────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────┬───────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Plan Tier                             │ Monthly Cost │ Dynamic Workflow Limits       │ Stateful Micro-App (Gadget) Caps│\n├───────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Free Tier (BYOK Hacker)**           │ **$0 / mo**  │ • Tier 1 Jobs only (Max 3/day)│ • 1 Active Gadget (Tier 1 only) │\n│                                       │              │ • Zero background crons       │ • 10MB SQLite storage           │\n├───────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Starter Plan (Solo Builder)**       │ **$29 / mo** │ • 2 Concurrent Workflows      │ • 3 Active Project Gadgets      │\n│                                       │              │ • 3 Scheduled Crons (Tier 2)  │ • 1 Background Indexer (Tier 3) │\n│                                       │              │ • Max 1-day sleep limit       │ • 50MB SQLite storage per DO    │\n├───────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Pro Plan (Venture Studio)**         │ **$99 / mo** │ • 10 Concurrent Workflows     │ • 15 Active Project Gadgets     │\n│                                       │              │ • 15 Scheduled Crons (Tier 2) │ • 5 Background Indexers (Tier 3)│\n│                                       │              │ • Full Multi-Day Sagas (T4)   │ • 250MB SQLite storage per DO   │\n├───────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Enterprise (Holding Company)**      │ **$299+/mo** │ • Unlimited Workflows         │ • Unlimited Gadgets \u0026 Indexers  │\n│                                       │              │ • Unlimited Crons \u0026 Sagas     │ • Dedicated DO Storage Pools    │\n└───────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────┴───────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n# 5. The Strategic Playbook: \"Vertical SaaS Owners\" vs. \"Forward Deployed Engineers (FDEs)\"\n\nYou asked: *\"Should it make it so that they feel like vertical SaaS owners or just FDEs? What\u0027s better?\"*\n\n### The Answer: Build the **\"FDE-to-Vertical-SaaS Graduation Ladder\"**.\n\nNeither archetype is sufficient on its own:\n* **The Problem with Pure Vertical SaaS:** Solo developers rarely succeed selling self-serve SaaS to traditional industries (dentists, law firms, general contractors) because traditional business owners **refuse to configure complex software on their own**.\n* **The Problem with Pure FDE Consulting:** Forward Deployed Engineering commands massive upfront fees ($10,000–$50,000), but it doesn\u0027t scale. If you are only an FDE, you are selling your time.\n\n### The Winning Model: The \"Foundry Agency\" Pipeline\n\nYour platform should empower developers to operate on a **3-stage graduation ladder**:\n\n```\n                  ┌──