      "text": "**Defining Automation Categories**\n\nI\u0027m currently focused on defining the taxonomy for classifying automations. I\u0027m distinguishing between company/business automations (operational pipelines, system-wide processes) and other types. This initial categorization provides a solid foundation for further differentiation within the automation landscape.\n\n\n**Categorizing Workflow Types**\n\nI\u0027m now refining the automation categorization further. I\u0027ve broken workflows down into three key types: financial rollups and automated operations, project extensions like CI/CD plugins, and task executions such as web scraping jobs. This distinction is crucial because the Space Assistant (CEO) requires different levels of oversight, failure alerts, and access controls for each type. For instance, a company automation failure necessitates immediate attention, unlike a task job.\n\n\n**Formalizing Workflow Taxonomy**\n\nI\u0027m formalizing the taxonomy. Class 1 includes company-wide automations, alerting the Space Assistant/Founder directly. Class 2, the project extension workflows, are bound to a project and post alerts to the project team room. Finally, Class 3 encompasses ephemeral task workflows that report back to the initiating agent. Each has a specific owner, scope, and purpose which is well defined now.\n\n\n**Defining On-Demand Sagas**\n\nI\u0027m working on the definition and application of on-demand sagas. These workflows are user-initiated, task-specific, and handle complex processes that exceed typical HTTP constraints, such as web scraping or video distillation. Their alerts come in the form of JSON structured reports, posted as inline cards within the relevant threads.\n\n\n",
