      "text": "**Defining the Core Feature**\n\nI\u0027m zeroing in on the fundamental functionality: automating tasks across different applications, like Gmail and Instagram, using Dynamic Workflows and Micro-Apps via connectors, specifically `ctx.connectors` or `ctx.mcp` / `ctx.vault`. I\u0027m focusing on the bridging aspect between those third-party apps and the core automation framework.\n\n\n**Evaluating Engine Trade-offs**\n\nI\u0027m now wrestling with the Composio versus Pipedream debate. The core is the MCP-native nature, edge-first approach, fine-grained actions, and the OAuth lifecycle. My mind is now also considering the implications of trigger vs. action models and their suitability. I\u0027m also grappling with the connector-level permissions and roles, envisioning how to enforce Read/Write access post-OAuth connection, balancing user needs and security. Finally, it\u0027s clear I need to address Context Tool Bloat, Inbound Triggers, Partial Failures and Thrid-Party API bans.\n\n\n**Refining Connector Integrations**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on streamlining user connections to third-party apps within Dynamic Workflows and Micro-Apps. Specifically, my thought process revolves around a seamless connection process: a halting action with an interactive popup if a connector is not yet linked, and granting access only to permissible tools. I\u0027m also re-evaluating the choice between Composio and Pipedream for handling OAuth, considering which offers better automation support.\n\n\n**Defining Trigger Handling**\n\nMy focus has shifted to designing a robust trigger system. While I\u0027m sticking with Composio for agent tool calling and managed authentication, I\u0027m now planning a clean Edge Webhook Ingestion Plane on Cloudflare for inbound triggers. Composio\u0027s trigger webhooks will route to Cloudflare Queues for streamlined integration. I\u0027m prioritizing building an efficient and reliable way to handle these trigger events within the architecture.\n\n\n**Implementing Access Control**\n\nI\u0027m now designing fine-grained RBAC for connectors in Dynamic Workflows. I\u0027m focusing on a Gatekeeper layer using Cloudflare Workers to enforce ACL policies assigned to connectors in `SpaceDO`. This will involve intercepting tool calls to validate permissions before they reach Composio, ensuring secure and controlled access based on defined roles and allowed/denied actions, such as preventing email sending based on a user\u0027s role.\n\n\n**Addressing Scope and Idempotency**\n\nI\u0027m now grappling with \"Silent Disconnect\" issues from scope mismatches and addressing idempotency. The plan: implement scope-aware pre-flight validation and incremental re-authentication. I\u0027m also planning to tackle the risk of duplicate executions from webhook replay storms. I\u0027ll need to define a solution that considers the failure modes of Zapier and Make, to create autonomous AI robustness.\n\n\n**Tackling Common Automation Failures**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on failure scenarios, specifically duplicated actions due to network retries, and designing solutions. I\u0027m focusing on the *idempotency-key* to prevent repeated emails or charges from automated retries. I\u0027m also addressing the infinite self-triggering loops with a global event counter and origin attribution. Then, I am focusing on schema drift with runtime JSON schema validation and an LLM-powered repair mechanism.\n\n\n**Clarifying Account Routing**\n\nI\u0027m now prioritizing scenarios with multiple connected accounts, such as choosing between `support@`, `billing@`, and `ceo@` when sending an email. My plan is to add explicit connection tagging, enabling alias routing, for example: `ctx.connectors.gmail.using(\"support@\")`.\n\n\\n\\n\n\n**Protecting Sensitive Data**\n\nI\u0027m also tackling data security, particularly the risk of accidental PII leakage. The strategy is to integrate Egress DLP filters into Outbound Workers. These filters will scan outgoing payloads for sensitive information like credit card numbers, SSNs, and private tokens before data leaves the system.\n\n\\n\\n\n\n**Managing Unattended Authentication**\n\nFinally, I\u0027m focusing on unattended authentication flows. I\u0027m focusing on solving the issue where background jobs requiring missing connections can\u0027t show a popup. The workflow will pause into a `PENDING_AUTH` state and trigger a notification.\n\n\n",
