      {
      "text": "**Defining the Architecture**\n\nI\u0027m currently focused on defining the core architecture for a 3-Tier Micro-App system leveraging Cloudflare\u0027s capabilities. I\u0027m prioritizing a clean, unified structure and exploring how to effectively integrate with the newest Cloudflare primitives. I am striving to achieve an architecture for an OS.\n\n\n**Refining the Tiers**\n\nI\u0027ve been drilling down into the specific components of each tier within the 3-Tier Micro-App system. I am clarifying the role of \"Project Canvas Viewports\" as workspace-bound UI extensions with direct access to the project filesystem. Stateless Artifacts, now defined as ephemeral snapshots, are leveraging Cloudflare Artifacts for versioning. Lastly, I\u0027m focusing on \"Cloudflare OS Gadgets\" as persistent, full-stack micro-apps.\n\n\n**Detailing the Gadgets**\n\nI\u0027m now deep-diving into the specifics of Cloudflare OS gadgets. Each will use a private SQLite database. A dynamic worker loader will handle V8 isolate backend logic, with a stateless MCP v2 Server exposing `/mcp` for agent automation. The MCP-UI will allow for interactive user actions. I\u0027m building out a granular installation and scoping hierarchy.\n\n\n**Exploring Cloudflare OS**\n\nI\u0027m currently focused on Cloudflare\u0027s 2026 OS release, particularly the \"Gadgets\" concept. The idea of isolated app instances, or \"Gadgets,\" for each document or tool sparks a lot of thoughts. Each Gadget is a sandboxed micro-app, offering intriguing possibilities. My focus right now is on understanding how this design might influence future app development and user interaction.\n\n\n**Analyzing Gadget Architecture**\n\nI\u0027m now diving into the details of the \"Gadget\" architecture, considering isolated iframes that communicate via Cap\u0027n Web RPC to server code. This approach creates a strong security boundary, preventing data leaks across gadgets. I\u0027m especially interested in how this isolation helps with sharing, which is handled at the platform level. My thoughts have moved on to blueprints: shareable Gadget templates. Finally, exploring security models, focusing on gatekeepers and scoping of these \"gadgets.\"\n\n\n**Defining Project Canvas Tiers**\n\nI\u0027m now clarifying the tiers within the project canvas viewports. Tier 1 involves direct access to the project\u0027s virtual file system. Tier 2 focuses on versioned artifacts such as rendered HTML previews, and Tier 3 explores on-demand stateful micro-apps. Each Gadget has its own isolated SQLite database. Also, the UI communicates over MCP-UI to a dynamic worker sandbox. Lastly, they expose an agent-friendly MCP v2 API so agents can collaborate inside the app.\n\n\n",
