      "text": "**Clarifying Hermes AI**\n\nI\u0027m currently focused on grasping the essence of Hermes AI\u0027s \"Bot Mode.\" Specifically, I\u0027m working to understand its core function within the broader Hermes AI ecosystem, particularly concerning multi-bot persona architecture. I\u0027m keen to dissect its architecture to understand how it operates.\n\n\n**Defining Hermes Bot Mode**\n\nI\u0027m now diving into the core of Hermes \"Bot Mode.\" It\u0027s becoming clear that a \"Bot\" in this context is essentially a dedicated profile, holding all the necessary configurations, memories, skills, and credentials. The configuration resides within a structured file path (`~/.hermes/profiles/\u003cname\u003e/`), which will be instrumental in the next phase of this.\n\n\n**Visualizing Bot Interactions**\n\nNow, I\u0027m fleshing out how bots will interact visually. A dedicated \"Bots\" pane will showcase named bots with previews and statuses. Each bot gets its own 1-on-1 chat. Moreover, I\u0027m thinking about bot groups where 2-6 bots can collaborate in a room. They\u0027ll have turn-taking, direct messaging, and the option to `@mention` in serial rounds per message. This includes agent inboxes for direct bot-to-bot comms. Identity is defined via files and memory scoped per bot.\n\n\n**Structuring Bot Memory**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on bot memory structures. I\u0027ve considered private, isolated memory for each specialist, a shared bank for each room or project to facilitate team memory, and a hybrid approach combining both. I\u0027m exploring the implications of these options, considering the benefits and trade-offs of each.\n\n**Integrating with AVOS Architecture**\n\nI\u0027m now investigating the integration of Hermes Bot Mode within the AVOS Cloudflare architecture. I\u0027m considering user questions about project integration, compatibility with the 4-tier thread structure, workspace sharing, and how to give each bot its unique identity, skills, and model connections. I\u0027m taking into account turn-taking, group chats, bot inboxes and credit tracking as edge cases.\n\n**Defining the Agent Entity**\n\nI\u0027m synthesizing the complete design, starting with the Universal Bot Entity. I\u0027m mapping Hermes Profiles to Cloudflare Durable Object Actors. I\u0027ve defined a Bot in Hermes as a profile, and now I\u0027m connecting it to an AVOS Persistent `NaelaoAgentDO` Persona Definition. I\u0027m considering storage using `SpaceDO` SQLite and R2.\n\n\n**Mapping Bot Profiles**\n\nI\u0027m now mapping the structure of individual bot profiles. I\u0027m focusing on attributes like the bot\u0027s unique identifier (e.g., `dev_bot`), its `SOUL.md` file (defining personality), attached scripts in the `skills/` directory, the pinned model (e.g., `@cf/meta/llama-3.3-70b`), the hybrid memory structure (private and shared), and the custom avatar. I\u0027m also fleshing out how the 4-Tier Thread Architecture supports bot functionality in the project team room. I\u0027m analyzing how direct `@mentions` and the round-robin arbitration function.\n\n\n**Clarifying Tiered Bot Interactions**\n\nI\u0027m currently elaborating on the tiered bot interaction model. Tier 3 now includes persistent 1-on-1 \"Agent Inboxes\" for direct bot-to-bot messaging. Tier 4 defines the \"Canonical Bot Chat\" as the primary 1-on-1 interface, similar to Hermes. This setup includes a scratchpad for each bot accessed via the bot roster.\n\n**Defining Workspace Coordination**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on how multiple bots will share files without creating collisions within the workspace. I\u0027m fleshing out read and write operations within the project\u0027s artifact repository. When writing, a bot will either create a dedicated Git branch or use atomic OCC hash-checked edit patches, ensuring orderly file coordination with a pull request handshake.\n\n\n**Addressing Edge Cases**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on unmentioned edge cases and developing robust solutions. The first addresses the potential \"ping-pong loop trap\" in bot group chats, where I\u0027ll enforce `max_deliberation_rounds \u003d 2` to prevent runaway token budgets. I\u0027m also planning for multi-model diversity within a single room, and, finally, will be tracking compute and token spend broken down per bot.\n\n\n",
