      "text": "**Defining Connector Scope**\n\nI\u0027m currently focused on how users will define the scope of their MCP connectors. The core concept is that a user explicitly selects whether a connector is **`PERSONAL`** or **`SHARED`** upon adding it (e.g., Gmail, Google Calendar, etc.). This binary choice will govern how the data from that connector is accessed and utilized within the system.\n\n\n**Clarifying Shared Contexts**\n\nI\u0027m now refining the behavior of connectors in shared environments. Specifically, personal connections will be disabled in public channels to prioritize user privacy and security. If a user tries to use a disabled personal connector in a public context, the AI will provide an interactive bridge to initiate the task in a secure, private scratchpad. This allows the user to still use the tool but prevents their personal connections from running in public channels.\n\n\n**Refining Private Scratchpad Flow**\n\nI\u0027ve been working on the handoff process to Tier 4 Private Scratchpads. When a user clicks to open in a private scratchpad, I\u0027m thinking about exactly *what* context is transferred to the new thread. It will create a new private scratchpad. I\u0027m also examining edge cases, UI/UX flows, and security requirements to finalize the personal vs. shared connector and forking system. The focus is making the transition seamless.\n\n\n**Defining Handoff Logic**\n\nI\u0027m now diving into the specifics of the handoff to private scratchpads and the subsequent return of sanitized results. Specifically, I\u0027m focusing on the context transfer: the original prompt and relevant project context should move over. Bob\u0027s data and other private info from team members won\u0027t be leaked. In the new private thread, Alice\u0027s Gmail connection is active, allowing for immediate task execution.\n\nI\u0027m also clarifying the \"Return Handoff\" process. Once a personal task is completed, I\u0027m ensuring there\u0027s a clear mechanism for sharing the results back to the team—without sharing any sensitive data. Action cards like \"Share Summary\" will be generated for ease of use.\n\n\n**Envisioning Ephemeral Actions**\n\nI\u0027m developing a method for one-time delegated actions using personal tokens without compromising privacy. I envision an approval flow where users like Alice can grant permission for specific actions within team channels through a private confirmation modal. This uses an \"Elicitation / Gatekeeper One-Time Approval Card\" to facilitate secure, single-use token grants, ensuring data visibility is strictly controlled. The \"apps/shell\" settings will include the ability to toggle connector scopes.\n\n\n**Implementing Connector Auditing**\n\nI\u0027m now implementing detailed audit logs for connector access. I\u0027ll log timestamps, bot names, thread IDs, and actions for each instance a bot uses a connector. A one-click revoke button for instant credential revocation is also being added for all edge workers.\n\nI\u0027m also considering bot-specific accounts and how to avoid confusion. If a shared account is active, the agent needs clear indicators: `gmail:company:support` in public, `gmail:personal:alice` in private. I\u0027m focusing on hardware-level tool schema stripping to prevent prompt injection, physically omitting personal tools in public channels.\n\n\n**Addressing Background Tasks**\n\nI\u0027m now focused on safeguarding personal credentials. My immediate concern involves scheduled background tasks; personal credentials must be inaccessible to unattended workflows unless a delegation lease is explicitly granted. The goal is to enforce stringent security and privacy protocols for these unattended operations.\n\n\n",
