      "text": "**Assessing Initial Capabilities**\n\nI\u0027m currently focused on evaluating the potential for this setup to emulate desktop functionalities. My initial assessment centers on whether a Tier 6 Container can truly offer Grokbot/Computer Use parity. This includes virtual displays, simulated user input, screen capture, and software installation.\n\n\n**Analyzing Complex Systems**\n\nI\u0027m now diving deeper, deconstructing the questions into manageable parts. My focus is on supporting multiple specialized containers seamlessly. I\u0027m wrestling with the intricacies of integrating varied containers (Python, Rust, Android, GUI) within a unified virtual filesystem. The goal is a unified workspace, but the challenge is simplicity. I\u0027m also exploring monetization strategies for tiered container usage. Furthermore, the competitive landscape with Grokbot is being actively analyzed.\n\n\n**Implementing Virtual Desktop**\n\nI\u0027ve been working on a virtual desktop implementation within the ephemeral Linux container. I\u0027m focusing on VNC/noVNC web interfaces and WebSocket frame grabbers for screen access. I plan to expose tools like `mouse_move`, `mouse_click`, and `type_text` through API endpoints in `container/server.mjs`. I\u0027m exploring the best approach for integrating these within AVOS, specifically how to connect the virtual desktop to Project Kinds and utilize a `VirtualDesktopCanvas` within the shell.\n\n\n**Orchestrating Container Access**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on a multi-container environment for each project, and I\u0027m tackling the synchronization challenge. The core problem is that a project needs three types of containers (GUI, Python, Rust), and all of them need to access the same project files without conflict. I\u0027m exploring an \"ArtifactFS / FUSE Hub Pattern\" to solve it, where each container works on its own ephemeral mount or sub-branch within the same file system, syncing with a central, Cloudflare-based data store.\n\n\n**Defining Billing Mechanisms**\n\nI\u0027m now deep in defining the billing system. My plan involves a tiered structure, starting with a base platform and metered billing for Tier 6 containers based on active wall time. Standard containers will cost 5 credits per minute, and heavier ones 25. I\u0027ve designed a pre-authorization system using `SpaceDO.wallet_ledger` to manage credit holds before spawning. My current focus is on strategies to reduce the Cloudflare bill through lazy spawning, aggressive hibernation after 90 seconds of inactivity, and optimizing resource allocation.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Efficiency \u0026 Cost**\n\nI\u0027m now prioritizing cost reduction and efficiency in the architectural design. My new plan involves completing 90% of tasks within Tier 1 SQLite FS and Tier 2 V8 Isolates, which is much cheaper. Only if binary or GUI is strictly necessary will I escalate to a container. I\u0027m also focusing on an \"ArtifactFS Lazy Hydration\" strategy to prevent downloading entire repositories into container disk.\n\n\n",
