  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     §05 — SPACE ASSISTANTS
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="sec" id="assistants">
    <span class="sec-ey">05 — Space Assistants</span>
    <h2>Space Assistants — Full OpenClaw Architecture, CF-Native</h2>
    <div class="bar"></div>
    <p class="lead">Space Assistants are the most complex and powerful feature in Naelao. They clone the full OpenClaw
      architecture — prompts, harness, self-modification, sub-agent spawning — but run on Cloudflare-native
      infrastructure: Durable Objects for the brain, on-demand containers for execution, R2 for the filesystem, Dynamic
      Workflows for self-modification. One assistant per user per Space by default. Admin can raise this limit.</p>

    <div class="pull">"The Space Assistant is OpenClaw, but it lives on Cloudflare instead of your Mac mini. It has the
      same powers — and more."</div>

    <h3>What OpenClaw Architecture Naelao Clones</h3>
    <p>OpenClaw (from the openclaw.ai project) is a self-hosted AI assistant that runs continuously on local hardware.
      Key architectural properties that Naelao adopts:</p>
    <ul class="fl">
      <li><strong>Continuous existence</strong> — OpenClaw doesn't start when you open a chat. It's always running,
        monitoring channels, taking scheduled actions. Naelao's AssistantDO achieves this via DO hibernation: the DO is
        always registered but only consumes CPU when active.</li>
      <li><strong>Self-modifying tools</strong> — OpenClaw can inspect and modify its own tool implementations. Naelao
        limits this to skill folders in R2 (not actual Worker code), plus Dynamic Worker loaders for new tool isolates.
        The assistant can write a new skill SKILL.md, add a new script, and deploy it as a new tool without a platform
        redeploy.</li>
      <li><strong>Sub-agent spawning</strong> — OpenClaw can launch sub-agents for complex tasks. Naelao's AssistantDO
        can dispatch NaelaoAgentDO instances from the Space marketplace or build custom ones using the SDK and Dynamic
        Worker Loaders.</li>
      <li><strong>Persistent memory</strong> — OpenClaw maintains SOUL.md and MEMORY.md. Naelao uses typed JSON in DO
        SQLite (never flat Markdown — see §19 Security for why this matters).</li>
      <li><strong>Channel integrations</strong> — OpenClaw monitors Slack, Discord, email, SMS. Naelao's AssistantDO has
        the same via webhooks registered in R2 channel configs.</li>
      <li><strong>Heartbeat loop</strong> — OpenClaw runs a continuous loop checking for tasks. Naelao uses CF Cron
        Triggers (free) to wake the AssistantDO every 5 minutes for a heartbeat check.</li>
      <li><strong>cron-jobs.json</strong> — OpenClaw's scheduled tasks. Naelao stores this in R2 at <span
          class="ic">spaces/{spaceId}/assistants/{userId}/cron-jobs.json</span> and the heartbeat reads and dispatches
        them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>AssistantDO — SQLite Schema</h3>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// durable-objects/assistant-do.ts
// The AssistantDO is larger than AgentDO because it has persistent channels,
// memory, scheduled tasks, and self-modification capabilities.</span>

private async initSchema() {
  this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(`
    <span class="cm">-- Core sessions (same as AgentDO)</span>
    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS sessions (
      id         TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
      channel    TEXT,   <span class="cm">-- null = web UI, 'slack'|'discord'|'telegram' for channels</span>
      created_at INTEGER DEFAULT (unixepoch('now') * 1000)
    );

    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS messages (
      id         TEXT PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT (lower(hex(randomblob(16)))),
      session_id TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES sessions(id),
      parent_id  TEXT REFERENCES messages(id),
      role       TEXT NOT NULL,
      content    TEXT NOT NULL,
      tool_name  TEXT,
      channel_meta TEXT,   <span class="cm">-- JSON: {channelId, authorId, authorName} for channel msgs</span>
      created_at INTEGER DEFAULT (unixepoch('now') * 1000)
    );
    CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_asst_msgs ON messages(session_id, created_at);

    <span class="cm">-- Typed memory — never flat Markdown</span>
    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS memory (
      key        TEXT NOT NULL,
      category   TEXT NOT NULL, <span class="cm">-- 'fact'|'preference'|'relationship'|'task'|'context'</span>
      value      TEXT NOT NULL, <span class="cm">-- JSON only, validated on write</span>
      source     TEXT,          <span class="cm">-- which conversation created this memory</span>
      confidence REAL DEFAULT 1.0,
      created_at INTEGER DEFAULT (unixepoch('now') * 1000),
      updated_at INTEGER DEFAULT (unixepoch('now') * 1000),
      PRIMARY KEY (key, category)
    );

    <span class="cm">-- Channel configurations (Slack, Discord, Telegram, Email)</span>
    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS channels (
      id          TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
      type        TEXT NOT NULL, <span class="cm">-- 'slack'|'discord'|'telegram'|'email'</span>
      config      TEXT NOT NULL, <span class="cm">-- JSON: {webhookUrl, botToken, channelId, ...}</span>
      enabled     INTEGER DEFAULT 1,
      last_active INTEGER,
      created_at  INTEGER DEFAULT (unixepoch('now') * 1000)
    );

    <span class="cm">-- Sub-agents spawned by this assistant</span>
    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS sub_agents (
      id          TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
      thread_id   TEXT NOT NULL,   <span class="cm">-- thread in the parent project</span>
      project_id  TEXT NOT NULL,
      status      TEXT DEFAULT 'running', <span class="cm">-- running|complete|failed</span>
      task        TEXT NOT NULL,   <span class="cm">-- original task description</span>
      result      TEXT,            <span class="cm">-- JSON result when complete</span>
      spawned_at  INTEGER DEFAULT (unixepoch('now') * 1000),
      completed_at INTEGER
    );

    <span class="cm">-- Self-modification log (skill additions, tool changes)</span>
    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS modification_log (
      id          TEXT PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT (lower(hex(randomblob(16)))),
      type        TEXT NOT NULL, <span class="cm">-- 'skill_added'|'skill_updated'|'workflow_created'|'tool_added'</span>
      target      TEXT NOT NULL, <span class="cm">-- skill name or workflow name</span>
      description TEXT NOT NULL,
      r2_path     TEXT,          <span class="cm">-- path in R2 where the new code was written</span>
      created_at  INTEGER DEFAULT (unixepoch('now') * 1000)
    );

    <span class="cm">-- Permission manifest (what the assistant is allowed to do)
    -- Stored in DO SQLite, NOT R2 — prevents skill code from modifying permissions</span>
    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS permissions (
      permission  TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
      granted     INTEGER DEFAULT 0,
      granted_by  TEXT,           <span class="cm">-- userId of admin who granted this</span>
      granted_at  INTEGER
    );

    <span class="cm">-- Active fibers (background tasks running via runFiber())</span>
    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS fibers (
      id         TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
      name       TEXT NOT NULL,
      status     TEXT DEFAULT 'running',
      snapshot   TEXT,
      created_at INTEGER DEFAULT (unixepoch('now') * 1000)
    );
  `);

  <span class="cm">// Initialize default permissions (all denied until admin grants)</span>
  const defaultPermissions = [
    'manage_members', 'manage_projects', 'view_billing',
    'create_agents', 'approve_resources', 'manage_workflows'
  ];
  for (const perm of defaultPermissions) {
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
      `INSERT OR IGNORE INTO permissions (permission, granted) VALUES (?, 0)`, perm
    );
  }
}</code></pre>

    <h3>The Heartbeat — Replacing OpenClaw's Continuous Loop</h3>
    <p>OpenClaw runs a continuous <span class="ic">while True</span> loop on local hardware. On Cloudflare, we cannot do
      this — a Worker cannot run forever. Instead, we use a CF Cron Trigger to wake the AssistantDO every 5 minutes. The
      heartbeat checks: Are there scheduled tasks to run? Are there pending sub-agent results? Are there new channel
      messages to process? This approach costs essentially zero when idle (cron triggers are free up to 5/worker/day
      limit, then negligible).</p>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// workers/sweeper/index.ts
// This runs every 5 minutes via CF Cron Trigger
// Wakes ALL active assistants to process their heartbeat</span>

export default {
  async scheduled(event: ScheduledEvent, env: Env): Promise&lt;void&gt; {
    <span class="cm">// Get all active product root space IDs from KV (PRODUCT_DB_REGISTRY)
    // 'active' = created in the last 7 days OR has had channel activity in last 30 days</span>
    const activeAssistants = await getActiveAssistants(env);
    
    <span class="cm">// Wake each AssistantDO for heartbeat check
    // We use Promise.allSettled so a single DO failure doesn't block others</span>
    await Promise.allSettled(
      activeAssistants.map(async ({ spaceId, userId }) => {
        const doId = env.NAELAO_ASSISTANT_DO.idFromName(
          `assistant:${spaceId}:${userId}`
        );
        const assistantDO = env.NAELAO_ASSISTANT_DO.get(doId);
        
        try {
          await assistantDO.fetch(new Request('http://assistant/heartbeat', {
            method: 'POST',
            body: JSON.stringify({ spaceId, userId, timestamp: Date.now() })
          }));
        } catch (e) {
          <span class="cm">// Log but don't throw — other assistants must still get their heartbeat</span>
          console.error(`[NAELAO] Heartbeat failed for ${spaceId}:${userId}`, e);
        }
      })
    );
  }
}

<span class="cm">// durable-objects/assistant-do.ts — heartbeat handler</span>
async handleHeartbeat(): Promise&lt;void&gt; {
  <span class="cm">// 1. Load cron-jobs.json from R2</span>
  const cronObj = await this.env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.get(
    `spaces/${this.spaceId}/assistants/${this.userId}/cron-jobs.json`
  );
  
  if (cronObj) {
    const cronJobs: CronJob[] = JSON.parse(await cronObj.text());
    const now = Date.now();
    
    for (const job of cronJobs) {
      if (this.shouldRunJob(job, now)) {
        <span class="cm">// Run job as a fiber so it doesn't block the heartbeat</span>
        void this.runFiber(`cron-${job.id}-${now}`, async (ctx) => {
          await this.executeJob(job, ctx);
        });
      }
    }
  }

  <span class="cm">// 2. Check for completed sub-agents</span>
  const runningSubAgents = this.ctx.storage.sql
    .exec(`SELECT id, thread_id, project_id FROM sub_agents WHERE status = 'running'`)
    .toArray();
  
  for (const sa of runningSubAgents) {
    await this.checkSubAgentStatus(sa.id as string, sa.thread_id as string);
  }

  <span class="cm">// 3. Update last heartbeat timestamp</span>
  await this.ctx.storage.put('last_heartbeat', Date.now());
}</code></pre>

    <h3>cron-jobs.json — The Task Schedule</h3>
    <p>This file lives in R2 and is the assistant's schedule of recurring tasks. The assistant can read it, modify it,
      add new jobs, and remove completed ones. Humans can also edit it via the UI. The format must be strict — the
      heartbeat parser is not forgiving.</p>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// R2 path: spaces/{spaceId}/assistants/{userId}/cron-jobs.json
// The assistant reads this on every heartbeat to decide what to run.</span>
{
  "jobs": [
    {
      "id": "daily-standup",
      "schedule": "0 9 * * 1-5",      <span class="cm">// cron expression: 9am Mon-Fri</span>
      "task": "Generate a standup summary of all active projects and post to Slack",
      "channel": "slack",              <span class="cm">// where to post the result</span>
      "enabled": true,
      "lastRun": null,                 <span class="cm">// ISO timestamp, null if never run</span>
      "nextRun": "2026-05-12T09:00:00Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "weekly-report",
      "schedule": "0 18 * * 5",       <span class="cm">// 6pm Friday</span>
      "task": "Create a weekly progress report for the team and send via email",
      "channel": "email",
      "enabled": true,
      "lastRun": null,
      "nextRun": "2026-05-10T18:00:00Z"
    }
  ],
  "version": "1.0",
  "updatedBy": "assistant",           <span class="cm">// 'assistant' or 'user'</span>
  "updatedAt": "2026-05-07T10:00:00Z"
}</code></pre>

    <div class="warn-box">
      <div class="warn-label">⚠ Junior Dev Trap: cron-jobs.json Parsing</div>
      <p>The heartbeat fires every 5 minutes. Do NOT parse cron expressions in the heartbeat handler using a heavy
        library — the Worker has a 10ms CPU limit per heartbeat invocation. Use a lightweight cron parser that
        pre-computes the next run time and stores it in the JSON. When the heartbeat fires, it only needs to check <span
          class="ic">if nextRun &lt;= now</span>. After running, update <span class="ic">lastRun</span> and compute the
        next <span class="ic">nextRun</span>. Never re-parse the cron expression on every heartbeat.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Self-Modification — How the Assistant Updates Its Own Skills</h3>
    <p>OpenClaw can modify its own code. For Naelao, we give the assistant two self-modification pathways: (1) skill
      folder updates in R2, and (2) Dynamic Worker Loader deployment for new tool isolates. We deliberately do NOT give
      it access to Worker TypeScript source code — that would require a CF deployment and could break the platform. The
      assistant modifies its <em>capabilities</em>, not its <em>runtime</em>.</p>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// durable-objects/assistant-do.ts — self-modification via skill update</span>

async addSkill(skillDefinition: {
  name: string;
  description: string;
  instructions: string;
  scripts?: { id: string; type: string; code: string }[];
}): Promise&lt;{ success: boolean; skillPath: string }&gt; {
  
  <span class="cm">// Validate skill name — only lowercase alphanumeric and hyphens</span>
  if (!/^[a-z0-9-]+$/.test(skillDefinition.name)) {
    throw new Error('Skill name must be lowercase alphanumeric with hyphens only');
  }

  const skillPath = `spaces/${this.spaceId}/assistants/${this.userId}/skills/${skillDefinition.name}`;

  <span class="cm">// Write SKILL.md to R2</span>
  const skillMd = this.generateSkillMd(skillDefinition);
  
  <span class="cm">// SECURITY: Run skill through scanner BEFORE writing to R2
  // The scanner checks for dangerous patterns (process.env access, eval, etc.)
  // See §19 Security for the full 27-pattern list</span>
  const scanResult = await this.env.NAELAO_SECURITY.scan(skillMd);
  if (!scanResult.safe) {
    throw new Error(`Skill rejected by security scanner: ${scanResult.reason}`);
  }

  await this.env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.put(`${skillPath}/SKILL.md`, skillMd);

  <span class="cm">// Write scripts to R2 if provided</span>
  if (skillDefinition.scripts) {
    for (const script of skillDefinition.scripts) {
      const scriptScan = await this.env.NAELAO_SECURITY.scan(script.code);
      if (!scriptScan.safe) {
        throw new Error(`Script '${script.id}' rejected: ${scriptScan.reason}`);
      }
      await this.env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.put(
        `${skillPath}/scripts/${script.id}.ts`, script.code
      );
    }
  }

  <span class="cm">// Log the self-modification</span>
  this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
    `INSERT INTO modification_log (type, target, description, r2_path) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)`,
    'skill_added', skillDefinition.name, skillDefinition.description, skillPath
  );

  <span class="cm">// Invalidate the skill cache so next agent run picks up the new skill</span>
  await this.ctx.storage.delete('skills_cache');

  return { success: true, skillPath };
}

<span class="cm">// Deploy a new tool as a Dynamic Worker Loader isolate
// The assistant writes TypeScript code, which is loaded into a V8 isolate on demand.
// This does NOT require a CF deployment — it loads at runtime.</span>
async addDynamicTool(toolDefinition: {
  name: string;
  description: string;
  code: string;          <span class="cm">// TypeScript that runs in a Dynamic Worker isolate</span>
  parameters: JSONSchema;
}): Promise&lt;{ success: boolean; toolUrl: string }&gt; {
  
  <span class="cm">// Security scan the tool code</span>
  const scan = await this.env.NAELAO_SECURITY.scan(toolDefinition.code);
  if (!scan.safe) throw new Error(`Tool code rejected: ${scan.reason}`);

  <span class="cm">// Store in R2</span>
  const toolPath = `spaces/${this.spaceId}/assistants/${this.userId}/dynamic-tools/${toolDefinition.name}.ts`;
  await this.env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.put(toolPath, toolDefinition.code);

  <span class="cm">// Register in SkillRegistryDO so it's available in next agent turn</span>
  const registryDO = this.env.NAELAO_SKILL_REGISTRY.get(
    this.env.NAELAO_SKILL_REGISTRY.idFromName(`skills:${this.spaceId}:assistant-${this.userId}`)
  );
  await registryDO.fetch(new Request('http://registry/register-dynamic', {
    method: 'POST',
    body: JSON.stringify({
      name: toolDefinition.name,
      description: toolDefinition.description,
      r2Path: toolPath,
      parameters: toolDefinition.parameters
    })
  }));

  this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
    `INSERT INTO modification_log (type, target, description, r2_path) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)`,
    'tool_added', toolDefinition.name, toolDefinition.description, toolPath
  );

  return { success: true, toolUrl: toolPath };
}</code></pre>

    <h3>Sub-Agent Spawning</h3>
    <p>The Space Assistant can spawn NaelaoAgentDO instances from the Space marketplace or build custom ones. This is
      how it handles tasks that are too large for its own context window or that require parallel execution.</p>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// durable-objects/assistant-do.ts — sub-agent spawning</span>

async spawnSubAgent(task: {
  agentDefinitionId: string;  <span class="cm">// from Space marketplace, or 'custom'</span>
  taskDescription: string;
  projectId: string;
  customDefinition?: AgentDefinition; <span class="cm">// if agentDefinitionId === 'custom'</span>
}): Promise&lt;{ subAgentId: string; threadId: string }&gt; {
  
  <span class="cm">// Create a new thread in the project for this sub-agent</span>
  const threadId = crypto.randomUUID();
  const subAgentId = crypto.randomUUID();

  <span class="cm">// Dispatch the agent run via naelao-agents Worker</span>
  <span class="cm">// The sub-agent runs as a background fiber (always background mode)</span>
  await this.env.NAELAO_AGENTS.fetch(new Request('http://agents/spawn', {
    method: 'POST',
    body: JSON.stringify({
      spaceId: this.spaceId,
      projectId: task.projectId,
      threadId,
      agentDefinitionId: task.agentDefinitionId,
      customDefinition: task.customDefinition,
      input: task.taskDescription,
      backgroundMode: true,
      parentAssistantId: `${this.spaceId}:${this.userId}` <span class="cm">// for result callback</span>
    })
  }));

  <span class="cm">// Track the sub-agent in SQLite</span>
  this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
    `INSERT INTO sub_agents (id, thread_id, project_id, task) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)`,
    subAgentId, threadId, task.projectId, task.taskDescription
  );

  return { subAgentId, threadId };
}

<span class="cm">// Called by sweeper when checking sub-agent status</span>
async checkSubAgentStatus(subAgentId: string, threadId: string): Promise&lt;void&gt; {
  <span class="cm">// Get the AgentDO for this thread and check its fiber status</span>
  const agentDoId = this.env.NAELAO_AGENT_DO.idFromName(
    `${this.spaceId}:${threadId}`
  );
  const agentDO = this.env.NAELAO_AGENT_DO.get(agentDoId);
  
  const statusResp = await agentDO.fetch(new Request('http://agent/status'));
  const status = await statusResp.json() as { status: string; result?: unknown };
  
  if (status.status === 'complete') {
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
      `UPDATE sub_agents SET status = 'complete', result = ?, completed_at = ? WHERE id = ?`,
      JSON.stringify(status.result), Date.now(), subAgentId
    );
    
    <span class="cm">// Notify the assistant so it can incorporate the result into its context</span>
    void this.runFiber(`sub-agent-result-${subAgentId}`, async (ctx) => {
      await this.processSubAgentResult(subAgentId, status.result);
    });
  }
}</code></pre>

    <h3>Channel Integrations</h3>
    <p>Slack, Discord, and Telegram all work through webhooks. The assistant registers webhook endpoints with each
      platform. When a message arrives, it hits the naelao-router, which routes it to the AssistantDO via the
      naelao-notifications Worker. The assistant processes it and sends a reply back through the same channel.</p>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// Setting up Slack integration — full flow</span>

<span class="cm">// 1. User configures Slack in Space Settings UI
// 2. Frontend calls: POST /api/spaces/{spaceId}/assistants/{userId}/channels
// 3. naelao-assistant Worker stores config in AssistantDO channels table
// 4. naelao-assistant Worker registers a webhook URL with Slack:
//    https://app.naelao.dev/webhooks/slack/{spaceId}/{userId}/{token}

<span class="cm">// Incoming Slack message hits naelao-router:
// POST /webhooks/slack/{spaceId}/{userId}/{token}</span>
async handleSlackWebhook(request: Request, params: WebhookParams): Promise&lt;Response&gt; {
  const body = await request.json() as SlackEvent;
  
  <span class="cm">// Verify Slack signature (REQUIRED — without this, anyone can send fake messages)</span>
  const signature = request.headers.get('X-Slack-Signature') ?? '';
  const timestamp = request.headers.get('X-Slack-Request-Timestamp') ?? '';
  const isValid = await verifySlackSignature(signature, timestamp, await request.text(), params.token);
  if (!isValid) return new Response('Unauthorized', { status: 401 });
  
  <span class="cm">// Handle Slack URL verification challenge (sent on webhook registration)</span>
  if (body.type === 'url_verification') {
    return Response.json({ challenge: body.challenge });
  }
  
  <span class="cm">// Route message to AssistantDO</span>
  const doId = this.env.NAELAO_ASSISTANT_DO.idFromName(
    `assistant:${params.spaceId}:${params.userId}`
  );
  const assistantDO = this.env.NAELAO_ASSISTANT_DO.get(doId);
  
  <span class="cm">// Process asynchronously — Slack requires response within 3 seconds</span>
  this.ctx.waitUntil(
    assistantDO.fetch(new Request('http://assistant/channel-message', {
      method: 'POST',
      body: JSON.stringify({
        channel: 'slack',
        messageText: body.event?.text ?? '',
        channelMeta: {
          channelId: body.event?.channel,
          authorId: body.event?.user,
          ts: body.event?.ts
        }
      })
    }))
  );
  
  return new Response('', { status: 200 }); <span class="cm">// Acknowledge immediately</span>
}

<span class="cm">// AssistantDO processes channel message and sends reply</span>
async handleChannelMessage(channel: string, text: string, meta: ChannelMeta): Promise&lt;void&gt; {
  <span class="cm">// Create or find the channel session</span>
  let sessionId = this.ctx.storage.sql
    .exec(`SELECT id FROM sessions WHERE channel = ?`, channel)
    .one()?.id as string | null;
  
  if (!sessionId) {
    sessionId = crypto.randomUUID();
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
      `INSERT INTO sessions (id, channel) VALUES (?, ?)`, sessionId, channel
    );
  }

  <span class="cm">// Add user message to session</span>
  this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
    `INSERT INTO messages (id, session_id, role, content, channel_meta) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)`,
    crypto.randomUUID(), sessionId, 'user', text, JSON.stringify(meta)
  );

  <span class="cm">// Run agent loop in a fiber (so heartbeat isn't blocked)</span>
  void this.runFiber(`channel-response-${Date.now()}`, async (ctx) => {
    const response = await this.executeAgentLoop(sessionId!, null, ctx);
    
    <span class="cm">// Send response back to the channel</span>
    await this.sendToChannel(channel, response, meta);
  });
}</code></pre>

    <h3>Assistant Configuration Spec</h3>
    <div class="sg">
      <div class="sk">Default count</div>
      <div class="sv">1 per user per Space. Space admin can raise this limit in Space Settings → Assistants → Max per
        user. Maximum 5 per user per Space.</div>
      <div class="sk">Container model</div>
      <div class="sv">On-demand CF Container (standard-1: ½ vCPU, 4 GiB RAM). Starts only when a task needs Linux
        execution. Default idle timeout: 30 minutes. Warm on wakeup from heartbeat: &lt;3s.</div>
      <div class="sk">R2 filesystem path</div>
      <div class="sv"><span class="ic">spaces/{spaceId}/assistants/{userId}/</span> mounted at <span
          class="ic">/data/assistant</span> when container starts. Contains: memory.json (deprecated, replaced by DO
        SQLite), skills/, cron-jobs.json, channel-configs/, dynamic-tools/</div>
      <div class="sk">Heartbeat frequency</div>
      <div class="sv">Every 5 minutes via naelao-sweeper cron trigger. Checks: cron-jobs.json, sub-agent status, pending
        channel messages, DO alarm wakeups.</div>
      <div class="sk">Memory storage</div>
      <div class="sv">Typed JSON in AssistantDO SQLite memory table. Key+category primary key. Schema-validated on
        write. Never flat Markdown. Injected into system prompt as structured XML on each turn.</div>
      <div class="sk">Permission manifest</div>
      <div class="sv">Stored in AssistantDO SQLite permissions table. NOT in R2 — prevents skill code from modifying its
        own permissions. Default: all permissions denied. Admin grants via Space Admin Dashboard.</div>
      <div class="sk">Self-modification scope</div>
      <div class="sv">Can write/update: skills in R2, cron-jobs.json, dynamic-tools (via Dynamic Worker Loader). Cannot
        modify: Worker source code, wrangler.jsonc, SpaceDO schema, other users' data, permission manifest.</div>
    </div>
    <h2>Space Assistants — OpenClaw + CF Thinker + Memory OS</h2>
    <div class="bar"></div>
    <p class="lead">Space Assistants represent the complete integration of <strong>OpenClaw-style proactive agency</strong>, 
      the <strong>CF Thinker (Execution Ladder)</strong> resource-budget model, product-level multi-tenant <strong>Spaces</strong>, 
      and a custom Cloudflare-native implementation of <strong>Memory OS</strong> — the state-of-the-art 6-layer memory stack designed for Hermes Agent.</p>

    <div class="pull">"By running on top of Cloudflare's serverless edge, a Space Assistant preserves continuous 24/7 existence, self-modifies its capabilities, and utilizes an air-gapped 6-layer Memory OS — with an idle cost of virtually zero."</div>

    <h3>Architecture Overview: The Four Pillars</h3>
    <div class="g2">
      <div class="card bl">
        <div class="card-lbl">Pillar 1</div>
        <div class="card-title">OpenClaw Proactive Core</div>
        <p>Clones OpenClaw's continuous loop paradigm. Uses CF Cron triggers to fire a 5-minute heartbeat check. Automatically executes scheduled actions via <code>cron-jobs.json</code>, monitors webhooks, manages Slack/Discord integrations, and spawns sub-agents dynamically.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card gr">
        <div class="card-lbl">Pillar 2</div>
        <div class="card-title">CF Thinker Execution Ladder</div>
        <p>Ensures that proactive heartbeats and memory lookups do not create high billing overhead. Simple tasks stay in Tier 1 (R2 direct) or Tier 2 (Isolate). The assistant only boots a Tier 5 container sandbox on demand for heavy bash/Python scripts.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card pu">
        <div class="card-lbl">Pillar 3</div>
        <div class="card-title">Spaces Tenant Isolation</div>
        <p>Strictly isolated across 6 security layers. A user's personal Space Assistant cannot escape the parent space. It writes to the product's centralized <code>SpaceDO</code> SQLite database, with all queries bound by <code>space_id</code> columns to prevent cross-sub-space leakage.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card tl">
        <div class="card-lbl">Pillar 4</div>
        <div class="card-title">6-Layer Memory OS</div>
        <p>Claudio Drews' Memory OS mapped to CF primitives. Synthesizes Workspace files, Session history, Structured Facts with trust scores, Fabric cross-session summaries, hybrid Vectorize search, and an auto-curated LLM Wiki in a closed loop.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>The 6-Layer Memory OS Pipeline</h3>
    <p>The standard memory storage in agents often relies on unstructured vector database dumps. Memory OS resolves this retrieval gap by establishing six independent local layers of context, coordinated by the AssistantDO's built-in SQLite database and the Space's R2 bucket:</p>

    <table class="t">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Layer</th>
          <th>Name</th>
          <th>Cloudflare Primitive</th>
          <th>Description & Retrieval Logic</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Layer 1</td>
          <td class="tn">Workspace</td>
          <td class="mono">R2 Workspace</td>
          <td>Holds active text reference blocks (<code>MEMORY.md</code>, <code>USER.md</code>, <code>CREATIVE.md</code>). Injected as static prefixes inside the system prompt to maximize CF AI Gateway prompt caching.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Layer 2</td>
          <td class="tn">Sessions</td>
          <td class="mono">DO SQLite state.db</td>
          <td>Provides high-speed, local session history. Fully indexed via SQLite FTS5 (<code>messages_fts</code>) for instantaneous, zero-latency full-text search across thread histories.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Layer 3</td>
          <td class="tn">Structured Facts</td>
          <td class="mono">DO SQLite memory_store.db</td>
          <td>Stores durable semantic triples (Entity-Relation-Attribute) with trust scores and decay rates. Merges identical entities using a Jaro-Winkler comparison script executed at the Isolate tier.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Layer 4</td>
          <td class="tn">Fabric</td>
          <td class="mono">DO SQLite + R2</td>
          <td>Cross-session context consolidator based on the Icarus framework. Runs LLM-driven session extraction post-run and exposes tools such as <code>fabric_recall</code> and <code>fabric_brief</code> to summarize work.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Layer 5</td>
          <td class="tn">Vector Database</td>
          <td class="mono">CF Vectorize + BM25</td>
          <td>Semantic memory search using 768d cosine vectors. Combined with BM25 sparse search implemented as an Isolate-tier tool to yield a low-latency Hybrid Retrieval system.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Layer 6</td>
          <td class="tn">LLM Wiki</td>
          <td class="mono">R2 + Vectorize</td>
          <td>Auto-curated concept and entity vault. An async background process (<code>wiki-continuous-ingest</code>) runs on session end to structure new learnings into wiki articles, which are re-indexed into Layer 5.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Memory OS SQLite Schema (AssistantDO)</h3>
    <p>The AssistantDO initializes the following tables in its constructor, ensuring local, low-latency, transactional persistence for facts, fabric summaries, and wiki concepts:</p>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">-- Run during AssistantDO initSchema()</span>
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS structured_facts (
  id          TEXT PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT (lower(hex(randomblob(16)))),
  entity      TEXT NOT NULL,
  relation    TEXT NOT NULL,
  attribute   TEXT NOT NULL,
  trust_score REAL NOT NULL DEFAULT 1.0,
  decay_rate  REAL NOT NULL DEFAULT 0.05,
  last_used   INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT (unixepoch('now') * 1000),
  updated_at  INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT (unixepoch('now') * 1000)
);
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_facts_entity ON structured_facts(entity);

CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS fabric_store (
  id          TEXT PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT (lower(hex(randomblob(16)))),
  session_id  TEXT NOT NULL,
  summary     TEXT NOT NULL,
  key_insights TEXT NOT NULL,  <span class="cs">-- JSON array of strings</span>
  created_at  INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT (unixepoch('now') * 1000)
);

CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS wiki_store (
  concept     TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  definition  TEXT NOT NULL,
  relationships TEXT NOT NULL, <span class="cs">-- JSON array of linked entities</span>
  updated_at  INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT (unixepoch('now') * 1000)
);</code></pre>

    <h3>Code: Surgical Recall Pre-Flight Check</h3>
    <p>Before any LLM turn, the AssistantDO runs a <strong>Surgical Recall</strong> query. It pulls from four memory layers at once, filters by relevance threshold, deduplicates, and formats the memory block into the system prompt:</p>
    <pre><code><span class="ck">import</span> { Anthropic } <span class="ck">from</span> <span class="cs">'@anthropic-ai/sdk'</span>;

<span class="ck">export async function</span> surgicalRecall(
  query: <span class="ck">string</span>,
  spaceId: <span class="ck">string</span>,
  userId: <span class="ck">string</span>,
  env: Env,
  sql: SqlStorage
): <span class="ck">Promise</span>&lt;<span class="ck">string</span>&gt; {
  
  <span class="cm">// Step 1: Skip trivial social queries (e.g. "thanks", "hello") to save tokens</span>
  <span class="ck">if</span> (isTrivialQuery(query)) {
    <span class="ck">return</span> <span class="cs">''</span>;
  }

  <span class="cm">// Step 2: Query 4 sources in parallel via Service Bindings and local SQL</span>
  const [fabric, vector, sessions, facts] = <span class="ck">await</span> <span class="ck">Promise</span>.all([
    queryFabric(query, sql),
    queryVectorize(query, spaceId, env),
    querySessions(query, sql),
    queryStructuredFacts(query, sql)
  ]);

  <span class="cm">// Step 3: Filter Vector and Fabric by relevance threshold (e.g., Cosine score > 0.72)</span>
  const filteredVector = vector.filter(v =&gt; v.score &gt; <span class="cn">0.72</span>);
  const filteredFabric = fabric.filter(f =&gt; f.score &gt; <span class="cn">0.70</span>);

  <span class="cm">// Step 4: Perform entity-deduplication before context injection</span>
  const consolidated = deduplicateMemoryOSContext([
    ...filteredFabric,
    ...filteredVector,
    ...sessions,
    ...facts
  ]);

  <span class="cm">// Step 5: Format as structured XML blocks for the LLM</span>
  <span class="ck">return</span> formatMemoryOSXml(consolidated);
}

<span class="ck">function</span> deduplicateMemoryOSContext(memories: MemoryBlock[]): MemoryBlock[] {
  const seen = <span class="ck">new</span> <span class="ck">Set</span>&lt;<span class="ck">string</span>&gt;();
  <span class="ck">return</span> memories.filter(m =&gt; {
    const key = `${m.layer}:${m.entity ?? m.text}`;
    <span class="ck">if</span> (seen.has(key)) <span class="ck">return false</span>;
    seen.add(key);
    <span class="ck">return true</span>;
  });
}</code></pre>

    <h3>Proactive Heartbeat Loop (OpenClaw-Native)</h3>
    <p>Rather than relying on a continuous server process, the AssistantDO maintains proactive execution by mapping its loop to serverless cron triggers and on-demand background fibers:</p>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="fstep">
        <div class="fn">1</div>
        <div class="ft"><strong>Cron Trigger:</strong> <code>naelao-sweeper</code> fires a cron trigger every 5 minutes, querying active Space listings and waking up AssistantDO instances.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="fstep">
        <div class="fn">2</div>
        <div class="ft"><strong>Schedule Check:</strong> The AssistantDO reads <code>cron-jobs.json</code> from R2 (Tier 1: Workspace) and identifies any due or overdue tasks.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="fstep">
        <div class="fn">3</div>
        <div class="ft"><strong>Fiber Dispatch:</strong> Due tasks are dispatched as background fibers via <code>runFiber()</code>, immediately releasing the main cron execution thread to keep CPU time low.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="fstep">
        <div class="fn">4</div>
        <div class="ft"><strong>Execution:</strong> The fiber evaluates the task. If a simple search is needed, it uses Tier 2 (Isolate). If it needs complex files, it boots a Tier 5 (Sandbox) container.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="fstep">
        <div class="fn">5</div>
        <div class="ft"><strong>Memory Consolidation:</strong> Once the task completes, a post-run fiber runs. It extracts key insights, writes to Layer 4 (Fabric), updates trust scores in Layer 3 (Facts), and triggers <code>wiki-continuous-ingest</code> to update Layer 6 (LLM Wiki).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>What a Space Assistant CAN and CANNOT Do</h3>
    <div class="g2">
