  <div class="sec" id="apps">
    <span class="sec-ey">08 — Naelao Apps</span>
    <h2>Naelao Apps — MCP-Native App Economy</h2>
    <div class="bar"></div>
    <p class="lead">Naelao Apps are MCP servers. Unlike Whop apps (web apps for humans), Naelao Apps serve both humans
      (via a UI) and agents (via MCP tool calls) simultaneously. This changes the entire economics of what an "app"
      means in an AI-native platform.</p>

    <h3>What Is a Naelao App?</h3>
    <p>A Naelao App has three components that must all exist together:</p>

    <div class="g3">
      <div class="box box-bl">
        <div class="box-lbl">1. MCP Server (Required)</div>
        <p>The core of the app. Implements the MCP protocol with tool definitions. Agents call these tools directly. The
          server runs as a Cloudflare Worker registered with naelao-mcp-hub.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="box box-tl">
        <div class="box-lbl">2. App UI (Optional)</div>
        <p>A frontend for human users. Either: (a) a Dynamic Worker loader generating UI from instructions.md, or (b) a
          CF Pages deployment. Naelao recommends (a) — it's dynamic and personalizable.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="box box-pu">
        <div class="box-lbl">3. App Manifest (Required)</div>
        <p>Declares: what MCP tools the app provides, what permissions it needs, whether it has a UI, pricing, and which
          Space Types it supports.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>The Dynamic UI Problem — And the Solution</h3>
    <p>Traditional apps have static UIs. But in an AI-native platform, the right answer is a UI that generates itself
      based on how the user uses the app. Here is the architecture for this:</p>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// Each Naelao App has an instructions.md in its R2 folder.
// This describes what the UI should show and how it should behave.
// A Dynamic Worker Loader generates the actual HTML from these instructions.</span>

<span class="cm">// R2 path: naelao-assets/apps/{appId}/ui/</span>
<span class="cm">//   instructions.md       — AI-readable UI spec</span>
<span class="cm">//   user-customizations/  — per-user UI customizations (grows over time)</span>
<span class="cm">//   generated-ui.html     — latest generated UI (cached)</span>

<span class="cm">// workers/apps-registry/index.ts — serve app UI</span>
async function serveAppUI(
  appId: string,
  spaceId: string,
  userId: string,
  env: Env
): Promise&lt;Response&gt; {
  
  <span class="cm">// Check if cached generated UI is fresh (max 1 hour)</span>
  const cacheKey = `app-ui:${appId}:${userId}`;
  const cached = await env.NAELAO_CONFIG_KV.get(cacheKey, 'json') as CachedUI | null;
  
  if (cached && Date.now() - cached.generatedAt &lt; 3600000) {
    <span class="cm">// Serve cached UI via Dynamic Worker Loader</span>
    return serveViaLoader(cached.htmlContent, env);
  }

  <span class="cm">// Load instructions.md and user customizations</span>
  const [instructionsObj, customizationsObj] = await Promise.all([
    env.NAELAO_ASSETS.get(`apps/${appId}/ui/instructions.md`),
    env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.get(`spaces/${spaceId}/app-customizations/${appId}/${userId}.md`)
  ]);

  const instructions = instructionsObj ? await instructionsObj.text() : 'Build a simple, clean app UI.';
  const customizations = customizationsObj ? await customizationsObj.text() : '';

  <span class="cm">// Generate fresh UI via LLM
  // This runs once per hour per user — negligible cost</span>
  const uiGeneration = await env.NAELAO_AI_GATEWAY.messages.create({
    model: 'claude-haiku-4-5', <span class="cm">// use haiku — fast and cheap for UI gen</span>
    max_tokens: 4096,
    messages: [{
      role: 'user',
      content: `Generate a complete, self-contained HTML page for this app UI.
Use only HTML, CSS, and vanilla JavaScript. No external dependencies.
The UI must connect to the app's API at /api/apps/${appId}/ for data.
Include dark mode support via CSS media query.

App instructions:
${instructions}

User customizations (apply these):
${customizations || 'None yet — use defaults from instructions.'}

Output ONLY the complete HTML. Nothing else.`
    }]
  });

  const htmlContent = uiGeneration.content[0].text ?? '&lt;h1&gt;Error generating UI&lt;/h1&gt;';

  <span class="cm">// Cache for 1 hour</span>
  await env.NAELAO_CONFIG_KV.put(cacheKey, JSON.stringify({
    htmlContent,
    generatedAt: Date.now()
  }), { expirationTtl: 3600 });

  return serveViaLoader(htmlContent, env);
}

<span class="cm">// Serve via Dynamic Worker Loader — &lt;5ms boot</span>
async function serveViaLoader(html: string, env: Env): Promise&lt;Response&gt; {
  <span class="cm">// The Dynamic Worker Loader takes the HTML and serves it from a V8 isolate</span>
  const loaderStub = env.NAELAO_DW_LOADER.get(
    env.NAELAO_DW_LOADER.idFromName('app-ui-loader')
  );
  return loaderStub.fetch(new Request('http://loader/serve', {
    method: 'POST',
    body: JSON.stringify({ html, contentType: 'text/html' })
  }));
}</code></pre>

    <h3>Where Does the App UI Live? — CF Pages vs Dynamic Workers</h3>
    <p>This is the most common architectural question for junior developers building Naelao Apps. The answer depends on
      the app's complexity:</p>
    <table class="t">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>UI Approach</th>
          <th>When to Use</th>
          <th>How It Works</th>
          <th>Personalization</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Dynamic Worker Loader (Recommended)</td>
          <td>Most apps — simple to moderate complexity</td>
          <td>LLM generates HTML from instructions.md. Served via V8 isolate. Cached in KV. Re-generated when
            instructions or usage patterns change.</td>
          <td class="tk">High — customizations.md updated as user interacts. UI rebuilds automatically.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">CF Pages (Static)</td>
          <td>Complex apps with rich UI — code editors, dashboards with charts</td>
          <td>Standard React/Vue app deployed to CF Pages. Fetches data from MCP Worker endpoints.</td>
          <td class="tn">Low — static app, manual updates only</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">No UI (Agent-only)</td>
          <td>Pure automation apps — data pipelines, background processors</td>
          <td>MCP server only. Agents call it directly. No user-facing component.</td>
          <td class="tn">N/A</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Building a Naelao App — Step by Step</h3>
    <ol class="nl">
      <li><strong>Write the MCP Server Worker:</strong> Create a standard Cloudflare Worker that implements MCP
        protocol. Use the <span class="ic">@naelao/mcp-server-sdk</span> package which wraps CF Workers with MCP request
        handling. Register tool definitions in the Worker's <span class="ic">tools.json</span>.</li>
      <li><strong>Write instructions.md:</strong> Plain English description of what the UI should look like. Include:
        what data to display, what actions the user can take, default layout preferences. This is what the LLM reads to
        generate the UI.</li>
      <li><strong>Write the app manifest:</strong> <span class="ic">naelao-app.json</span> in your project root.
        Declares your MCP tools, required permissions, pricing, and supported Space Types.</li>
      <li><strong>Test locally with Miniflare:</strong> Run <span class="ic">npx naelao dev</span> which starts
        Miniflare with the MCP hub routing, Dynamic Worker Loader, and a test Space.</li>
      <li><strong>Deploy to Naelao:</strong> Run <span class="ic">npx naelao publish</span> which deploys the Worker to
        CF, registers the manifest with naelao-apps-registry, and uploads instructions.md and assets to R2.</li>
      <li><strong>Users install the app:</strong> Space admin finds it in the Naelao Apps marketplace. One click
        installs: MCP server registered with their naelao-mcp-hub instance, app shows in their Space UI, their agents
        can immediately call the MCP tools.</li>
    </ol>

