  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     §26 — NAELAO APPS ECONOMY: DEEPER ANALYSIS
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="sep"></div>
  <div class="sec" id="apps-economy">
    <span class="sec-ey">26 — Apps Economy</span>
    <h2>Naelao Apps Economy — Where Is It Headed?</h2>
    <div class="bar"></div>
    <p class="lead">You asked: "where is the apps economy headed since apps should be more agentic or more mini-AGI or
      should they just provide the service like Uber gives the driver to customers?" This section reasons through the
      answer with concrete architecture implications.</p>

    <h3>The Three Models of App Value</h3>
    <div class="g3">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-lbl">Model 1 — Data Provider</div>
        <div class="card-title">The Uber Model</div>
        <p>App owns proprietary data + relationships. Agents call the app to access that data. The app is the moat, not
          the computation. Example: Uber provides drivers. A Naelao App that provides real estate listings, legal
          precedents, or insurance quotes — the agent does the reasoning, the app provides the supply.</p>
        <p><strong>Architecture:</strong> App is primarily an MCP tool server wrapping a data API. No UI needed. Agents
          query it constantly. Revenue per API call.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card bl">
        <div class="card-lbl">Model 2 — Service Executor</div>
        <div class="card-title">The Stripe Model</div>
        <p>App executes actions agents cannot do alone (process payments, send emails, post social media). Agents decide
          what to do. Apps do the doing. Example: Stripe App processes payments. Twilio App sends SMS. GitHub App
          creates PRs.</p>
        <p><strong>Architecture:</strong> MCP tools that perform external actions. Credentials stored in
          naelao-credentials. No agent needed inside the app — the Naelao agent calls the app's tools.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card gr">
        <div class="card-lbl">Model 3 — Intelligence Layer</div>
        <div class="card-title">The Mini-AGI Model</div>
        <p>App IS the intelligence for a specific domain. Legal contract analysis app doesn't just retrieve contracts —
          it understands them. Medical literature app doesn't just search PubMed — it reasons over the results. The app
          has specialized intelligence the general agent lacks.</p>
        <p><strong>Architecture:</strong> MCP tools backed by fine-tuned models or RAG over proprietary corpora. The
          app's intelligence IS the moat. Competitors can't replicate by switching to a better general LLM.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>The Answer: All Three Models Coexist, Each for Different Apps</h3>
    <p>Naelao's MCP-native app model supports all three simultaneously. The key insight: <strong>the MCP protocol is
        neutral on how the server implements the tools</strong>. The app developer chooses whether their tool is a data
      lookup, an action executor, or an intelligence layer. The agent calling the tool doesn't care — it just calls a
      tool and gets a result.</p>
    <p>This means the Naelao App ecosystem will naturally stratify: commodity apps (weather, public data, basic actions)
      will be free or near-free. Data network effect apps (proprietary datasets, relationships) will be
      subscription-priced. Intelligence apps (domain-specific fine-tuned models) will command premium pricing and be the
      hardest to replicate.</p>

    <h3>Dynamic UI — The Right Architecture</h3>
    <p>You asked: "does this mean we are using CF Pages for the UI? or is the UI personalized over time?" Here is the
      definitive answer:</p>
    <div class="g2">
      <div class="box box-tl">
        <div class="box-lbl">Default: Dynamic Worker Loader UI</div>
        <p>Every app starts with a Dynamic Worker Loader UI generated from instructions.md. The LLM re-generates the
          HTML every hour based on the instructions and any user customizations accumulated in
          user-customizations/{userId}.md.</p>
        <p>As the user uses the app more, the Space Assistant (or the app itself via an MCP tool call) updates
          user-customizations/{userId}.md with learned preferences: "User prefers compact table view over card view",
          "User always sorts by date descending", "User has 3 saved searches they use every day".</p>
        <p>The next time the UI regenerates, it incorporates these preferences automatically. The UI becomes more
          personalized over time without the developer writing a single line of personalization code.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="box box-bl">
        <div class="box-lbl">Complex Apps: CF Pages with Personalization Hook</div>
        <p>Apps with rich interactivity (code editors, multi-step forms, real-time dashboards) use CF Pages. But they
          still hook into the personalization system via the Naelao Apps SDK: <span
            class="ic">naelao.getPersonalization(userId)</span> returns the user's accumulated preferences as a JSON
          object the app can apply.</p>
        <p>CF Pages apps are deployed once. Personalization is applied at runtime via the preferences object. No
          per-user deploy needed. The developer writes generic UI; Naelao makes it personal.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>The Dynamic Pages Question</h3>
    <p>You asked about "dynamic pages like they did for dynamic workflows — where it follows the tenant." This is the
      right question. Here is the architecture:</p>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// Dynamic App Pages — tenant-following UI
// Just like Dynamic Workflows follow the tenant via R2 + Worker Loader,
// Dynamic App Pages follow the tenant via their instructions.md in R2.
// 
// When user requests the app UI:
// 1. Load instructions.md from naelao-assets/apps/{appId}/ui/instructions.md (global template)
// 2. Load user-customizations/{spaceId}/{userId}.md from naelao-workspace (user's personalization)
// 3. LLM generates HTML combining both
// 4. Dynamic Worker Loader serves it
// 5. Cache for 1 hour in KV
//
// When user interacts with the app:
// The MCP server (via tool call) can write back to user-customizations.md:
// "User sorted by date 5 times — add default sort preference"
//
// Next UI generation incorporates this. The UI evolves with usage.
// This is the "app that lives and learns on Naelao" model.</span>

async function updateUserCustomizations(params: {
  appId: string;
  spaceId: string;
  userId: string;
  newLearning: string;  <span class="cm">// what the app learned about this user</span>
  env: Env;
}): Promise&lt;void&gt; {
  const key = `spaces/${params.spaceId}/app-customizations/${params.appId}/${params.userId}.md`;
  
  <span class="cm">// Read existing customizations</span>
  const existing = await params.env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.get(key);
  const currentContent = existing ? await existing.text() : '# User Preferences

';
  
  <span class="cm">// Append new learning</span>
  const updated = currentContent + `
- ${new Date().toISOString()}: ${params.newLearning}`;
  await params.env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.put(key, updated);
  
  <span class="cm">// Invalidate the UI cache so next request regenerates with updated preferences</span>
  await params.env.NAELAO_CONFIG_KV.delete(`app-ui:${params.appId}:${params.userId}`);
}</code></pre>
  </div>


  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     §27 — EXECUTION LADDER (DEEP DIVE)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="sep"></div>
  <div class="sec" id="execution-ladder">
    <span class="sec-ey">27 — Execution Ladder</span>
    <h2>Execution Ladder — All 5 Tiers, Complete Implementation</h2>
    <div class="bar"></div>
    <p class="lead">The Execution Ladder is one of the most important architectural ideas in Naelao, borrowed from the
      Project Think SDK blog post. Every agent tool call is routed to the minimum-cost execution environment that can
      handle it. Getting this right cuts container costs by 60-80% compared to always using a container. Getting it
      wrong means agents are slow, expensive, or both.</p>

    <div class="box box-or">
      <div class="box-lbl">The Core Principle</div>
      <p>Never use a $0.015/hr container when a $0.00003/request V8 isolate will do. Never use a V8 isolate when a
        $0.000001/operation direct R2 call will do. Each tier is only used when lower tiers genuinely cannot handle the
        task. The agent does NOT choose the tier — the platform routes automatically based on the tool being called.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Tier 1 — Workspace (Direct R2 Operations)</h3>
    <p><strong>Cost:</strong> ~$0.0000045 per operation (R2 Class A operation pricing). <strong>Latency:</strong>
      5-50ms. <strong>Limit:</strong> No compute — pure storage reads/writes.</p>
    <p>Tier 1 is for operations that are purely about reading or writing data with no computation. File reads, file
      writes, directory listings, version history queries. These go straight to R2 via the SpaceR2 helper — no Worker
      instantiation beyond what's already handling the request, no container, no isolate.</p>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// Tier 1 tools — direct R2, no compute isolate needed
// These run inside the naelao-agents Worker that's already handling the HTTP request.
// Zero additional cost beyond the R2 operation itself.</span>

const TIER_1_TOOLS = new Set([
  'read_file', 'write_file', 'list_directory', 'delete_file',
  'move_file', 'copy_file', 'file_exists', 'get_file_metadata',
  'read_version_history', 'append_to_file', 'read_todo',
  'write_todo', 'read_knowledge', 'list_knowledge'
]);

async function executeTier1Tool(
  toolName: string,
  params: Record&lt;string, string&gt;,
  context: ToolContext
): Promise&lt;ToolResult&gt; {
  const r2 = new SpaceR2(context.env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE, context.spaceId);
  
  switch (toolName) {
    case 'read_file': {
      const obj = await r2.get(`workspace/${params.path}`);
      if (!obj) return { output: { error: `File not found: ${params.path}` } };
      return { output: { content: await obj.text(), path: params.path } };
    }
    case 'write_file': {
      <span class="cm">// Acquire lock before write — prevent concurrent corruption</span>
      const projectDO = getProjectDO(context);
      const lock = await projectDO.acquireLock({ path: params.path, lockedBy: context.agentId });
      if (!lock.acquired) {
        return { output: { error: `File locked by ${lock.lockedBy}, retry in ${Math.round(((lock.expiresAt??0) - Date.now())/1000)}s` }};
      }
      await writeFileWithVersioning({
        path: params.path, content: params.content,
        writtenBy: context.agentId, spaceId: context.spaceId,
        projectId: context.projectId, env: context.env
      });
      await projectDO.releaseLock(params.path, context.agentId);
      return { output: { success: true, path: params.path, bytes: params.content.length } };
    }
    case 'list_directory': {
      const listing = await r2.list(`workspace/${params.path ?? ''}`);
      return {
        output: {
          files: listing.objects.map(o => ({
            name: o.key.split('/').pop(),
            path: o.key.replace(`spaces/${context.spaceId}/workspace/`, ''),
            size: o.size,
            modified: o.uploaded.toISOString()
          }))
        }
      };
    }
    default:
      throw new Error(`Unknown Tier 1 tool: ${toolName}`);
  }
}</code></pre>

    <h3>Tier 2 — Isolate (V8 Dynamic Worker)</h3>
    <p><strong>Cost:</strong> ~$0.0003 per million CPU-ms. <strong>Cold start:</strong> &lt;5ms. <strong>Limit:</strong>
      TypeScript/JavaScript only, no filesystem, no native binaries.</p>
    <p>Tier 2 is for computation that TypeScript can handle without a Linux environment. Web search via fetch(), API
      calls, data transforms with JSON/CSV, formula evaluation, code generation that will be previewed as an artifact.
      V8 isolates boot in under 5ms — from the user's perspective, this is instant.</p>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// Tier 2 tools — V8 Dynamic Worker isolate
// The isolate is created fresh per tool call via the Dynamic Worker Loader.
// No persistent state — results returned synchronously.
// Web Crypto API, fetch(), TextEncoder/Decoder all available.</span>

const TIER_2_TOOLS = new Set([
  'web_search', 'api_call', 'render_artifact', 'parse_json',
  'parse_csv', 'transform_data', 'calculate', 'generate_hash',
  'encode_base64', 'decode_base64', 'validate_json_schema',
  'extract_urls', 'markdown_to_html', 'minify_json'
]);

async function executeTier2Tool(
  toolName: string,
  params: Record&lt;string, string&gt;,
  context: ToolContext
): Promise&lt;ToolResult&gt; {
  <span class="cm">// Generate a TypeScript program to run in the V8 isolate
  // The program implements the tool logic and returns a JSON result</span>
  
  if (toolName === 'web_search') {
    <span class="cm">// web_search runs the Tavily API via fetch() inside a Dynamic Worker</span>
    const searchCode = `
export default {
  async fetch(request) {
    const { query, maxResults = 5 } = await request.json();
    const resp = await fetch('https://api.tavily.com/search', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify({
        api_key: TAVILY_KEY,
        query,
        max_results: maxResults,
        search_depth: 'advanced'
      })
    });
    const data = await resp.json();
    return Response.json({ results: data.results ?? [] });
  }
}`;
    const loaderDO = context.env.NAELAO_DW_LOADER.get(
      context.env.NAELAO_DW_LOADER.idFromName(`tier2:${context.spaceId}`)
    );
    const result = await loaderDO.fetch(new Request('http://loader/execute', {
      method: 'POST',
      body: JSON.stringify({
        code: searchCode,
        input: { query: params.query, maxResults: parseInt(params.maxResults ?? '5') },
        bindings: { TAVILY_KEY: context.env.TAVILY_API_KEY }
      })
    }));
    return { output: await result.json() };
  }

  if (toolName === 'api_call') {
    <span class="cm">// Delegated to the api-caller platform skill (also Tier 2, cf-worker type)
    // SSRF protection enforced inside handleApiCall()</span>
    return handleApiCall({
      url: params.url,
      method: params.method ?? 'GET',
      headers: params.headers ? JSON.parse(params.headers) : {},
      body: params.body,
      spaceId: context.spaceId
    }, context.env);
  }

  throw new Error(`Unknown Tier 2 tool: ${toolName}`);
}</code></pre>

    <h3>Tier 3 — NPM (Isolate + Runtime Package Resolution)</h3>
    <p><strong>Cost:</strong> ~$0.0005 per million CPU-ms (slightly higher due to package resolution). <strong>Cold
        start:</strong> &lt;10ms. <strong>Limit:</strong> Node-compatible npm packages only, no native binaries.</p>
    <p>Tier 3 is Tier 2 with runtime npm package access. Use when the task needs a TypeScript library that isn't
      available natively (e.g., a specific date parsing library, a Markdown parser, a ZIP file handler). Package
      resolution happens at load time, not at runtime — Cloudflare's infrastructure resolves the packages before the
      isolate starts.</p>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// Tier 3 — same as Tier 2 but with npm package imports
// The Dynamic Worker Loader resolves packages at load time</span>
const tier3Code = `
import { marked } from 'marked';     // npm package — resolved by DW Loader
import JSZip from 'jszip';           // npm package

export default {
  async fetch(request) {
    const { markdown } = await request.json();
    const html = marked.parse(markdown);
    return Response.json({ html });
  }
}`;

<span class="cm">// Declare npm dependencies in the code comment so DW Loader knows to resolve them
// The DW Loader parses import statements and pre-resolves packages
// This is why Tier 3 is slightly slower to start than Tier 2 — package resolution</span></code></pre>

    <h3>Tier 4 — Browser (CF Browser Rendering via CDP)</h3>
    <p><strong>Cost:</strong> ~$0.02 per 1000 requests (CF Browser Rendering pricing). <strong>Cold start:</strong>
      ~100-200ms (Chrome instance). <strong>Limit:</strong> Web pages only — cannot access the container filesystem or
      run arbitrary code.</p>
    <p>Tier 4 is specifically for web browser automation. Navigate URLs, click elements, fill forms, take screenshots,
      extract structured data from rendered pages (including JavaScript-heavy SPAs). This uses CF Browser Rendering —
      managed headless Chrome at Cloudflare's edge. The naelao-browser Worker provides a CDP proxy shim that exposes a
      simplified API to agents.</p>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// workers/browser/index.ts — The Moltworker-pattern CDP proxy
// Agents call this via MCP tool call, not directly.
// The naelao-browser Worker provides a stable API over the raw CDP protocol.</span>

export default {
  async fetch(request: Request, env: Env): Promise&lt;Response&gt; {
    const url = new URL(request.url);
    const { spaceId, action, params } = await request.json();

    <span class="cm">// Validate space access before opening any browser session</span>
    await validateSpaceAccess(request, spaceId, env);

    <span class="cm">// Open a browser session via CF Browser Rendering
    // Sessions are scoped per request — no session state persists between tool calls</span>
    const browser = await puppeteer.launch(env.NAELAO_BROWSER);
    const page = await browser.newPage();

    try {
      switch (action) {
        case 'navigate_and_extract': {
          await page.goto(params.url, { waitUntil: 'networkidle0', timeout: 30000 });
          const title = await page.title();
          const text = await page.evaluate(() => document.body.innerText);
          const links = await page.evaluate(() =>
            Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('a[href]'))
              .map(a =&gt; ({ text: a.textContent?.trim(), href: (a as HTMLAnchorElement).href }))
              .slice(0, 50)  <span class="cm">// limit to 50 links to avoid huge payloads</span>
          );
          return Response.json({ title, text: text.slice(0, 10000), links });
        }
        case 'screenshot': {
          await page.goto(params.url, { waitUntil: 'networkidle0', timeout: 30000 });
          const screenshot = await page.screenshot({ type: 'png', fullPage: params.fullPage ?? false });
          <span class="cm">// Store screenshot in R2 and return the R2 key
          // Agents can then use read_file to retrieve it or pass the key to artifact-builder</span>
          const screenshotKey = `spaces/${spaceId}/workspace/screenshots/${Date.now()}.png`;
          await env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.put(screenshotKey, screenshot);
          return Response.json({ screenshotPath: screenshotKey.replace(`spaces/${spaceId}/`, '') });
        }
        case 'fill_form': {
          await page.goto(params.url, { waitUntil: 'networkidle0', timeout: 30000 });
          for (const [selector, value] of Object.entries(params.fields as Record&lt;string, string&gt;)) {
            await page.type(selector, value);
          }
          if (params.submitSelector) {
            await page.click(params.submitSelector);
            await page.waitForNavigation({ timeout: 15000 });
          }
          return Response.json({ success: true, finalUrl: page.url() });
        }
        default:
          return Response.json({ error: `Unknown browser action: ${action}` }, { status: 400 });
      }
    } finally {
      await browser.close(); <span class="cm">// ALWAYS close — leaked sessions accumulate costs</span>
    }
  }
};</code></pre>

    <h3>Tier 5 — Sandbox (CF Container, Full Linux)</h3>
    <p><strong>Cost:</strong> ~$0.015/hr (standard-1: ½ vCPU, 4 GiB RAM). <strong>Cold start:</strong> 2-3 seconds.
      <strong>Idle:</strong> Sleeps after 10 minutes, R2 persists all state. <strong>Limit:</strong> Nothing — full
      Linux with Python, Node, Bash, git, and any package.</p>
    <p>Tier 5 is the container. Use only when the task genuinely needs a Linux environment: running Python scripts with
      numpy/pandas, executing Bash commands, running a local web server, compiling code, or any task that needs a real
      filesystem, installed system tools, or native binaries.</p>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// durable-objects/sandbox-do.ts — Complete SandboxDO implementation</span>

export class SandboxDO extends DurableObject {
  private containerRef: SandboxStub | null = null;
  private lastActiveAt: number = 0;

  private async initSchema() {
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(`
      CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS exec_log (
        id         TEXT PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT (lower(hex(randomblob(16)))),
        command    TEXT NOT NULL,
        exit_code  INTEGER,
        stdout     TEXT,
        stderr     TEXT,
        duration_ms INTEGER,
        executed_at INTEGER DEFAULT (unixepoch('now') * 1000)
      );
      CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS container_state (
        key   TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
        value TEXT NOT NULL
      );
      INSERT OR IGNORE INTO container_state VALUES ('status', 'stopped');
      INSERT OR IGNORE INTO container_state VALUES ('last_started', '0');
    `);
  }

  async fetch(request: Request): Promise&lt;Response&gt; {
    const url = new URL(request.url);
    
    if (url.pathname === '/ensure-running') return this.ensureRunning(request);
    if (url.pathname === '/exec')           return this.exec(request);
    if (url.pathname === '/status')         return this.getStatus();
    if (url.pathname === '/stop')           return this.stop();
    
    return new Response('Not Found', { status: 404 });
  }

  private async ensureRunning(request: Request): Promise&lt;Response&gt; {
    const { spaceId, projectId } = await request.json();
    
    const status = this.ctx.storage.sql
      .exec("SELECT value FROM container_state WHERE key = 'status'")
      .one()?.value as string;
    
    if (status === 'running' && this.containerRef) {
      this.lastActiveAt = Date.now();
      return Response.json({ status: 'already_running' });
    }

    <span class="cm">// Start the container via CF Sandbox SDK
    // Container name includes spaceId for isolation (Layer 6 of tenant isolation)</span>
    const containerName = this.buildContainerName(spaceId, projectId);
    const sandbox = getSandbox(this.env.NAELAO_SANDBOX, containerName);
    
    <span class="cm">// Mount R2 as /workspace — THIS IS THE KEY STEP
    // Without this, the container has no access to the agent's files.
    // After mount, ALL files in spaces/{spaceId}/workspace/ appear at /workspace/</span>
    await sandbox.mountBucket(this.env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE, '/workspace', {
      prefix: `spaces/${spaceId}/projects/${projectId}/workspace/`
    });

    <span class="cm">// Run the container sync script inside the container
    // This sets up the WebSocket back-channel and applies permission manifest</span>
    await sandbox.exec('bash', ['/opt/naelao/container-sync.sh'], {
      env: {
        SPACE_ID: spaceId,
        PROJECT_ID: projectId,
        NAELAO_INTERNAL_TOKEN: this.env.NAELAO_INTERNAL_TOKEN,
        AGENT_DO_ID: this.agentDoId ?? ''
      }
    });

    this.containerRef = sandbox;
    this.lastActiveAt = Date.now();

    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
      "UPDATE container_state SET value = ? WHERE key = 'status'", 'running'
    );
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
      "UPDATE container_state SET value = ? WHERE key = 'last_started'", Date.now().toString()
    );

    <span class="cm">// Set alarm to check for idle timeout (10 minutes)
    // If the container hasn't been used, it sleeps and costs stop</span>
    await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + 10 * 60 * 1000);

    return Response.json({ status: 'started', containerName });
  }

  private async exec(request: Request): Promise&lt;Response&gt; {
    if (!this.containerRef) {
      return Response.json({ error: 'Container not running. Call /ensure-running first.' }, { status: 409 });
    }

    const { command, args, env: extraEnv, workingDir } = await request.json();
    const startMs = Date.now();

    <span class="cm">// Validate path if working directory is specified</span>
    const cwd = workingDir ? validateContainerPath(workingDir) : '/workspace';

    try {
      <span class="cm">// ALWAYS use argument arrays — never string interpolation (CVE-2026-24763)</span>
      const result = await this.containerRef.exec(command, args, {
        env: extraEnv,
        cwd
      });

      const duration = Date.now() - startMs;
      this.lastActiveAt = Date.now();

      <span class="cm">// Log execution for audit (not optional)</span>
      this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
        'INSERT INTO exec_log (command, exit_code, stdout, stderr, duration_ms) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)',
        `${command} ${args.join(' ')}`,
        result.exitCode,
        (result.stdout ?? '').slice(0, 10000),  <span class="cm">// truncate large outputs</span>
        (result.stderr ?? '').slice(0, 5000),
        duration
      );

      return Response.json({
        exitCode: result.exitCode,
        stdout: result.stdout ?? '',
        stderr: result.stderr ?? '',
        durationMs: duration
      });
    } catch (error) {
      return Response.json({
        error: `Execution failed: ${(error as Error).message}`,
        exitCode: -1
      }, { status: 500 });
    }
  }

  <span class="cm">// Alarm handler — called when idle timeout fires</span>
  async alarm(): Promise&lt;void&gt; {
    const idleMs = Date.now() - this.lastActiveAt;
    const IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS = 10 * 60 * 1000; <span class="cm">// 10 minutes</span>

    if (idleMs &gt;= IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS && this.containerRef) {
      <span class="cm">// Container has been idle — let it sleep
      // R2 mount persists — when container restarts, all files are still there</span>
      this.containerRef = null;
      this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
        "UPDATE container_state SET value = 'stopped' WHERE key = 'status'"
      );
    } else {
      <span class="cm">// Still active — reset alarm</span>
      await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS - idleMs);
    }
  }

  private buildContainerName(spaceId: string, projectId: string): string {
    <span class="cm">// Short, deterministic, includes spaceId for isolation
    // Format: naelao-{8char spaceId}-{8char projectId}</span>
    const sp = spaceId.replace(/-/g, '').slice(0, 8);
    const pj = projectId.replace(/-/g, '').slice(0, 8);
    return `naelao-${sp}-${pj}`;
  }
}

<span class="cm">// Path traversal validation (from §19 Security)</span>
function validateContainerPath(agentPath: string): string {
  const { resolve, normalize } = require('path');
  const WORKSPACE_ROOT = '/workspace';
  const normalized = normalize(agentPath);
  const absolute = resolve(WORKSPACE_ROOT, normalized.startsWith('/') ? normalized.slice(1) : normalized);
  if (!absolute.startsWith(WORKSPACE_ROOT + '/') &amp;&amp; absolute !== WORKSPACE_ROOT) {
    throw new Error(`Path traversal blocked: "${agentPath}" resolves outside /workspace`);
  }
  return absolute;
}</code></pre>

    <h3>Execution Ladder Routing in Orchestrator</h3>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// agentpress/orchestrator.ts — The full routing logic
// This is called on EVERY tool execution. Get it right.

// Rule: tools are assigned a tier in TOOL_TIERS.
// If a tool isn't in TOOL_TIERS, default to Tier 5 (safest but most expensive).
// Agents cannot override tier assignments — this is a platform-level decision.</span>

export async function routeAndExecute(
  toolCall: ParsedToolCall,
  context: ToolContext
): Promise&lt;ToolResult&gt; {
  const toolName = toolCall.toolName;
  const tier = TOOL_TIERS[toolName]?.tier ?? 'sandbox'; <span class="cm">// default to Tier 5</span>

  switch (tier) {
    case 'workspace':
      return executeTier1Tool(toolName, toolCall.parameters, context);
    
    case 'isolate':
      return executeTier2Tool(toolName, toolCall.parameters, context);
    
    case 'npm':
      return executeTier3Tool(toolName, toolCall.parameters, context);
    
    case 'browser':
      <span class="cm">// Browser tools call naelao-browser Worker via Service Binding</span>
      return context.env.NAELAO_BROWSER_SVC.fetch(new Request('http://browser/execute', {
        method: 'POST',
        body: JSON.stringify({ spaceId: context.spaceId, action: toolName, params: toolCall.parameters })
      })).then(r =&gt; r.json());
    
    case 'sandbox':
      <span class="cm">// Ensure container is running before dispatching any sandbox tool</span>
      if (!context.sandboxStarted) {
        await context.sandboxDO.fetch(new Request('http://sandbox/ensure-running', {
          method: 'POST',
          body: JSON.stringify({ spaceId: context.spaceId, projectId: context.projectId })
        }));
        context.sandboxStarted = true; <span class="cm">// only start once per agent loop iteration</span>
      }
      return context.sandboxDO.fetch(new Request('http://sandbox/exec', {
        method: 'POST',
        body: JSON.stringify({ command: toolName, args: Object.values(toolCall.parameters) })
      })).then(r =&gt; r.json());
    
    default:
      throw new Error(`Unknown execution tier: ${tier} for tool: ${toolName}`);
  }
}</code></pre>
