    <span class="sec-ey">58 — Global Deployment</span>
    <h2>Multi-Region & Global Deployment — Cloudflare's Edge</h2>
    <div class="bar"></div>
    <p class="lead">Naelao runs natively on Cloudflare's global edge network — 310+ cities worldwide. This section
      documents what "global" means in practice for each component, where data actually lives, and how to reason about
      latency for users in different regions.</p>

    <h3>Where Each Component Lives</h3>
    <table class="t">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Component</th>
          <th>Where It Runs</th>
          <th>Data Location</th>
          <th>Latency Impact</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">CF Workers (all 22)</td>
          <td>Nearest CF PoP to the user (310+ locations)</td>
          <td>Stateless — no data</td>
          <td>~5-20ms from user to Worker globally</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Durable Objects</td>
          <td>One location per DO instance. CF pins the DO to the location where it was first accessed.</td>
          <td>DO SQLite in CF's infrastructure at pinned location</td>
          <td>~10-50ms from Worker to DO (cross-region possible)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">R2 (naelao-workspace)</td>
          <td>CF's global storage layer (not region-specific)</td>
          <td>Globally replicated with CF's storage infrastructure</td>
          <td>~10-50ms for R2 reads from any PoP</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">SpaceDO SQLite</td>
          <td>SpaceDO: co-located with the CF PoP handling the request (DO placement is automatic)</td>
          <td>SQLite data in the DO instance, physically near the request origin</td>
          <td>&lt;3ms for local DO reads — no network hop, no connection pool</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">CF AI Gateway</td>
          <td>CF's global edge — nearest PoP handles the request</td>
          <td>Cached responses at PoP level (if cache hit)</td>
          <td>~10ms overhead over direct Anthropic call</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">CF Containers</td>
          <td>User's nearest container-capable PoP</td>
          <td>Container FS is ephemeral. R2 is the persistent store.</td>
          <td>2-3s cold start. Warm: same as Worker latency.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">CF Browser Rendering</td>
          <td>CF's browser fleet (specific regions)</td>
          <td>No persistent data</td>
          <td>~200ms cold start for new browser session</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">CF Queues</td>
          <td>CF global queue infrastructure</td>
          <td>Messages replicated globally</td>
          <td>~50-100ms for enqueue + worker pick-up</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>DO Pinning — Critical for Agent Continuity</h3>
    <p>Durable Objects are pinned to a location when first accessed. If a user in London first accesses their AgentDO,
      it's pinned in a European CF location. Subsequent requests from the same user route to that location. This is
      excellent — the DO is near the user's primary access pattern.</p>
    <p>The problem: if the user's IP changes (VPN, travel), subsequent requests still go to the same DO location — but
      now the Worker executing the request is in a different region than the DO. This adds ~50-150ms cross-region
      latency for DO calls. This is unavoidable and acceptable — DO consistency requires a single location.</p>
    <pre><code><span class="cm">// Do NOT try to "optimize" DO location — let CF handle it.
// The worst thing you can do is try to manually pin DOs to specific regions
// by creating them with region-specific names. CF's automatic pinning
// based on first-access is the correct behavior.

// What you SHOULD do: minimize DO round-trips by batching operations.
// Instead of 3 separate DO calls, batch them into one:

// BAD: 3 round-trips to DO
const session = await agentDO.getSession(threadId);
const memory = await agentDO.getMemory();
const fibers = await agentDO.getFibers();

// GOOD: 1 round-trip to DO
const { session, memory, fibers } = await agentDO.getAgentState(threadId);
// The DO handles all reads internally in one request.</span></code></pre>

    <h3>Latency Budget — What a Typical Agent Turn Costs</h3>
    <table class="t">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Operation</th>
          <th>Latency</th>
          <th>Optimization</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">User request → naelao-router</td>
          <td>~10ms (CF edge)</td>
          <td>Cannot optimize — this is physics</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Auth validation via naelao-auth</td>
          <td>~5ms (Service Binding)</td>
          <td>SpaceDO cache eliminates most DB calls</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Message persist to SpaceDO SQLite</td>
          <td>~1–3ms (DO local write, no network hop)</td>
          <td>Fire-and-forget, don't block LLM call</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Skill loading from R2</td>
          <td>~30ms (first load)</td>
          <td>Cached in SkillRegistryDO SQLite after first load</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Knowledge search (Vectorize)</td>
          <td>~40ms</td>
          <td>Run in parallel with message persist</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">LLM first token (Claude Sonnet)</td>
          <td>~800-1200ms</td>
          <td>Prompt caching reduces this to ~300ms on cache hits</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Tool execution (Tier 1, R2 read)</td>
          <td>~20ms</td>
          <td>Tier 1 is the fastest — use it when possible</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Tool execution (Tier 2, Dynamic Worker)</td>
          <td>~50ms</td>
          <td>Parallel execution for independent tools</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Tool execution (Tier 5, Container)</td>
          <td>~3000ms cold, ~100ms warm</td>
          <td>Keep container warm by running tasks batched</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Total foreground turn (simple question)</td>
          <td>~1200ms (time to first token)</td>
          <td>With cache hit: ~500ms</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tl">Total foreground turn (code execution)</td>
