        <h3>Cross-Origin Communication (postMessage)</h3>
        <p>Because panels run on <code>ui.naelao.app</code>, they cannot access the Shell's <code>localStorage</code>,
          DOM, or WebSocket. All communication is through a strictly typed JSON protocol validated on both sides:</p>
        <pre><code><span class="cm">// shared/ui-protocol.ts — the full message contract</span>

export type ShellToAppMessage =
  | { type: 'CONTEXT';        spaceId: string; agentId: string; theme: 'light'|'dark'; locale: string }
  | { type: 'PERSONALIZATION'; data: Record&lt;string, unknown&gt; }
  | { type: 'AGENT_STATUS';   status: 'idle'|'running'|'error' }
  | { type: 'THEME_CHANGE';   theme: 'light'|'dark' };

export type AppToShellMessage =
  | { type: 'NAVIGATE';       path: string }
  | { type: 'NOTIFY';         level: 'info'|'success'|'warning'|'error'; message: string }
  | { type: 'GET_CONTEXT' }
  | { type: 'RESIZE_WIDGET';  height: number }
  | { type: 'OPEN_DRAWER';    appId: string }
  | { type: 'RUN_AGENT';      prompt: string; agentId?: string }
  | { type: 'UPDATE_BADGE';   slot: string; count: number }
  | { type: 'WRITE_LEARNING'; key: string; value: unknown };  <span class="cm">// Writes to UIInstructionsDO</span>

<span class="cm">// ── App panel implementation (inside the iframe) ─────────────────────</span>
class NaelaoAppPanel {
  private shellOrigin = 'https://app.naelao.dev';

  constructor() {
    window.addEventListener('message', (e) => {
      if (e.origin !== this.shellOrigin) return;
      this.handleShellMessage(e.data as ShellToAppMessage);
    });
    <span class="cm">// Request initial context on load</span>
    this.postToShell({ type: 'GET_CONTEXT' });
  }

  private handleShellMessage(msg: ShellToAppMessage) {
    switch (msg.type) {
      case 'CONTEXT':
        this.init(msg.spaceId, msg.agentId, msg.theme);
        break;
      case 'THEME_CHANGE':
        document.documentElement.dataset.theme = msg.theme;
        break;
    }
  }

  notify(message: string, level: 'info'|'success'|'warning'|'error' = 'info') {
    this.postToShell({ type: 'NOTIFY', level, message });
  }

  runAgent(prompt: string) {
    this.postToShell({ type: 'RUN_AGENT', prompt });
  }

  private postToShell(msg: AppToShellMessage) {
    window.parent.postMessage(msg, this.shellOrigin);
  }
}

<span class="cm">// ── Shell listener (inside the main window) ──────────────────────────</span>
window.addEventListener('message', (event) => {
  if (event.origin !== 'https://ui.naelao.app') return;
  const msg = event.data as AppToShellMessage;

  switch (msg.type) {
    case 'NOTIFY':        showToast(msg.message, msg.level);       break;
    case 'NAVIGATE':      router.push(msg.path);                   break;
    case 'RESIZE_WIDGET': resizeIframe(event.source, msg.height);  break;
    case 'RUN_AGENT':     agentDispatch(msg.prompt, msg.agentId);  break;
    case 'WRITE_LEARNING': uiInstructionsDO.writeLearning(msg.key, msg.value); break;
  }
});</code></pre>

        <h3>UIInstructionsDO Personalisation</h3>
        <p>Every App panel is backed by a <code>UIInstructionsDO</code> instance, scoped to
          <code>{spaceId}:{appId}</code>. As the user interacts with the panel, the App writes structured "UI learnings"
          back to the Shell via <code>WRITE_LEARNING</code> messages. The DO persists these learnings. When the iframe
          reloads, the Shell injects the current learnings as a <code>personalization</code> JSON object in the URL
          hash:</p>
        <pre><code><span class="cm">// The Shell constructs the iframe src with personalization injected</span>
function buildPanelUrl(app: SlotDeclaration, learnings: Record&lt;string, unknown&gt;): string {
  const url = new URL(app.panelUrl);
  <span class="cm">// Hash is not sent to the server — safe for sensitive preferences</span>
  url.hash = encodeURIComponent(JSON.stringify({
    personalization: learnings,
    theme: getCurrentTheme(),
    locale: navigator.language,
  }));
  return url.toString();
}

<span class="cm">// The App reads it on load</span>
const hash = decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1));
const { personalization, theme, locale } = JSON.parse(hash);
<span class="cm">// e.g. personalization = { "preferDarkCards": true, "hideAdvancedFilter": false }</span></code></pre>

        <h3>Slot Conflict Resolution</h3>
        <p>Multiple Apps or a Space Type and an App can both declare the same slot. <code>UIOrchestrationDO</code>
          resolves conflicts by priority:</p>
        <ol class="fl">
          <li><strong>Space Type declarations</strong> have the highest priority. A Space Type that claims
            <code>main-content</code> for its own dashboard will always win over an App's request for the same slot.
          </li>
          <li><strong>Explicitly pinned Apps</strong> (pinned by the user in Settings) take the next priority level.
          </li>
          <li><strong>Recently installed Apps</strong> take the next level.</li>
          <li><strong>Sidebar and drawer slots are additive</strong> — multiple Apps can each have a sidebar icon; they
            stack vertically in install order. Only <code>main-content</code> and <code>dashboard-widget</code> are
            exclusive per-position.</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="box box-tl">
          <div class="box-lbl">Security Boundary Summary</div>
          <p>The Shell and App panels are on different origins by design. The Shell is on <code>app.naelao.dev</code>;
            panels are on <code>ui.naelao.app</code>. The browser's same-origin policy prevents panel code from
            accessing Shell cookies, <code>localStorage</code>, the WebSocket connection, or any DOM outside the iframe.
            The <code>postMessage</code> protocol is the only bridge, and both sides validate the <code>origin</code>
            header on every message. An App that sends a malformed or unknown message type is silently ignored — never
            executed.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     A8 — RemoteLoop: Custom Agent Loops
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
      <div class="sep"></div>
      <div class="sec" id="remoteloop-appendix">
        <span class="sec-ey">A8 — Custom Intelligence</span>
        <h2>RemoteLoop — Platform-Internal Custom Agent Brains</h2>
        <div class="bar"></div>
        <p class="lead">While Naelao provides three standard loops (Chat, Content, Task), some
          <strong>Naelao-built</strong> Space Types require unique reasoning patterns that cannot be expressed as
          variants of these models. <code>RemoteLoop</code> is the internal protocol that allows Naelao's own Workers to
          implement custom agent reasoning while delegating tool execution, security enforcement, and streaming to the
          core platform. <strong>This interface is not exposed to community developers.</strong> Community developers
          who need custom reasoning should publish a Naelao App with a custom MCP server instead — the MCP tool boundary
          provides the necessary isolation without exposing conversation history or Scoped Tokens.</p>

        <h3>The Inverse Proxy Pattern</h3>
        <p>The name "RemoteLoop" reflects the inversion: normally a Worker calls out to an LLM. In RemoteLoop, the
          agent's core (<code>AgentDO</code>) calls out to the Loop Worker, which runs an LLM chain internally and calls
          <em>back</em> to Naelao for any tool execution. The platform remains in control of all side-effects.</p>

        <div class="flow">
          <div class="fstep">
            <div class="fn">1</div>
            <div class="ft"><strong>Trigger:</strong> User message arrives at <code>AgentDO</code>. The DO reads the
              Space Type manifest and finds a <code>customLoopWorker</code> binding.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="fstep">
            <div class="fn">2</div>
            <div class="ft"><strong>Token issuance:</strong> <code>AgentDO</code> mints a short-lived Scoped Token (TTL:
              120s) bound to <code>spaceId</code> + <code>agentId</code> + the tool permissions declared in the
              manifest.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="fstep">
            <div class="fn">3</div>
            <div class="ft"><strong>Delegation:</strong> <code>AgentDO</code> POSTs the full
              <code>RemoteLoopRequest</code> to the Loop Worker via a CF Service Binding (no public network hop).</div>
          </div>
          <div class="fstep">
            <div class="fn">4</div>
            <div class="ft"><strong>Reasoning:</strong> The Loop Worker runs its custom LLM chain (Tree-of-Thought,
              recursive planner, etc.). It has no platform bindings of its own — it cannot read R2, call SpaceDO, or
              write to KV directly.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="fstep">
            <div class="fn">5</div>
            <div class="ft"><strong>Tool callbacks:</strong> When the Loop Worker needs to execute a tool (read_file,
              run_code, call_api), it calls the Naelao Scoped API at <code>https://api.naelao.dev/scoped/v1/tools</code>
              with the Scoped Token. Naelao validates the token, checks the tool against the allowlist, runs the
              security scanner, and returns the result.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="fstep">
            <div class="fn">6</div>
            <div class="ft"><strong>Streaming:</strong> The Loop Worker returns a <code>ReadableStream</code> of tokens
              to <code>AgentDO</code>, which validates the stream and forwards it over WebSocket to the user's browser.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3>RemoteLoop Request/Response Interfaces</h3>
        <pre><code><span class="cm">// shared/types/remoteloop.ts</span>

export interface RemoteLoopRequest {
  <span class="cm">// Identity</span>
  spaceId:  string;
  agentId:  string;
  turnId:   string;              <span class="cm">// UUID for this specific turn — used for idempotency</span>
  typeId:   string;              <span class="cm">// Space Type ID, e.g. 'mini-agi'</span>

  <span class="cm">// Conversation state</span>
  messages: Array&lt;{
    role:    'user' | 'assistant' | 'system' | 'tool';
    content: string;
    toolCallId?: string;         <span class="cm">// Present when role === 'tool'</span>
  }&gt;;

  <span class="cm">// Tool surface available to this Loop</span>
  availableTools: Array&lt;{
    name:        string;
    description: string;
    inputSchema: Record&lt;string, unknown&gt;;  <span class="cm">// JSON Schema</span>
  }&gt;;

  <span class="cm">// Credential for calling back to Naelao</span>
  scopedToken:   string;         <span class="cm">// JWT, signed by Naelao, TTL 120s</span>
  scopedApiBase: string;         <span class="cm">// Always 'https://api.naelao.dev/scoped/v1'</span>

  <span class="cm">// Optional context injected by the Space Type</span>
  spaceContext?: Record&lt;string, unknown&gt;;  <span class="cm">// e.g. { "caseNumber": "2024-CV-1234" }</span>
}

<span class="cm">// The Loop Worker must return a streaming Response</span>
<span class="cm">// Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8</span>
<span class="cm">// The stream is a sequence of UTF-8 text chunks (token fragments)</span>
<span class="cm">// Tool call results are interspersed as JSON lines prefixed with 'TOOL_RESULT:'</span>

<span class="cm">// Scoped API — tool execution endpoint</span>
export interface ScopedToolRequest {
  tool:    string;               <span class="cm">// Must be in the availableTools list</span>
  input:   Record&lt;string, unknown&gt;;
  turnId:  string;               <span class="cm">// Must match the turnId from the RemoteLoopRequest</span>
}

export interface ScopedToolResponse {
  result:  unknown;
  error?:  { code: string; message: string };
  latency: number;               <span class="cm">// ms</span>
}</code></pre>

        <h3>Calling the Scoped API from a Loop Worker</h3>
        <pre><code><span class="cm">// Inside a RemoteLoop Worker</span>
async function callTool(
  toolName: string,
  input: Record&lt;string, unknown&gt;,
  ctx: RemoteLoopRequest
): Promise&lt;unknown&gt; {
  const response = await fetch(`${ctx.scopedApiBase}/tools`, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'application/json',
      'Authorization': `Bearer ${ctx.scopedToken}`,
    },
    body: JSON.stringify({
      tool:   toolName,
      input,
      turnId: ctx.turnId,
    }),
  });

  if (!response.ok) {
    const err = await response.json() as { error: { code: string; message: string } };
    throw new Error(`Tool ${toolName} failed: ${err.error.message}`);
  }

  const { result } = await response.json() as ScopedToolResponse;
  return result;
}</code></pre>

        <h3>Worked Example — Legal Verticals Tree-of-Thought Loop</h3>
        <pre><code><span class="cm">// workers/naelao-legal-loop/src/index.ts</span>
<span class="cm">// Runs a 3-branch Tree-of-Thought: generates 3 legal analyses in parallel,</span>
<span class="cm">// scores them against a legal-reasoning rubric, streams the best one.</span>

export default {
  async fetch(request: Request, env: Env): Promise&lt;Response&gt; {
    const req = await request.json() as RemoteLoopRequest;

    <span class="cm">// Step 1: Generate 3 parallel analysis branches</span>
    const branches = await Promise.all([
      runAnalysisBranch(req, 'conservative'),
      runAnalysisBranch(req, 'balanced'),
      runAnalysisBranch(req, 'expansive'),
    ]);

    <span class="cm">// Step 2: Score each branch</span>
    const scored = await scoreBranches(branches, req);

    <span class="cm">// Step 3: Pick the highest-scoring branch</span>
    const winner = scored.sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score)[0];

    <span class="cm">// Step 4: Fetch supporting case law via the Scoped API</span>
    const cases = await callTool('knowledge_search', {
      query: winner.legalQuestion,
      topK: 5,
    }, req);

    <span class="cm">// Step 5: Stream the final answer with citations</span>
    const finalAnswer = await streamFinalAnswer(winner, cases, req);

    return new Response(finalAnswer, {
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain; charset=utf-8' },
    });
  }
}

async function runAnalysisBranch(
  req: RemoteLoopRequest,
  stance: 'conservative'|'balanced'|'expansive'
): Promise&lt;Branch&gt; {
  <span class="cm">// Call the LLM with a stance-specific system prompt</span>
  const prompt = buildBranchPrompt(req.messages, stance);
  return callLLM(prompt, env.AI);  <span class="cm">// CF Workers AI binding</span>
}</code></pre>

        <h3>Security Constraints the Loop Worker Cannot Bypass</h3>
        <table class="t">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Attempted Bypass</th>
              <th>Why It Fails</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="tl">Call a tool not in <code>availableTools</code></td>
              <td>Scoped API validates the tool name against the manifest allowlist. Returns 403.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="tl">Reuse the Scoped Token after the turn</td>
              <td>Token TTL is 120s. After expiry, all Scoped API calls return 401.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="tl">Use a different <code>turnId</code> to replay a tool call</td>
              <td>Naelao tracks <code>turnId</code> usage. A token cannot be used with a different <code>turnId</code>
                than the one it was issued for.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="tl">Access another space's files</td>
              <td>The token is bound to <code>spaceId</code>. File paths that don't match
                <code>workspace/{spaceId}/</code> return 403.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="tl">Make outbound HTTP calls directly</td>
              <td>Loop Workers have no egress. All side-effects must go through the Scoped API. Workers AI bindings are
                the only permitted direct call.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="tl">Return a response body larger than 4 MB per turn</td>
              <td><code>AgentDO</code> enforces a 4 MB stream size limit. Excess bytes are dropped and a truncation
                notice is appended.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">Why Community Developers Get MCP, Not RemoteLoop</div>
          <p>RemoteLoop gives the Worker full visibility of the conversation history, the Scoped Token, and the user's
            tool surface — all in a single request body. A community developer operating a RemoteLoop Worker could log
            conversation history, exfiltrate Scoped Tokens, or abuse the tool surface in ways the user has not consented
            to. The MCP tool-call model gives community developers a much narrower surface: they receive only the
            specific tool name and its typed input, nothing else. The security tradeoff is intentional and permanent.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     A9 — Agent Environment Declaration
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
      <div class="sep"></div>
      <div class="sec" id="agent-env-declaration">
        <span class="sec-ey">A9 — Runtime Constraints</span>
        <h2>Agent Environment Declaration — Layer 1 Deep Dive</h2>
        <div class="bar"></div>
        <p class="lead">The Agent Environment section of the manifest (Layer 1) is the "Hardware Spec" for the AI. It
          tells the provisioner exactly how to configure the <code>AgentDO</code> and its associated
          <code>SandboxDO</code>. This is where you define the difference between a "Light Chatbot" and a "Heavy Coding
          Agent." Every field in <code>agentConfig</code> has a direct runtime effect — this is not documentation, it is
          code.</p>

        <h3>Complete agentConfig Field Reference</h3>
        <table class="t">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Field</th>
              <th>Type</th>
              <th>Default</th>
              <th>Runtime Effect</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="mono">defaultExecutionTier</td>
              <td class="tl">"workspace" | "isolate" | "npm" | "browser" | "sandbox"</td>
              <td class="tl">"isolate"</td>
              <td>If the LLM calls a tool without specifying a tier, the orchestrator uses this tier. Set to "workspace"
                for writing-only agents (Sites). Set to "sandbox" for Developer and Enterprise.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="mono">containerIdleTimeoutMs</td>
              <td class="tl">number</td>
              <td class="tl">600000</td>
              <td>0 = containers disabled. -1 = always warm (never idle). Any positive value = SandboxDO sets a DO Alarm
                to terminate the container after this many ms of inactivity. Directly controls cost.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="mono">loopModel</td>
              <td class="tl">"chat" | "task-completion" | "content" | "knowledge-graph" | "federated-query" |
                "file-edit" | "worker-task"</td>
              <td class="tl">"chat"</td>
              <td>Selects which loop function in <code>agentpress/loops/</code> handles agent execution. Each loop has
                different iteration limits, output targets, and self-correction behaviors.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="mono">alwaysLoadedSkills</td>
              <td class="tl">string[]</td>
              <td class="tl">[]</td>
