        <div class="sec" id="three-tier-platform">
          <span class="sec-ey">48 — Business Architecture</span>
          <h2>The Three-Tier Platform Model</h2>
          <div class="bar"></div>

          <p class="lead">Naelao operates as a three-tier infrastructure platform. This is not a feature — it is the
            fundamental business architecture that governs every API call, every permission check, every billing
            calculation, and every data isolation guarantee in the system. Every developer working on this codebase must
            internalize this model before touching a single file.</p>

          <div class="box box-or">
            <div class="box-lbl">Architectural Law — Not a Guideline</div>
            <p style="margin:0;font-size:13.5px;">Every data access, every DO instantiation, every KV read, every R2
              bucket operation, and every database query in the system is scoped to one of these three tiers. There is
              no operation in Naelao that is tier-agnostic. If you cannot identify which tier an operation belongs to,
              you have not understood the operation. Stop. Identify the tier. Then write the code.</p>
          </div>

          <h3>Tier 0 — Naelao (Infrastructure Owner)</h3>
          <p>Naelao owns the execution environment entirely. Workers, Durable Objects, Cloudflare bindings, billing
            rails, auth primitives, the agent runtime, storage buckets, and the agent loop itself are all Tier 0
            concerns. No Tier 1 builder can modify, bypass, or inspect this layer. Naelao is to builders what AWS is to
            Anthropic, what Stripe is to Shopify, what Twilio is to any application built on top of it. The
            infrastructure is invisible to end users and opaque to builders — they consume it through defined APIs only.
          </p>
          <p>Tier 0 controls: the credit metering system, the agent execution loop, container orchestration, the AI
            gateway, the KV routing layer, all Cloudflare service bindings, the platform-level auth primitives (JWT
            signing keys, refresh token issuance), and the wholesale billing calculation engine.</p>

          <h3>Tier 1 — Builders (Product Owners)</h3>
          <p>Builders are companies or individuals who build complete products on top of Naelao infrastructure. They are
            the equivalent of Anthropic building Claude, Harvey AI building a legal product, Cursor building a coding
            assistant, or a SaaS founder building an industry-specific AI tool. A Tier 1 builder controls their end user
            pool and authentication flow, their product UI and custom domain, their agent configuration and system
            prompts, their pricing model and revenue strategy, and their admin functionality. They do not control: the
            infrastructure running their product, the agent loop execution model, the security boundaries between
            tenants, or Naelao's billing rates.</p>
          <p>Builders pay Naelao at wholesale compute and credit rates. They charge their end users at whatever rates
            they choose. The margin between wholesale and retail is the builder's business model.</p>

          <h3>Tier 2 — End Users (Product Consumers)</h3>
          <p>End users sign into the Tier 1 product. They have zero awareness of Naelao. They use "LexSpace" or
            "CodePilot" or "FirmAI" — whatever the builder named and branded their product. Naelao branding is
            suppressed entirely at the product layer. End users are not Naelao platform users. They are product users.
            Their identity, sessions, data, and billing relationship are all scoped to the product they signed up for —
            not to Naelao.</p>

