          <div class="bar"></div>

          <p class="lead">Naelao itself has a UI. Two distinct surfaces: the <strong>Naelao Platform</strong> (for
            Naelao's own team and builder onboarding) and the <strong>Builder Dashboard</strong> (for Tier 1 builders
            creating and managing their products). Neither surface is ever seen by end users of builder products.</p>

          <div class="pull">"Naelao is not a product. Naelao is the factory that builds products. The factory is never
            shown to the factory's customers."</div>

          <h3>Surface 1 — The Naelao Platform (platform.naelao.app)</h3>
          <p>Used by the Naelao team. Contains: the builder registration and approval flow, the global infrastructure
            dashboard (active DOs, KV usage, R2 storage, credit metering health), the conversation log access approval
            queue (where Naelao staff manually reviews builder requests to enable <code>conversationLogAccess</code>),
            the wholesale billing console, and the global product directory. From the platform, Naelao staff can suspend
            builder products, approve or reject conversation log access requests, view aggregated usage across all
            builders (without reading individual conversations), and trigger infrastructure maintenance events.</p>

          <h3>Surface 2 — The Builder Dashboard (dashboard.naelao.app)</h3>
          <p>The Builder Dashboard is the primary interface for Tier 1 builders. It is designed to feel like Vercel,
            Stripe, or Cloudflare's developer dashboard — infrastructure-first, precise, no consumer-product aesthetics.
            It serves a technical audience comfortable with API keys, webhooks, auth providers, and credit metering.</p>

