          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Local Tunnel Security — User's Entire Filesystem Accessible via Path Traversal</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>Local Tunnel Security — User's Entire Filesystem Accessible via Path Traversal LocalTunnelDO relays commands to the user's local machine via a WebSocket. The permission system stores granted paths in SQLite and validates them with prefix matching: "a grant for /home/user/projects also covers /home/user/projects/myapp ." The traversal vulnerability: a command like cat /home/user/projects/../../../etc/passwd passes the prefix check because the path starts with the granted prefix — the ../ sequences are never resolved before comparison. An agent (or a prompt-injected instruction, see T-68) can read any file on the user's machine by constructing a path that starts with a granted prefix but…</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p> Additional Gaps in LocalTunnelDO Security Symlink escape: Even with realpathSync , a symlink inside the granted path pointing outside it ( /home/user/projects/secrets → /etc/ssl/private ) escapes the check unless symlinks are validated too. realpathSync resolves them — but the resolved target must also be under the grant. Command injection: The command relay passes the agent's command string to the local shell. A command like cat file.txt; rm -rf ~ passes as a single string. The local CLI must sanitize or use execFile with argument arrays, never exec with string interpolation. Gap T-83 — High</p>
        </div>
        <pre style="background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--brd);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;margin-top:12px;"><code style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--cy);line-height:1.6;">// WRONG — in LocalTunnelDO (path.startsWith is not safe):
const granted = db.exec(&#x27;SELECT path FROM permissions WHERE can_read=1&#x27;).rows.map(r=&gt;r[0]);
const allowed = granted.some(g =&gt; requestedPath.startsWith(g));  // traversal bypasses this

// RIGHT — resolve the path canonically BEFORE the prefix check
// This must be done in the LOCAL CLI agent (Node.js side), not in the DO,
// because the DO doesn&#x27;t know the local filesystem&#x27;s real paths

// Local CLI agent (runs on user&#x27;s machine) — command validator:
import path from &#x27;node:path&#x27;;
import fs from &#x27;node:fs&#x27;;

function resolveAndCheck(requestedPath: string, grantedPaths: string[]): string {
  // Resolve to absolute canonical path (resolves ../, symlinks, etc.)
  const resolved = fs.realpathSync(path.resolve(requestedPath));

  const allowed = grantedPaths.some(g =&gt; {
    const grantedResolved = fs.realpathSync(path.resolve(g));
    // Check that resolved is UNDER the granted path (with trailing slash to avoid prefix tricks)
    return resolved === grantedResolved || resolved.startsWith(grantedResolved + path.sep);
  });

  if (!allowed) throw new Error(`Access denied: ${requestedPath} is not under any granted path`);
  return resolved;
}
// This runs locally — the DO only sends commands, never validates paths itself
// The CLI agent is the only security boundary for local filesystem access</code></pre>
