          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">R2 Path Prefixes — No Filesystem-Level Isolation; Path Traversal via Crafted Keys</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>R2 Path Prefixes — No Filesystem-Level Isolation; Path Traversal via Crafted Keys R2 organizes spaces by prefix: `spaces/{spaceId}/...`. There is no filesystem-level isolation — R2 is a flat key-value store. An agent running in Space A (ID: `abc-123`) could theoretically craft a path like `spaces/abc-123-../../../other-space/secret-file` and attempt to traverse out of its namespace. While modern S3-compatible APIs don't interpret `..` in keys (they're treated as literal characters), the risk is that agents could list prefixes and discover sibling spaces if the prefix matching is loose. For example, `SpaceR2.list('spaces/abc-123')` might match both `spaces/abc-123/` and…</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Required Fix Add a trailing slash to all R2 path prefixes: `spaces/{spaceId}/` with a required trailing slash. Enforce this in the SpaceR2 helper — never allow a path that doesn't start with exactly `spaces/{spaceId}/`. In the listing function, check that returned keys match exactly the pattern `spaces/{spaceId}/*` and reject any keys with adjacent space IDs. Consider using R2 signed URLs with tight expiry windows instead of giving agents direct R2 access — this reduces the risk of path crafting entirely. Severity: Medium — R2 prefix isolation depends on strict string matching, not filesystem barriers. Malformed paths or prefix matching bugs could allow adjacent spaces to be listed or accessed. T-154 — ConcurrencyDO</p>
