      <div id="G-OPE-03" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-OPE-03</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Provisioner Rollback — Migrations Are Declared Irreversible, Rollback SQL Is Optional</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>Provisioner Rollback — Migrations Are Declared Irreversible, Rollback SQL Is Optional The Provisioner's compensation log pattern is sound for phases 2–4 (KV slug lock, SpaceDO row, DO assembly). But phase 3 SQL migrations have a critical gap: `rollbackSql` is optional . If a migration does not include a rollback SQL, and a later phase fails, the compensating run skips that migration entirely. The SpaceDO has had DDL applied (e.g. `ALTER TABLE … ADD COLUMN`) that is now orphaned — the space record is deleted but the schema change persists in the DO's SQLite storage. On the next provision attempt with the same DO name, the schema is already partially applied, and the idempotent `IF NOT…</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Required Fix Make `rollbackSql` required for any migration that adds columns or tables. Fail manifest validation at publish time if a migration lacks its inverse. Alternatively, provision into a new DO name on every attempt (include a generation counter in the DO name) so partial-migration DOs are never reused. Add a `migrationVersion` table to the DO SQLite so the provisioner can detect partially-applied migrations on retry. Severity: High — Silent data corruption on retry after partial provision failure. Hardest to debug because the error appears at runtime, not at provision time. T-129 — Space Type Versioning</p>
        </div>
        <pre style="background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--brd);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;margin-top:12px;"><code style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--cy);line-height:1.6;">Phase 3: SQL migration runs → adds column &#x27;tax_rate&#x27; to custom_invoices
Phase 4: DO assembly fails (binding not found in env)
Compensation: DELETE FROM spaces WHERE id = ?  ← space row removed
BUT: rollbackSql not provided → &#x27;tax_rate&#x27; column stays in DO SQLite

Next provision attempt:
→ new spaceId → new DO → clean slate ✓  (if different DO name)
→ same spaceId retry → same DO → ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS passes, 
  but no data integrity check, index on tax_rate may be missing</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div id="G-OPE-04" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-OPE-04</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Canary Rollout — Error Spike Detection Uses a 5% Threshold With No Sensitivity Tuning</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>Canary Rollout — Error Spike Detection Uses a 5% Threshold With No Sensitivity Tuning The `SpaceTypeRegistryDO` canary engine auto-rolls back a minor version when `errorRate &gt; 0.05`. This threshold is hard-coded. There is no specification for: (a) what counts as an "error" in the Analytics Engine query — a 4xx HTTP response? An uncaught exception? A billing deduction failure? (b) whether the 5% is measured over all event types or only `agent_run_failed` events; (c) what the minimum sample size is before the threshold is meaningful (a Space with 2 users and 1 error triggers a rollback at 50%); (d) what happens if the Analytics Engine query itself fails — the `checkErrorRateForVersion()`…</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Required Fix Define exactly which Analytics Engine event types count as errors for canary evaluation. Add a minimum sample gate: only evaluate error rate when at least N=50 events have been recorded for the canary cohort. Wrap `checkErrorRateForVersion()` in try-catch; on failure, set canary to paused (not rolled back, not progressed) and alert. Make the error threshold a manifest-level configuration field so different Space Types can tune their own rollback sensitivity. Severity: Medium — Canary rollouts with low traffic volumes will produce incorrect rollback decisions. A query failure silently freezes the rollout at 5%. T-130 — Space Type Versioning</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-OPE-05" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-OPE-05</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Major Version Opt-In — No Deadline, No Auto-Upgrade, No Incompatibility Guard</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>Major Version Opt-In — No Deadline, No Auto-Upgrade, No Incompatibility Guard Major version bumps are "opt-in only" — Space Admins see an "Upgrade Available" banner and choose when to migrate. The spec does not define: (a) what happens if a Space never upgrades and the old Worker code is sunset; (b) whether Naelao can force-upgrade after a deadline; (c) whether a v1 Space can install an App that requires v2 of its Space Type; (d) how long old migration endpoints stay alive. If a legal-verticals v1.0 Space stays on v1 indefinitely and the v1 Worker is removed after 12 months, the Space simply stops working with no warning.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Required Fix Add a `supportedUntil` field to the Space Type version record. Notify Space Admins 30 days before EOL. Auto-upgrade to major if admin does not act within EOL window (with a forced maintenance window). Add an `minSpaceTypeVersion` field to App manifests so the Apps marketplace enforces compatibility gates. Keep migration endpoints alive for at least 90 days after a major version ships, with a deprecation header on responses after 60 days. Severity: Medium — Spaces on old major versions will silently break when old Workers are removed. Compatibility between Apps and Space Type versions is completely unguarded. T-131 — ProjectDO</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-OPE-06" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-OPE-06</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">File Lock TTL — Agent Crash Orphans the Lock for 60 Seconds, Blocking All Writers</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>File Lock TTL — Agent Crash Orphans the Lock for 60 Seconds, Blocking All Writers The `ProjectDO.acquireLock()` method releases stale locks via a 60-second `expires_at` TTL. This is the only recovery mechanism. If an agent run is cancelled mid-write (user presses Stop, or the DO crashes), the lock is orphaned for up to 60 seconds. During that window every other agent or user trying to write the same file gets `{ acquired: false }` — the spec does not define whether the caller should retry, show an error, or queue behind the lock. The frontend has no mechanism to show "locked by Agent X — wait 45s" because the lock holder info is only returned from the DO, not surfaced to the UI layer.…</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Required Fix Halve the default TTL to 30 seconds and make it configurable per call. Add an admin-force-release endpoint on ProjectDO (`POST /force-release-lock`) callable by the naelao-admin Worker for stuck locks. Define the UI state when a lock is held: show a spinner with "File locked by [agent name], retrying…" and auto-retry every 5 seconds. The sweeper's cleanup path should call force-release (not releaseLock) to avoid the ownership check throwing. Severity: Medium — Every agent crash produces a 60-second write blockout on affected files. In multi-agent projects this silently stalls parallel agents. T-132 — ProjectDO</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-OPE-07" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-OPE-07</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">3-Way Merge Is Line-Based Only — Binary Files and JSON Silently Corrupt on Concurrent Write</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>3-Way Merge Is Line-Based Only — Binary Files and JSON Silently Corrupt on Concurrent Write The `mergeTextFile()` implementation is a line-based split on `\n`. It handles the three trivial cases (both same, only A changed, only B changed) correctly, but when both agents modify the same file concurrently it produces a `&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; / ======= / &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;` conflict marker block. This is only meaningful for plain text. The spec applies this merge to all file types listed in the comment: "Markdown, code, JSON". JSON files with conflict markers are syntactically invalid and will crash any consumer that parses them. Binary files (images, PDFs, WASM) should never go through a text merge at all — the…</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Required Fix Before merging, inspect file extension. Binary types (`.png .jpg .pdf .wasm .zip`) must not be merged — keep the latest write and log a conflict event to the activity feed. JSON files: parse both versions, use a structural JSON merge (last-write-wins per key, with conflict logging) rather than line-based. Enforce that `mergeTextFile()` is only reachable when a lock was not acquired (i.e., document the exact call site) or remove it and rely purely on the lock system. Severity: High — JSON files written by two concurrent agents will silently become invalid JSON with no error. Any downstream parse will throw, and the user sees no indication of data corruption. T-133 — AgentPress Error Processor</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-OPE-08" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-OPE-08</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Error Processor — Retry Budget Never Defined; shouldRetry: true Errors Can Loop Forever</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>Error Processor — Retry Budget Never Defined; `shouldRetry: true` Errors Can Loop Forever The AgentPress Error Processor marks some errors as `shouldRetry: true` (e.g., `ERR_TOOL_SYNTAX` for malformed XML). The orchestrator is expected to retry the tool call automatically. However the spec defines no retry budget. There is no `maxRetries` counter, no back-off delay, and no escalation path when retries are exhausted. An LLM that consistently produces malformed XML (due to a model bug or a very long context that pushes the XML template out of context) will retry indefinitely, consuming tokens and credits until the run is force-cancelled by the sweeper's heartbeat timeout (which is 10…</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Required Fix Add `maxRetries: number` and `backoffMs: number` to the `ProcessedError` interface. Default: `maxRetries: 3, backoffMs: 1000`. The orchestrator must track a per-error-code retry counter per run turn. When `maxRetries` is exceeded, escalate to a fatal run error even if `isFatal` was originally false. For `isFatal: true` errors, the orchestrator must write a `status: 'awaiting_user'` row in the run WAL and suspend the agent loop (not terminate). The WebSocket connection stays alive. A user response reactivates the loop. Severity: High — A model stuck on malformed XML will spin for 10 minutes consuming tokens. Fatal errors that require user input have no mechanism to actually pause and await that input. T-134 — Operator Shell Slot System</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-OPE-09" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-OPE-09</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Analytics Blob Field Mapping — Not Versioned; Schema Changes Break Queries</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>Analytics Blob Field Mapping — Not Versioned; Schema Changes Break Queries The mapEventToDataPoint() function maps event fields to Analytics Engine blob and doubles arrays. For example, AgentRunEvent maps to: `blobs: [type, userId, agentId, runId, agentDefinitionId, mode, errorCode]`. If a new event field is added later (e.g., model name), the mapping must change: the new field goes into blob[7], and all previous events had 7 blobs. Old queries looking for blob[6] (errorCode) now get the wrong data. The mapping is not versioned, so there's no way to know which blob index corresponds to which field across time.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Required Fix Version the mapping: include a `schema_version` in the Analytics Engine data point. When queries run, they check the schema version and use the correct blob/doubles mapping. Alternatively, use named fields instead of positional indexing: Analytics Engine supports JSON payloads, so use `blobs: { type: "...", userId: "...", agentId: "..." }` instead of positional arrays. Document the blob field order explicitly and never change it retroactively. If a new field is needed, add it to the END of the blobs array. Severity: Medium — Analytics blob field positions not versioned. Schema changes break old queries. Historical analytics data becomes uninterpretable. T-168 — Space Type Architecture</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-OPE-10" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-OPE-10</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Space Type Version Compatibility — No Backward Compatibility Check; Unknown Loop Models Crash</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>Space Type Version Compatibility — No Backward Compatibility Check; Unknown Loop Models Crash The space type manifest includes a version field (semver). Minor version bumps are auto-upgraded; major version bumps require user opt-in migration. However, there is no validation that the platform supports the specified loopModel. If a community developer publishes a space type with `loopModel: 'quantum-reasoning'` (fictional, never implemented), the platform doesn't reject it at install time. When an agent tries to run in that space, it attempts to load the custom loop worker, which doesn't exist, and crashes. The manifest doesn't validate against an enum of supported loop models.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
