          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Federated Learning LLM Judge — Confidence Threshold at 0.95 With No Fallback; Rejection Silent</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>Federated Learning LLM Judge — Confidence Threshold at 0.95 With No Fallback; Rejection Silent The `proposeLearning()` method calls an LLM judge to determine if a proposed learning is data-free before accepting it. The LLM must return `{"safe": true, "confidence": 0.95+}` to be accepted. However: (a) the LLM can hallucinate and return a false positive, approving a learning that actually contains company data; (b) if the judge returns `confidence: 0.93` (below threshold), the learning is rejected silently — there is no fallback or escalation to a human reviewer; (c) the judge is called on every proposal, but there is no caching of the judgment, so similar proposals are re-evaluated…</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Required Fix Add a `judgmentCache` table keyed by proposal fingerprint. Reuse judgments for identical proposals across instances. For borderline confidences (0.85–0.95), escalate to the Worker creator via a notification instead of silently rejecting. Let them decide whether to accept the learning. Add a max-tokens budget to the judge LLM call (200 is already specified) and add retry logic if the LLM times out or returns invalid JSON. Require human approval for federated learnings that will change agent behavior in production (confidence threshold for auto-approval should be configurable). Severity: Medium — LLM judge can hallucinate and approve unsafe learnings. Borderline proposals are silently rejected with no human review path. Judgment results are never cached. T-139 — Naelao Apps</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-MIS-11" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;margin-bottom:6px;">
