          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Domain Matching — Hardcoded Provider Domains; Custom Domains Undetected and Credentials Not Injected</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>Domain Matching — Hardcoded Provider Domains; Custom Domains Undetected and Credentials Not Injected The `getCredentialForUrl()` method matches URLs to providers using a hardcoded lookup: `PROVIDER_DOMAINS = { 'github': ['api.github.com', 'github.com'], ... }`. If a customer has a custom GitHub instance at `github.internal.company.com`, the domain matching fails because 'github.internal.company.com' is not in the hardcoded list. The method returns empty headers and the API call fails without auth. Users would need to manually provide the API key in headers, defeating the purpose of the credentials system. Additionally, new providers are not easy to add — they require editing the hardcoded…</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Required Fix Allow users to specify custom domain patterns when storing credentials: `{ provider: 'github', customDomains: ['github.internal.company.com'] }`. Store these patterns in the mcp_credentials table. On getCredentialForUrl, check both hardcoded domains and custom domains from the database. Add a provider registry table so new providers can be added via the UI without modifying Worker code. Severity: Medium — Custom domains and private API endpoints don't match hardcoded provider domains. Credentials aren't auto-injected, breaking authentication for non-standard deployments. T-158 — file-manager Skill</p>
