    <!-- =====================================================================
         APPENDIX B — NEW GAP RESOLUTIONS (N01–N30)
         30 additional technical gaps discovered in deep audit, June 2026
         ===================================================================== -->
    <div id="gap-resolutions-b" style="margin-top:80px;padding-top:40px;border-top:2px solid var(--bl);">

      <h1 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:52px;font-weight:700;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.03em;line-height:1.05;margin-bottom:16px;">
        Appendix B — Deep Audit Gap Resolutions
      </h1>
      <p class="lead">30 additional technical gaps discovered in a second-pass deep audit, focusing on Cloudflare
        platform hard limits, features that will not work at all as specified, and costs that will surprise at scale.
        Gaps are labelled G-N01 through G-N30. These are in addition to the 125 gaps resolved in Appendix A.</p>

      <div class="box box-rd" style="margin:32px 0 48px;">
        <div class="box-lbl">Severity Ratings</div>
        <p><strong>CRITICAL</strong> — The feature does not work at all as specified; will fail at deploy time or first
          run. <strong>HIGH</strong> — The feature works under light load but breaks in production, causes data loss,
          or creates a security hole. <strong>MEDIUM</strong> — Will cost significantly at scale or cause
          reliability degradation over time.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Cloudflare Platform Limits</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N01" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N01</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">CRITICAL</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">NaelaoAgentDO — CF Worker CPU Time Budget Exceeded by Agent Loop</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>NaelaoAgentDO's agent loop runs inside a single DO request handler: build the system prompt, inject memory items, serialize all MCP tool schemas, call the LLM API (network wait), parse the XML response, and dispatch tool calls. The CPU-bound phases alone (prompt construction iterating 20 memory items + all skill schemas + all MCP schemas) consume 5–15 ms of CPU time before the network call even starts. On standard Cloudflare Workers the CPU time budget is 10 ms on the free plan and 30 s on the Unbound billing model. The spec never declares NaelaoAgentDO (or any agent-loop Worker) as Unbound, which means the agent product will hit CPU timeout on the default Worker configuration and will not run at all.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Declare every Worker that houses an agent loop as Unbound in wrangler.jsonc: set limits.cpu_ms = 30000 for NaelaoAgentDO, AssistantDO, and any DO whose request handler drives an agent iteration. Add an architecture rule: 'Any Durable Object that calls an LLM API must be deployed as an Unbound Worker.' Validate this in CI by grepping wrangler.jsonc for each DO class and asserting limits.cpu_ms is present.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N03" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N03</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">CRITICAL</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Dynamic TypeScript Execution — CF Workers Prohibit eval() and Dynamic import()</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The Dynamic Worker Loader (Artifacts) and agent-written workflow code are described as TypeScript loaded from R2 at runtime and executed. CF Workers explicitly disable eval(), new Function(), and dynamic import() of URLs — these are prohibited by the V8 isolate sandbox. There is no way to execute arbitrary JavaScript loaded from a URL inside a CF Worker. The only way to run agent-written code is to deploy a new Worker via the CF Workers API (requires CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN, takes ~30 seconds, creates a new isolate). The spec never mentions the deployment step, its latency, or its API token requirement. The Artifacts feature and all dynamic code execution will fail at runtime with a 'Code generation from strings disallowed' error.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Use CF Workers for Platforms (Dispatch Namespace) as the execution model for agent-written code. When an agent writes a workflow script: (1) upload the compiled JS to the dispatch namespace via the Workers API, (2) assign it a unique script name (artifact:{spaceId}:{hash}), (3) invoke it via the dispatch namespace binding. Alternatively, restrict agent-written logic to a JSON-based DSL (step definitions, conditional branches, tool call sequences) interpreted by a fixed platform Worker — no code generation needed. Declare a dispatch_namespaces binding in wrangler.jsonc and a CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN secret for the Workers API deployment step.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N04" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N04</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">CRITICAL</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">SpaceDO SQLite — 1 GB Per-Account Ceiling, Not Per-DO</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>CF Durable Objects SQLite storage has a 1 GB limit per Cloudflare account, not per DO instance. The spec treats SpaceDO as if each space gets an unlimited personal SQLite database and stores threads, messages, memories, knowledge chunks, workflow definitions, and file metadata all in SpaceDO SQLite. With 100 active spaces the average per-space budget is 10 MB. A single space with a large knowledge base (5,000 chunks × 2 KB each = 10 MB) consumes the entire per-space budget. When the account-wide 1 GB limit is reached, all writes to all SpaceDOs fail simultaneously with no warning to the user.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Move large data out of DO SQLite into purpose-fit stores: knowledge chunks and message bodies → R2 (blob storage, no per-account size cap); relational cross-space queries → Cloudflare D1 (serverless SQLite with larger limits). DO SQLite should hold only hot-path metadata: active session IDs, permission grants, run state, schema version. Add a storage monitor: a DO alarm that fires when ctx.storage.sql.databaseSize() exceeds 800 MB account-wide (query via CF API from naelao-admin) and sends an alert before the ceiling is hit.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N06" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N06</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">CF Workflows — 1,000 Step Hard Limit Kills Long-Running Agents</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>CF Workflows (the engine behind runFiber() and Dynamic Workflows) have a hard limit of 1,000 steps per workflow instance. The spec describes long-running agent workflows with loops: 'run until task is complete,' retry loops, multi-step research pipelines, and MiniAGI orchestration trees. A research agent making 10 tool calls per iteration over 150 iterations consumes 1,500 steps and is silently terminated mid-run. The spec never mentions this limit or a strategy for workflows requiring more than 1,000 steps.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Implement workflow checkpointing at step 800: serialize the current agent state (conversation history, tool results, pending goals) to R2 at fibers/{spaceId}/{fiberId}/checkpoint.json. Complete the current workflow instance and immediately spawn a continuation workflow that restores from the checkpoint and resumes execution. The runFiber() API must transparently chain continuations so callers see a single logical workflow regardless of how many CF Workflow instances it spans internally.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N07" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N07</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">CF Queue — 128 KB Message Limit Silently Drops Large Tool Results</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>CF Queues have a 128 KB maximum message size. The spec passes tool results through CF Queues between workers (agent loop → tool executor → result delivery). Tool results routinely exceed 128 KB: a read_file of a large source file, a web_search returning a long article, execute_python stdout from a data processing script, or a base64-encoded screenshot. CF Queues reject oversized messages with no dead-letter; the message is silently dropped. The agent receives no tool output and either hallucinates a result or errors with a confusing 'no tool response' message.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Any tool result exceeding 64 KB must be stored in R2 before being placed in the queue. The queue message contains only a reference: { type: 'r2_ref', key: 'tool-results/{runId}/{toolCallId}', size: bytes }. The queue consumer checks for type === 'r2_ref' and fetches from R2 before injecting the result into the agent context. Add a MAX_INLINE_RESULT_BYTES = 65536 constant and enforce it in every tool executor. This also prevents context overflow from raw tool output.</p>
        </div>
        <pre style="background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--brd);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;margin-top:12px;"><code style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--cy);line-height:1.6;">// Tool result dispatch — auto-promotes large results to R2
async function dispatchToolResult(env: Env, result: ToolResult): Promise&lt;void&gt; {
  const body = JSON.stringify(result);
  if (body.length &gt; MAX_INLINE_RESULT_BYTES) {
    const key = `tool-results/${result.runId}/${result.callId}`;
    await env.R2.put(key, body, { httpMetadata: { contentType: &#x27;application/json&#x27; } });
    await env.QUEUE.send({ type: &#x27;r2_ref&#x27;, key, size: body.length, callId: result.callId });
  } else {
    await env.QUEUE.send({ type: &#x27;inline&#x27;, ...result });
  }
}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N15" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N15</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Analytics Engine — All Spec Queries Use Wrong SQL Dialect; Every Dashboard Breaks</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>CF Analytics Engine uses ClickHouse-compatible SQL queried via a GraphQL API. The spec shows Analytics Engine queries written as standard PostgreSQL SQL with JOIN across tables, named columns, HAVING clauses, and percentile_cont() aggregations. CF Analytics Engine has no JOIN support (single flat event model), uses blob1–blob20 and double1–double20 for all fields (no named columns), and uses quantile() not percentile_cont(). Every analytics query in the spec will fail with a syntax or schema error at runtime. This means the entire observability layer — agent run metrics, credit usage charts, space growth dashboards — produces no data.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Rewrite all Analytics Engine queries to use the actual CF schema. Define a schema mapping file (analytics-schema.ts) that documents which slot each field occupies: e.g. blob1=event_name, blob2=space_id, blob3=agent_id, double1=credits_used, double2=duration_ms. Example corrected query: SELECT blob1 AS event_name, SUM(double1) AS total_credits FROM naelao_analytics WHERE blob1 = 'agent_run' AND timestamp &gt; NOW() - INTERVAL 7 DAY GROUP BY blob1. Validate all queries against CF Analytics Engine's API before any dashboard ships. Add a CI step that runs each analytics query against a CF staging account and asserts non-null results.</p>
        </div>
        <pre style="background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--brd);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;margin-top:12px;"><code style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--cy);line-height:1.6;">// analytics-schema.ts — CF Analytics Engine field mapping
export const ANALYTICS_SCHEMA = {
  // blob fields (string, up to 1KB each)
  event_name: &#x27;blob1&#x27;,   // e.g. &#x27;agent_run&#x27;, &#x27;tool_call&#x27;, &#x27;credit_debit&#x27;
  space_id:   &#x27;blob2&#x27;,
  agent_id:   &#x27;blob3&#x27;,
  tool_name:  &#x27;blob4&#x27;,
  model_id:   &#x27;blob5&#x27;,
  // double fields (float64)
  credits_used:  &#x27;double1&#x27;,
  duration_ms:   &#x27;double2&#x27;,
  token_count:   &#x27;double3&#x27;,
} as const;</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N23" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N23</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">MEDIUM</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">CF KV Namespace — 1,000 Account Limit Broken by Dynamic Namespace Creation</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-bl">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>CF allows a maximum of 1,000 KV namespaces per account. The spec uses KV for skill caching, session token storage, space type metadata caching, and potentially other per-space fast-access stores. If any component creates KV namespaces dynamically at runtime (e.g. one namespace per space, one per skill), the platform hits the account limit at 1,000 spaces and all subsequent KV operations for new resources fail. The spec is ambiguous about whether KV namespaces are statically declared in wrangler.jsonc (safe) or created at runtime via the KV API (dangerous).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>All KV namespaces must be statically declared in wrangler.jsonc as named bindings: SKILL_CACHE, SESSION_STORE, SPACE_TYPE_CACHE. Never create KV namespaces at runtime. For per-space data, use key namespacing within a single KV namespace: key pattern space:{spaceId}:{field}. Document in architecture principles: 'KV namespaces are account-level infrastructure resources. The platform uses a fixed set of N namespaces declared at deploy time. Runtime namespace creation is prohibited.' Add a CI check that counts KV namespace bindings in wrangler.jsonc and fails the build if the count exceeds 20 (leaving headroom for growth).</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N30" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N30</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">MEDIUM</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Durable Object Class Count — 20+ Classes Approaching 500/Account Limit</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-bl">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>CF accounts are limited to 500 Durable Object classes per account. The spec already defines 20+ named DO classes and the extensibility model allows space types to register custom DOs and the Artifacts feature deploys dynamic Workers (potentially with associated DOs). As third-party space types grow, each registering one or more custom DO classes, the account limit will be approached. The spec contains no DO class budget policy, no governance process for new DO class approval, and no alternative pattern for space types that need custom DO-like behaviour without consuming a class slot.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Document the DO class budget in architecture principles: 'DO classes are scarce account-level resources (500/account limit). The platform reserves 50 for first-party infrastructure DOs. Third-party space types must use the AssistantSandboxDO moltworker pattern (dispatch namespace workers) rather than registering new DO classes. Any new first-party DO class requires platform architecture review.' Track DO class count in the naelao-admin dashboard. Add a CI gate that counts DO class declarations in all wrangler.jsonc files and fails the build if the count exceeds 40.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Security &amp; Multi-Tenant Isolation</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N02" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N02</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">CRITICAL</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">SpaceDO SQLite — Application-Level 'RLS' Has No Enforcement Mechanism</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The spec repeatedly references 'RLS policies' and 'Row Level Security' for SpaceDO's SQLite database. Cloudflare DO SQLite (via ctx.storage.sql) is standard SQLite — it does not support PostgreSQL-style RLS (CREATE POLICY, ALTER TABLE ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY). Every 'RLS policy' in the spec is an application-level WHERE space_id = ? clause added manually to each query. There is no query builder, ORM, or ESLint rule that enforces this clause. A single developer forgetting the WHERE clause on any query — a list endpoint, a search handler, an admin route — silently returns data from all tenants. This is an undetectable multi-tenant data breach that requires only one missing WHERE clause anywhere in the codebase.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Define a mandatory SpaceDB wrapper class that takes spaceId in its constructor and appends AND space_id = ? to every query via a typed query builder. Direct ctx.storage.sql.exec() calls must be banned via an ESLint no-restricted-syntax rule that forbids importing ctx.storage.sql directly in any module except SpaceDB. The wrapper must be the only path to SpaceDO's SQLite. Add an integration test that calls every list/search endpoint without a spaceId header and asserts it returns 0 rows, not rows from other spaces.</p>
        </div>
        <pre style="background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--brd);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;margin-top:12px;"><code style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--cy);line-height:1.6;">// SpaceDB wrapper — the ONLY path to SpaceDO SQLite
class SpaceDB {
  constructor(private sql: SqlStorage, private spaceId: string) {}
  exec&lt;T&gt;(query: string, ...params: unknown[]): T[] {
    // automatically append space isolation to every WHERE clause
    const isolated = query.includes(&#x27;WHERE&#x27;)
      ? query.replace(/WHERE/i, &#x27;WHERE space_id = ? AND&#x27;)
      : query + &#x27; WHERE space_id = ?&#x27;;
    return [...this.sql.exec&lt;T&gt;(isolated, this.spaceId, ...params)];
  }
}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N17" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N17</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Community Skills via npm — No Version Pinning Enables Supply Chain Attack</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>Skills loaded from npm packages default to the latest tag when no version is pinned in the skill manifest. A community skill author whose npm account is compromised can publish a malicious new version that all agents using that skill silently receive on the next skill cache miss. The spec mentions SHA256 verification of the currently cached skill file, but this provides no protection if the new (malicious) version is fetched and cached. There is no version pinning requirement, no integrity hash in the skill manifest, and no approval gate for community skill version upgrades.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Skill manifests must include a required version field (semver, not 'latest') and an integrity field (sha512 hash in npm subresource integrity format, e.g. sha512-abc123==). SkillRegistryDO must reject skill installations where version === 'latest' or integrity is absent. When a new skill version is published: (1) the platform flags it as pending-review, (2) builders receive an in-app notification, (3) builders explicitly approve the upgrade in their space settings. Auto-upgrades are disabled for all community skills. Platform skills (first-party) can auto-upgrade within a major version.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Agent Runtime &amp; Workflows</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N05" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N05</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">runFiber() Result Delivery — Caller Cannot Receive the Result</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>runFiber() starts a CF Workflow instance and the spec implies the result is returned to the calling agent. CF Workflows are asynchronous — the caller receives a workflow instance ID immediately; the result is produced later. If a fiber runs for 30 minutes, the original HTTP request that launched it has timed out after CF's 30-second limit. The agent loop that awaits the fiber result will hang until the Worker request times out, returning an empty or error response. The spec never specifies how the eventual fiber result is delivered back to the agent — no polling endpoint, no WebSocket push, no callback mechanism.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>runFiber() must return immediately with { fiberId, status: 'running' }. On completion, the workflow writes its result to R2 at a well-known key (fibers/{spaceId}/{fiberId}/result.json) and sets a 'completed' flag in SpaceDO SQLite. The agent polls GET /fibers/{fiberId}/result with exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s, max 30s). For streaming results, the Space Assistant WebSocket connection is used to push fiber.complete events to the frontend in real time. The agent loop must treat fiber results as async tool results, not synchronous return values.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N10" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N10</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Skills (SKILL.md) — No Execution Runtime Specified; Skills Are Inert Documentation</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>Skills are defined as SKILL.md files with YAML frontmatter describing tool names, parameters, descriptions, and examples. A SKILL.md is a markdown document — it cannot be executed. The spec never defines a skill executor: a component that reads the SKILL.md, extracts a tool implementation, and runs it with actual inputs. The implied model is that the LLM reads the SKILL.md and generates the correct tool calls itself (prompt engineering). This means skill reliability is entirely LLM-dependent: skills cannot be unit-tested deterministically, cannot call external APIs with guaranteed request shapes, and cannot enforce parameter contracts. The entire skill marketplace is built on an unspecified and undeterministic foundation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Define a concrete skill execution model: skills are implemented as CF Worker endpoints registered in the dispatch namespace. Each skill has a worker entry point (skill.worker.ts) that exports a fetch() handler. The SKILL.md describes the API; the skill.worker.ts implements it. SkillRegistryDO stores the dispatch namespace script name alongside the SKILL.md. When the agent calls a skill tool, naelao-mcp-hub routes the call to the skill's dispatch namespace worker — deterministic execution, testable, sandboxed. Skills that are LLM-mediated (no code implementation) must be explicitly marked type: 'llm-mediated' in SKILL.md so builders understand the reliability model.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N11" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N11</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">MCP Tool Calls via naelao-mcp-hub — 30s CF Worker Timeout Truncates Long Tools</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>naelao-mcp-hub proxies MCP tool calls from agents to external MCP app servers using Server-Sent Events (SSE). CF Workers have a 30-second response timeout (wall clock). A long-running MCP tool — a web scraper, a code compiler, a PDF parser, or any tool that itself calls an LLM — will have its SSE stream terminated by CF after 30 seconds. The MCP client (NaelaoAgentDO) receives a truncated or empty response with no error signal. The agent sees a null tool result and may hallucinate, retry indefinitely, or stall. The spec never addresses this timeout in the MCP hub design.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>For MCP tool calls expected to take &gt;10 seconds, naelao-mcp-hub must use an async pattern: (1) Accept the tool call request and immediately return { callId, status: 'pending' } within the 30s window. (2) A DO (McpProxyDO) opens the long-lived connection to the external MCP server and streams partial results to R2. (3) The agent polls GET /mcp/calls/{callId}/result until status is 'complete'. Tool manifests must declare an estimated_duration field; tools with estimated_duration &gt; 10s automatically use the async path.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N14" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N14</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">LoRA Fine-Tuning — Together AI Job Poll Runs in 30s Worker, Not a Durable Workflow</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The fine-tuning pipeline calls Together AI to start a training job, then polls GET /v1/fine-tunes/{jobId} for completion. Together AI fine-tuning jobs run for 30 minutes to 4+ hours. The polling code is shown in the naelao-sweeper cron, which has a 30-second execution budget. A single cron invocation cannot track a 4-hour job. If the sweeper fires every 5 minutes and the job fails between polls, the failure is missed and FinetuneJobDO stays in a 'running' state permanently. The spec never specifies what happens to the model deployment step if the sweeper crashes mid-poll.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Replace the sweeper-poll approach with a CF Workflow per fine-tune job. The workflow: step 1 — POST to Together AI to start the job and save jobId. step 2 — step.sleep('1h'). step 3 — GET job status; if still running, goto step 2 (up to 24 iterations = 24 hours max). step 4 — on success, trigger model registration in SpaceDO and notify the builder. step 5 — on failure, write error to FinetuneJobDO and alert. CF Workflows survive across sleep periods making them the correct primitive for multi-hour async jobs.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N24" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N24</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Sub-Agent Recursion — No Maximum Depth; Exponential Resource Exhaustion</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>MiniAGI and the general sub-agent system allow an orchestrator agent to spawn sub-agents, which can spawn their own sub-agents. The spec describes sub-tree cancellation as a gap but never specifies a maximum recursion depth. An agent with a complex goal can spawn 10 sub-agents, each of which spawns 10 more, creating 100 leaf agents each consuming compute and credits. At depth 3 with branching factor 5: 125 concurrent agents. A misconfigured or malicious agent can create an exponential tree that drains the user's credit balance and platform resources in seconds before any billing circuit breaker can respond.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Enforce a hard maximum sub-agent depth of 5 (configurable per plan tier: free=3, pro=5, enterprise=8). Pass the current depth in every spawn payload: { ..., _meta: { depth: currentDepth + 1, rootRunId } }. AssistantDO must reject spawn requests where _meta.depth &gt; maxDepth with ERR_MAX_DEPTH_EXCEEDED. This check must also be enforced in ConcurrencyDO to prevent bypass via direct DO stub calls. Log all depth-exceeded rejections to Analytics Engine for abuse monitoring.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Billing &amp; Payments</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N08" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N08</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">WalletDO — Single DO Serializes All Credit Debits, Bottleneck at Scale</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>WalletDO is a single Durable Object per user. DO request handlers execute sequentially — one at a time. Every agent action that costs credits sends a debit request to WalletDO. In MiniAGI mode with 10 concurrent sub-agents each making one tool call per second, WalletDO receives 10 debit requests per second, each requiring a SQLite read-modify-write (~5 ms). The 10th request waits ~45 ms in queue. At 50 sub-agents (a realistic MiniAGI orchestration tree) every tool call adds 250 ms of queuing latency — making the agent feel frozen to the user. The spec's MiniAGI and Launchpad features require exactly this level of parallelism.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Replace per-call debits with a credit reservation model: at agent run start, call walletDO.reserve(estimatedCredits) to pre-allocate a credit pool for the entire run. Sub-agents draw from a local in-memory counter (no DO call). At run end, call walletDO.commit(actualUsed, reservationId) to settle the actual amount. If actual &gt; reserved, the overage is debited in the commit. This reduces WalletDO calls from N-per-tool-call to 2-per-agent-run regardless of sub-agent count. Add walletDO.reserve(), walletDO.commit(), and walletDO.cancel() to the WalletDO API spec.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N22" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N22</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Stripe Webhook — No Idempotency; Retries Cause Double Credit Top-Ups</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>When a user purchases credits via Stripe, Stripe sends a webhook to naelao-billing's POST /webhooks/stripe. Stripe retries webhooks on 5xx responses or timeouts, delivering the same event up to 3 times within 24 hours. The spec's handleCheckoutComplete() function calls walletDO.topUp(credits) with no check for whether this Stripe payment intent has already been processed. A single Stripe infrastructure blip causing a 2-second response delay (past Stripe's 1-second webhook timeout) results in a retry and a double credit grant. This is trivially exploitable by an attacker who can intercept and replay webhook calls.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Add a processed_stripe_events table to naelao-billing's SpaceDO SQLite: CREATE TABLE processed_stripe_events (event_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, processed_at TEXT). Before applying any webhook: check if event_id exists, return HTTP 200 immediately if so without crediting. On successful credit application, INSERT the event_id. This INSERT must be in the same SQLite transaction as the credit UPDATE. Use the Stripe event ID (evt_xxx) as the dedup key, not the payment intent ID. Respond to Stripe within 5 seconds to avoid spurious retries.</p>
        </div>
        <pre style="background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--brd);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;margin-top:12px;"><code style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--cy);line-height:1.6;">async function handleStripeWebhook(event: StripeEvent, spaceDb: SpaceDB) {
  // idempotency check — must be first
  const existing = spaceDb.exec(
    &#x27;SELECT event_id FROM processed_stripe_events WHERE event_id = ?&#x27;,
    event.id
  );
  if (existing.length &gt; 0) return new Response(&#x27;already processed&#x27;, { status: 200 });

  // process in a single atomic transaction
  spaceDb.exec(&#x27;BEGIN&#x27;);
  spaceDb.exec(&#x27;INSERT INTO processed_stripe_events VALUES (?, ?)&#x27;, event.id, new Date().toISOString());
  spaceDb.exec(&#x27;UPDATE wallet SET credits = credits + ? WHERE id = ?&#x27;, creditsToAdd, userId);
  spaceDb.exec(&#x27;COMMIT&#x27;);
}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Scalability</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N09" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N09</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">SpaceTypeRegistryDO — Global Singleton Hot-Spot With No Cache Layer</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>SpaceTypeRegistryDO is instantiated as a single global Durable Object ('global' instance key) that handles all space type lookups, canary rollout decisions, install confirmations, and version checks. Every space creation and every page load that needs to verify the current space type version must query this one DO. DOs process requests sequentially. At 1,000 active spaces each doing a space type version check on page load (a cold start scenario after a deploy), all 1,000 requests queue behind each other in a single DO. The spec shows no caching layer in front of SpaceTypeRegistryDO.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Cache space type metadata in CF KV with a 60-second TTL, keyed by space_type_id:version:metadata. SpaceTypeRegistryDO is only queried on cache miss or on explicit canary rollout decisions (which are infrequent). For the hot path (version check on every space load), a Worker-level in-memory LRU cache (Map with 100-entry cap, 30s TTL) eliminates KV round-trips within a single isolate lifetime. SpaceTypeRegistryDO becomes a write-path-only component in steady state.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N28" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N28</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">MEDIUM</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">R2 Single Bucket — No Per-Space Rate Limiting Creates Noisy Neighbour</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-bl">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The spec uses a single R2 bucket (naelao-workspace) for all user spaces with path prefix isolation (spaces/{spaceId}/...). R2 has undocumented per-bucket throughput limits. One power user running an agent that performs continuous file tree traversal or knowledge base re-indexing (1,000+ R2 operations per minute) can consume the bucket's API rate allowance and cause throttling errors (429/503) for all other users' file operations simultaneously. The spec specifies no per-space R2 operation rate limit.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Implement per-space R2 operation rate limiting in the file-manager skill. Track R2 op count in SpaceDO SQLite: a r2_ops_current_minute INTEGER counter reset by a DO alarm every 60 seconds. Maximum 200 R2 operations per minute per space on free plans, 1,000 on pro. Reject operations above the limit with 429 Too Many Requests and a Retry-After: 60 header. Log throttle events to Analytics Engine for noisy-neighbour detection. Enterprise plans get dedicated R2 buckets provisioned at space creation.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Missing Specifications</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N12" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N12</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">EmDash CMS — Sites Space Type Foundation Is Completely Unspecified</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The Sites space type is described as 'AI-Managed Web Publishing (EmDash)' in multiple sections. EmDash is referenced as a CMS layer that manages content collections, generates site structure, handles builds, and serves dynamic routes. However EmDash has zero specification in the entire document: no API contract, no Worker endpoints, no content schema, no CF Pages build pipeline, no R2 asset storage layout. EmDash is not a known public product or open-source project — it appears to be a placeholder name for a system that has not been designed. The entire Sites space type (one of the 8 primary space types) cannot be built without this specification.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Replace 'EmDash' with a concrete Sites CMS specification. Content model: SpaceDO SQLite tables — pages (id, slug, title, content_r2_key, status), collections (id, name, schema_json), assets (id, r2_key, mime, size). Build pipeline: agent writes content to SpaceDO → triggers CF Pages deploy via Pages API (POST /accounts/{id}/pages/projects/{name}/deployments) → static assets served from R2 behind a custom domain. Preview mode: serve draft content via a Worker (GET /{slug}?preview=true reads from SpaceDO directly). Publishing: flip page.status from 'draft' to 'published' and trigger a Pages rebuild. Specify all of this in a new 'naelao-sites' worker section.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N13" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N13</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">VAPI Voice Calls — Webhook Callback Receiver Never Specified</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The spec integrates VAPI for agent voice calls and notifications. VAPI initiates calls asynchronously and delivers the transcript and outcome via a POST webhook to a callback URL. The spec never specifies a VAPI webhook receiver: which Worker handles POST /webhooks/vapi, how VAPI's HMAC signature is verified, where call transcripts are stored, or how the result is routed back to the agent that triggered the call. Without this receiver, voice calls are fire-and-forget: the agent can start a call but never learns what was said, whether the call connected, or what action the customer took. Voice is described as a key Launchpad feature for customer-facing agents.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Add POST /webhooks/vapi to naelao-notifications. Handler steps: (1) Verify X-VAPI-Signature HMAC-SHA256 against the webhook secret. (2) Parse the VAPI call event (call.ended, call.failed, transcript.ready). (3) Write to SpaceDO SQLite: INSERT INTO vapi_calls (call_id, space_id, transcript, outcome, duration_ms, ended_at). (4) Push a WebSocket message to the Space Assistant: { type: 'vapi.call_complete', callId, transcript, outcome }. (5) If an agentRunId is attached to the call metadata, resume the waiting agent fiber with the call result as a tool response.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N27" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N27</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">CRITICAL</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">BuilderAccountDO — Zero Schema; Blocker for Auth, Rate-Limiting, and Billing Stack</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>BuilderAccountDO is the first object checked on every API request and the authoritative store for builder identity, plan tier, feature flags, API rate limits, and billing status. Despite being the most critical Durable Object in the system, the spec provides zero implementation detail: no SQLite table definitions, no API endpoints, no alarm handlers, no field names, no plan tier values, no stub TypeScript. Every other component — auth middleware, rate limiting, plan enforcement, billing, credit checks — depends on BuilderAccountDO existing and returning data. Without its schema, the entire auth and billing stack is blocked.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Specify BuilderAccountDO in full. SQLite schema: CREATE TABLE builders (id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, email TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL, plan TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'free', credit_balance_cents INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0, api_key_hash TEXT, rate_limit_rpm INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 60, status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active', created_at TEXT, trial_ends_at TEXT). API endpoints: GET / (return builder profile), PUT /plan (upgrade plan), POST /credits/add (add credits), GET /usage (current month usage). Alarms: monthly_reset (first of each month — reset RPM counters), trial_expiry (fire at trial_ends_at — downgrade to free if not upgraded). Auth: every naelao-* Worker must stub BuilderAccountDO.verify(apiKeyHash) returning { builderId, plan, rateLimit }.</p>
        </div>
        <pre style="background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--brd);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;margin-top:12px;"><code style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--cy);line-height:1.6;">// BuilderAccountDO — minimum viable schema
const SCHEMA = `
  CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS builders (
    id            TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
    email         TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    plan          TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT &#x27;free&#x27;,
    credit_cents  INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    api_key_hash  TEXT,
    rate_limit_rpm INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 60,
    status        TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT &#x27;active&#x27;,
    created_at    TEXT DEFAULT (datetime(&#x27;now&#x27;)),
    trial_ends_at TEXT
  );
`;</code></pre>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Operational</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N16" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N16</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">MEDIUM</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">APNs Certificate — Expires After Exactly 1 Year With No Rotation Alert</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-bl">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The spec stores the Apple Push Notification service (APNs) p8 key in the secrets store for iOS push notification delivery. APNs p8 keys are valid for exactly 1 year. When the key expires all iOS push notifications silently stop delivering — APNs returns a 401 AuthenticationTokenExpired error, but without a logged alert this goes unnoticed until users report that notifications stopped working. The spec never stores the expiry date, never schedules a rotation reminder, and never specifies the APNs environment (production vs. sandbox).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>In naelao-credentials, store apns_key_id, apns_team_id, apns_key (p8 content), apns_key_expires_at (ISO8601), and apns_environment ('production' | 'sandbox'). Add a naelao-sweeper cron check that runs monthly: if apns_key_expires_at is within 30 days, send an email alert to the platform admin via Resend with subject 'ACTION REQUIRED: APNs key expires in N days'. Document the manual rotation procedure: generate a new p8 key in Apple Developer portal, update naelao-credentials, restart naelao-notifications.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N21" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N21</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Canary Rollout — Schema Migrations Are Irreversible; No Rollback SQL Specified</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>Canary rollouts for space type version upgrades run SpaceDO SQLite schema migrations (ALTER TABLE, CREATE INDEX, CREATE TABLE). SQLite ALTER TABLE operations are largely irreversible: columns cannot be dropped in older SQLite versions, and CF's SQLite version is not specified. If a canary version has a bug discovered after 5% of spaces have migrated, rolling back the Worker code is possible, but rolling back the schema is not. The migrated spaces now run old Worker code against a schema that old code does not understand. The spec mentions rollback as a goal but provides zero rollback SQL for any migration.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>All schema migrations must be backward-compatible: only add columns with DEFAULT values, never drop or rename existing columns. Each migration file must include a companion V{N}_rollback.sql that undoes the change (e.g. removes an added column via CREATE TABLE ... AS SELECT ... excluding the new column, then rename). Migration state is tracked in schema_migrations table (version INTEGER, applied_at TEXT, checksum TEXT). CI runs both forward and rollback migrations against a test SpaceDO before any canary ships. The canary deployment pipeline must run the rollback migration on all affected spaces before rolling back the Worker code.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N25" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N25</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">MEDIUM</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-sweeper — CF Cron Has No Delivery Guarantee; Critical Ops Must Use DO Alarms</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-bl">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>naelao-sweeper is a CF cron Worker running every 5 minutes. CF Worker crons have no delivery guarantee — they are 'at least once' and may fire late, early, or be skipped entirely during CF infrastructure maintenance. The spec relies on the sweeper for time-critical operations: assistant heartbeat checks, WAL entry cleanup, subscription expiry enforcement, fine-tuning job polling, and scheduled cron-job dispatch. A skipped sweeper invocation means expired subscriptions stay active, stale WAL entries accumulate, and scheduled agent jobs don't fire. A double-fire triggers the already-known cron double-dispatch race condition.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Classify sweeper operations by time-sensitivity. Critical (subscription expiry, WAL cleanup): migrate to DO alarms on the relevant DO — alarms are guaranteed-once delivery. Semi-critical (heartbeat checks, job polling): keep in sweeper but make strictly idempotent by checking timestamps, never assuming fixed intervals. All sweeper operations must use compare-and-swap patterns: read last_processed_at, skip if &lt; 4 minutes ago, process and write new timestamp in one DO transaction. This makes double-fires safe.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Developer Space</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N18" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N18</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Multiple Local Machines Connected to Same Space — No Conflict Model</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The spec allows a builder to connect their local machine to a Developer Space via LocalTunnelDO. It does not address the case where two team members connect their laptops to the same Space simultaneously. LocalTunnelDO would have two active WebSocket connections and would need to decide which local machine handles a given execute_bash or read_file call. Without a specified routing model, both machines may respond to the same tool call (first response wins, second dropped), or the DO may route to an arbitrary machine. File system commands run against two different file systems producing non-deterministic results.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>LocalTunnelDO must enforce at most one active local machine connection per Space. On a second connection attempt, reject with HTTP 409: { error: 'LOCAL_MACHINE_CONFLICT', message: 'Machine alice-laptop is already connected. Disconnect it first or use container execution.' }. Store the active connection's machine_name and connected_at in DO SQLite. Expose a DELETE /local-tunnel endpoint to force-disconnect a stale session. For teams that genuinely need multi-machine routing, define named machine slots in the skill manifest: tool_routing.local_machine: 'alice-laptop'.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Infrastructure Cost</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N19" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N19</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">MEDIUM</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">CF Analytics Engine — 25M Event/Month Free Quota Exceeded at 10k Users</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-bl">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>CF Analytics Engine has a 25 million data point monthly limit on free plans. The spec fires an Analytics Engine event for every agent action: tool calls, message sends, credit debits, space loads, and skill invocations. At 10,000 active users each making 50 tool calls per day the platform generates 10,000 × 50 × 30 = 15 million tool-call events per month, plus message, load, and credit events pushing the total above 50 million. Analytics Engine billing above the free tier is $0.25 per million events. At 100,000 active users the analytics bill alone exceeds $5,000/month — never mentioned in the infrastructure cost model.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Batch analytics writes: accumulate events in a Worker-isolate in-memory buffer (max 25 events or 5 seconds, whichever comes first) and flush as a single CF Analytics Engine batch write (up to 25 data points per API call). This reduces API calls by 25× with no loss of data. For very high-volume events (individual token counts), sample at 10%: record 1 in 10 events and multiply the value by 10 in dashboard queries. Add an estimated analytics cost line to the infrastructure cost model: N users × 150 events/day × 30 days × $0.25/1M = cost per month.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Real-Time Collaboration</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N20" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N20</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">MEDIUM</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">ProjectDO Presence — In-Memory Presence Map Lost on DO Hibernation</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-bl">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>ProjectDO maintains a presence map (which users are editing which files, cursor positions, active locks) in a JavaScript Map in DO memory. When CF hibernates the DO after a period of inactivity, the in-memory presence map is destroyed. When the next user connects, presence starts empty — all other connected users appear to have left, even if they reconnected after hibernation. Cursor positions and edit locks are silently lost. The spec describes hibernation as the normal DO lifecycle but provides no persistence strategy for presence state.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Persist the presence map to DO SQLite on every change (debounced 500ms): INSERT OR REPLACE INTO presence (user_id, file_path, cursor_line, cursor_col, last_seen) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP). On DO startup (webSocketMessage handler first call after hibernation), reload presence from SQLite and broadcast a presence-sync event to all connected clients. Evict stale presence entries where last_seen &lt; NOW() - 60 seconds. Client sends a presence heartbeat every 30 seconds to keep the entry alive.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Notifications</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N26" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N26</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">MEDIUM</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Push Notification Ordering — FCM/APNs Do Not Preserve Message Sequence</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-bl">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>FCM (Android) and APNs (iOS) explicitly do not guarantee message delivery order. The spec sends rapid status update sequences via push notifications: 'task started' → 'tool called' → 'task complete' or 'payment processed' → 'payment confirmed'. These notifications may arrive at the user's device in any order. For transactional sequences (payment processing) out-of-order delivery is a correctness issue: the user sees 'payment confirmed' before 'payment processing'. No sequence numbers or timestamps are included in notification payloads in the spec.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Include sequence_number (monotonically increasing integer per space, stored in SpaceDO SQLite) and sent_at (ISO8601 timestamp) in every notification payload. The mobile client must buffer notifications received out of order and display them in sequence_number order. For transactional notifications (payment, auth, billing) use email delivery via Resend in addition to push — SMTP maintains delivery order within a session. Mark transactional notifications with priority: 'high' in the FCM/APNs payload to reduce OS-level batching delay.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Third-Party Integrations</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N29" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">G-N29</span>
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);background:rgba(232,105,42,.1);padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;">HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Together AI — Single Shared API Key; One User's Overuse Blocks Everyone</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>naelao-together-ai uses a single platform-level Together AI API key for all users' LLM inference calls, fine-tuning jobs, and model hosting. If a heavy user triggers Together AI rate limits on the shared key (requests-per-minute or daily token cap), the entire platform loses access to open-source model inference simultaneously. There is no per-user key isolation, no key pooling, and no fallback key rotation. The spec mentions BYOK for the AI Gateway but not for Together AI specifically, leaving all users dependent on the shared platform key.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Implement per-user Together AI key injection: builders on Pro+ plans can supply their own Together AI API key stored in naelao-credentials (TOGETHER_AI_KEY). naelao-together-ai checks for a space-level key first; falls back to the platform pool key. For the platform pool: maintain 3–5 Together AI API keys (TOGETHER_AI_KEY_1 … _5) and round-robin requests across them with per-key rate limit tracking in a ConcurrencyDO. If a key hits a 429, mark it in backoff for 60 seconds and route to the next key.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

