    <div id="gap-resolutions-c" style="margin-top:80px;padding-top:40px;border-top:2px solid var(--pu);">

      <h1 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:52px;font-weight:700;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.03em;line-height:1.05;margin-bottom:16px;">
        Appendix C — Second-Pass Audit Gap Resolutions
      </h1>
      <p class="lead">22 additional critical gaps found in a targeted second-pass audit focusing on concrete code-level
        defects: wrong SQL syntax, reversed array operations, hardcoded strings, missing error handlers, and
        architectural chokepoints that will OOM or deadlock under production load. Labels G-N31 through G-N52.</p>

      <div class="box box-pu" style="margin:32px 0 48px;">
        <div class="box-lbl">Why These Gaps Are Especially Dangerous</div>
        <p>Unlike architectural gaps (which are caught in design review), these are implementation-level defects that
          appear correct on first reading. The RegExp lastIndex bug looks like normal pattern matching. The
          splice-in-reverse loop looks like a valid array manipulation. The 'unknown' hardcode looks like a reasonable
          default. Each will pass code review and only manifest under specific runtime conditions — concurrent load,
          large datasets, long agent sessions — making them extremely difficult to diagnose in production.</p>
