      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Scheduling &amp; Workflows</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N31" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--rd);background:rgba(239,68,68,.12);">G-N31 · CRITICAL</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">WorkflowRegistryDO cron Parser — Day-of-Month Field Skipped; Range/List Expressions Return NaN</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>parseCronToNextTimestamp() destructures the cron string as const [min, hour, , month, dow] = cron.split(' ') — the third field (day-of-month) is deliberately skipped with a blank slot. Any workflow scheduled with a specific day (e.g. '0 9 15 * *' — 9am on the 15th) fires on every day of the month because the day-of-month constraint is never checked. Additionally, the comparison uses parseInt(dow) === next.getUTCDay() which means any range expression ('1-5' for weekdays) or list ('1,3,5') returns NaN from parseInt(), which never equals a day number — so any workflow using standard cron range/list syntax silently never fires. The O(n) synchronous loop runs up to 10,080 iterations (7 days × 1440 min/day) inside the DO's CPU budget with no progress log.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Replace the hand-rolled parser entirely. Use a CF-compatible bundled cron-parser library (e.g. cronstrue, cron-parser compiled to ESM). At minimum fix the implementation: (1) parse all 5 fields including day-of-month, (2) handle range (1-5), list (1,3,5), and step (*/15) syntax using set-based matching not parseInt, (3) replace the O(n) minute-loop with direct timestamp arithmetic. Store computed next_run_at in the DB and use a CF Alarm to fire at that exact time rather than iterating minutes.</p>
        </div>
        <pre style="background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--brd);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;margin-top:12px;"><code style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--cy);line-height:1.6;">// Corrected cron next-timestamp using cron-parser (bundled)
import parser from &#x27;cron-parser&#x27;;

function parseCronToNextTimestamp(cron: string, after = new Date()): number {
  const interval = parser.parseExpression(cron, {
    currentDate: after,
    utc: true,
  });
  return interval.next().getTime();
}</code></pre>
