          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-N32 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">WorkflowRegistryDO error_count — Cumulative, Never Reset; Transient Errors Permanently Pause Healthy Workflows</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The auto-pause logic increments error_count on every failure but never resets it on success. A workflow that runs successfully 100 times, hits a transient network error, then succeeds 2 more times, then hits another transient error accumulates error_count = 2 and pauses on the next error — even though it has been mostly healthy. The SQL UPDATE workflows SET error_count=error_count+1 has no corresponding reset on successful execution. The pause threshold check reads the pre-increment value so the pause triggers after 3 lifetime errors regardless of when they occurred or whether they were consecutive. Paused workflows produce no notification — operators discover the failure only when a scheduled task simply stops running.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Track consecutive_error_count (reset to 0 on success, increment on failure) separately from total_error_count (append-only for observability). Auto-pause only when consecutive_error_count &gt;= 3. On successful execution: UPDATE workflows SET consecutive_error_count=0, last_success_at=? WHERE id=?. When a workflow is auto-paused send a notification via naelao-notifications to the space owner: subject 'Workflow paused after 3 consecutive failures', body includes the workflow name, last error message, and a resume link.</p>
