      <div id="G-N52" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-N52 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">WorkflowRegistryDO Alarm Dispatches Workflows Sequentially — Long Dispatch Blocks All Other Scheduled Jobs</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>WorkflowRegistryDO uses a single CF Alarm to fire workflow dispatch. When the alarm fires, it iterates all due workflows and dispatches them sequentially within the single DO execution context. If Workflow A's dispatch (invoking an agent, calling an LLM) takes significant time, it blocks Workflows B, C, and D. DO handlers have a 30-second timeout. At any meaningful workflow density — 100 spaces each with an hourly workflow all scheduled at the top of the hour — sequential dispatch within 30 seconds is impossible. Workflows beyond the first N dispatched are silently skipped with no retry or catch-up for the missed cycle.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>In the alarm handler, fan out workflow dispatch to a CF Queue rather than executing dispatches inline: for each due workflow, call env.WORKFLOW_DISPATCH_QUEUE.send({ workflowId, scheduledAt }) — this is fast (microseconds per send, not seconds per dispatch). The alarm handler completes quickly regardless of how many workflows are due. A separate Queue consumer Worker handles the actual dispatch: it receives individual workflow jobs, invokes the agent, and handles retries via CF Queue's built-in retry semantics (exponential backoff, dead-letter on repeated failure). This decouples scheduling latency from execution latency.</p>
        </div>
        <pre style="background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--brd);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;margin-top:12px;"><code style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--cy);line-height:1.6;">// In the DO alarm handler — fast fan-out only
async alarm() {
  const dueWorkflows = this.db.exec&lt;{id: string}&gt;(
    `SELECT id FROM workflows WHERE next_run_at &lt;= datetime(&#x27;now&#x27;) AND status=&#x27;active&#x27;`
  );

  // Send to queue — microseconds each, not seconds
  await Promise.all(
    dueWorkflows.map(wf =&gt;
      this.env.WORKFLOW_DISPATCH_QUEUE.send({
        workflowId: wf.id,
        scheduledAt: new Date().toISOString(),
      })
    )
  );

  // Reschedule alarm for next dispatch window
  this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + 60_000);
