      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Security</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N33" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--rd);background:rgba(239,68,68,.12);">G-N33 · CRITICAL</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">27-Pattern Security Scanner — Module-Level Global RegExp With /g Flag; Concurrent Requests Corrupt Scan Results</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The BLOCKED_PATTERNS array is declared at module scope with RegExp literals using the g (global) flag. JavaScript RegExp objects with the g flag maintain lastIndex state between calls to .test() or .exec(). In a CF Worker handling concurrent requests within the same isolate, two simultaneous calls to scanCode() share the same RegExp objects. Request A's lastIndex is partially advanced when Request B reads it, causing alternating matches to be missed (lastIndex skips ahead) or phantom matches to be reported (stale lastIndex applied to a different string). Security bypass rate approaches 50% under concurrent load. An attacker submitting code concurrently with another user can reliably bypass the scanner. CF Workers do not serialize requests within an isolate.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Move BLOCKED_PATTERNS inside the scanCode() function body so each call gets fresh RegExp instances with lastIndex=0, OR reconstruct the RegExp on each use: new RegExp(pat.source, pat.flags.replace('g', '')). Remove the g flag entirely since .test() on a non-global RegExp is stateless and correct for presence detection. Add a concurrent-request unit test: call scanCode() twice simultaneously with payloads containing patterns at different positions and assert both return the correct result.</p>
        </div>
        <pre style="background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--brd);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;margin-top:12px;"><code style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--cy);line-height:1.6;">// WRONG — module-level stateful RegExp with /g flag
const BLOCKED_PATTERNS = [
  { pattern: /process\.env\./g, severity: &#x27;critical&#x27; },
];

// CORRECT — reconstruct per call, no /g flag needed for .test()
function scanCode(code: string) {
  const PATTERNS = [
    { pattern: /process\.env\./, severity: &#x27;critical&#x27; },
    { pattern: /require\([&#x27;&quot;]child_process[&#x27;&quot;\)]/, severity: &#x27;critical&#x27; },
  ];
  return PATTERNS.filter(p =&gt; p.pattern.test(code));
}</code></pre>
