          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-N48 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Dynamic Worker Loader — No Sandbox Memory Limit; Malicious Code Exhausts Host Worker Heap</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The Dynamic Worker Loader evaluates space code in the same V8 heap as the host Worker. There is no memory quota applied to evaluated code. A malicious or buggy space script that allocates large buffers (new ArrayBuffer(100 * 1024 * 1024)) consumes the shared 128 MB Worker memory limit, causing OOM for both the space code and the host infrastructure. A tight loop in space code also consumes the Worker's 30s wall-clock timeout, blocking all requests sharing the isolate. If the loader is a singleton DO (a common pattern), a single malicious space takes down code execution for all spaces globally until the DO restarts.</p>
