          <p>Implement the full VAPI webhook lifecycle: (1) POST /webhooks/vapi in naelao-notifications — verify X-VAPI-Signature HMAC, parse call.ended event, extract transcript and outcome. (2) When initiating the call, store a pending escalation record in SpaceDO SQLite: INSERT INTO escalations (call_id, fiber_id, space_id, expires_at). (3) On webhook receipt, look up the fiber by call_id, write the result to DO storage, set a CF Alarm to wake the DO and resume the fiber with the call result as a tool response. (4) Add a timeout alarm (10 minutes default) after which the escalation auto-rejects with outcome='no_answer' so the fiber is never permanently stuck.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">LoRA Fine-Tuning</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N38" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-N38 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">LoRA Training Data Loaded Entirely Into Worker Memory — Files Over 50 MB Crash With OOM</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The fine-tuning pipeline reads the entire training dataset into memory: const trainingData = await trainingObj.text(). CF Workers have a 128 MB memory limit per isolate. A production fine-tune with 10,000 training examples × 500 tokens each is 20–50 MB of JSONL. The .text() call materializes the entire file as a JavaScript UTF-16 string (2× byte count), JSON.parse() creates another copy, and the Together AI API call serializes a third copy. Three copies of a 50 MB file = 150 MB, exceeding the 128 MB hard limit. The Worker OOM-crashes, killing the training job mid-execution with no recovery path. Bootstrap mode — the path from annotation flywheel to autopilot — depends entirely on this pipeline succeeding.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Use the R2 object's .body (ReadableStream) instead of .text(). Stream the training data directly to Together AI's upload endpoint using a pass-through TransformStream: await fetch('https://api.together.ai/v1/files', { method: 'POST', body: trainingObj.body }). If Together AI requires a known Content-Length, use the R2 object's .size property from the metadata response. For multi-part uploads (files &gt; 100 MB), use a CF Workflow with step.do() per chunk so memory is freed between steps. Never buffer more than 5 MB in a single Worker at a time.</p>
        </div>
        <pre style="background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--brd);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;margin-top:12px;"><code style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--cy);line-height:1.6;">// WRONG — loads entire file into memory
const trainingData = await r2Object.text(); // OOM for large files

// CORRECT — stream directly to Together AI
const r2Object = await env.R2.get(`spaces/${spaceId}/finetune/${jobId}/train.jsonl`);
if (!r2Object) throw new Error(&#x27;Training data not found&#x27;);

const uploadResp = await fetch(&#x27;https://api.together.ai/v1/files&#x27;, {
  method: &#x27;POST&#x27;,
  headers: {
    &#x27;Authorization&#x27;: `Bearer ${togetherApiKey}`,
    &#x27;Content-Type&#x27;: &#x27;application/jsonl&#x27;,
    &#x27;Content-Length&#x27;: String(r2Object.size),
  },
  body: r2Object.body, // ReadableStream, never buffered
});</code></pre>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Quality &amp; CI</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N39" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-N39 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-eval — Returns 'started' via waitUntil(); CI Gate Has No Way to Read the Score</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The eval endpoint uses ctx.executionCtx.waitUntil(runEvaluationSuite(...)) and immediately returns { status: 'started', datasetPath }. The CI integration blocks deployment on eval score thresholds, but waitUntil() runs the eval suite after the response is sent — the CI pipeline receives 'started' with no way to retrieve the final score. There is no polling endpoint, no webhook callback, no KV or DO slot where CI can find the completed result. The eval suite runs (or times out at 30s) with results going nowhere. CI either deploys without a score (defeating the quality gate) or hangs indefinitely. The safety net preventing degraded LoRA checkpoints from reaching production is non-functional.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Implement an async eval job model: (1) On POST /eval/run, write a job record to KV with status 'running': eval-job:{jobId} = { status, startedAt }. Return { jobId } immediately. (2) runEvaluationSuite() writes results back to KV on completion: eval-job:{jobId} = { status: 'complete', scores, passedGate, modelId }. (3) Add GET /eval/jobs/:jobId returning the KV record. (4) CI polls GET /eval/jobs/:jobId every 10s until status === 'complete', then reads scores and applies the threshold gate (fail the deploy if passedGate === false). Add a 30-minute CI timeout with a clear error message.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Memory OS</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N40" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-N40 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Memory OS decay_rate — Field Defined in Schema, Never Read or Applied; Relevance Scores Never Decay</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The memory layer schema defines decay_rate REAL NOT NULL DEFAULT 0.05 and last_used INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT (unixepoch('now') * 1000) on the structured facts table. The Memory OS spec describes time-based relevance decay as a core mechanism for preventing stale facts from dominating retrieval. However there is no query, alarm, or scheduled job anywhere in the spec that reads decay_rate or last_used to compute a decayed relevance score. Retrieval queries select facts by relevance_score directly without applying decay. Over time old facts from early sessions accumulate the same relevance score as recent ones, causing the assistant to surface stale information with equal priority to current context. The columns consume storage but provide no value.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Apply decay at query time by computing an effective score in the SELECT: ORDER BY (relevance_score * exp(-decay_rate * (unixepoch('now') * 1000 - last_used) / 86400000.0)) DESC. This requires no scheduled job and is always current. Additionally register a daily DO Alarm that prunes facts below a minimum effective score threshold (e.g. 0.01): DELETE FROM facts WHERE relevance_score * exp(-decay_rate * (unixepoch('now')*1000 - last_used) / 86400000.0) &lt; 0.01. Update last_used = unixepoch('now') * 1000 whenever a fact is retrieved and used in a response.</p>
        </div>
        <pre style="background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--brd);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;margin-top:12px;"><code style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--cy);line-height:1.6;">// Memory retrieval with time-decay applied
const facts = db.exec(`
  SELECT content, source,
         relevance_score * exp(
           -decay_rate * (unixepoch(&#x27;now&#x27;) * 1000 - last_used) / 86400000.0
         ) AS effective_score
  FROM facts
  WHERE space_id = ?
  ORDER BY effective_score DESC
  LIMIT 20
`, [spaceId]);</code></pre>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Security &amp; Multi-Tenant Isolation</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N41" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--rd);background:rgba(239,68,68,.12);">G-N41 · CRITICAL</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">AssistantDO Session Lookup — Channel Type Only as Key; All Slack Users Share One Session</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>Session resolution queries SELECT id FROM sessions WHERE channel = ? with the channel type (e.g. 'slack') as the only discriminator. Every user who messages the Space Assistant via Slack — regardless of which Slack channel or which user DM — is routed into the same single session. User A's conversation history, shared files, and stated credentials are visible in User B's assistant context. The session has no user_id, external_channel_id, or thread_id component. In a multi-user space (a company with multiple Slack users) this constitutes unauthorized cross-user data access. Secrets shared in one Slack DM contaminate the context of all other Slack interactions in the same space.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Change the session key to include platform-specific channel and user identifiers: WHERE channel = ? AND external_channel_id = ? AND external_user_id = ?. Add external_channel_id VARCHAR and external_user_id VARCHAR columns to the sessions table with NOT NULL constraints. Create a composite unique index: CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_session_channel ON sessions (space_id, channel, external_channel_id, external_user_id). Extract these from incoming webhook payloads — Slack provides both channel and user fields in every event object. Existing sessions without these fields must be migrated (backfilled with a sentinel value and invalidated).</p>
        </div>
        <pre style="background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--brd);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;margin-top:12px;"><code style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--cy);line-height:1.6;">// WRONG — all Slack users share one session per space
SELECT id FROM sessions WHERE channel = &#x27;slack&#x27; AND space_id = ?

// CORRECT — one session per user per channel thread
SELECT id FROM sessions
WHERE channel = ?
  AND external_channel_id = ?   -- e.g. Slack channel C123
  AND external_user_id = ?      -- e.g. Slack user U456
  AND space_id = ?</code></pre>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Performance</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N42" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-N42 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-portal — Portal Code Fetched From R2 on Every Request; No Caching; High Latency and Cost</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The portal loader executes on every incoming HTTP request: env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.get(`spaces/${spaceId}/portal/index.ts`) followed by portalObj.text(), then passes the raw source code to the Dynamic Worker Loader DO for evaluation. Every page view triggers an R2 GET operation (latency 50–100ms) plus a full code evaluation cycle in the DO. At 1,000 page views per day per space this is 1,000 R2 GETs per day per space purely for code loading. R2 charges $0.36 per million CLASS B operations (GET). There is no ETag, conditional GET, Cache API usage, or in-memory cache. Under moderate traffic, this hits R2 operation rate limits and makes every portal page response 50–100ms slower than it needs to be.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Cache the compiled portal artifact in the CF Cache API. On first request: fetch from R2, compile/validate, cache using cache.put() with a cache key of `https://naelao-portal-cache/${spaceId}/${portalEtag}` and a Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600 header. On subsequent requests: check cache.match() first; serve from cache if hit. On portal code update (when the builder saves changes): call cache.delete() for all cached versions of that spaceId. Store the compiled bundle (not raw TypeScript) so evaluation cost is paid once at upload time, not once per request.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">LoRA &amp; Bootstrap Mode</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N43" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-N43 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Bootstrap Annotation LLM Judge — No Model, No Prompt, No Rubric; Quality Gate Is Unmeasurable</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>Bootstrap mode defines a quality gate of 'average quality score &gt;= 7.5/10' and 'LLM judge scores stored per annotation'. No model is specified for the judge (Claude? Together AI? GPT-4?), no system prompt or rubric is defined for what constitutes a 1 vs 7 vs 10 quality score, and no prompt template is shown. Without a fixed rubric, different model versions score the same annotation differently, making the 7.5 threshold meaningless as models change over time. Historical score comparisons are invalid across judge model upgrades. The primary safety mechanism preventing low-quality data from entering LoRA training is undefined — anyone implementing it will implement it differently.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Specify the judge completely in code: (1) model: pin a specific model + version (e.g. claude-haiku-4-5-20251001 for cost efficiency at annotation scale). (2) system_prompt: include explicit rubric with score bands — 1–3: factually wrong or harmful; 4–6: partially correct; 7–8: correct and helpful; 9–10: ideal, cite-worthy. (3) Store judge_model_version alongside every score in the annotations table so historical queries can filter by judge version. (4) Add 5% human spot-check sampling: randomly flag 1 in 20 annotations for human review in a naelao-admin queue to calibrate automated scores against human judgment.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Agent Runtime &amp; Context</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N44" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--rd);background:rgba(239,68,68,.12);">G-N44 · CRITICAL</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">StreamingXMLParser truncateMiddle() — Inserts Messages in Reverse Order; Context Window Corruption</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The context truncation function uses keep.splice(1, 0, messages[i]) inside a loop iterating i from messages.length-1 down to 1 (reverse order). splice(1, 0, item) always inserts at index 1. On iteration 1: [first, last]. On iteration 2: [first, second-to-last, last]. On iteration 3: [first, third-to-last, second-to-last, last]. The result is a conversation history where recent messages appear in reverse chronological order between the first and last message. The LLM receives scrambled conversation history, misattributes statements to the wrong turn, and produces incoherent responses. No error is thrown — the request is silently sent to the LLM with corrupted context. This triggers on every agent session that exceeds the context window limit, which is the common case for any long-running agent.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Replace the splice-based loop with a single array operation: const keep = [messages[0], ...messages.slice(-(targetCount - 1))]. This correctly keeps the first message (system prompt or initial context) and the most recent (targetCount - 1) messages in original chronological order. Add a unit test: assert that truncateMiddle([m0,m1,m2,m3,m4], 3) returns [m0, m3, m4] — not [m0, m4, m3] or any other order.</p>
        </div>
        <pre style="background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--brd);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;margin-top:12px;"><code style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--cy);line-height:1.6;">// WRONG — splice at index 1 in reverse loop produces reversed messages
for (let i = messages.length - 1; i &gt;= 1; i--) {
  keep.splice(1, 0, messages[i]); // always inserts at [1], reversing order
  if (keep.length &gt;= targetCount) break;
}

// CORRECT — keep first message + most recent (targetCount-1) messages
function truncateMiddle(messages: Message[], targetCount: number): Message[] {
  if (messages.length &lt;= targetCount) return messages;
  return [messages[0], ...messages.slice(-(targetCount - 1))];
}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Scalability</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N45" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-N45 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">SpaceTypeRegistryDO — Global Singleton Write Bottleneck; Memory Ceiling at Large Catalog Size</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>SpaceTypeRegistryDO is instantiated with idFromName('global-space-type-registry'), making it a single DO instance globally. All space type registrations, lookups, canary decisions, and version checks route to this one SQLite-backed DO. CF DOs serialize write transactions — one at a time. During a product launch or viral event with 1,000 concurrent space creations all checking the latest space type version, all 1,000 requests queue behind each other in this single DO. A large space type catalog with thousands of entries and full schema definitions loaded into the 128 MB DO memory ceiling causes OOM crashes that take down space type resolution globally for all users until the DO restarts.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Shard the registry by category using idFromName(`space-type-registry:${category}`). Use a two-tier design: a lightweight index DO stores only type IDs, names, and category tags; individual SpaceTypeDO instances (one per space type) store the full schema, system prompt addenda, and versioning data. The index DO is queried for discovery (lightweight); individual SpaceTypeDO instances are fetched only for installation. Front the index DO with a KV cache (TTL 60s) for read-heavy paths (version checks on every page load). SpaceTypeRegistryDO becomes a write-path-only component in steady state.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-N49" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-N49 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">AppRegistryDO — Global Singleton With No Pagination; Full Table Scan OOMs at Scale</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>AppRegistryDO is instantiated with idFromName('global-app-registry'), making it a single DO instance globally. App listing queries retrieve all published apps for the marketplace. As the catalog grows to thousands of entries, SELECT * FROM apps WHERE status='published' performs a full table scan, loads all results into the DO's 128 MB memory, and serializes the entire result set in the response. At 10,000 apps with full metadata (descriptions, schemas, permissions, icon URLs) this easily exceeds the 128 MB memory ceiling. The DO OOM-crashes, taking down app discovery for all users globally with no graceful degradation. There is no LIMIT/OFFSET or cursor-based pagination visible in the app listing endpoints.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Add mandatory pagination to all app listing queries: SELECT id, name, category, rating FROM apps WHERE status='published' AND id &gt; ? ORDER BY id LIMIT 50 — cursor-based pagination on an indexed id column. AppRegistryDO stores only index data (id, name, category, rating, install_count). Individual AppDO instances (one per app) store full metadata fetched on demand. Shard the registry by category using idFromName(`app-registry:${category}`) to distribute write load and reduce per-shard memory usage. Cache category indexes in KV (TTL 60s) for the marketplace browse path.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Agent Runtime &amp; Error Handling</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N46" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-N46 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">runFiber() Launched With void — Unhandled Exceptions Leave Fiber State 'running' Forever</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The agent loop is launched as: void this.runFiber('agent-run-'+fiberId, async (ctx) =&gt; { await this.executeAgentLoop(...) }). The void operator discards the returned Promise. If executeAgentLoop() throws an unhandled exception (network timeout, LLM API error, OOM, uncaught tool failure) and it escapes runFiber()'s catch handler, the fiber row in SQLite remains status='running' indefinitely. There is no outer .catch() to detect this. Dead fibers in 'running' state prevent session garbage collection, appear as hanging agent sessions in operator dashboards, and may block new runs on the same session if the spec includes a guard against concurrent fibers.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Remove void and handle the promise explicitly: this.runFiber(...).catch(async (err) =&gt; { await this.ctx.storage.sql.exec( "UPDATE fibers SET status='failed', error=?, ended_at=datetime('now') WHERE id=?", [err.message, fiberId]); this.broadcastError(sessionId, err); }). Ensure runFiber() itself wraps the callback in try/catch and always transitions fiber state to either 'completed' or 'failed'. Register a DO Alarm as watchdog: if any fiber remains 'running' for more than 10 minutes, transition it to 'failed' and push a timeout event to the client WebSocket.</p>
        </div>
        <pre style="background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--brd);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;overflow-x:auto;margin-top:12px;"><code style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--cy);line-height:1.6;">// WRONG — void discards errors, fiber stays &#x27;running&#x27; on exception
void this.runFiber(`agent-run-${fiberId}`, async (ctx) =&gt; {
  await this.executeAgentLoop(sessionId, userMsgId, ctx);
});

// CORRECT — always settle fiber state
this.runFiber(`agent-run-${fiberId}`, async (ctx) =&gt; {
  await this.executeAgentLoop(sessionId, userMsgId, ctx);
}).catch(async (err: Error) =&gt; {
  await this.db.exec(
    `UPDATE fibers SET status=&#x27;failed&#x27;, error=?, ended_at=datetime(&#x27;now&#x27;) WHERE id=?`,
    [err.message, fiberId]
  );
  this.broadcast({ type: &#x27;fiber.failed&#x27;, fiberId, error: err.message });
});</code></pre>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Billing &amp; Payments</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N47" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-N47 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">WalletDO x402 Cross-Space Payment — Nested DO Calls Risk Deadlock; Funds Lost on Timeout</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The x402 payment flow orchestrates both the payer's debit and the payee's credit through WalletDO instances. If the orchestration calls the payer's WalletDO which then internally calls the payee's WalletDO within the same request chain, and CF routes both to the same DO instance (naming collision, or if a space pays itself), the nested DO fetch deadlocks: the outer request holds the DO's single-thread lock while waiting for the inner request, which cannot execute because the lock is held. CF DOs do not support reentrant locking. After 30 seconds the outer DO times out. The debit may have already been committed to SQLite, resulting in funds permanently lost from the payer with no credit to the payee.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Never nest WalletDO calls. Use a PaymentCoordinatorDO as the orchestrator: it receives the payment request, calls payer WalletDO.debit() in one independent request, then calls payee WalletDO.credit() in a second independent request. Use a two-phase commit log in PaymentCoordinatorDO: write 'debit_pending', execute debit, write 'debit_done', execute credit, write 'complete'. A recovery alarm handles any state stuck at 'debit_done' by retrying the credit. Add a guard rejecting self-payments before initiating the flow: if payerSpaceId === payeeSpaceId return 400 error.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:64px;margin-bottom:8px;">Operational</h2>
      <div class="bar"></div>

      <div id="G-N50" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-N50 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Space Provisioner — Returns Space ID Immediately; Race With Premature API Calls Corrupts DO State</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am">
          <div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
          <p>The space creation endpoint calls the provisioner via ctx.waitUntil() and returns the new space ID immediately. The client immediately begins making requests to the new space — adding members, installing apps, configuring settings — before the provisioner has completed creating SpaceDO, AgentDO, AssistantDO, and WalletDO instances and their SQLite schemas. These requests arrive at DOs that do not yet exist or whose schemas are incomplete. CF creates a new empty DO instance on first access, but when the provisioner subsequently initializes the same DO it finds conflicting state. The resulting corruption manifests as missing tables, duplicate rows, and wrong schema versions — difficult to diagnose and often permanent.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;">
          <div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
          <p>Add status: 'provisioning' | 'ready' | 'error' to the space record in the central registry (SpaceTypeRegistryDO or a dedicated SpaceIndexDO). All API endpoints targeting a space must check status === 'ready' before proceeding, returning 503 Service Unavailable with Retry-After: 5 while provisioning is in progress. The provisioner transitions status to 'ready' only after all DO initializations complete successfully in sequence: SpaceDO → AgentDO → AssistantDO → WalletDO. Register a provisioning watchdog DO Alarm: if status remains 'provisioning' for more than 60 seconds, transition to 'error' and send an operator alert.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

