    <!-- =====================================================================
         APPENDIX D — THIRD-PASS AUDIT GAP RESOLUTIONS (D01–D22)
         22 gaps found in targeted audit of workers, credentials, spaces, and shell
         ===================================================================== -->
    <div id="gap-resolutions-d" style="margin-top:80px;padding-top:40px;border-top:2px solid var(--tl);">
      <h1 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:52px;font-weight:700;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.03em;line-height:1.05;margin-bottom:16px;">
        Appendix D — Third-Pass Audit Gap Resolutions
      </h1>
      <p class="lead">22 additional gaps found by auditing naelao-threads, naelao-knowledge, naelao-credentials,
        naelao-spaces, naelao-admin, naelao-together-ai, naelao-sweeper, external portals, pricing enforcement,
        and the Operator Shell. Labels G-D01 through G-D22. Running total (including Appendix E): 223 gaps across all five appendices.</p>
      <div class="box box-tl" style="margin:32px 0 48px;">
        <div class="box-lbl">Pattern Observed</div>
        <p>This pass reveals a consistent pattern: <strong>delete operations are incomplete</strong> (no Vectorize
          cleanup on doc delete, no R2 cleanup on space delete, no token purge on credential refresh),
          <strong>list operations are unordered or unpaginated</strong> (no ORDER BY on messages, no pagination on
          sweeper fan-out), and <strong>enforcement exists only in prose</strong> (feature gates, depth limits,
          uniqueness constraints). Each category represents a class of defect to audit for in any remaining sections.</p>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D01" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D01 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-threads — No ORDER BY on Message Listing; Messages Render Out-of-Sequence After Any Mutation</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>SQLite does not guarantee row return order without an explicit ORDER BY clause. The message listing query uses SELECT * FROM messages WHERE thread_id = ? without ORDER BY created_at ASC. After any DELETE, UPDATE, or WAL compaction the physical row order diverges from insertion order. An edited or deleted-and-reinserted message appears out of sequence. The agent also receives tool results out of order during context reconstruction, misinterpreting conversation flow and producing incorrect responses.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>Add ORDER BY created_at ASC, id ASC to every message listing query, using id as a tiebreaker for same-millisecond inserts. Add a composite index: CREATE INDEX idx_messages_thread_order ON messages(thread_id, created_at ASC, id ASC) to avoid a full table scan on large threads. Add a regression test that inserts messages with explicit timestamps out of insertion order and asserts they are returned in timestamp order.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D02" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D02 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-threads — No Maximum Message Content Size; 10 MB Message Crashes Agent DO With OOM</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>The threads worker accepts message content without a size limit. An agent that includes large file contents, base64 images, or raw tool output directly in a message body can create a 5–50 MB message. When the agent DO reconstructs context it loads all messages for the thread into memory simultaneously. A thread with three 5 MB messages = 15 MB, pushing total DO memory (system prompt + tool schemas + response buffer) over the 128 MB limit and crashing the agent DO mid-run with no recovery.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>Enforce MAX_MESSAGE_CONTENT_BYTES = 131072 (128 KB) at the naelao-threads write endpoint. Content exceeding this limit must be written to R2 (key: messages/{spaceId}/{messageId}/content) and replaced in the message row with an R2 reference marker. The agent context reconstruction fetches R2 references lazily. Return HTTP 413 Content Too Large for messages that exceed the limit before attempting to store them.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D03" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D03 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-knowledge — Vectorize.deleteByIds() Never Called on Document Removal; Stale Vectors Poison Search Forever</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>When a knowledge document is deleted from SpaceDO SQLite, the worker removes the document and chunk records from the database but makes no corresponding call to vectorize.deleteByIds(chunkIds). The deleted document's vector embeddings remain permanently in the Vectorize index. Every semantic search query returns results from deleted documents. Users who delete sensitive documents (outdated policies, wrong information, confidential drafts) find their agents still citing the deleted content indefinitely.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>In the document deletion handler, after deleting from SQLite: (1) query SELECT vector_id FROM knowledge_chunks WHERE document_id = ? to collect all vector IDs, (2) call env.VECTORIZE.deleteByIds(vectorIds), (3) only commit the SQLite delete if the Vectorize delete succeeds. Wrap in a compensation pattern: if Vectorize delete fails, log to a pending_vector_deletes table and retry via sweeper cron. Add a nightly reconciliation job that compares SQLite chunk IDs with Vectorize index contents and purges orphaned vectors.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D04" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D04 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-knowledge — Embedding Model Version Not Tracked; Model Upgrade Silently Breaks All Semantic Search</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>All embeddings and queries must use the same model to produce comparable vectors — mixing models produces meaningless cosine similarity scores. The spec stores no embedding_model field in knowledge_chunks or in Vectorize vector metadata. If the platform upgrades the embedding model (for quality or cost), existing vectors are in the old model's vector space while new query embeddings use the new space. Similarity scores between old vectors and new queries are random noise. The agent receives irrelevant or inversely-ranked knowledge results with no error signal.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>Add embedding_model TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'text-embedding-ada-002' and embedding_model_version TEXT to knowledge_chunks. Store the model name in Vectorize vector metadata ({ model: 'text-embedding-ada-002' }). At query time, always embed the query with the same model version recorded in SpaceDO's knowledge_config. Provide a POST /knowledge/re-embed?model=new-model admin endpoint that re-embeds all chunks using the new model and atomically swaps the index. Never allow mixed-model vectors in the same Vectorize namespace.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D05" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--rd);background:rgba(239,68,68,.12);">G-D05 · CRITICAL</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-credentials — Master Key Rotation Instantly Renders All Stored Credentials Unreadable</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>Credentials are encrypted at rest with a platform master key. When the master key is rotated (security best practice, or forced after a breach), all previously encrypted credentials cannot be decrypted with the new key. The spec never specifies a re-encryption procedure, dual-key transition period, or key version metadata in credential records. A key rotation event instantly makes every stored credential — API keys, OAuth tokens, webhook secrets — permanently unreadable for all spaces simultaneously. All third-party integrations stop working with no warning or recovery path.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>Store credentials with key version metadata: { key_version: 2, iv: '...', ciphertext: '...' }. The credential store maintains the last two key versions simultaneously during rotation. Rotation procedure: (1) generate new key version N+1, (2) background CF Workflow re-encrypts all credentials using N+1 (paginated, 100 at a time), (3) once all re-encrypted confirm via count check, retire key N. The decryption path tries the current key version first and falls back to N-1 during the rotation window. Track rotation status in a key_rotation_jobs table in admin SQLite.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D06" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D06 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-credentials — No Access Audit Log; Credential Reads Leave No Forensic Trail</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>The credentials worker returns plaintext credentials to agents on demand. There is no audit log recording which agent accessed which credential, at what time, from which space, or for which tool call. A compromised agent that exfiltrates credentials via a malicious MCP tool call leaves no forensic trail. Anomaly detection (an agent accessing credentials it has never accessed before at 3am) is impossible. SOC 2 Type II and ISO 27001 both require audit trails for access to sensitive credentials — without this, the platform cannot pass either certification.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>Every credential read must write an immutable audit entry to CF Analytics Engine (append-only, cannot be modified): event_name=credential_access, blob2=credential_id, blob3=space_id, blob4=agent_id, blob5=tool_name, double1=access_timestamp_ms. Surface the audit log in naelao-admin with filtering by credential_id and time range. For the highest-sensitivity credentials (payment API keys, OAuth tokens with write access), add a real-time alert if the credential is accessed outside its declared usage pattern.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D07" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D07 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-spaces — Space Deletion Leaves R2 Files, Vectorize Vectors, and KV Entries as Permanent Orphans</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>The space deletion endpoint deletes the SpaceDO record but does not clean up: (1) R2 files under spaces/{spaceId}/ — R2 has no recursive delete, each file must be listed and deleted individually; (2) Vectorize vectors in the space's namespace; (3) KV entries prefixed with space:{spaceId}:. Without cleanup, storage costs accumulate for deleted spaces. At scale, deleted-space orphan data could account for 30–50% of platform storage costs. This is also a GDPR Article 17 violation — right to erasure requires deletion of all personal data including R2 files and Vectorize vectors.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>Implement an async deletion pipeline via CF Workflow: (1) Set space status to 'deleting'. (2) Workflow step: list and delete all R2 objects under spaces/{spaceId}/ using R2 cursor pagination (1000 objects per page). (3) Workflow step: call env.VECTORIZE.deleteByMetadata({ space_id: spaceId }) to purge all space vectors. (4) Workflow step: scan and delete KV keys by prefix. (5) Workflow step: mark SpaceDO as deleted and clear its SQLite. (6) Set status to 'deleted'. Each step is independently retriable if it fails.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D08" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D08 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-spaces — Member Role Downgrade Does Not Invalidate Active Sessions; Terminated Admins Retain Privileges</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>When a space owner downgrades a member from 'admin' to 'viewer' or removes them entirely, the naelao-spaces worker updates the member record in SpaceDO SQLite. However the downgraded member's active WebSocket connections and cached session tokens are not invalidated. The session token still carries the old role claim. For the duration of their session — potentially hours if tokens have long TTLs — the downgraded member retains elevated privileges. A terminated admin has a window to perform destructive admin actions before their session expires.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>Role change must immediately invalidate sessions: (1) Update the member record in SpaceDO SQLite. (2) Broadcast a role.changed WebSocket event to all active connections for that user in that space — the client re-authenticates on receipt. (3) Write a revoked_sessions entry in KV: session:{userId}:{spaceId}:revoked_at = {ISO8601} with TTL equal to max token lifetime. All auth middleware must check if the session was created before the user's last role change for that space. Close all WebSocket connections for the affected user from the DO side immediately on role change.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D09" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D09 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">External Portal Slugs — Per-Owner Uniqueness Allows Brand Squatting; 'apple.naelao.com' Registerable by Anyone</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>Space slugs used for {slug}.naelao.com portals are unique per space owner. Two different builders can register the same slug acme, creating routing ambiguity for acme.naelao.com. Additionally builders can register slugs like apple, google, anthropic, or naelao itself — creating brand impersonation at {brand}.naelao.com. There is no reserved slug list and no global uniqueness enforcement. The CF Worker routing {slug}.naelao.com requests cannot resolve which space to serve when duplicate slugs exist.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>Slugs must be globally unique across the entire platform. Add a global portal_slugs table in a dedicated SlugRegistryDO or platform D1: slug TEXT PRIMARY KEY, space_id TEXT NOT NULL, registered_at TEXT. On registration, check global uniqueness before permitting. Maintain and enforce a reserved slug list covering: Fortune 500 company names, known brand names, Naelao's own product names, and common infrastructure words (www, api, admin, mail, app, blog, shop, store, auth, login). The reserved list must be updateable by the platform team without a code deploy.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D10" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D10 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-admin — No Separate Auth Path; Admin Endpoints Share User Auth System; Single Vuln Grants Platform Admin</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>naelao-admin handles platform-level operations: audit log access, space deletion, builder suspension, DLQ management. The spec does not specify a separate authentication mechanism for admin endpoints. If admin endpoints use the same API key/JWT system as user endpoints, a JWT algorithm confusion attack or auth bypass in user auth code also grants admin access. There is no separation of duties and no privileged access workstation requirement. The same Worker process that handles public user requests also handles admin requests.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>Admin endpoints must be protected by CF Access (Zero Trust) with a separate policy requiring a Naelao team member identity (Google Workspace SSO + hardware security key MFA). The naelao-admin Worker must never be reachable from the public internet without CF Access in front of it. In code, verify both the CF-Access-JWT-Assertion header (signed by CF's own CA) AND a secondary platform admin API token stored as a separate CF Worker Secret. These are two independent auth factors at the API level.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D11" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D11 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-admin — DLQ Consumer Logic Never Specified; Critical Failures Silently Discarded After 4 Days</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>The naelao-admin worker has a CF Queue dead-letter queue (DLQ) binding. CF retains dead-letter messages for 4 days then discards them permanently. The DLQ consumer handler — the code that processes dead-letter messages — is never specified in the admin worker. Permanent failures (failed space provisioning, failed billing events, failed webhook delivery after all retries) accumulate in the DLQ for 4 days then vanish silently. Failed billing events represent direct revenue loss. Failed provisioning creates zombie spaces. No operator is alerted.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>Specify the DLQ consumer in naelao-admin: for each dead-letter message: (1) parse message_type from the payload, (2) write a permanent failure record to admin SQLite: platform_failures (id, message_type, payload_json, failure_reason, failed_at, retry_count), (3) send an admin alert via Resend with failure details, (4) for billing events specifically, create a manual review task in the admin dashboard. Unknown message types must be logged with full payload for investigation. The consumer must be idempotent — re-processing a dead-letter message that was already logged must be a no-op.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D12" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D12 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Pricing Tier Feature Gates — Enforced Only in UI/Docs, Not in Worker Code; Free Users Access All Paid Features via API</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>The pricing spec describes tier-gated features: free tier is limited to 3 spaces, 1,000 credits/month, no MiniAGI, no fine-tuning, no custom domains. There is no middleware or BuilderAccountDO method shown that enforces these limits in actual worker code. A free user making API calls directly bypasses any frontend enforcement and can create unlimited spaces, run MiniAGI, trigger fine-tuning jobs, and configure custom domains. All plan limits exist in UI copy and documentation only — not in the backend API layer.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>BuilderAccountDO must expose a checkFeatureAccess(feature: string): { allowed: boolean, reason?: string, upgrade_url?: string } method. Every worker endpoint that exposes a gated feature must call this before proceeding: const check = await builderDO.checkFeatureAccess('mini_agi'); if (!check.allowed) return new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: 'PLAN_LIMIT', ...check }), { status: 403 }). Define a PLAN_FEATURES constant mapping plan names to allowed features and limits. All enforcement is in the worker backend. Add an integration test that calls every gated endpoint with a free-tier auth token and asserts 403.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D13" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D13 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Credit Exhaustion Mid-Run — No Specified Behavior; Agent Either Runs Free or Crashes Without Warning</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>If a user's credit balance reaches zero during a long-running agent task, WalletDO.debit() returns an INSUFFICIENT_CREDITS error. The spec never specifies what the agent loop does with this error. The likely outcomes are: (a) the error is ignored and the agent continues running without paying (free compute), or (b) the error propagates as an unhandled exception that crashes the DO mid-task losing all partial work without notifying the user. Neither outcome is acceptable. There is no specified grace credit mechanism, graceful stop behavior, or checkpoint-and-resume on credit exhaustion.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>Define explicit credit exhaustion behavior: when WalletDO.debit() returns INSUFFICIENT_CREDITS: (1) catch this error specifically in the agent loop (not as a generic error), (2) complete the current tool call if already in flight, (3) send a credits.exhausted WebSocket event to the client immediately, (4) write the current agent state checkpoint to R2 at runs/{spaceId}/{runId}/checkpoint.json, (5) stop execution cleanly and mark the run as status: 'paused_insufficient_credits'. The user tops up credits and calls POST /runs/{runId}/resume to continue from the checkpoint.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D14" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);">G-D14 · MEDIUM</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">External Portal Slug Routing — No Cache Layer; Registry DO Queried on Every Page View</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-bl"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>When {slug}.naelao.com receives a request, the routing Worker must resolve which space to serve. If this resolution queries a registry DO or KV without caching, every page view makes a DO or KV round-trip (20–50ms) before serving any content. For a popular portal receiving 10,000 requests per minute, this is 10,000 registry lookups per minute — adding 50ms to every page load and potentially exhausting DO request rate limits. No caching layer is shown in the portal routing path in the spec.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>Cache the slug → spaceId mapping in CF Cache API with a 60-second TTL: const cached = await cache.match(`https://naelao-slug-cache/${slug}`). On cache miss, resolve from the registry and store with Cache-Control: public, max-age=60. On slug update or deletion, call cache.delete() for all cached versions of that slug. For even faster resolution, maintain a KV entry portal-slug:{slug} = spaceId with a 5-minute TTL — KV reads are ~1ms vs 20–50ms for DO reads.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D15" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D15 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-together-ai Streaming — Token Count Lost on Client Disconnect; Tokens Consumed but Not Billed</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>The streaming chat proxy streams tokens from Together AI to the client. Token counting for billing happens from the usage field in the final SSE chunk. If the client disconnects mid-stream (browser close, network drop, CF client-side timeout), the final usage SSE chunk is never received, the token count is never written to billing, and tokens consumed from Together AI are not debited from the user's credit balance. A user who intentionally disconnects before stream completion gets free LLM inference. This compounds into significant unpaid usage for users running many long requests.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>Before streaming begins, pre-deduct an estimated credit amount based on the prompt token count (calculable before inference starts from the request payload). After streaming completes or the client disconnects, use ctx.waitUntil() to reconcile actual vs estimated usage via Together AI's completions API. Credit any over-deducted estimate back to WalletDO atomically. This is the same credit reservation pattern recommended in G-N08: reserve(estimate) at start, commit(actual) at end. The reconciliation must run even if the client WebSocket is closed.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D16" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D16 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-sweeper — No Pagination on Space Fan-Out; Hits CF 1000 Subrequest Limit at 1001 Active Spaces</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>The sweeper's fan-out logic retrieves all active spaces and makes a DO stub call or HTTP request for each. CF Workers have a hard 1,000 subrequest limit per invocation. When the platform has more than 1,000 active spaces, the sweeper processes the first 1,000 spaces and silently stops — the remaining spaces never get heartbeat checks, WAL cleanup, or scheduled job dispatch. With 10,000 spaces, 90% of the platform is unmonitored per sweeper cycle. There is no error thrown — the subrequest limit is hit silently.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>Process spaces in batches of 100 per sweeper invocation using cursor pagination. Store last_processed_space_id in KV. Each cron invocation: fetch the next 100 spaces after the cursor (SELECT id FROM spaces WHERE id &gt; ? AND status='active' ORDER BY id LIMIT 100), process them, update the cursor. With a 5-minute cron interval and 100 spaces per run, a full sweep of 10,000 spaces takes ~8 hours — acceptable for maintenance operations. Time-critical operations (subscription expiry, alarm watchdogs) must use per-space DO alarms, not sweeper fan-out.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D17" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--rd);background:rgba(239,68,68,.12);">G-D17 · CRITICAL</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Operator Shell Slot System — Third-Party App Content Injected Into DOM Without Sandboxing; Stored XSS</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>The Operator Shell Slot System allows third-party Naelao Apps to inject UI components into shell slots (sidebar, header, tool output). If slot content is rendered using innerHTML or equivalent without sanitization, any third-party app can inject arbitrary JavaScript executing in the context of the Naelao shell — stealing session tokens, reading conversation data, exfiltrating credentials, making API calls as the authenticated user. The spec does not mention iframe sandboxing or HTML sanitization for slot content. Every installed app is a potential stored XSS vector.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>All third-party slot content must be rendered in sandboxed iframes: &lt;iframe srcdoc='{content}' sandbox='allow-scripts' style='...'&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;. The sandbox='allow-scripts' attribute (without allow-same-origin) prevents the iframe from accessing the parent page's DOM, cookies, or localStorage. Communication between shell and slot iframe must go only through postMessage with strict origin validation (parent validates origin === app's registered origin). Never use innerHTML to render any third-party content. Apply a Content-Security-Policy to the shell page that blocks inline scripts.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D18" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D18 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">Testing Spec '700+ Tests' — Category Descriptions, Not Test Cases; Zero Concurrency or Hibernation Tests Specified</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>The testing specification describes test suites organized by component category. The 700+ tests claim is aspirational, not a concrete enumeration. Reading the section reveals: (1) tests are described at category level without specifying what each test asserts or what inputs/outputs are expected; (2) no concurrency tests exist — no test submits simultaneous requests and asserts correct serialization; (3) no hibernation recovery tests — no test hibernates a DO and verifies state restoration; (4) no chaos tests for network failure injection; (5) the workerd configuration for Vectorize, Analytics Engine, and AI Gateway mock bindings is never specified. The 700+ number is a target, not a deliverable.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>For each gap found across all four appendices, add a specific named regression test with exact assertions. Required test categories not yet specified: (1) Concurrency tests — 50 simultaneous requests to shared DOs, assert no data corruption; (2) Hibernation tests — force DO hibernation via test API, assert WebSocket reconnect restores session; (3) Chaos tests — inject network failures between workers, assert correct error propagation; (4) Boundary tests — exact limit values (1000 steps, 128KB message, 20 Vectorize results). Specify the miniflare.config.ts with mock bindings for all CF primitives used. Count actual it() blocks not estimated categories.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D19" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);">G-D19 · MEDIUM</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-knowledge — Vectorize topK Hardcoded at 20; Large Knowledge Bases Return Incomplete Results</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-bl"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>Vectorize returns at most 20 results per query by default. For a space with 5,000+ indexed chunks covering many distinct topics, the most semantically relevant chunk for a niche query may rank 35th in cosine similarity — below the 20-result cutoff — even though the agent has the answer indexed. The agent responds as if it has no knowledge on that topic, appearing to have amnesia. The limit is hardcoded and not surfaced to builders. There is no way to know why the agent 'doesn't know' something without directly querying Vectorize.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>Increase the default Vectorize topK to 50 (Vectorize supports up to 100 per query). Allow builders to configure knowledge.retrieval_limit (10–100) in space settings, stored in SpaceDO. Implement re-ranking: after retrieving 50 candidate chunks, apply a cross-encoder re-ranker (Together AI embedding model or a local small model) to select the top 5–10 most relevant chunks for context injection. Add a knowledge retrieval debug field to agent run logs: retrieved_chunks: [{id, score, document_title}] so builders can diagnose retrieval failures.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D20" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--bl);background:rgba(59,130,246,.12);">G-D20 · MEDIUM</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-credentials — Revoked OAuth Tokens Retained After Refresh; Historical Token Exposure on Breach</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-bl"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>When an OAuth token is refreshed, the new token is written to the credentials store. The spec does not specify whether the old token is deleted. If old token versions are retained for audit or rollback, the credentials store accumulates a history of previously-valid tokens — some of which may still be valid (OAuth providers don't always invalidate refreshed tokens immediately). If an attacker gains read access to the credentials store through any other vulnerability, they have access not only to current tokens but to all historical tokens, expanding the blast radius significantly.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>On OAuth token refresh: immediately overwrite the old token value with the new token in a single atomic write (UPDATE credentials SET value_encrypted=?, refreshed_at=? WHERE id=?). Do not retain token history in the credentials table. If token change history is needed for audit, log only the credential_id and refresh_timestamp to CF Analytics Engine — never the token value. Add an explicit purge of any accidentally retained credential_versions rows after each successful refresh.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D21" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D21 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">RemoteLoop — No Caller Authentication; SSRF Risk if Reachable From Public Internet</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>RemoteLoop executes platform-internal agent logic and may make sub-requests to internal workers, DOs, or external URLs on behalf of callers. The caller-to-RemoteLoop authentication is not specified. If RemoteLoop trusts callers based on network position alone and is reachable via a public route, an attacker can craft requests that make RemoteLoop fetch internal CF metadata services or internal DO stubs not intended to be externally accessible — a classic SSRF attack. There is no signature verification, no nonce, and no explicit restriction to CF Service Bindings only.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>RemoteLoop must require a signed request from callers: HMAC-SHA256 over (payload + timestamp) using REMOTE_LOOP_SECRET (CF Worker Secret, never public). Include a nonce field (UUID) and reject requests where the nonce has been seen within the last 5 minutes (stored in KV). RemoteLoop must be accessible only via CF Service Bindings (Worker-to-Worker calls using Service binding), never via a public HTTP route. Add this to architecture principles: 'RemoteLoop has no public URL. All calls go through the Service binding declared in wrangler.jsonc.'</p></div>
      </div>

      <div id="G-D22" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
        <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
          <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;color:var(--am);background:rgba(245,158,11,.12);">G-D22 · HIGH</span>
          <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;">naelao-spaces — Sub-Space Hierarchy Depth Limit Stated in Prose, Never Enforced in API; Infinite Nesting Crashes Hierarchy Queries</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="box box-am"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p>The spec describes a maximum sub-space depth (3–5 levels depending on plan). This limit appears in product documentation but is never enforced in the naelao-spaces API handler. A client making API calls directly can create arbitrarily deep nesting. Queries that traverse the hierarchy (permission checks, billing rollups, member enumeration) use recursive SQL CTEs. At 100 levels deep, a recursive CTE hits SQLite's default recursion limit (1,000 for WITH RECURSIVE), crashing the query and leaving the hierarchy in an inconsistent state. Billing rollup cron compounds exponentially on deep hierarchies.</p></div>
        <div class="box box-gr" style="margin-top:12px;"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p>Add a depth INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 column to the spaces table (populated at creation from parent's depth + 1). Before creating a sub-space: SELECT depth FROM spaces WHERE id = ? (one O(1) lookup, not recursive). If depth + 1 &gt; MAX_DEPTH_FOR_PLAN, return 400 ERR_MAX_HIERARCHY_DEPTH. MAX_DEPTH_FOR_PLAN: free=2, pro=3, enterprise=5. For all existing recursive CTE queries, add LIMIT clause and recursion depth cap: WITH RECURSIVE ... WHERE depth &lt; 10 LIMIT 10000 to prevent runaway recursion on malformed or malicious hierarchies.</p></div>
      </div>

