  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">On logout, the refresh token is deleted from KV (key refresh:&lt;token&gt;). KV is eventually consistent: a delete in one Cloudflare region may not propagate to other regions for up to 60 seconds. During this window an attacker who captured the refresh token can POST it to /v1/auth/refresh from a CF region that has not yet received the delete, obtain a new access token, and continue accessing the API. The same window applies to member revocation: the revoked:userId:spaceId KV key written on member removal may not be visible in all regions for up to 60 seconds, allowing a removed member to continue making authenticated API calls.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Use a Durable Object for refresh token storage instead of KV. DOs are strongly consistent — a delete is immediately visible to all subsequent requests to that DO instance:

// Replace KV refresh token storage with a TokenRevocationDO
// workers/naelao-auth/src/index.ts
const tokenId = env.TOKEN_REVOCATION_DO.idFromName('refresh:' + tokenHash);
const stub = env.TOKEN_REVOCATION_DO.get(tokenId);
// On logout: await stub.fetch('/revoke', { method: 'POST' })
// On refresh: const valid = await stub.fetch('/check') — returns 200 if token exists, 410 if revoked

For member revocation, write revoked:userId:spaceId into SpaceDO SQLite (strongly consistent) and check it there rather than in KV. The naelao-router revocation check must call SpaceDO, not read from KV.</p></div>
