  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">WalletDO expires_at and daily_reset_at Stored as TEXT — Inconsistent Format Risks Silent Incorrect Comparisons</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">WalletDO stores reservation expires_at and daily_reset_at as TEXT. SQLite TEXT column comparisons work correctly for ISO 8601 format ("2024-01-15T10:30:00Z" &lt; "2024-01-16T10:30:00Z") but only if all values use the same format with timezone designation. If some code paths write datetime strings without the Z suffix (e.g., new Date().toISOString().slice(0,19)) and others write with Z, the comparisons fail silently: "2024-01-15T10:30:00" &gt; "2024-01-15T10:30:00Z" in lexicographic comparison, so records with the non-Z format never expire. The date('now') comparison used in daily_reset_at checks compares only the date portion, which is correct, but mixing with datetime() in the same column causes inconsistency.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Store all timestamps in WalletDO as INTEGER (Unix milliseconds) not TEXT. This is unambiguous and avoids all timezone and format inconsistency issues:

// In WalletDO schema — change TEXT to INTEGER for all timestamp columns
expires_at   INTEGER NOT NULL,  -- unix ms
daily_reset_at INTEGER NOT NULL, -- unix ms
updated_at   INTEGER NOT NULL   -- unix ms

// Update all reads/writes accordingly:
// Instead of: WHERE expires_at < datetime('now')
// Use:        WHERE expires_at < ?   [pass Date.now() as the parameter]

// Sweep expired reservations:
this.sql.exec('DELETE FROM reservations WHERE expires_at &lt; ?', Date.now());
// Daily reset check:
if (wallet.daily_reset_at &lt; startOfTodayMs()) { /* reset */ }</p></div>
