    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">GAP-320</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EAB308;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#EAB30818;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">16_naelao_registry.md</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">WorkflowRegistryDO alarm() Fan-Out Has No LIMIT — Full Cursor Spread OOMs on Large Workflow Sets, 30s Timeout Risk</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">WorkflowRegistryDO alarm() fans out due workflows with [...due] spread, which pulls the entire SQL cursor result into a JavaScript array in memory with no row limit. Cloudflare Durable Objects have a 128MB memory ceiling. If thousands of workflows are due simultaneously (e.g., after a platform outage where alarms backed up), this spread allocates a large array and can exhaust DO memory. Additionally, DO alarm() has a 30-second execution timeout. Promise.all() on thousands of queue sends can exceed this timeout. A mid-execution timeout means some workflows are dispatched and some are not, with no partial-completion tracking — the next alarm invocation re-dispatches only the still-overdue workflows (if next_run_at is updated transactionally), but if the alarm times out before the DB update, some workflows get double-dispatched.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add a LIMIT to the due workflows query and process in batches within the alarm, scheduling another immediate alarm if more remain:

// WorkflowRegistryDO – alarm()
async alarm(): Promise&lt;void&gt; {
  const BATCH = 50; // safe well within 30s and memory limits
  const due = [...this.sql.exec&lt;{id:string}&gt;(
    'SELECT id FROM workflows WHERE next_run_at &lt;= datetime("now") AND status="active" LIMIT ?', BATCH
  )];
  if (due.length === 0) { /* schedule next alarm at soonest next_run_at */ return; }
  // Update next_run_at BEFORE dispatching (prevents double-dispatch on timeout)
  this.sql.exec('BEGIN');
  for (const wf of due) {
    this.sql.exec('UPDATE workflows SET next_run_at=computeNext(cron) WHERE id=?', wf.id);
  }
  this.sql.exec('COMMIT');
  await Promise.allSettled(due.map(wf =&gt; this.env.NAELAO_WORKFLOW_DISPATCH.send({ workflowId: wf.id })));
  if (due.length === BATCH) {
    // More rows may remain — wake immediately to process next batch
    await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + 100);
  } else {
    // Schedule next alarm at next due workflow time
    const next = [...this.sql.exec&lt;{t:string}&gt;('SELECT MIN(next_run_at) AS t FROM workflows WHERE status="active"')][0]?.t;
    if (next) await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(new Date(next).getTime());
  }
}</p></div>
</div>

