    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">GAP-322</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EAB308;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#EAB30818;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">14_naelao_mcp_hub.md</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">MCP Hub invalidateToolCache Lists Only First 1000 Keys and Issues Unbounded Parallel Deletes — Incomplete Invalidation and KV Rate Limit Risk</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">invalidateToolCache() in naelao-mcp-hub calls kv.list({ prefix: 'tool-schemas:' + spaceId + ':' }) once with no cursor loop, getting at most 1000 keys. For spaces with many agents the remainder of the cache is never invalidated, leaving stale tool schemas in KV. Additionally, Promise.all(listed.keys.map(k =&gt; kv.delete(k.name))) issues all deletes simultaneously. Cloudflare KV has internal rate limiting: while there is no publicly documented per-worker hard limit, issuing 1000 simultaneous delete requests risks throttling responses. Failed deletes from rate limiting are not retried, silently leaving stale cache entries.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add cursor pagination and batch the deletes:

// workers/naelao-mcp-hub/src/index.ts
async function invalidateToolCache(spaceId: string, kv: KVNamespace): Promise&lt;void&gt; {
  let cursor: string | undefined;
  do {
    const listed = await kv.list({ prefix: 'tool-schemas:' + spaceId + ':', limit: 1000, cursor });
    // Delete in batches of 25 to avoid KV rate limits
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; listed.keys.length; i += 25) {
      await Promise.all(listed.keys.slice(i, i + 25).map(k =&gt; kv.delete(k.name)));
    }
    cursor = listed.list_complete ? undefined : listed.cursor;
  } while (cursor);
}</p></div>
</div>

<div id="GAP-323" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">GAP-323</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EAB308;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#EAB30818;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">05_naelao_auth.md</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">PBKDF2 Password Hash Storage Format Unspecified — Ambiguous Encoding Risks Verify-Always-Pass or Verify-Always-Fail</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">naelao-auth stores PBKDF2 hashed passwords in KV as user:hash:&lt;email&gt; with "16-byte salt stored as prefix". The format for reading back is never specified. The verification step must extract the 16-byte salt, re-derive the key with the same parameters, and compare. If the storage format is ambiguous (raw bytes vs hex vs base64, with or without a length prefix, with or without a separator between salt and hash) then different code generators will implement the format differently. A mismatch causes either: (1) all logins fail (salt extraction reads wrong bytes, re-derived key never matches), or (2) all logins pass (comparison logic is wrong and compares empty arrays). Raw bytes stored in KV are also susceptible to UTF-8 corruption if the KV client encodes the value as a string.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Specify the exact encoding in 05_naelao_auth.md:

// workers/naelao-auth/src/jwt.ts – hashPassword()
async function hashPassword(password: string): Promise&lt;string&gt; {
  const salt = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(16));
  const keyMaterial = await crypto.subtle.importKey('raw', new TextEncoder().encode(password), 'PBKDF2', false, ['deriveBits']);
  const derived = await crypto.subtle.deriveBits({ name: 'PBKDF2', salt, iterations: 100_000, hash: 'SHA-256' }, keyMaterial, 256);
  // Format: base64(salt):base64(hash) — unambiguous, no binary in KV
  return btoa(String.fromCharCode(...salt)) + ':' + btoa(String.fromCharCode(...new Uint8Array(derived)));
}

async function verifyPassword(password: string, stored: string): Promise&lt;boolean&gt; {
  const [saltB64, hashB64] = stored.split(':');
  const salt = Uint8Array.from(atob(saltB64), c =&gt; c.charCodeAt(0));
  const expectedHash = Uint8Array.from(atob(hashB64), c =&gt; c.charCodeAt(0));
  const keyMaterial = await crypto.subtle.importKey('raw', new TextEncoder().encode(password), 'PBKDF2', false, ['deriveBits']);
  const derived = new Uint8Array(await crypto.subtle.deriveBits({ name: 'PBKDF2', salt, iterations: 100_000, hash: 'SHA-256' }, keyMaterial, 256));
  return crypto.subtle.timingSafeEqual ? crypto.subtle.timingSafeEqual(derived, expectedHash)
    : derived.every((b, i) =&gt; b === expectedHash[i]); // fallback constant-time compare
}</p></div>
</div>

