  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">Five "SpaceDO Methods" Are PostgreSQL plpgsql Functions — They Cannot Run in DO SQLite</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">§57 is titled "SpaceDO Methods — Replacing All Stored Functions" but every one of its five code listings is a PostgreSQL stored function written in plpgsql: CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION ... LANGUAGE plpgsql SECURITY DEFINER. None of them can run in Cloudflare Durable Object SQLite. The specific problems: (1) plpgsql is not a valid SQLite language — DO SQLite has no stored procedure engine. (2) RETURNS TABLE is PostgreSQL syntax. (3) SECURITY DEFINER and auth.uid() are Supabase RLS constructs. (4) p_before TIMESTAMPTZ and INTERVAL '24 hours' are PostgreSQL types. (5) FOR UPDATE row locking (in deduct_space_credits) is not supported in SQLite. Any developer following this section literally will attempt to run plpgsql in SpaceDO SQLite and receive runtime errors on every call.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace all five plpgsql functions with TypeScript DO methods using DO SQLite exec(). Example — get_llm_formatted_messages becomes a method on SpaceDO:

// durable-objects/SpaceDO.ts
async getLlmFormattedMessages(params: {
  threadId: string; spaceId: string; limit?: number; before?: number;
}): Promise&lt;Array&lt;{ role: string; content: string; created_at: number }&gt;&gt; {
  const { threadId, spaceId, limit = 100, before } = params;
  const sql = before
    ? 'SELECT role, content, created_at FROM messages WHERE thread_id = ? AND space_id = ? AND deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at &lt; ? AND role != ? ORDER BY created_at ASC LIMIT ?'
    : 'SELECT role, content, created_at FROM messages WHERE thread_id = ? AND space_id = ? AND deleted_at IS NULL AND role != ? ORDER BY created_at ASC LIMIT ?';
  const args = before
    ? [threadId, spaceId, before, 'system', limit]
    : [threadId, spaceId, 'system', limit];
  return this.ctx.storage.sql.exec&lt;{ role: string; content: string; created_at: number }&gt;(sql, ...args).toArray();
}

Apply the same translation for all 5 functions: use ? placeholders, unixepoch('now')*1000 instead of NOW(), and rely on DO single-threaded execution for atomicity instead of FOR UPDATE.</p></div>
</div>

