    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EAB308;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#EAB30818;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§57 — SpaceDO Methods (deduct_space_credits)</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">deduct_space_credits() Uses PostgreSQL FOR UPDATE Row Lock — Not Supported in SQLite / DO SQLite</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The deduct_space_credits stored function in §57 uses PostgreSQL row-level locking:

SELECT balance_usd INTO v_current_balance
FROM space_credits
WHERE space_id = p_space_id
FOR UPDATE;

FOR UPDATE is a PostgreSQL (and MySQL) SELECT modifier that acquires a row-level write lock, preventing concurrent transactions from modifying the row until the current transaction commits. SQLite does not support FOR UPDATE — the syntax is a parse error. If translated literally to DO SQLite, the deduction function fails on every call, making credit deduction completely broken. The purpose of FOR UPDATE (preventing concurrent deductions that would overdraw the balance) is actually unnecessary in DO SQLite because Durable Objects have single-threaded execution — no two queries can run concurrently inside a single DO instance. The atomicity concern is real but the solution is different.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Remove FOR UPDATE — DO single-threaded execution provides the same guarantee. Use a single atomic UPDATE with a conditional WHERE clause instead:

// durable-objects/SpaceDO.ts — deductSpaceCredits()
async deductSpaceCredits(params: {
  amountUsd: number; reason: string; runId?: string;
}): Promise&lt;{ success: boolean; newBalance?: number; error?: string }&gt; {
  const { amountUsd, reason, runId } = params;

  // Atomic check-and-deduct in one statement — DO single-threaded execution prevents races
  // No FOR UPDATE needed; no concurrent queries possible in a single DO instance
  const result = this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
    'UPDATE space_credits SET balance_usd = balance_usd - ? WHERE space_id = ? AND balance_usd >= ? RETURNING balance_usd',
    amountUsd, this.spaceId, amountUsd
  ).one();

  if (!result) {
    const current = this.ctx.storage.sql
      .exec('SELECT balance_usd FROM space_credits WHERE space_id = ?', this.spaceId).one();
    return { success: false, error: 'Insufficient credits: balance $' + (current?.balance_usd ?? 0) + ', required $' + amountUsd };
  }

  this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
    'INSERT INTO credit_transactions (space_id, type, amount_usd, reason, run_id, balance_after) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)',
    this.spaceId, 'debit', amountUsd, reason, runId ?? null, result.balance_usd
  );
  return { success: true, newBalance: result.balance_usd as number };
}</p></div>
</div>

<div id="G-J16" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-J16</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EAB308;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#EAB30818;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§63 — External Portal System (KV Routing)</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">Portal Slug→SpaceId Routing Stored in KV — Up to 60-Second Eventual Consistency Window Causes "Portal Not Found" After Creation</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">provisionExternalPortal() writes the portal slug routing entry to KV:

await env.NAELAO_CONFIG_KV.put(`portal-slug:${slug}`, spaceId);

The naelao-portal Worker reads this KV key on every portal request:

const spaceId = await env.NAELAO_CONFIG_KV.get(`portal-slug:${slug}`);
if (!spaceId) return new Response('Portal not found', { status: 404 });

Cloudflare KV has eventual consistency with up to 60 seconds of propagation delay for newly written keys in remote regions. A user who creates a space with an external portal and immediately navigates to their portal URL ({slug}.naelao.com) will receive "Portal not found" for up to 60 seconds. This is a jarring first-use experience and can be misinterpreted as a deployment failure. The provisioning confirmation email/webhook and the UI success state fire before the portal is actually reachable.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Store the canonical portal slug routing in SpaceDO SQLite (strongly consistent), fall back to KV only as a cache:

// In naelao-portal Worker — two-layer lookup:
async function resolveSlugToSpaceId(slug: string, env: Env): Promise&lt;string | null&gt; {
  // Layer 1: KV cache (fast, eventually consistent)
  const cached = await env.NAELAO_CONFIG_KV.get('portal-slug:' + slug);
  if (cached) return cached;

  // Layer 2: SpaceTypeRegistryDO (strongly consistent — source of truth)
  const registryDO = env.NAELAO_SPACE_TYPE_REGISTRY.get(
    env.NAELAO_SPACE_TYPE_REGISTRY.idFromName('global-space-type-registry')
  );
  const resp = await registryDO.fetch(new Request('http://registry/portal-slug/' + slug));
  if (!resp.ok) return null;
  const { spaceId } = await resp.json() as { spaceId: string };

  // Back-fill KV cache
  await env.NAELAO_CONFIG_KV.put('portal-slug:' + slug, spaceId, { expirationTtl: 3600 });
  return spaceId;
}</p></div>
</div>

<div id="G-J17" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-J17</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EAB308;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#EAB30818;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§24 — Execution Ladder Tier 5 (routeAndExecute)</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">routeAndExecute() Tier 5 Passes Object.values(toolCall.parameters) as Positional args — Property Order Does Not Guarantee Correct CLI Argument Sequence</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The Tier 5 sandbox dispatch in routeAndExecute() maps tool parameters to a command's positional arguments array:

body: JSON.stringify({
  command: toolName,
  args: Object.values(toolCall.parameters)
})

Object.values() returns values in insertion order of properties. The insertion order of a parsed JSON object is theoretically deterministic (string keys in insertion order), but: (1) The LLM that generates tool calls may not output parameters in the order the tool's argument list expects. (2) Different JSON parsers may not preserve insertion order for non-integer keys in all environments. (3) For CLI tools, argument order is semantic — python script.py arg1 is different from python script.py arg2. A Tier 5 tool "run_python" with parameters { script: 'main.py', arg: 'production' } vs { arg: 'production', script: 'main.py' } would produce different command invocations depending purely on LLM output ordering. This causes intermittent, non-deterministic failures that are extremely hard to debug.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Tier 5 tools must define an explicit parameter schema that maps named parameters to their correct positional positions. routeAndExecute() should use the schema to build the args array, not Object.values():

// Tool parameter schema — defines ordered arg list for Tier 5 CLI tools
const TIER_5_TOOL_SCHEMAS: Record&lt;string, { argsOrder: string[] }&gt; = {
  'run_python':  { argsOrder: ['script', 'args'] },
  'run_bash':    { argsOrder: ['command'] },
  'run_node':    { argsOrder: ['script'] },
  'run_pytest':  { argsOrder: ['test_path', 'flags'] },
};

// In routeAndExecute() Tier 5 path:
const schema = TIER_5_TOOL_SCHEMAS[toolName];
const orderedArgs = schema
  ? schema.argsOrder.map(key =&gt; toolCall.parameters[key]).filter(v =&gt; v !== undefined)
  : Object.values(toolCall.parameters);  // fallback for undeclared tools

body: JSON.stringify({ command: toolName, args: orderedArgs })</p></div>
</div>

<div style="margin:48px 0 0;padding:24px 0 0;border-top:1px solid #1E293B;">
  <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">MEDIUM SEVERITY — 3 GAPS</span>
</div>

<div id="G-J18" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-J18</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#3B82F618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§56 — Analytics Engine (mapEventToDataPoint)</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">Analytics Engine indexes Array Passes Two Values — AE Uses Only First Index, Second Value Is Silently Ignored</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">mapEventToDataPoint() in §56 constructs every data point with:

const base = {
  indexes: [event.spaceId ?? 'global', event.type],
  timestamp: new Date(event.timestamp)
};

Workers Analytics Engine's writeDataPoints() accepts an indexes field that is an array of strings, but AE uses only the first element as the primary queryable index (index1 in SQL). The second element (event.type) is accepted without error but is silently ignored — it is NOT queryable as index2. Dashboard queries that filter by event.type on an index column will always return empty results. This creates confusion: the spec implies both spaceId and event.type are indexed, but only spaceId is. Queries like WHERE index2 = 'agent_run_complete' used in dashboard templates will always return zero rows.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Use only one index value, and move event_type to a blob field (which it already is as blobs[0]):

const base = {
  indexes: [event.spaceId ?? 'global'],  // single index — spaceId is the partition key
  timestamp: new Date(event.timestamp ?? Date.now())  // guard against undefined timestamp
};

Update all admin dashboard queries to filter by blob1 (event_type in blobs[0]) instead of index2:

-- WRONG:
WHERE index2 = 'token_usage'

-- CORRECT (event_type is in blob1):
WHERE blob1 = 'token_usage'

This matches the AE schema definition already shown in §56 where blobs[0]=event_type.</p></div>
</div>

<div id="G-J19" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-J19</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#3B82F618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§25 — Execution Ladder Tier 5 / SandboxDO</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">SandboxDO initSchema() Has No Visible Call Site — SQLite Tables May Not Exist on First Request</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The §25 SandboxDO listing defines private async initSchema() which creates the exec_log and container_state tables. However, no call to initSchema() appears in the spec's DO implementation. The fetch() handler routes directly to ensureRunning(), exec(), getStatus(), or stop() — none of which call initSchema(). On the very first request to a new SandboxDO instance, exec() attempts to INSERT INTO exec_log (...) before the table exists, throwing a SQLite "no such table" error. ensureRunning() attempts to SELECT value FROM container_state before that table exists, throwing another error. All Tier 5 tool executions fail on every new project's first use.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Call initSchema() in the DO constructor, or use the standard DO constructor pattern:

export class SandboxDO extends DurableObject {
  constructor(ctx: DurableObjectState, env: Env) {
    super(ctx, env);
    // Initialize schema synchronously — ctx.storage.sql.exec is synchronous
    ctx.storage.sql.exec(`
      CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS exec_log (
        id          TEXT PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT (lower(hex(randomblob(16)))),
        command     TEXT NOT NULL,
        exit_code   INTEGER,
        stdout      TEXT,
        stderr      TEXT,
        duration_ms INTEGER,
        executed_at INTEGER DEFAULT (unixepoch('now') * 1000)
      );
      CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS container_state (
        key   TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
        value TEXT NOT NULL
      );
      INSERT OR IGNORE INTO container_state VALUES ('status', 'stopped');
      INSERT OR IGNORE INTO container_state VALUES ('last_started', '0');
    `);
  }
  // ... fetch(), exec(), alarm() etc.
}

The constructor runs once when the DO is first constructed or reconstructed after eviction, so CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS is safe and idempotent.</p></div>
</div>

<div id="G-J20" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-J20</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#3B82F618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§24 — Execution Ladder Tier 4 (Browser / fill_form)</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">Browser Tier 4 fill_form Action Passes Form Field Values Through CDP — Passwords and PII Visible in CF Browser Rendering Infrastructure Logs</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The §24 Browser Tier 4 fill_form implementation uses page.type() to send keystrokes:

for (const [selector, value] of Object.entries(params.fields as Record&lt;string, string&gt;)) {
  await page.type(selector, value);
}

params.fields is passed as a JSON body from the agent tool call to the naelao-browser Worker and then forwarded as CDP Input.dispatchKeyEvent commands. CF Browser Rendering uses headless Chrome managed by Cloudflare's infrastructure, and CDP session data — including all keystrokes dispatched via page.type() — passes through CF's infrastructure layer. If an agent fills a login form with a real user password, or submits a payment form with a card number, those values appear in CDP session logs at the infrastructure level. This is not specific to CF Browser Rendering but affects any managed headless browser service. The spec does not document this risk or recommend mitigations.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add a documented restriction and implement field classification:

1. Document the risk in the spec for §24 Tier 4 and in the naelao-browser Worker:
   "WARNING: All values passed to fill_form are transmitted as CDP keystrokes through Cloudflare infrastructure. Do not use fill_form with passwords, payment card numbers, SSNs, or other sensitive credentials."

2. Add field classification scanning in the naelao-browser Worker before processing fill_form:
// workers/browser/index.ts
const SENSITIVE_SELECTORS = [/password/i, /secret/i, /card/i, /cvv/i, /ssn/i, /token/i];
const SENSITIVE_TYPES = ['password', 'hidden'];

for (const [selector, value] of Object.entries(params.fields)) {
  const isSensitive = SENSITIVE_SELECTORS.some(r =&gt; r.test(selector)) ||
    (await page.$eval(selector, el =&gt; (el as HTMLInputElement).type)).match(/^(password|hidden)$/);
  if (isSensitive) {
    // Use page.evaluate() + element.value = ... instead of page.type() to avoid CDP keystroke logs
    await page.evaluate(
      ([sel, val]) =&gt; { (document.querySelector(sel) as HTMLInputElement).value = val; },
      [selector, value]
    );
  } else {
    await page.type(selector, value);
  }
}</p></div>
</div>

