<div id="G-K14" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K14</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#F9731618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§28 naelao-threads</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">void agentDO.fetch() Fire-and-Forget in Worker Without ctx.waitUntil() — Agent Run May Be Killed Before Starting</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">In POST /threads/:id/messages, foreground mode dispatches the agent with:
  void agentDO.fetch(new Request('http://agent/run', { method: 'POST', ... }));

The void keyword discards the Promise. In a Cloudflare Worker, once the Response is returned to the caller, the Worker's execution context is eligible for termination. Any in-flight promises not registered with ctx.waitUntil() may be abandoned before the agent DO fetch completes. The agent run may never start, or may be cut off mid-initialization.

Note: This is the same issue documented in G-J14 from §28. Both identify the same code path — this entry confirms the pattern from a fresh read of the full section.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace void agentDO.fetch(...) with c.executionCtx.waitUntil(agentDO.fetch(...)). In Hono, the ExecutionContext is accessible via c.executionCtx. Similarly, the fire-and-forget POST http://agent/warm in POST /threads should use waitUntil:
  c.executionCtx.waitUntil(
    agentDO.fetch(new Request('http://agent/run', {
      method: 'POST',
      body: JSON.stringify({ threadId, message, spaceId, projectId })
    }))
  );
Review all void promise patterns in naelao-threads, naelao-router, and naelao-portal-router for the same issue.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K15" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K15</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#F9731618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§02 naelao-router</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">KV-Based Rate Limiting in naelao-router Is Eventually Consistent — Allows Up to 300x Over-Limit Under Concurrent CF PoP Load</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The naelao-router rate limiting uses NAELAO_CONFIG_KV with a sliding-window counter pattern: get(key) → count + 1 → put(key, count+1). KV is eventually consistent — writes propagate within 60 seconds across regions. Under concurrent traffic from multiple CF PoPs, two requests can both read count=99, both increment to 100, and both write 100, while the true count should be 101. At Cloudflare network scale (300+ active PoPs), the effective rate limit can be exceeded by a factor proportional to the number of active PoPs. This KV-based rate limiting provides no meaningful protection and will consistently allow more requests than the declared limit, rendering it ineffective against burst attacks.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace KV-based rate limiting with a RateLimitDO using SQLite atomic operations:
  // In RateLimitDO:
  this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
    `INSERT INTO rate_counts (key, window_start, count) VALUES (?, unixepoch('now'), 1)
     ON CONFLICT (key, window_start) DO UPDATE SET count = count + 1
     RETURNING count`,
    windowKey
  );

The DO's single-threaded model guarantees atomicity. The spec documents a similar pattern in BuilderAccountDO.checkRateLimit() — apply the same pattern at the router level. Route rate-limit checks through the existing ConcurrencyDO or a dedicated per-user RateLimitDO named idFromName('ratelimit:${userId}:${windowKey}').</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K16" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K16</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#F9731618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§02 naelao-auth</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">KV Token Revocation Check Suffers 60-Second Eventual Consistency Lag — Revoked Tokens Remain Valid After Security Incidents</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The router performs a KV revocation check (revoked:userId:spaceId in NAELAO_CONFIG_KV) before forwarding requests. KV is eventually consistent with up to 60 seconds of propagation lag. A token revoked after a security incident (compromised account, user ban, emergency revocation) will still pass the KV check for up to 60 seconds at any PoP that has not yet received the write. For a platform handling enterprise customer data and financial transactions, a 60-second window after an emergency revocation is an unacceptable security gap.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Move revocation state to SpaceDO SQLite accessed via Service Binding — strongly consistent within the DO. For high-throughput paths, implement a 30-second in-memory cache in naelao-auth backed by a DO revocation check on cache miss:
  // In naelao-auth Worker:
  const cached = this.revocationCache.get(tokenId); // in-memory Map with TTL
  if (cached === undefined) {
    const result = await spaceDO.fetch(new Request('http://space/check-revocation?tokenId=' + tokenId));
    const { revoked } = await result.json();
    this.revocationCache.set(tokenId, { revoked, expires: Date.now() + 30_000 });
  }
This gives fast-path performance with strong consistency: the maximum revocation lag is 30 seconds (single-region DO), not 60 seconds across global PoPs.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K17" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K17</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#F9731618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§79 — naelao-notifications</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">Notification Idempotency Key Written After Delivery — Failed Channel Causes Duplicate Send on Retry</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The notification consumer reads the idempotency key, then sends all channels in Promise.all([email, push, slack, discord]), then writes the idempotency key. If any channel send fails (e.g., email succeeds but push throws), the Promise.all rejects, the idempotency key is never written, and msg.retry() is called. On retry, the email is sent again (duplicate). The TOCTOU race between get(idempotencyKey) and put(idempotencyKey) also allows concurrent worker instances to both read null and both deliver. This can result in users receiving duplicate email alerts, Slack messages, or push notifications during any transient channel failure.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Restructure to write-before-deliver:
  1. Write the idempotency key with status: 'processing' BEFORE any delivery attempt.
  2. Track per-channel delivery status in individual KV keys: notif_lock:${id}:email, notif_lock:${id}:push, etc.
  3. On retry: skip channels already marked complete in their per-channel KV key.
  4. Only when all channels succeed (or exhaust their retries): write status: 'complete' and call msg.ack().

  This ensures each channel is delivered at most once per notification event, regardless of partial failures.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K18" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K18</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#F9731618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§78 — naelao-admin DLQ</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">Admin DLQ Retry: queue.send() + ACK Are Non-Atomic — ACK Failure Causes Double-Delivery into Primary Agent Queue</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The DLQ retry handler calls await NAELAO_AGENT_QUEUE.send(payload) followed by a separate POST to the CF REST API to ACK/delete the DLQ message. If send() succeeds but the ACK call fails (network error, CF API outage), the message remains in the DLQ. The next retry operation (manual or automated) will re-enqueue the same message, causing double-delivery into the primary agent queue. Since CF Queues is at-least-once delivery, duplicate agent runs may be created for the same thread message, causing visible duplicate responses to users.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Before re-enqueuing, store the messageId in a KV key (dlq-retried:${messageId}) with TTL 24h. On retry: check this key first, skip if already retried. After successful re-enqueue: immediately attempt ACK. If ACK fails, the KV check prevents a second re-enqueue on the next retry attempt. Log ACK failures to Analytics Engine for monitoring. This provides at-most-once re-enqueue semantics despite the non-atomic API.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K19" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K19</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#F9731618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§81 — naelao-feedback</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">Feedback Log R2 Append Is a Racy Read-Modify-Write — Concurrent Feedback Events Cause Silent Data Loss</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The feedback worker appends to spaces/${spaceId}/agents/${agentId}/feedback-log.jsonl via:
  get(key) → .text() → concatenate new entry → put(key, newContent)

R2 has no native atomic append. If two feedback events for the same agent arrive concurrently, both calls read the same existing content, both append their respective entry, and one put() overwrites the other. The overwritten entry is silently lost. As the file grows unbounded, the full read-rewrite also consumes increasing amounts of memory and bandwidth per feedback event — a 10,000-entry log file is re-read and re-written on every feedback event.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Use R2's onlyIf: { etagMatches: existingEtag } conditional PUT for optimistic concurrency:
  const obj = await env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.get(logKey);
  const existing = obj ? await obj.text() : '';
  const etag = obj?.etag;
  const result = await env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.put(logKey, existing + JSON.stringify(entry) + '
', {
    onlyIf: etag ? { etagMatches: etag } : { etagDoesNotMatch: '*' }
  });
  if (result === null) { /* 412 conflict: retry with exponential backoff */ }

Alternatively, write individual feedback events to separate R2 objects keyed by ${timestamp}-${crypto.randomUUID()}.jsonl and aggregate at read time. This eliminates the read-modify-write entirely.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K20" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K20</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#F9731618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§80 — naelao-eval</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">Eval Suite Runs Sequential for...of Loop Inside waitUntil() — Guaranteed CPU Timeout for Any Dataset Larger Than ~2 Items</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">runEvaluationSuite() runs inside c.executionCtx.waitUntil() and iterates test cases in a for...of loop: each case creates an AgentDO, calls POST http://agent/run-sync, and awaits the result sequentially. A typical GAIA Level-3 agent run takes 15–60 seconds. With a 100-item dataset, sequential execution = 1,500–6,000 seconds of CPU time. CF Workers have a 30-second CPU limit (not wall-clock) for waitUntil(). The eval suite always crashes before completion for any dataset larger than ~2 items. The CI integration shows timeout: 300 seconds but polls synchronously — both the Worker and the CI step always timeout.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement eval as a CF Workflow where each step.do('eval-case-N', ...) gets its own CPU budget and retries on failure:
  export class EvalWorkflow extends WorkflowEntrypoint {
    async run(event, step) {
      const testCases = await step.do('load-dataset', async () => loadTestCases(event.params));
      const results = await Promise.all(
        testCases.map((tc, i) => step.do('eval-case-' + i, async () => runTestCase(tc)))
      );
      await step.do('aggregate-results', async () => writeResultsToR2(results));
    }
  }
Use p-limit(5) to run at most 5 test cases in parallel within each Workflow step batch. Store partial results in R2 and aggregate at the end.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K21" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K21</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#F9731618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§80 — naelao-eval</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">naelao-eval eval_runs SQLite Schema Uses PostgreSQL-Only Types and Functions — Table Cannot Be Created in SpaceDO</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The eval_runs table schema uses:
  • UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid() — not valid SQLite
  • JSONB column type — does not exist in SQLite
  • TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW() — not valid SQLite
  • Score history query uses DATE_TRUNC('day', ...), INTERVAL '30 days', NOW(), $1/$2 placeholders

None of these are valid SQLite. JSONB does not exist in SQLite; use TEXT. gen_random_uuid() does not exist; use lower(hex(randomblob(16))). DATE_TRUNC and INTERVAL do not exist; use strftime. The table cannot be created and the score history query will throw, making the entire eval tracking and trend analysis system non-functional.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Rewrite the schema for SpaceDO SQLite:
  CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS eval_runs (
    id TEXT PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT (lower(hex(randomblob(16)))),
    suite_name TEXT NOT NULL,
    model_id TEXT NOT NULL,
    pass_rate REAL NOT NULL,
    details TEXT NOT NULL, -- JSON string, not JSONB
    created_at INTEGER DEFAULT (unixepoch('now') * 1000)
  );

Rewrite the score history query:
  SELECT strftime('%Y-%m-%d', datetime(created_at/1000, 'unixepoch')) as day, AVG(pass_rate) as avg_pass
  FROM eval_runs
  WHERE suite_name = ? AND created_at > (unixepoch('now') - 30*86400) * 1000
  GROUP BY day ORDER BY day DESC</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K22" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K22</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#F9731618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§62 — SpaceTypeRegistryDO Provisioner</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">Space Provisioner Step 1 Calls PostgreSQL Stored Function create_space_with_defaults() — Guaranteed Runtime Failure Aborts Every Space Creation</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The provisioner's Step 1 calls:
  await db.query('SELECT create_space_with_defaults($1, $2, $3, $4, $5)', [...]);

This is the PostgreSQL stored function from §57 that does not exist in SpaceDO SQLite (documented in G-K03). The function call throws immediately, the entire 10-step provisioning fails, and the catch block calls deprovisionSpace() with provisioned = ['space_do']. Since the space was never actually created (the function failed), the deprovisioner attempts to delete R2 prefixes and mark the space deleted — which may corrupt other spaces if spaceId collides with an existing entry. Every single space creation in the entire Naelao platform fails at Step 1.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace the db.query() call with a direct POST to the SpaceDO that executes equivalent SQLite INSERT logic:
  const spaceDO = env.NAELAO_SPACE_DO.idFromName(spaceId);
  const stub = env.NAELAO_SPACE_DO.get(spaceDO);
  const initResult = await stub.fetch(new Request('http://space/init', {
    method: 'POST',
    body: JSON.stringify({ spaceId, name, ownerId, spaceType, planTier })
  }));
  if (!initResult.ok) throw new Error('Space init failed: ' + await initResult.text());

The SpaceDO's /init handler runs the equivalent INSERT statements using this.ctx.storage.sql.exec() with ? placeholders and datetime('now').</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K23" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K23</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#F9731618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§62 — SpaceTypeRegistryDO Provisioner</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">Provisioner Step 5 Loads SQL Migration from R2 and Runs via db.query() — PostgreSQL Incompatibility and SQL Injection Vector</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Step 5 of the provisioner loads a migration SQL file from R2 (env.NAELAO_ASSETS.get(migrationPath)), replaces {SPACE_ID} with the actual spaceId via string substitution, then calls await db.query(scopedSql). This has two critical problems: (1) db.query() is PostgreSQL/Hyperdrive syntax — not valid for SpaceDO SQLite, which uses this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(); (2) running arbitrary SQL strings from R2 objects with string interpolation of spaceId is a SQL injection vector if the space name or type ID can contain SQL metacharacters. An attacker who can write to the migration SQL template in R2 can execute arbitrary SQL against SpaceDO.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Remove the R2-based SQL migration pattern entirely. SpaceDO initializes schema in its TypeScript constructor using ctx.blockConcurrencyWhile(() => initSchema()). Space-type-specific tables should be declared as conditional CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS statements in the SpaceDO constructor keyed on the space type ID:
  if (spaceType === 'analytics') {
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS analytics_events (...)`)
  }
This eliminates the R2 read, db.query() call, and string interpolation vulnerability entirely. Schema versioning can be implemented via a schema_version INTEGER in the state table.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K24" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K24</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#F9731618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§17 — LoRA Fine-Tuning</span>
