    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#3B82F618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§22 — Artifacts Dynamic Worker Loader</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">wrapArtifactCode() Template Literal Injection — ${...} Expressions in User Code Are Expanded, Leaking Worker Environment Variables</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The HTML artifact wrapper uses:
  const html = \`${code.replace(/\`/g, '\`')}\`;

The code replaces backticks but does NOT escape ${...} template literal expressions. An HTML artifact containing ${env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY} or ${globalThis} will interpolate those values into the served HTML when the wrapper string is evaluated. The 27-pattern scanner blocks process.env. but not template literal interpolation of runtime globals. A malicious artifact author can craft content like ${''.constructor.constructor('return env')()} to exfiltrate Worker environment variables into the served HTML.

Note: G-J10 identified the backtick interpolation XSS vector. This entry documents the additional ${...} expression expansion vector that remains even after backtick escaping.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Use JSON serialization instead of template literal embedding to safely include user code:
  // Before (vulnerable):
  const html = \`${code.replace(/\`/g, '\`')}\`;

  // After (safe):
  const html = JSON.stringify(code); // produces a quoted string literal with all special chars escaped

Alternatively, store the artifact code in R2 and serve it via a separate GET /artifact/{id}/content endpoint with Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none' headers. This separates the serving boundary from the wrapper execution context entirely.</p></div>
</div>
