    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#3B82F618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§43 — naelao-sweeper</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">Sweeper Abandoned Agent Run Detection Uses NOW() - INTERVAL '10 minutes' PostgreSQL Syntax — Query Throws on Every Sweeper Tick</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The sweeper queries for abandoned runs with:
  WHERE status = 'running' AND updated_at &lt; NOW() - INTERVAL '10 minutes'

This is PostgreSQL syntax. SpaceDO SQLite uses datetime('now', '-10 minutes') or (unixepoch('now') - 600) * 1000 for millisecond timestamps. The query throws a SQLite syntax error on every sweeper tick. Abandoned agent runs are never cleaned up: their concurrency slots remain held in ConcurrencyDO, eventually exhausting the per-user concurrency limit and blocking all new agent runs for the affected space.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace PostgreSQL INTERVAL syntax with SQLite equivalents based on the timestamp column type:

For INTEGER millisecond timestamps (recommended):
  WHERE status = 'running' AND updated_at &lt; (unixepoch('now') - 600) * 1000

For TEXT ISO timestamps:
  WHERE status = 'running' AND updated_at &lt; datetime('now', '-10 minutes')

Apply the same fix to all other INTERVAL-based queries in the sweeper, including the stale project detection and memory consolidation sweep queries.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K45" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K45</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#3B82F618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§56 — Bootstrap Autopilot Activation</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">Bootstrap Autopilot Activation Has No Atomic Check-and-Transition — Concurrent Checkers Can Trigger Duplicate Activations</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The autopilot activation flow checks five independent criteria (R2 file count, annotation quality score, FinetuneJobDO status, admin approval flag, eval pass rate) and transitions the space to autopilot mode. These checks are not atomic — two concurrent processes (the daily QA standup fiber and the fine-tune completion handler) can both check all five criteria, both find them met, and both trigger the activation sequence. This may result in duplicate activate_autopilot workflow instances, duplicate notifications to the space admin, and duplicate billing state transitions that double-charge the space for activation.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add a single-writer atomicity guard using SpaceDO SQLite:
  const result = this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
    `UPDATE spaces SET status = 'autopilot', activated_at = ?
     WHERE id = ? AND status = 'bootstrap'`,
    Date.now(), this.spaceId
  );
  if (result.rowsWritten === 0) {
    return; // Another process already activated, or not in bootstrap state — skip
  }
  // Only one process reaches here — proceed with activation steps
  await triggerActivationWorkflow();
  await sendActivationNotification();</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K46" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K46</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#3B82F618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§78 — naelao-admin / §09 wrangler.jsonc</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">naelao-dlq Dead Letter Queue Binding Not Declared in wrangler.jsonc — Failed Messages Are Discarded, Not Routed to DLQ</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The sweeper's dead letter queue processing calls the CF REST API with queues/naelao-dlq/messages. The wrangler.jsonc bindings section shows Queue bindings for NAELAO_AGENT_QUEUE, NAELAO_ANALYTICS_QUEUE, and NAELAO_NOTIFICATIONS_QUEUE but does not declare naelao-dlq as a dead letter queue or as a consumer binding. Without dead_letter_queue: 'naelao-dlq' configured on the source queue's settings, failed messages are discarded after max_retries exhaustion. The entire DLQ infrastructure (sweeper sweep, admin retry endpoint) operates on a queue that never receives any messages.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">In wrangler.jsonc, add the DLQ declaration to the NAELAO_AGENT_QUEUE configuration:
  [[queues.producers]]
  binding = "NAELAO_AGENT_QUEUE"
  queue = "naelao-agent-queue"
  dead_letter_queue = "naelao-dlq"  // ADD THIS

Create the naelao-dlq queue resource in the CF dashboard or via Terraform. Declare a consumer binding for the sweeper Worker:
  [[queues.consumers]]
  queue = "naelao-dlq"
  binding = "NAELAO_DLQ"
  max_retries = 0</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K47" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K47</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#3B82F618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§63 — naelao-portal-router / §09 wrangler.jsonc</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">Portal Router References Undeclared env.PORTAL_ROUTES KV Binding — Deploy-Time Binding Error on All Portal Routing Calls</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The portal Worker routing logic references env.PORTAL_ROUTES as a separate KV binding in some code paths. The wrangler.jsonc only declares three KV namespaces (NAELAO_CONFIG_KV, NAELAO_CIRCUIT_KV, NAELAO_ARTIFACT_KV). PORTAL_ROUTES is not declared. Workers deployed without a declared binding throw TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined when accessing env.PORTAL_ROUTES, causing all portal routing to fail with 500 errors. The spec appears to have been partially refactored to use NAELAO_CONFIG_KV for slug lookups but left residual PORTAL_ROUTES references in other code paths.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Audit all env.PORTAL_ROUTES references in the portal router and replace them with env.NAELAO_CONFIG_KV. Use a consistent key prefix for portal slug entries: portal-slug:${slug} → spaceId. Remove PORTAL_ROUTES from any wrangler configurations where it may appear. Add a wrangler.jsonc validation step to CI that ensures all env.* references in Worker source files have corresponding binding declarations.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K48" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K48</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#3B82F618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§81 — naelao-categorizer</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">Categorizer Cron Has No Idempotency — CF At-Least-Once Cron Fires Cause Double-Categorization and Doubled LLM Costs</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The categorizer's scheduled() handler queries up to 50 stale projects and immediately calls LLM for each. If CF Cron fires the handler twice within the same minute (at-least-once delivery), all 50 projects are double-categorized: two LLM calls per project, two UPDATE projects SET tags = ... calls (second overwrites first with potentially different LLM output), and doubled LLM costs. The stale-project query filters on last_categorized_at &lt; NOW() - 24 hours, but both invocations run before any last_categorized_at update, so both see the same 50 projects.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Use a SpaceDO SQLite advisory lock at the start of scheduled():
  try {
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
      `INSERT INTO categorizer_locks (id, locked_at) VALUES ('singleton', unixepoch('now')*1000)`
    );
  } catch {
    return; // Lock exists — another instance is running, skip this invocation
  }
  try {
    await runCategorizationBatch();
  } finally {
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(`DELETE FROM categorizer_locks WHERE id = 'singleton'`);
  }
The sweeper cleans up stale locks (handles crashes): DELETE FROM categorizer_locks WHERE locked_at &lt; unixepoch('now')*1000 - 300000.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K49" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K49</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#3B82F618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§81 — naelao-feedback Daily Standup</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">Daily Standup Clears Feedback Log After LLM Analysis — Feedback Written During 2-Second LLM Call Window Is Permanently Lost</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The daily standup fiber reads feedback-log.jsonl from R2, analyzes it with Claude (~2 seconds), writes improvement-addendum.md, then clears the log with env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.put(logKey, ''). Any feedback event written to the log after the get() read but before the final put('') clear is permanently lost (overwritten with empty string). The window spans two LLM calls (~2 seconds each), making this race practically certain to occur in active production spaces with regular feedback activity.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Use a timestamp-based snapshot approach instead of in-place clear:
  // Rename current log to a dated snapshot before processing
  const snapshotKey = logKey.replace('.jsonl', '-' + Date.now() + '.jsonl');
  const existing = await env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.get(logKey);
  if (existing) {
    const content = await existing.text();
    await env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.put(snapshotKey, content);
    await env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.delete(logKey); // New feedback writes to fresh logKey
  }
  // Process the snapshot file — new feedback goes to the fresh logKey
  await analyzeAndWriteAddendum(snapshotKey);
  // Sweeper archives or deletes old snapshot files after 30 days</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K50" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K50</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#3B82F618;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§22 — Artifacts wrapArtifactCode() React</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">React Artifact Wrapper Imports from react-dom/server — Unavailable in Tier 2 Worker Isolate Without npm Resolution</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The React artifact wrapper generates code that starts with:
  import { renderToString } from 'react-dom/server';

CF Workers do not have React in their default runtime; react-dom/server must be bundled or resolved at deploy time. The Dynamic Worker Loader uses Tier 2 (V8 isolate, no npm resolution) for artifacts. Without Tier 3 (NPM) resolution, the import statement fails with a module-not-found error when the artifact Worker is executed, making all React artifacts non-functional.

Note: G-J06 identified that the React type generates renderToString(&lt;App /&gt;) with JSX. This gap confirms the additional problem: even without JSX, the react-dom/server import itself fails in Tier 2.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Route React artifacts through the Tier 3 (NPM) execution tier in the execution ladder. Update TOOL_TIERS['render_artifact'] to 'npm' instead of 'isolate' for React-type artifacts. Alternatively, add React and React-DOM to the DW Loader's pre-bundled module list (bundled at deploy time, not resolved at runtime). Document clearly in the spec that React artifacts require Tier 3 dispatch and cannot run in the default Tier 2 isolate environment.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-K51" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#E8692A18;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">G-K51</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#10B981;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#10B98118;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;">LOW</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;padding:3px 8px;white-space:nowrap;">§30 — ConcurrencyDO</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">ConcurrencyDO Tier Limits Are Hardcoded Constants — Cannot Update Without Worker Redeployment; Subscription Upgrades Not Wired to ConcurrencyDO</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">const TIER_LIMITS = { free: 1, maker: 3, pro: 10, team: 25, enterprise: 100 } is a module-level constant in ConcurrencyDO. Changing a tier limit requires redeploying all Workers. Additionally, the billing_tier is read from the DO's local config table (initialized to 'free') and is never updated when a space upgrades their subscription. A space that upgrades from Free to Pro continues to be limited to 1 concurrent run until the DO is explicitly updated. The spec shows no endpoint or mechanism for the billing Worker to notify ConcurrencyDO of tier changes after subscription upgrades.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Store tier limits in KV (NAELAO_CONFIG_KV.get('tier-limits')) with a 1-hour in-memory cache in the DO:
  const tierConfig = await env.NAELAO_CONFIG_KV.get('tier-limits', { type: 'json' })
    ?? { free: 1, maker: 3, pro: 10, team: 25, enterprise: 100 };
  const limit = tierConfig[currentTier];

Add a POST /update-tier endpoint to ConcurrencyDO called by the billing Worker on subscription changes:
  app.post('/update-tier', async (c) => {
    const { spaceId, newTier } = await c.req.json();
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec('UPDATE config SET value = ? WHERE key = ?', newTier, 'billing_tier');
    return c.json({ success: true });
  });</p></div>
</div>
