  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:clamp(16px,2.5vw,22px);font-weight:600;color:var(--tx);margin:0 0 16px;">Per-User Personalization Injected in Iframe src URL Hash — Leaks Email and Plan Tier to Browser History</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The operator shell injects per-user personalization (theme, locale, userId, email, planTier) into the embedded App iframe's src URL as a hash fragment:
  iframeEl.src = appUrl + '#' + encodeURIComponent(JSON.stringify({ theme, locale, userId, email, planTier }));

URL hashes appear in browser history (visible to any browser extension with tabs permission), can be captured by browser sync services, and may be reflected server-side if the App does server-side rendering of the hash. Sensitive user attributes (email address, plan tier, userId) are unnecessarily exposed in a URL that is visible outside the JavaScript execution context.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Remove personalization from the URL entirely. Load the iframe with a neutral URL and send personalization after load via postMessage with a verified target origin:
  iframeEl.src = appUrl; // No hash fragment
  iframeEl.addEventListener('load', () => {
    iframeEl.contentWindow?.postMessage(
      { type: 'INIT_PERSONALIZATION', theme, locale, userId, planTier },
      new URL(appUrl).origin  // verified target origin
    );
  });
This keeps all personalization data within the JavaScript execution boundary and out of URLs.</p></div>
