<section id="appendix-n">
<div style="margin:80px 0 0;padding:60px 0 0;border-top:2px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.15em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:12px;">Appendix N</div>
  <h2 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:36px;font-weight:700;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.02em;margin:0 0 16px;">Sixth Pass — Uploaded File Gap Cross-Reference</h2>
  <p style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:15px;color:var(--mt);max-width:720px;line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 40px;">24 new gaps identified from cross-referencing naelao_missing_technical_gaps_combined1.html against Appendices A–M. Each gap was keyword-searched against the existing 449 documented gaps; only those with zero or confirmed-distinct matches are included. Covers: compaction algorithm, DO schema migration, locationHint, CF Access key rotation, blockConcurrencyWhile, clock skew, DLQ consumer, injection scanner stub, tool output overflow, SSL provisioning race, JSON.parse crash, isAllowedOrigin body, sub-agent credit pre-deduction, credential log leakage, workflow cancel, MiniAGI per-step grants, Space Assistant context bleed, container sync script, local agent re-auth, slot limit enforcement, tool manifest staleness, R2/container filesystem divergence, and multi-container billing race.</p>
</div>

<div id="G-N01" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N01</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§agentpress — context-manager</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Context Window Compaction — Missing Algorithm</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">`agentPress.compactContext(sessionId)` is called when `stopReason === 'max_tokens'` but the function body is never specified anywhere in the spec. Context compaction is the hardest problem in production agent systems — a bad algorithm loses information silently; agent quality degrades with no error thrown. Three strategies exist: truncation (drops oldest messages, loses early goals), LLM summarisation (accurate but adds latency/cost at the worst moment), and structured extraction (extract facts to agent_memory before dropping). None of the three is chosen or specified. The function is a stub that is invoked on every context-overflow event.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Specify `compactContext()` using structured extraction: (1) extract tool results from old messages into the run WAL, (2) extract user-stated preferences and task state into agent_memory, (3) delete the raw messages. On LLM extraction failure, fall back to truncation-only and log the degraded compaction. Never let a compaction failure crash the agent run. Cache the generated summary in a `summaries` table keyed by thread and `covers_up_to_message_id` — regenerate only when new messages push the boundary forward.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N02" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N02</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-spaces — SpaceDO</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">SpaceDO Schema Migration — No Strategy Specified</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">SpaceDO initialises its SQLite schema using `IF NOT EXISTS` on every table. This is correct for first deployment and completely wrong for every subsequent deployment. If a column is added to a SpaceDO table after initial creation, existing DOs skip the `CREATE TABLE` (already exists) and the `ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN` is never reached. Every existing SpaceDO in production runs the old schema while newly created DOs run the new schema. DO SQLite has no external migration runner — migrations must execute inside the DO on its first request after a deployment. The spec defines no such mechanism. The platform cannot safely evolve its schema after the first production deployment.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add a versioned migration runner inside SpaceDO using `ctx.blockConcurrencyWhile(() => this.migrate())` in the constructor. Maintain a `schema_version` table (the one table that never changes). On each DO wake, check the stored version against `CURRENT_SCHEMA_VERSION`. If behind, run each migration in order (v1 → v2 → ... → current). Each migration wraps schema changes in try/catch to handle already-applied columns. Increment `CURRENT_SCHEMA_VERSION` on every schema change. Apply the same pattern to every stateful DO that has evolving schema: AssistantDO, ProjectDO, WalletDO, LocalTunnelDO.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N03" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N03</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-spaces — DO creation</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Durable Object Location Affinity — locationHint Never Used</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Durable Objects run in exactly one Cloudflare data centre for their entire lifetime. Every request to that DO from anywhere in the world incurs a round trip to that fixed location. A SpaceDO created in a US data centre answers queries from Cape Town or Tokyo with 150–300ms of DO round-trip latency baked into every agent call. The spec makes no mention of the `locationHint` parameter in DO creation, which allows specifying the approximate region where the DO should be placed. SpaceDO is created once per product — wherever it is created is where it lives forever. NaelaoAgentDO is created per thread with no hint. Every AgentDO for a Cape Town user may be instantiated in a US data centre, adding ~200ms to every agent loop iteration.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Pass `locationHint` when creating SpaceDO and NaelaoAgentDO. For SpaceDO: use the builder's billing region or their CF geo at product-creation time. Map to CF hint values: `wnam`, `enam`, `weur`, `eeur`, `apac`, `oc`, `afr`, `me`, `sam`. For NaelaoAgentDO (created per thread): use `request.cf?.region` from the incoming request to pin the AgentDO near the user. Document the constraint: `locationHint` is a hint not a guarantee, and once a DO is created in a region it stays there — the thread-level strategy is best-effort but substantially better than no hint at all.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N04" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N04</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-admin — CF Access JWT verification</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">CF Access Public Key Caching — Key Rotation Causes Silent Auth Failure</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The admin auth flow fetches CF Access RS256 public keys from `{team}.cloudflareaccess.com/cdn-cgi/access/certs` and caches them for 5 minutes in KV. Cloudflare rotates these signing keys periodically. When CF issues new JWTs signed with key B, the cache still holds key A (TTL not expired). A valid admin JWT signed with key B arrives, the verifier finds no matching key in the cache, and returns `AUTH_INVALID`. A legitimate admin is locked out — not because their token is invalid, but because the cache is stale. This persists for up to 5 minutes and recurs on every CF key rotation. The same bug exists in the Clerk/OIDC and builder-native JWT paths which also cache fetched public keys.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement refresh-on-miss-by-kid: parse the JWT header to extract the `kid` (key ID). Look up the kid in the cached JWKS. If not found (unknown kid = likely a new rotated key), re-fetch the certs immediately from the CF endpoint, update the cache, then look up again. Only fail AUTH_INVALID after a fresh fetch confirms the kid is genuinely unknown. This eliminates the rotation-window lockout. Apply the same kid-based refresh logic to all JWKS verification paths (Clerk, OIDC providers, builder-native). Create a shared `verifyJWKS(token, env)` helper used by all auth paths.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N05" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N05</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-concurrency — ConcurrencyDO / WalletDO / ProjectDO</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">DO Input Gates — Concurrent await Interleaves Read-Modify-Write State</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Durable Objects are single-threaded but not single-tasked. While one handler awaits (an LLM call, R2 read, Service Binding), the DO can begin processing a second incoming request. If both handlers read-modify-write the same state across an await boundary, their mutations interleave and corrupt each other. ConcurrencyDO's check-and-reserve is the clearest case: read slot count (await SQLite), check if under limit, insert a slot (await SQLite). Between the read and insert, a second check-and-reserve can run, read the same count, and both reserve — exceeding the concurrency limit the DO exists to enforce. The spec's DOs doing multi-step state mutations across awaits — ConcurrencyDO, WalletDO, ProjectDO file locks — do not specify `blockConcurrencyWhile` anywhere.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Wrap every read-modify-write sequence that spans an await boundary in `ctx.blockConcurrencyWhile(async () => { ... })`. This blocks the DO from processing other events until the critical section completes, making the sequence atomic. Specifically required: ConcurrencyDO.checkAndReserve(), WalletDO.deduct() and WalletDO.reserveCredits(), ProjectDO file lock acquisition, all idempotency-key check-and-set operations. The cost is queued processing of concurrent requests — but correctness is the only acceptable outcome for slot enforcement and money operations.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N06" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N06</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-auth / §naelao-billing</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Clock Skew Across PoPs — Time-Boundary Comparisons Use No Tolerance</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec uses `Date.now()` in 85+ places for billing windows, token expiry comparisons, idempotency TTLs, and alarm scheduling. Cloudflare runs code across hundreds of PoPs with well-synchronised but not microsecond-identical clocks. A JWT with `exp` set by PoP A is validated by PoP B — if B's clock is slightly ahead, a valid token is rejected as expired. Billing events timestamped at 23:59:59.998 by one Worker may fall into the wrong billing period when the rollup Worker's clock differs. Idempotency locks that expire in 24h can appear expired slightly early under skew, allowing duplicates. All `Date.now()` boundary comparisons are written as exact equality or hard thresholds with no tolerance buffer.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add a `CLOCK_SKEW_TOLERANCE_MS = 30_000` constant (30 seconds). Apply it to all time-boundary comparisons: `now < exp + CLOCK_SKEW_TOLERANCE_MS` for JWT expiry, `windowStart - CLOCK_SKEW_TOLERANCE_MS` for billing window edge events. For billing rollups, assign billing-period membership at the rollup Worker using the rollup Worker's clock at processing time — not the event's originating timestamp. Make idempotency TTLs generous (2× the expected lock duration) — the cost of a slightly-too-long lock is small; the cost of a too-short one is a duplicate action on money or notifications.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N07" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N07</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-queues — NAELAO_DLQ binding</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Dead-Letter Queue — NAELAO_DLQ Binding Exists, Consumer Worker Does Not</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec defines `NAELAO_DLQ` as a Queue binding and routes failed messages from `naelao-notifications`, `naelao-agent-runs`, and `naelao-analytics-queue` into it after 3 failed retries. There is no `workers/naelao-dlq/index.ts` file anywhere in the spec. No Worker consumes this queue. Failed messages enter the DLQ, sit until they expire (CF default: 4 days), and are permanently discarded with no alert, no retry logic, and no preserved record. What is being silently lost: agent run triggers (user action never happened, no error shown), billing alerts and human-in-loop approval requests (approval checkpoint hangs forever), and billing events (credits never deducted, usage is free by accident — discovered only at month-end reconciliation).</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Create `workers/naelao-dlq/index.ts` as a queue consumer for NAELAO_DLQ. On each DLQ message: (1) Write the full payload to R2 for forensics (`dlq/{originalQueue}/{failedAt}/{uuid}.json`) — never lose the payload. (2) Alert based on severity: agent-run DLQ messages → critical builder notification; billing DLQ messages → Analytics Engine entry for reconciliation; notification DLQ messages → ops Slack webhook. (3) Always `msg.ack()` — re-queuing a DLQ message is almost never correct. Add the queue consumer binding to wrangler.jsonc for each Worker that has a DLQ route.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N08" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N08</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§architecture-rules / §agent-builder</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Prompt Injection Scanner — Referenced as 27 Patterns, Never Enumerated</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec calls a '27-pattern scanner' for detecting prompt injection in system prompts at save time and in tool output before context insertion. The scanner is called in `architecture-rules.ts` and in the Agent Builder's save flow. The actual 27 patterns are never defined anywhere in the spec — the function body is a stub. Consequence: builders can save agents with system prompts containing injection payloads (`ignore all previous instructions`, `you are now DAN`, fake tool call JSON, API key patterns), and tool outputs containing jailbreak instructions are inserted directly into the LLM context without any sanitization. The scanner call exists but the scanner is a no-op.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Define all patterns explicitly in `shared/prompt-scanner.ts`. Required categories: (1) Direct override attempts: `ignore previous instructions`, persona override (`you are now DAN`), constraint disregard, fake `[SYSTEM]`/`[INST]` tags, fake XML role tags. (2) Data exfiltration: instructions to send conversation history, repeat system prompt. (3) Permission escalation: bypass approval/checkpoint instructions, false permission claims, fake tool_call JSON injection. (4) Code execution injection (tool output only): eval(), Function(), __proto__, process.env, require('child_process'). (5) Token manipulation: unicode floods, zero-width chars, character floods. (6) Naelao-specific: env binding names (NAELAO_*) in prompts, API key patterns (sk-ant-, Bearer). Severity: 'block' for dangerous patterns at both save time and runtime; 'warn' for suspicious patterns at save time only.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N09" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N09</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-agents — tool execution</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Tool Output Can Overflow the Context It Is Inserted Into</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">`MAX_ARTIFACT_CODE_BYTES` is referenced as the truncation limit for tool results but is never given a value and the truncation logic is never implemented. A web search returning 500KB HTML, a `read_file` of a large R2 document, or a container `exec_command` producing megabytes of stdout is inserted wholesale into the LLM context. This causes two simultaneous failures: the LLM API call exceeds Claude's 200K token context window (API error, run fails), and the single tool call consumes the entire token budget. The agent crashes with no graceful degradation and must be restarted from the beginning.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Define `TOOL_OUTPUT_LIMITS` per tool type in `shared/tool-result-sanitizer.ts`: web_search → 8,000 chars (extract text, discard HTML/CSS/JS); read_file → 32,000 chars with `[truncated at 32K chars]` suffix; exec_command → 16,000 chars of stdout tail (errors are at the end, not the head); api_call → 16,000 chars; DEFAULT → 8,000 chars. For tool outputs exceeding 64KB, store the full result in R2 and insert only a reference (`{ type: 'r2_ref', key, size }`) into the context — the queue consumer fetches from R2 when needed. Apply sanitization as a mandatory post-processing step in every tool executor, not opt-in per tool.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N10" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N10</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-portals — custom domain provisioning</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">SSL Certificate Provisioning Race — Custom Domain Serves HTTP During Verification Window</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">When a builder adds a custom domain, the spec provisions a Cloudflare SSL for SaaS certificate and polls every 60 seconds until `custom_domain_verified = true`. During the verification window (up to 5 minutes, potentially longer for DNS propagation edge cases), the domain resolves but has no valid certificate. HTTP requests during this window receive either a certificate error (`ERR_CERT_AUTHORITY_INVALID`) or serve over plain HTTP. The spec has no specified behaviour for the pending state — no 'domain pending' page, no HTTP-to-maintenance redirect, no user-facing error page. For a B2B launch where the builder shares the link before verification completes, end-users see a browser security warning — a reputational problem with no mitigation.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Until `custom_domain_verified = true`, the custom domain's CF routing should serve a single static 'Domain verification in progress — please check back shortly' page over HTTPS using the `{slug}.naelao.app` wildcard cert (always valid). This requires the domain router to check the `custom_domain_verified` flag in DOMAIN_REGISTRY_KV before routing — return the pending page for unverified domains instead of proxying to the product. Add a builder webhook notification when verification completes so they know it is safe to share the link. Increase the polling frequency to every 15 seconds (from 60) during the first 5 minutes after domain submission.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N11" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N11</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-config — KV reads</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Unguarded JSON.parse on KV Values — One Corrupt Key Crashes All Requests</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec calls `JSON.parse(raw)` directly on KV-retrieved values in at least 28 places — including the space type manifest, temple configuration, user settings, and product config. KV values are strings. If a value was written with a partial write, truncated by a network error, or corrupted by a bad deployment, `JSON.parse` throws a `SyntaxError` that propagates up and crashes the entire Worker request with HTTP 500. A single corrupted KV entry for a shared key (the space type manifest) means every request to every product using that space type crashes until the value is manually repaired. There is no error handling, no fallback, and no alert.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace all direct `JSON.parse(raw)` calls with a guarded helper: `safeParseJSON<T>(raw, fallback, label)` wraps the parse in try/catch, logs the corruption (visible in Tail Workers), and returns the fallback. For critical shared keys (space type manifest, product config) use `requireParseJSON<T>(raw, label)` which throws a named `KVCorruptionError` — caught by the top-level Hono error handler to return 503 and trigger a PagerDuty/Slack alert. The 503 (service unavailable) vs 500 (internal error) distinction matters for SLA tracking and automatic retry behaviour in client code.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N12" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N12</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-router — WebSocket upgrade guard</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">isAllowedOrigin — WebSocket CORS Guard Called But Body Not Specified</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec correctly calls `isAllowedOrigin(origin, env)` before accepting WebSocket upgrades as a defence against unauthenticated WebSocket upgrade attacks (CVE-2026-25253 class). The function call is present everywhere. The function body is never specified — what origins are allowed, how builder custom domains are included, whether the check is case-sensitive, whether port numbers must match. A stub that always returns `true` passes all tests and leaves the WebSocket endpoint fully open to the attack it was designed to block. This is a security gap that could be shipped without detection because the tests only verify that the function is called, not what it checks.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Specify `isAllowedOrigin(origin, env)` in `shared/cors.ts`: (1) Reject any call with no origin (browser always sends Origin on WebSocket upgrades). (2) Allow static platform origins: `https://app.naelao.dev`, `https://naelao.dev`, `https://dashboard.naelao.app`. (3) Allow any `*.naelao.app` subdomain with `https:` protocol. (4) For custom domains, check a module-scope cache (Map refreshed every 5 minutes by a background KV read) — never make an async KV call in the hot WebSocket upgrade path. Enforce HTTPS-only: no `http://` origins allowed. Match port numbers exactly — `https://example.com:8080` is NOT the same as `https://example.com`.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N13" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N13</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-agents — MiniAGI / sub-agent spawning</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Sub-agent Credit Pre-deduction — Referenced in Comment, Never Implemented</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec includes the comment: 'Sub-agent credit allocation must be deducted BEFORE spawning (atomic). If deduction fails, do not spawn.' This is correct design. The implementation is not specified. The sub-agent spawning code paths (MiniAGI recursive planner, sub-agent tool) show sub-agents being spawned with billing handled by the post-run billing handler. Credits are checked post-run, not pre-run. A builder who has run out of credits can spawn unlimited sub-agents — MiniAGI can spawn 50 sub-agents in a recursive plan, each running for 30 seconds, total cost $4.00 against a $0.20 balance. Post-run billing produces 50 failed deductions and a $3.80 loss. With new-account creation, this is a systematic free-compute exploit.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement the pre-deduction reserve pattern in `spawnSubAgent()`: (1) Before spawning, call `WalletDO.reserveCredits({ amount: estimatedCredits, label, expiryMs: 5min })`. This is atomic in WalletDO using `blockConcurrencyWhile` — concurrent spawns cannot double-spend the same balance. (2) If reserve fails (insufficient balance), return `CREDITS_EXHAUSTED` error — do not spawn. (3) After sub-agent completes, call `WalletDO.settleReserve(reserveId, actualCreditsUsed)` to convert the reserve to an actual deduction. (4) On sub-agent failure, call `WalletDO.releaseReserve(reserveId)` to return the reserved credits. The reserve-settle-release pattern prevents both free-compute exploits and over-charging for sub-agents that run cheaper than estimated.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N14" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N14</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-router — middleware ordering</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Credential URL Parameters Stripped at Router But Not in Logs</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec defines `CREDENTIAL_URL_PARAMS = ['token','apiKey','api_key','key','secret','gatewayUrl']` and strips them from URLs before routing. The stripping happens AFTER the logging middleware runs. A builder who passes an API key as a URL parameter (which they will, despite being told not to) has that credential logged to Analytics Engine and Tail Workers before the strip occurs. Analytics Engine records are readable by anyone with `analytics:read` permission — including team members who do not have secrets access. The credential is now in the analytics store indefinitely, accessible without the secrets manager permissions that were supposed to protect it.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Move credential parameter stripping to the FIRST middleware, before any logging. The stripping middleware must mutate `c.req.raw` to replace credential param values with `[REDACTED]` before the request URL reaches any logging code. Additionally: the Tail Worker that sends requests to Analytics Engine must redact `Authorization`, `Cookie`, and `X-API-Key` header values before calling `writeDataPoint()`. Add a lint rule that enforces the middleware ordering: logging middleware must appear after the strip middleware in every Worker's Hono app configuration. Apply the same strip to response logging — API responses that echo back credential-containing request fields must be sanitized before logging.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N15" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N15</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-workflows — WorkflowRegistryDO</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Dynamic Workflow Cancellation — CF Instance ID Never Stored; No Cancel Endpoint</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">CF Workflows are durable multi-step task systems. Once started, the spec provides no cancellation path. A workflow running 47 steps over 6 hours cannot be stopped if a bug is discovered in step 3 — it runs to completion, burning credits on every step. The CF Workflows API supports cancellation via the workflow instance ID (`instance.terminate()`), but the spec never stores the CF Workflows instance ID after `workflow.create()`. Without the instance ID, the running workflow cannot be referenced for cancellation, status queries, or forced termination. The dashboard has no 'Cancel workflow' button because there is no endpoint to call.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">On workflow start in `WorkflowRegistryDO.startWorkflow()`: store the CF Workflows instance ID (`instance.id`) in the `workflow_runs` table alongside the run ID. Add `cancelWorkflowRun(runId)` method: look up `cf_instance_id` from SQLite, call `env[bindingName].get(cfInstanceId).then(i => i.terminate())`, update status to 'cancelled'. Expose two new REST endpoints in `naelao-workflows`: `GET /workflows/{id}/runs` (list active runs with status and cancellable flag) and `POST /workflows/{id}/runs/{runId}/cancel`. Add the cancel button to the dashboard workflow run view. On cancellation, release any credit reserves held for the workflow's estimated remaining steps.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N16" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N16</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-miniagi — access control matrix</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">MiniAGI Access Grant Expiry — Grants Checked Once at Plan Start, Not Per Step</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">MiniAGIDO's access control matrix supports time-limited grants (`expires_at` field, null = permanent). A grant for 'this user can query the HR domain for the next 24 hours' expires at a timestamp. The expiry check runs only at the start of a query — when the recursive plan is initialised. A MiniAGI recursive plan can run for hours across many steps. A user whose access grant expires mid-run continues using the grant for the remainder of the run because the expiry check ran once at plan initiation. A user whose access was explicitly revoked mid-run (e.g., terminated employee) still receives the full query result from all remaining plan steps.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Re-check access grants at each recursive planning step boundary. MiniAGI's recursive planner has natural step checkpoints — add grant re-validation at the start of each step for every domain the step accesses. If a grant has expired: terminate that branch gracefully with `{ status: 'access_revoked', domain, message: 'Access to this data domain expired during execution.' }` — do not continue with stale access. For active grants nearing expiry (within 5 minutes), emit a warning in the step result so the orchestrator can notify the user before the next step. Also apply this pattern to the general sub-agent access grant system, not just MiniAGI.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N17" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N17</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-spaces — SpaceAssistantDO</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Space Assistant Shared In-Memory Context — Concurrent Users Bleed State</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">SpaceAssistantDO is a single DO per space handling WebSocket connections from all users in that space simultaneously. The spec's channel handler routes messages by userId, but the assistant's in-memory context — current conversation state, loaded soul.md, short-term memory cache — is shared across all concurrent sessions. User A's conversation bleeds into User B's if both are active in the same DO instance simultaneously. More critically: if User A's message causes sensitive data to be loaded into the assistant's in-memory context (Q3 revenue figures, HR records), User B's concurrent request can receive a response influenced by User A's loaded context. This is a data privacy vulnerability in any multi-user Team or Enterprise space.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Maintain per-user context isolation in SpaceAssistantDO using a `userContexts = new Map<string, UserAssistantContext>()` keyed by userId. Each entry holds: `conversationHistory`, `loadedMemory`, and `lastActiveAt` for that user only. The `webSocketMessage` handler must route to and process using ONLY the requesting user's context — never a shared context. Evict idle user contexts after 30 minutes of inactivity to prevent memory growth in high-traffic spaces. Note: these per-user contexts are in-memory and lost on DO hibernation — this is acceptable for the Space Assistant (the next user turn reloads context from SpaceDO SQLite). The cross-user context bleed is the critical issue; isolation must be built before any multi-user space goes live.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N18" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N18</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-containers — container sync</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Container Sync Script — Referenced in Tests, Never Specified</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's test suite asserts: 'Files written in container appear in R2 workspace after sync' and 'Container sync script validates permission manifest on start.' There is no sync script defined anywhere in the spec — no file path, no implementation, no trigger mechanism, no Docker image reference. It exists only in test expectations. Consequently: (1) the test suite fails on these assertions, (2) files agents write to the container filesystem during a run are not persisted to R2 unless mountBucket is write-through (which the spec never confirms — this is the core ambiguity). On container idle-sleep, local writes are lost. The entire container filesystem persistence story depends on an unspecified component.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Specify the container sync script at `/opt/naelao/sync.sh` inside the `naelao-base` Docker image. Define two modes: (a) On container start (pull): download from R2 any workspace files that don't exist locally or are newer in R2. (b) On stop / periodic (push): flush write-back cache (`sync`) and confirm R2 mount is current. Trigger the push from: the SandboxDO idle alarm before `container.destroy()`, a periodic alarm every 5 minutes while running, and debounced 10 seconds after any `write_file` tool call. Critically: resolve the mountBucket semantics ambiguity — confirm whether CF Containers' `mountBucket` is write-through (writes go directly to R2) or copy-on-write (requires explicit push). The sync story cannot be fully specified until this is confirmed in CF Containers documentation.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N19" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N19</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-containers — ProjectDO</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">checkLocalAgentConnected — Called But Never Implemented; No Timeout</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">`ProjectDO.getActiveToolManifest()` calls `await this.checkLocalAgentConnected()` to decide whether to include local machine tools. The method is called in the spec but its body is never defined — it is a missing implementation that will throw `TypeError: this.checkLocalAgentConnected is not a function` at runtime. Even if implemented, it must make a cross-DO network call to `LocalTunnelDO` on every agent run start. Without a timeout, a slow or erroring LocalTunnelDO blocks the entire tool manifest rebuild, blocking the agent run before it starts. A 500ms timeout on the cross-DO call is the minimum; a cached connection state is the correct solution.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement `checkLocalAgentConnected()` with a 500ms abort timeout on the cross-DO fetch to LocalTunnelDO. On any error or timeout: return `false` and allow the agent run to proceed without local tools — never block the run. Better: cache the connection state in ProjectDO SQLite. Have LocalTunnelDO write a status update to ProjectDO when it connects and when it closes: `POST http://project/local-agent-status { connected: boolean }`. `checkLocalAgentConnected()` then reads from the cache (zero network hop, synchronous). The cache is invalidated by LocalTunnelDO's webSocketClose handler. This eliminates the cross-DO call on every single agent run start.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N20" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N20</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-containers — ProjectDO.addContainer</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Project Container Slot Limit — Stated in Prose, Never Enforced in Code</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec states: 'free plan: 1 slot, pro: 5 slots, enterprise: unlimited.' This is described in prose only. There is no enforcement in `ProjectDO.addContainer()`, no check against the builder's plan tier, and no error path for limit exceeded. A free-plan user can call `addContainer()` six times and provision six marketplace containers — each running its own CF Container, generating billing events, and consuming credits — with nothing preventing it. The slot count is visible in billing events but the provisioning code has no gate. The limit exists as text in the spec but is entirely absent from the code path that enforces it.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add slot limit enforcement as the first check in `ProjectDO.addContainer()`: count active slots (`SELECT COUNT(*) FROM container_slots WHERE status != 'removed'`), look up the builder's plan from SpaceDO billing table, apply plan limits (`free:1, pro:5, scale:20, enterprise:Infinity`). Return a 402 response with `SLOT_LIMIT_REACHED` error and the upgrade prompt if at limit. Note the implicit policy: on the free plan, the base SandboxDO counts as the 1 allowed slot — free-plan users cannot add marketplace containers. This policy must be stated explicitly in the UI to avoid confusion. Add a plan-change hook that re-checks slot counts when a builder downgrades.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N21" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N21</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-containers — ProjectContainerDO</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Tool Manifest Stale When Container Goes to Sleep — No Invalidation on Stop</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">`getActiveToolManifest()` queries `container_slots WHERE status = 'running'`. When a marketplace container idles and `ProjectContainerDO.alarm()` calls `stop()`, the status is updated in ProjectContainerDO's local storage but `ProjectDO` (which owns the manifest) is never notified. The tool manifest remains stale — the stopped container's tools are still listed as available. An agent calling `query_postgres` on a sleeping container triggers a 2–3 second cold start with no user indication of what is happening. If the cold start fails (image pull error, resource exhaustion), the agent sees a tool error with no context. There is no 'tool warming up' indicator and no timeout handling specified for the cold-start path.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add a notification call from `ProjectContainerDO.stop()` to ProjectDO: `POST http://project/container-status { slotId, status: 'stopped' }`. ProjectDO.onContainerStatusChange() updates the slot status and rebuilds the active manifest. Add a `containerStatus: 'running' | 'sleeping' | 'stopped'` and `warmUpSeconds?: number` field to ToolDefinition. When an agent calls a tool on a sleeping container, inject a note into the agent's system prompt context: 'Note: [TOOL_NAME] is sleeping — first call will take ~3 seconds to warm up.' On cold-start failure, return a structured `{ type: 'container_error', reason: 'cold_start_failed', suggestion: 'Try again in 30 seconds' }` tool result rather than crashing the agent run.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N22" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N22</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-containers — executeWorkspaceTool</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">R2 Workspace vs Container Filesystem Diverge — read_file and execute_python See Different Content</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The `read_file` workspace tool reads directly from R2: `env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.get('spaces/{spaceId}/workspace/{path}')`. The `execute_python` tool runs code inside the container which reads from the container's mounted `/workspace`. These represent the same logical workspace — but if `mountBucket` is not perfectly write-through (which the spec never confirms; see G-N18), they can diverge. An agent that writes a file via `execute_python` (container filesystem) and immediately reads it via `read_file` (R2 direct) may read stale content — the agent's own newly-written data appears older than it should. This coherence failure causes incorrect agent reasoning with no error, no exception, and no signal that the content is stale.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Enforce a single canonical write path policy per workspace location: when the container is running, writes to paths under `mountPath` MUST route through the container (via `execute_python` or `write_file_in_container` tool), not via the direct R2 `write_file` path. In `executeWorkspaceTool()`, detect when `write_file` targets a path under `mountPath` while the container is running and reroute the write through SandboxDO to the container, preserving coherence. The direct R2 `write_file` path is for files outside the container mount only. Document this constraint explicitly in the agent's system prompt and tool descriptions so the LLM understands when to use each write path.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N23" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N23</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-containers — LocalTunnelDO /connect</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Local Agent Re-auth on Reconnect — New WebSocket Has No Identity Verification</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">When the local agent CLI reconnects after a WebSocket drop, it sends a new upgrade request to `LocalTunnelDO`'s `/connect` endpoint. The handler accepts the WebSocket immediately with no authentication check. Any process that knows the WebSocket URL — derivable from the publicly routable DO name `tunnel:{spaceId}:{userId}` — can connect and be accepted as the legitimate local agent. An attacker on the same network as the user (or who can guess/intercept the DO name) can connect and receive tool calls forwarded to the 'local machine', including file system reads, code execution requests, and local credential access — the full set of tools the local agent is trusted to execute.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement token-based handshake on `/connect`: require a short-lived JWT in the URL (`?token=...`). The token is generated by the dashboard when the user clicks 'Connect local machine', signed with `NAELAO_JWT_SECRET`, containing `{ spaceId, userId, jti: uuid, exp: now+60s }`. In the `/connect` handler: verify the JWT signature, check that `spaceId` and `userId` match the DO's identity, verify `exp` is not past, and check the `jti` against a `used_connect_tokens` table (single-use — reject if already seen). On verification failure: return 401/403. On success: record the `jti` as used, accept the WebSocket. The 60-second token expiry means an intercepted token URL is useless after 60 seconds.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-N24" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-N24</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-containers — WalletDO multi-container billing</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Multi-Container Billing Race — WalletDO.deduct() Missing blockConcurrencyWhile</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">A project with 3 running marketplace containers emits billing events from 3 separate `ProjectContainerDO` instances independently to `NAELAO_ANALYTICS_QUEUE`. The queue consumer processes a batch and makes 3 concurrent Service Binding calls to the same `WalletDO.deduct()`. If `WalletDO.deduct()` does not use `blockConcurrencyWhile`, two of the three calls can read the same pre-deduction balance simultaneously and both succeed even if together they would exceed the balance. Example: 3 deductions of 100 cents on a 250-cent balance all read 250 cents, all succeed (250 > 100 each), and the balance goes to -50 cents. The DO is single-threaded but the concurrent awaits allow the reads to interleave before the writes.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add `ctx.blockConcurrencyWhile()` to `WalletDO.deduct()` and any other balance-modifying methods. The `blockConcurrencyWhile` ensures the read-check-write sequence for each deduction is atomic with respect to other concurrent deductions on the same WalletDO. Concurrent callers queue behind each other — the throughput cost is minimal (each deduction takes microseconds) and the correctness guarantee is absolute. Apply the same fix to `WalletDO.credit()`, `reserveCredits()`, and `settleReserve()`. This is the same issue as G-N05 (DO Input Gates) applied specifically to the multi-container billing path and documented separately because the billing context and failure mode differ.</p></div>
</div>

<div style="margin:32px 0 0;padding:32px;background:#0A1628;border:1px solid #1E293B;border-radius:8px;">
  <p style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;color:#64748B;margin:0;line-height:1.8;">
    Appendix N complete — sixth pass from uploaded file cross-reference. Combined gap audit total: A (125) + B (30) + C (22) + D (22) + E (24) + F (26) + G (26) + H (15) + I (34) + J (20) + K (51) + L (23) + M (31) + N (24)
    = <strong style="color:var(--or);">473 documented technical gaps</strong> with required fixes.
  </p>
</div>
</section>

<section id="appendix-o">
<div style="margin:80px 0 0;padding:60px 0 0;border-top:2px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.15em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:12px;">Appendix O</div>
  <h2 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:36px;font-weight:700;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.02em;margin:0 0 16px;">Seventh Pass — Full Three-Band Spec Re-Audit</h2>
  <p style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:15px;color:var(--mt);max-width:760px;line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 40px;">41 new gaps from a fresh full sweep of the spec body (lines 1408&ndash;32367) across three independent analysis bands, each cross-referenced against the existing 473 documented gaps and confirmed distinct. This pass surfaces concrete runtime-logic defects beyond the recurring Postgres-on-SQLite theme: malformed wrangler config, DNS-rebinding SSRF, startsWith origin/path bypasses, fail-open LLM judges, invalid Hono APIs, .getTime() on string columns, a ConcurrencyDO with no fetch() handler, base64 stack overflow, zero-token billing rejection, orphaned Vectorize vectors, Promise.race timer leaks, r2_mounted state divergence, tool-result reordering breaking tool_use IDs, a SpaceDO ReferenceError on the hottest path, a day-of-month-blind cron parser, non-transactional migrations, duplicate function signatures, R2 append races, unenumerable orphan DOs, portal cache cross-serving, dollar/cent ledger corruption, quota races, undefined ctx.manifest, mismatched postMessage origins, a CI eval gate that never gets a score, and double-quoted SQLite identifiers.</p>
</div>

<div id="G-O01" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O01</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§22 schema — wrangler.jsonc</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Malformed wrangler.jsonc — Orphaned Hyperdrive Remnant Breaks Config Parse</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The 'no Hyperdrive' block leaves a stray object `{ "binding": "SPACE_DO", "class_name": "SpaceDO", "localConnectionString": "postgresql://..." }` followed by `],` after a `//`-commented line, inside what should be valid JSONC. This is a dangling object/array with a DO binding that carries a Postgres connection string for a SQLite-backed DO. Wrangler fails to parse the config and the deploy never starts.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Remove the stray object and `],` entirely. SpaceDO is already declared correctly in the `durable_objects.bindings` array above — a Durable Object never has a `localConnectionString`. Delete the Hyperdrive remnant cleanly and validate wrangler.jsonc with `wrangler deploy --dry-run` in CI to catch config drift before it reaches production.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O02" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O02</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§19 security — handleApiCall SSRF</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">SSRF Filter Checks Hostname String, Not Resolved IP — DNS Rebinding Bypass</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">`handleApiCall()` runs `BLOCKED_IP_RANGES` regexes against `parsedUrl.hostname` and compares the hostname to literal `BLOCKED_HOSTS`. A DNS name like `internal.attacker.com` that resolves to `169.254.169.254` (CF metadata) or an RFC1918 address passes every check because the hostname is not an IP literal. There is no DNS resolution or post-connect IP validation, so the metadata-service / internal-range protection is defeated by any attacker-controlled hostname (classic DNS rebinding).</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Validate the actual resolved IP, not the textual hostname. Resolve the host (or use a connect-time IP check) and test the resolved address against the blocked ranges; reject IPv4-mapped IPv6 forms and re-validate on every redirect hop (a 302 to an internal IP bypasses a one-time check). Combine with an explicit per-space egress allowlist so only known-good destinations are reachable, matching the section's own stated intent.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O03" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O03</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§03 AgentDO — fetch() WebSocket origin guard</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">AgentDO WebSocket Origin Check Uses startsWith — Prefix-Spoof Bypass</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The AgentDO upgrade path validates origin with `allowed.some(a =&gt; origin.startsWith(a))` against `['https://app.naelao.dev','https://naelao.dev']`. `https://app.naelao.dev.attacker.com` satisfies `startsWith('https://app.naelao.dev')`, defeating the CVE-2026-25253-class mitigation. The router copy in §19 correctly uses exact matching, so this DO-level copy is inconsistent and strictly weaker — an attacker targets the weaker path.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Use exact equality against the allowlist (`allowed.includes(origin)`) to match the router implementation. Never use `startsWith` for origin validation — a suffix-attached attacker domain always passes. Factor origin validation into a single shared helper used by both the router and every DO upgrade path so the two can never diverge again.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O04" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O04</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§06 workers-sp / §09 mini-agi — LLM judge parse</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">LLM Judge Output Parsed as content[0].text — Fails Open on Privacy Gate</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">`proposeLearning`, `extractSkillCandidate`, the federated-learning privacy judge, and MiniAGI all do `JSON.parse(judgment.content[0].text ?? '{"safe": false}')`. This assumes `content[0]` is always a text block and the model returns bare JSON. With prompt caching, a leading prose sentence, or a `thinking`/`tool_use` block first, `content[0].text` is undefined and the parse silently defaults — for the privacy/safety gate this can misclassify unsafe data as safe (fail-open), the worst possible direction for a security control.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Find the first block where `type === 'text'`, strip code fences, and parse defensively. Treat any parse failure or missing text block as `safe:false` for privacy/safety judges (fail-closed), never as a silent skip. Prefer structured tool-use output (a forced JSON schema tool) over free-text JSON so the response shape is guaranteed.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O05" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O05</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§12 verticals — getAnnotationTask</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">R2-Based Annotation Task Lock Has Check-Then-Act Race — Double Annotation and Double Pay</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">`getAnnotationTask` does `list({prefix,limit:1})` → `get` → `put(in-progress)` → `delete(queue key)`. Two experts polling concurrently both `list` the same object, both `get` it, both `put` their own in-progress lock, and both `delete` the same queue key. The task is annotated twice and paid twice. R2 has no atomic move and `list` is eventually consistent, so the claim that this 'prevents double-annotation' is false.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Move task hand-out into a single-threaded DO that allocates task IDs atomically, or claim the in-progress key with an R2 conditional write (`onlyIf`/etag) and treat a precondition failure as 'already taken'. Never rely on list+get+delete ordering across concurrent callers for mutual exclusion.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O06" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O06</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 assistants — executeAgentLoop call site</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">executeAgentLoop Called With Wrong Arguments and a Return It Never Produces</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">In §03, `executeAgentLoop(sessionId, startMsgId, fiberCtx)` returns `void` and takes a string `startMsgId`. In §05 it is invoked as `const response = await this.executeAgentLoop(sessionId!, null, ctx)` — passing `null` for `startMsgId` (later used in an INSERT `parent_id`) and assigning a `response` that the function never returns. The code then calls `sendToChannel(channel, response, meta)`, sending `undefined` to the channel.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Give `executeAgentLoop` a defined return contract (the final assistant message) and a nullable `startMsgId` with explicit handling, or fetch the last assistant message from SQLite after the loop completes. Never send the `undefined` return value to the channel — validate the payload before dispatch.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O07" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O07</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§16 skills / §18 artifacts — BLOCKED_PATTERNS</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Stateful Global Regexes (g flag) Race Across Concurrent .test() Scans</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">`BLOCKED_PATTERNS` use the global `g` flag and are iterated with `check.pattern.test(content)` then `check.pattern.lastIndex = 0`. Global-flag `RegExp.test` advances `lastIndex`, so reusing the shared regex object across concurrent scans (multiple skills loaded in parallel via `Promise.all` in `loadSkillsForAgent`) yields nondeterministic matches — a malicious skill can slip through when `lastIndex` is mid-string from a concurrent scan. The reset is also skipped on the early-return path.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Drop the `g` flag for membership tests (a non-global regex's `.test()` is stateless), or compile a fresh `new RegExp(pattern.source, flags)` per scan call. Never share global-flag regex objects across concurrent `.test()` invocations — the shared `lastIndex` is a correctness and security hazard.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O08" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O08</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§18 artifacts — wrapArtifactCode</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">HTML Artifact Wrapper Escapes Backticks But Not ${} — Template-Literal Injection</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">For `type === 'html'`, the harness builds `const html = `${code.replace(/`/g,'\`')}`;` — it escapes backticks but not `${...}` sequences. Agent- or attacker-supplied HTML containing `${...}` is interpreted as a template-literal substitution inside the generated Worker source, allowing arbitrary expression execution in the artifact isolate (and breaking benign content that legitimately contains `${`). The security scanner runs on raw code but does not neutralize template-literal metacharacters.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Do not embed user HTML inside a template literal at all. Pass it as a base64- or JSON-encoded string constant (`const html = atob("...")` or `const html = JSON.parse("...")`) so no `${}` substitution or backtick parsing can occur, then serve it. Encoding sidesteps the entire class of template-literal injection.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O09" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O09</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 assistants — handleHeartbeat</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Heartbeat Written to storage.put() But Sweeper Queries SQL — Liveness Never Detected</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">`handleHeartbeat` writes `await this.ctx.storage.put('last_heartbeat', Date.now())` (the KV-style DO storage namespace), while the sweeper and §02 spec detect stale runs by querying SQLite for `heartbeat_age &gt; 10 minutes`. The timestamp lands in the `put()` namespace but the failure-detection logic reads SQL, so stale-run detection never observes the heartbeat. There is also no SQL `last_heartbeat` column on the fibers/runs table for the sweeper query to read.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Standardize liveness on one store. Persist `last_heartbeat` (and per-fiber heartbeats) into a SQLite column the sweeper actually queries, or change the sweeper to read `storage.get('last_heartbeat')`. Do not split liveness state across `put()` and `sql` — the two namespaces are invisible to each other.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O10" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O10</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#10B981;background:#10B98118;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">LOW</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§09 mini-agi — query partial-access path</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">MiniAGI Denied-Domain Names Disclosed to Unauthorized Users</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">When access is partial, `MiniAGIDO.query` injects `deniedDomains.join(', ')` of domain IDs into the LLM prompt and tells the user which domains they could not access. For confidential domains this discloses the existence and names of restricted data domains to users who have no authorization for them — a metadata leak that the access-control system exists to prevent. An attacker enumerates the platform's sensitive domain taxonomy simply by asking broad questions.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">For denied domains, reveal only a generic 'some data was not accessible for this request' without enumerating confidential domain identifiers to non-authorized roles. Log the specific denied domains server-side for audit, but never echo restricted domain names into a prompt or user-facing response.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O11" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O11</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#10B981;background:#10B98118;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">LOW</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§10 spaces — middleware.ts cookie scoping</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Non-httpOnly Space Cookies Are Client-Writable — Cross-Space Access Vector</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The middleware sets `naelao_space_id` and `naelao_root_space_id` with `httpOnly:false` 'so frontend JS can read them', and the prose states 'every API call uses the space_id cookie to determine space context'. Because the cookie is JS-readable and writable, a user can set `naelao_space_id` to a sub-space they don't belong to; any Worker that trusts the cookie for scoping (rather than re-deriving from the validated JWT membership) grants cross-space access.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Treat the cookie as a non-authoritative UI hint only. Always resolve the effective spaceId from the JWT / naelao-auth membership check server-side, and reject any request whose cookie spaceId is not an authorized space for the authenticated user. Never make an access-control decision from a client-writable cookie.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O12" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O12</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§27 naelao-threads — Hono API usage</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Invalid Hono APIs — c.req.cookie?.() and c.param() Are Not Real Methods</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The threads middleware reads `c.req.cookie?.('naelao_space_id')` and handlers call `c.param('id')`. Hono exposes cookies via `getCookie(c, name)` from `hono/cookie`, not `c.req.cookie()`, and route params via `c.req.param('id')`, not `c.param('id')`. The optional chaining on `c.req.cookie?.()` silently masks the missing method (always undefined), so space context is never read; `c.param('id')` throws or returns undefined.</p></div>
