  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">In `GET /threads`, the cursor is built as `threads[threads.length-1].created_at.getTime().toString()`. SQLite returns `created_at` as a number or ISO string, never a JS `Date` object, so `.getTime` is not a function. Every paginated response throws the moment `hasNextPage` is true — pagination is broken for any thread list exceeding one page.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Store `created_at` as INTEGER milliseconds and use it directly as the cursor, or wrap with `new Date(row.created_at).getTime()` when the column is an ISO string. Make the column type and the cursor logic consistent, and add a test that fetches a second page.</p></div>
