    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§90 WorkflowRegistryDO — parseCronToNextTimestamp</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Cron Parser Skips Day-of-Month and Mishandles Ranges/Steps — Wrong/Duplicate Schedules</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">`const [min, hour, , month, dow] = cron.split(' ')` deliberately discards the 3rd field (day-of-month), so `0 9 1 * *` (09:00 on the 1st) fires every day. It also supports only `*` or a single integer per field — `*/5`, `1-5`, and `1,15` are not handled, so `'*/5 * * * *'` does `parseInt('*/5') → NaN`, never matches, and falls back to a 24h default. Month matching compares `getUTCMonth()+1` against a possibly-NaN value.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Use a real cron parser that supports all five fields plus lists, ranges, and steps, and honors day-of-month. At minimum, parse the day-of-month field and treat any unparseable field as a hard error rather than silently degrading to daily/24h. Add tests for `*/5`, day-of-month, and weekday expressions.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O30" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
