    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O30</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§76 SpaceTypeRegistryDO — /migrate</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Major Migration Runs ALTER TABLE + R2 Move Non-Transactionally and Returns success Unconditionally</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The migration Worker fires `ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN` then an R2 `/move` as two independent `fetch` calls and returns `{success:true}` regardless of either outcome — neither response's `ok` is checked. If the R2 move fails after the ALTER succeeds, the schema is half-migrated with no compensation; re-running double-applies. No `fromVersion` guard is persisted, so a retried opt-in re-runs the ALTER.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Check each response's `ok` and fail loudly on partial completion. Make the column add idempotent (it uses `ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS`, but the R2 move is not idempotent — guard it). Record completion in a `schema_migrations` table keyed by `(spaceId, toVersion)` and refuse to re-run a completed migration. Surface partial failure instead of returning unconditional success.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O31" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O31</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§80 naelao-eval — evaluateOutput</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Two evaluateOutput Definitions With Incompatible Signatures — Extended Scorers Get Wrong Arguments</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The section defines `evaluateOutput(actual, expected, type, env)` and later, under 'Extended Scorer Types', redefines `evaluateOutput(actual, testCase, env, spaceId)` with an incompatible parameter list. The caller in `runEvaluationSuite` uses the 4-arg `(actualOutput, testCase.expected, scorerType, env)` form, so the extended scorers (`file-exists`, `json-field`, `numeric-within`) that need `testCase` and `spaceId` receive `scorerType` and `env` in the wrong slots and silently misbehave. The llm-judge scorer also does an unguarded `JSON.parse(judgement.content[0].text)`.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Consolidate to a single `evaluateOutput` signature and update the call site to pass `testCase` and `spaceId` so extended scorers get their required arguments. Wrap the judge parse in try/catch returning `{passed:false}` on malformed output.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O32" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O32</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§81 naelao-categorizer — analyzeFailure</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">feedback-log.jsonl Appended via R2 Read-Modify-Write — Concurrent Feedback Lost, Truncate Races Writers</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">`analyzeFailure` appends to `feedback-log.jsonl` by `get` then `put(existing + entry)` — a read-modify-write on R2 with no concurrency control. R2 has no append, so concurrent negative-feedback events lose entries (last write wins). The daily review then `put(logKey, '')` to clear the log, racing with in-flight appends and silently dropping feedback that arrived during the review.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Write each feedback entry as its own R2 object (`feedback/{ts}-{uuid}.json`) and `list()` them at review time, or route appends through a per-agent DO that serializes writes. Never truncate a shared mutable object that concurrent writers are appending to — archive-then-delete the individual objects instead.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O33" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O33</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§80 naelao-eval — temporary AgentDOs</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Eval Creates AgentDOs by Random idFromName and Never Destroys Them — Unenumerable Orphans</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Each test case creates `env.NAELAO_AGENT_DO.idFromName('${spaceId}:eval-project:${testThreadId}')` with a fresh UUID and runs it, but nothing calls `/destroy` afterward. The claim that 'naelao-sweeper deletes these temporary DO instances' is unworkable: DOs addressed by random `idFromName` leave no enumerable index, so a sweeper has no way to find them. Their SQLite storage persists and bills indefinitely.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Record each eval DO ID in a list/table and explicitly POST `/destroy` (which deletes the DO's storage) after the run, or reuse a single eval DO with table-per-case isolation. A sweeper cannot enumerate DOs it has no record of — cleanup must be driven by an index the eval runner maintains.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O34" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O34</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§A6 external-portals — portal router cache</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Portal Router Caches Before Checking Method — Non-GET Responses Cached and Cross-Served</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The portal router consults `caches.default.match` before any method check and populates the cache for any response whose `Cache-Control` includes `max-age`, keyed on `route.spaceId + pathname` with the query string dropped. A cacheable response to one user's POST or authenticated request can be served to another user, and distinct query strings collide on one cache entry. There is also no WebSocket-upgrade bypass.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Only consult and populate the cache for GET requests, include the full URL (query string) in the cache key, and add `Vary`/auth awareness so authenticated responses are never shared. Detect `Upgrade: websocket` and bypass cache/dispatch for live connections.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O35" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O35</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§A6 external-portals — attachCustomDomain</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">attachCustomDomain Spreads a Host String as if It Were a PortalRoute — Empty Route Written</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">`provisionExternalPortal` writes `portal:space:${spaceId}` = a bare `platformHost` string. `attachCustomDomain` then reads that key with `get(..., 'json')`, casts the result to `PortalRoute`, and spreads `{...existingRoute}` — but the key holds a plain string, not JSON, so the parse yields null and the spread produces a route missing `spaceId`, `enabled`, and `scopedToken`. The router then 404s the custom domain or serves a route with no space context.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Read the actual route object from `portal:host:${platformHost}` (which stores the JSON `PortalRoute`), spread that, and write it under the custom hostname. Keep `portal:space:` as a pointer-only key and never spread it as a route object.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O36" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O36</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§87 naelao-billing — /community/split</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Community Revenue Split Mixes Dollars and Cents — Ledger Amounts Corrupted</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">In `/community/split`, `amount` is treated as dollars for `creatorCents = Math.floor(amount * 0.80 * 100)`, but the ledger insert stores `gross_amount_usd = amount/100` and `platform_amount_usd = (amount - creatorCents)/100` — mixing dollars (`amount`) with cents (`creatorCents`). Gross is recorded 100× too small, and the platform amount subtracts cents from dollars, yielding a large negative number. The revenue ledger is systematically corrupt.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Pick one unit and use it consistently. If `amount` is USD: `creatorUsd = amount*0.8`, `platformUsd = amount*0.2`, store those directly, and convert to cents only for the Stripe transfer field (`Math.round(amount*0.8*100)`). Add an invariant test that gross == creator + platform.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O37" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O37</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§88 naelao-spaces — quota enforcement</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Sub-Space / Member Quota Is Check-Then-Insert in the Worker — Race Exceeds Plan Limits</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">`/spaces` and `/members/invite` read `SELECT COUNT(*) ... WHERE parent_space_id=?` (or a member count), compare to the plan quota, then later `INSERT`. Between the count and the insert, concurrent requests both pass the check and both insert, exceeding `maxSubSpaces`/`maxMembers`. The §88 narrative claims the DO 'prevents concurrent provisioning from racing past the limit', but the shown code does the count in the Worker against the DB, not atomically in a DO.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Enforce the limit inside SpaceDO with `blockConcurrencyWhile` so the count and insert run in one serialized method, or use a conditional `INSERT ... WHERE (SELECT COUNT(*)...) &lt; limit` backed by a unique/trigger guard. A Worker-side count-then-insert cannot be made race-safe across concurrent requests.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O38" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O38</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§75 remoteloop — dispatchRemoteLoop</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">dispatchRemoteLoop Reads ctx.manifest That Was Never Assigned — TypeError on Delegation</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">`dispatchTurn` resolves `const manifest = await ctx.spaceTypeRegistry.getManifest(ctx.spaceTypeId)` into a local variable and passes `manifest.customLoopWorker` onward, but inside `dispatchRemoteLoop` the token issuance reads `ctx.manifest.agentEnvironment.agentPermissions` and the request body reads `ctx.toolManifest` — neither of which was ever assigned on `ctx`. `ctx.manifest` is undefined, so reading `.agentEnvironment` throws.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Pass the resolved `manifest` into `dispatchRemoteLoop` as an argument (or assign `ctx.manifest = manifest` before delegating) and read permissions/tools from that object. Ensure `ctx.toolManifest` is populated before the remote loop is dispatched.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O39" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O39</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§A7 operator-shell — postMessage origins</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Shell and Panel postMessage Origin Constants Disagree — Cross-Frame Messages Silently Dropped</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The app panel pins `shellOrigin = 'https://app.naelao.dev'` and only accepts messages whose `e.origin` matches it, while the shell listener accepts only `event.origin === 'https://ui.naelao.app'`, and the prose says panels run on `ui.naelao.app`. The two sides whitelist different origins, so at least one direction of CONTEXT/THEME messaging is silently dropped — panels never receive context, or the shell ignores panel events, with no error surfaced.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Define exactly one shell-origin constant and one panel-origin constant, shared by both sides, and verify the shell actually sends from the origin the panel whitelists (and vice versa). Reconcile `app.naelao.dev` vs `ui.naelao.app` everywhere so the allowlists are provably symmetric.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O40" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O40</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§80 naelao-eval — /eval/run CI gate</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">/eval/run Returns Immediately via waitUntil But CI Expects a Synchronous Score</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">`app.post('/eval/run')` fires `c.executionCtx.waitUntil(runEvaluationSuite(...))` and returns `{status:'started'}` with no `score`, ignoring the request's `waitForCompletion:true` and `timeoutSeconds` flags. The GitHub Actions gate does `SCORE=$(echo $RESULT | jq '.score')` and compares `null &lt; 70` — so the score is always null and the deploy gate either always blocks or errors on `bc`.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Honor `waitForCompletion`: when set, `await runEvaluationSuite()` (within Worker time limits) and return the final `score`; otherwise expose an `/eval/status` endpoint for CI to poll until the run completes. Do not advertise a flag the handler silently drops — the deploy gate depends on a real score.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-O41" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
  <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-O41</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#10B981;background:#10B98118;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">LOW</span>
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§83 WorkflowRegistryDO — sweepSchedules SQL</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">WHERE status = "active" Uses Double Quotes — Identifier, Not String Literal, in SQLite</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The §83 `sweepSchedules` query is `SELECT * FROM workflows WHERE status = "active" AND schedule IS NOT NULL AND next_run &lt;= ?`. In SQLite, double quotes denote an identifier (a column name), not a string literal. `"active"` is interpreted as a column reference and only resolves to the string by SQLite's legacy fallback quirk — fragile, and it breaks outright under strict/`PRAGMA` modes. The §90 complete version correctly uses single quotes.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Use single quotes for the string literal: `WHERE status = 'active'`. Standardize on single-quoted string literals throughout all DO SQL, and enable strict-quoting in tests so the identifier-vs-literal confusion is caught rather than silently tolerated.</p></div>
</div>

<div style="margin:32px 0 0;padding:32px;background:#0A1628;border:1px solid #1E293B;border-radius:8px;">
  <p style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;color:#64748B;margin:0;line-height:1.8;">
    Appendix O complete &mdash; seventh pass, full three-band spec re-audit. Combined gap audit total: A (125) + B (30) + C (22) + D (22) + E (24) + F (26) + G (26) + H (15) + I (34) + J (20) + K (51) + L (23) + M (31) + N (24) + O (41)
    = <strong style="color:var(--or);">514 documented technical gaps</strong> with required fixes.
  </p>
</div>
