  <div id="G-P23" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-P23</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§assistants — AssistantDO.handleChannelMessage()</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">handleChannelMessage Session Scoped to Channel Type Only — Cross-User Conversation Bleed</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">SELECT id FROM sessions WHERE channel = ? finds a session by channel type alone (e.g., 'slack'). All users who message via Slack share the same session row, causing every user's messages to be concatenated into one conversation context. User B's question is answered in the context of User A's entire session history — a privacy violation and a source of incorrect LLM responses for every user after the first.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Scope the session query by both channel and user: SELECT id FROM sessions WHERE channel = ? AND user_id = ?. Extract user_id from the incoming message payload (Slack provides event.user; other providers have equivalent user identifiers). Ensure the sessions table has a UNIQUE constraint on (channel, user_id) and an index on (channel, user_id) for efficient lookup.</p></div>
  </div>
