<section id="appendix-r" style="padding:60px 0 40px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 24px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.15em;text-transform:uppercase;">Appendix R</span>
    <h2 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:36px;font-weight:700;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.02em;margin:8px 0 6px;">Tenth-Pass Full-Spectrum Technical Gap Analysis</h2>
    <p style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:15px;color:var(--tx);line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 40px;">35 new gaps covering: eval() prohibition breaking artifacts, float money storage, shell injection via LLM tool names, space isolation timing, DO non-storage I/O interleaving, GDPR ghost-vector deletion, space cleanup cascade, CF Workflows 1000-step limit, embedding model versioning, streaming billing on disconnect, slug race conditions, skill scanner obfuscation bypass, Hono body double-read, Workflow replay side effects, and CF platform limit gaps.</p>

  <div id="G-R01" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R01</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§artifact / dynamic-workers — Artifact execution engine</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Dynamic TypeScript eval() Prohibited in CF Workers — Artifacts Feature Is Non-Functional</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec describes an Artifacts system where agents write TypeScript to R2 and execute it at runtime. CF Workers' V8 isolate explicitly prohibits runtime code generation from strings: eval(), new Function(string), and dynamic import() of data-URI strings all throw "Code generation from strings disallowed" at runtime, regardless of Content Security Policy headers. Any implementation that loads TypeScript source from R2 and attempts to execute it via eval() or new Function() will fail 100% of the time. The entire artifact execution feature as described is non-functional on the CF Workers runtime.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Use CF Workers for Platforms (Dispatch Namespace) to execute agent-written code. Workers for Platforms allows deploying arbitrary user-written Workers into a namespace at runtime via the API and invoking them via a dispatch Worker: const ns = env.DISPATCH_NAMESPACE; const worker = ns.get(workerId); return worker.fetch(request). Alternatively, restrict agent-generated logic to a JSON-based DSL (step definitions, conditional branches, tool call sequences) interpreted by a fixed platform Worker with no dynamic code execution. Never pass agent-generated strings to eval() or new Function().</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R02" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R02</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-billing / §schema — SpaceDO / BillingDO</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Money Stored as REAL (Float) in SQLite — Rounding Errors Compound Over Millions of Transactions</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">SpaceDO's SQLite schema declares balance_usd REAL, amount_usd REAL, and credits_limit REAL. JavaScript and SQLite IEEE 754 doubles cannot represent all decimal fractions exactly: 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.30000000000000004. Billing calculations like (inputTokens / 1_000_000) * 3.00 propagate rounding error into every stored balance. Over millions of agent runs, rounding errors accumulate into unaccounted revenue in the tens-of-thousands-of-dollars range. Additionally, the code path that calculates remaining credits uses Infinity cast to number when credits_limit is null — Infinity - positive_number = Infinity, making every comparison against a budget nonsensical.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Store all monetary values as INTEGER in micro-cents (1 USD = 100,000,000 micro-cents) or as TEXT in exact decimal format: ALTER TABLE spaces CHANGE balance_usd balance_microcents INTEGER DEFAULT 0. Perform all arithmetic in integer micro-cents: const costMicrocents = Math.round((inputTokens * MODEL_PRICE_PER_TOKEN_MICROCENTS)); then use SQLite integer arithmetic. Never store financial values as REAL/float. Replace the Infinity fallback with a large sentinel integer (e.g., 9_007_199_254_740_991 for "unlimited") and document what the sentinel means.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R03" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R03</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§27 — execution-ladder / §sandbox-do — SandboxDO.exec()</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Shell Injection via LLM-Supplied Tool Name — Unsanitized Command Passed Directly to exec()</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's SandboxDO routes tool calls by using the LLM-produced toolName directly as part of the shell command dispatched to the execution environment. There is no whitelist check between "LLM selects a tool name" and "that name becomes a shell argument." A prompt-injection attack can cause the LLM to produce toolName values like "ls; rm -rf /workspace" or tool argument strings that embed shell metacharacters. Even if the sandbox is isolated, the agent can destroy its own workspace, exfiltrate file contents via DNS channels, or exhaust disk quota — all through tool name / argument injection.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Whitelist every tool name against a hardcoded registry before dispatch: const ALLOWED_TOOLS = new Set(["read_file","write_file","list_directory","bash","python"]); if (!ALLOWED_TOOLS.has(toolName)) throw new Error(`Unknown tool: ${toolName}`). For bash/python execution, pass code as a stdin pipe rather than shell argument: exec({cmd: ["python3"], stdin: codeFromLLM}) so that the code string is never interpolated into a shell command. For file path arguments, normalize with path.posix.normalize() and assert the result starts with /workspace/ before use. Treat all LLM-generated strings as untrusted user input.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R04" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R04</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-agent-do / §naelao-threads-impl — NaelaoAgentDO.run()</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Space Isolation Check Fires After Database Writes — Attacker Records Session Before Receiving 403</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">NaelaoAgentDO.run() validates that the incoming request's spaceId matches this.spaceId only after creating a session row and inserting the user message into DO SQLite. An attacker who sends a cross-space request will have their message persisted, a session ID allocated, and token usage tentatively recorded — all before the 403 is returned. This means cross-space messages appear in audit logs as legitimate sessions, token allocations are charged to the wrong space's quota, and session IDs from other spaces are disclosed in error response bodies.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Move the space isolation check to the very first line of NaelaoAgentDO.run() before any database operations: if (this.spaceId !== spaceId) return new Response("Forbidden", {status: 403}). Also remove spaceId from the error response body (do not disclose what spaceId the DO was actually configured for). Additionally, add space_id to the sessions table WHERE clause on every query: SELECT id FROM sessions WHERE thread_id = ? AND space_id = ? — preventing a session from one space being used in another even if the check is bypassed.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R05" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R05</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-agent-do — AssistantDO / NaelaoAgentDO</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Non-Storage I/O (fetch, R2 writes) Inside DO Allows Request Interleaving — State Corruption</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">CF Durable Objects' input gate only protects storage operations (this.ctx.storage.*). Calls to fetch(), env.R2.put(), env.QUEUE.send(), and similar non-storage I/O do NOT block incoming requests during their execution. If AssistantDO issues a fetch() to an external LLM API (which may take 5-30 seconds), concurrent WebSocket messages or heartbeat requests can arrive and interleave — reading stale in-memory state (this.activeRuns, this.lastHeartbeat, etc.) while the first request awaits the network call. This causes: lost increments to in-memory counters, race conditions in the agentic loop state machine, and double-processing of scheduled jobs.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Two mitigations: (1) For mutable in-memory state accessed around await boundaries, use blockConcurrencyWhile() to serialize: await this.ctx.blockConcurrencyWhile(async () =&gt; { const state = readInMemoryState(); await fetch(externalAPI); writeInMemoryState(newState); }). This blocks all other requests until the critical section completes. (2) Eliminate reliance on mutable in-memory class fields for any state that must survive concurrent access — store it in DO SQLite (which IS protected by the input gate) and reload it around each await boundary. Document which class fields are safe to read across await (read-only, initialized once) vs. unsafe (mutated during request handling).</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R06" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R06</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-knowledge — Knowledge deletion handler</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Vectorize.deleteByIds() Never Called on Document Deletion — Ghost Vectors Accumulate, GDPR Violation</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">When a knowledge document is deleted via the DELETE /knowledge/documents/:id endpoint, the handler removes rows from the DO SQLite knowledge_chunks table but never calls env.NAELAO_VECTORIZE.deleteByIds(chunkVectorIds). The deleted document's embedding vectors remain indexed in Vectorize indefinitely. This causes: (1) semantic search returns results from documents the user has explicitly deleted — a correctness failure and a GDPR Article 17 (Right to Erasure) violation, (2) the Vectorize index grows without bound and costs accumulate for vectors that serve no purpose, (3) a content moderation scenario where a user deletes harmful content but it continues surfacing in search.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Before executing the DELETE SQL, query all vector IDs for the document: const chunks = [...this.ctx.storage.sql.exec("SELECT vector_id FROM knowledge_chunks WHERE document_id = ?", docId)]; const vectorIds = chunks.map(r =&gt; r.vector_id).filter(Boolean). Then atomically delete from both: await Promise.all([this.ctx.storage.sql.exec("DELETE FROM knowledge_chunks WHERE document_id = ?", docId), env.NAELAO_VECTORIZE.deleteByIds(vectorIds)]). Add a nightly reconciliation CF Workflow that queries all vector IDs in Vectorize metadata and cross-references with knowledge_chunks to find and delete orphans from any missed deletions.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R07" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R07</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-spaces-full — Space deletion endpoint</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Space Deletion Leaves R2, Vectorize, and KV Orphans — No Cascade Cleanup, Unbounded Storage Cost</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The space deletion endpoint removes the SpaceDO and its SQLite records but does not clean up: R2 objects under spaces/{spaceId}/ (workspace files, skill archives, session exports), Vectorize vectors with metadata.space_id = spaceId, KV entries prefixed with space:{spaceId}:*, or nested sub-space DOs. For a space with 10,000 workspace files and a rich knowledge base, this leaves gigabytes of R2 storage billing indefinitely post-deletion. R2 charges $0.015/GB/month — 100 deleted spaces each with 5 GB of orphaned files = $7,500/month in phantom charges. GDPR Article 17 requires data erasure.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement deletion as a CF Workflow to handle the multi-step, long-running cleanup: step 1: list and delete R2 objects with cursor pagination (do { page = await env.R2.list({prefix: `spaces/${spaceId}/`, cursor}); await Promise.all(page.objects.map(o =&gt; env.R2.delete(o.key))); cursor = page.cursor; } while (page.truncated)), step 2: env.NAELAO_VECTORIZE.deleteByMetadata({filter: {space_id: {$eq: spaceId}}}), step 3: scan and delete KV entries (list with prefix), step 4: mark SpaceDO as deleted (not hard-delete immediately — wait for Workflow completion). Add a space_deletion_status SQLite table tracking workflow progress.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R08" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R08</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§workflows — CF Workflows / runFiber()</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">CF Workflows 1,000 Step Hard Limit — Long-Running Agent Tasks Silently Terminate Mid-Run</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">CF Workflows enforces a hard limit of 1,000 steps per workflow instance. A research agent running an autonomous task loop with 10 tool calls per iteration × 150 iterations = 1,500 steps will be silently terminated at step 1,000 with no error propagated to the user and no continuation. The spec describes fibers running until task completion with no mention of the step limit, no checkpoint strategy, and no continuation workflow pattern. Agents that hit this limit appear to hang — the fiber status goes to "failed" but the user was never told the task was truncated.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement checkpoint-and-continue for long-running fibers. Track step_count within the workflow. At step 800 (80% of limit): step.do("checkpoint", async () =&gt; { const state = gatherCurrentState(); await env.R2.put(`fibers/${fiberId}/checkpoint.json`, JSON.stringify(state)); return {checkpointed: true, state} }). Then await step.sleep(100) and spawn a continuation workflow: const next = await env.FIBER_WORKFLOW.create({params: {fiberId, resumeFromCheckpoint: true}}). The continuation workflow loads the checkpoint and continues. This creates a transparent chain of workflow instances from the user's perspective while staying within the 1,000 step limit per instance.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R09" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R09</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-knowledge — knowledge_chunks schema</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Embedding Model Version Not Tracked — Model Upgrade Silently Invalidates All Existing Vectors</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The knowledge_chunks table stores chunk text and vector_id but no embedding_model field. Vectorize vectors embed documents in a specific model's vector space (e.g., @cf/baai/bge-base-en-v1.5 produces 768-dimensional vectors). When the platform upgrades the embedding model (to a different dimension count or different model family), new query embeddings live in a different vector space from existing indexed vectors. Cosine similarity between a new query vector and an old indexed vector becomes essentially random noise. The knowledge search feature silently degrades to returning random results with no error.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add embedding_model TEXT NOT NULL to the knowledge_chunks DDL and to the Vectorize vector metadata: await env.NAELAO_VECTORIZE.insert([{id: chunkId, values: embedding, metadata: {space_id, doc_id, model: EMBEDDING_MODEL}}]). On knowledge search, verify the query uses the same model as indexed chunks: const models = [...new Set(chunks.map(c =&gt; c.embedding_model))]; if (models.some(m =&gt; m !== CURRENT_MODEL)) throw new Error("Knowledge index needs re-embedding; model has changed"). Implement a re-embedding Workflow triggered on model upgrades: iterate all chunks, re-embed with new model, deleteByIds old, insert new vectors with updated model metadata.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R10" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R10</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-threads-impl / §ai-gateway — createSSEStream / token billing</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Token Billing Skipped When Streaming Client Disconnects — Free Inference via Intentional Disconnect</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec records token usage only when the streaming response reaches its final SSE chunk ([DONE] marker). If a client intentionally disconnects after receiving the first streamed token, the LLM continues generating to completion (the server side does not know the client disconnected until it tries to write to the closed socket) but the final billing event is never written. An attacker can receive the first sentence of a response, disconnect, and pay zero tokens. With a 100K-token context window, a single "free" run costs $0.30-$3.00 in unrecorded inference cost.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Pre-deduct an estimated credit before streaming starts, then reconcile after the stream ends regardless of client connection state. Pattern: (1) const estimatedCost = estimateTokens(messages) * COST_PER_TOKEN; await deductCredits(spaceId, estimatedCost, {provisional: true}), (2) start streaming via ctx.waitUntil() so the stream continues even if the Response has been sent, (3) after stream completion (or AbortError from disconnected client), record actual token counts and refund the difference: await reconcileCredits(spaceId, estimatedCost, actualCost). The key is using ctx.waitUntil() to keep the token recording alive after the Response returns, and using a provisional charge that prevents double-spending.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R11" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R11</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-agent-do — NaelaoAgentDO session queries</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Session Queries Lack space_id Predicate — Cross-Space Session Hijacking</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Multiple DO SQLite queries in NaelaoAgentDO look up sessions by thread_id alone: SELECT id FROM sessions WHERE thread_id = ?. The returned session is used to authorize subsequent operations (appending messages, running tools, accessing memory). An attacker who discovers a valid thread_id from one space can send a request to a different space's AgentDO and receive that thread's session, gaining access to another space's conversation history, tool execution context, and memory. The DO sharding means space B's AgentDO holds thread data for space B — but if a session lookup doesn't validate space_id, a crafted request can land on the correct DO with a foreign thread_id.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add space_id to every session lookup and all child table queries: SELECT * FROM sessions WHERE thread_id = ? AND space_id = ?. The space_id should be stored in the sessions row at insert time (INSERT INTO sessions (thread_id, space_id, ...) VALUES (?, ?, ...)) and validated on every subsequent access. Additionally, add a foreign-key-like application-level assertion: after loading the session, assert session.space_id === this.spaceId before proceeding with any operation. This is defense-in-depth in addition to the early-check fix from G-R04.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R12" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R12</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-spaces-full / §naelao-admin — Product conversation access flag</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Conversation Log Access Flag Settable Without Admin Approval — GDPR Data Processing Bypass</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's product PATCH endpoint allows builders to set conversationLogAccess: true on their product, enabling them to read user conversation histories. The endpoint does not verify that conversationAccessApprovedBy is populated with a valid Naelao admin ID. Any builder can enable this flag immediately via API, reading user private conversations without the human review process that data protection laws (GDPR Article 6, CCPA) require before enabling conversation data processing. This is a consent and authorization bypass, not a theoretical privacy risk.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">In the product PATCH handler, if the request includes conversationLogAccess: true: (1) verify the requesting user is a Naelao platform admin (check against a naelao_admins KV list or a platform-admin JWT claim), (2) if not admin, return 403 "Conversation log access requires Naelao admin approval"; (3) if admin, set both conversationLogAccess = true AND conversationAccessApprovedBy = adminId AND conversationAccessApprovedAt = now(). Only the naelao-admin Worker should be able to set this flag. Regular builders should submit a request that creates a pending approval record, not directly toggle the flag. Audit all toggles to Analytics Engine.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R13" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R13</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — space-assistants — buildMemoryContext()</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Memory Context Silently Capped at 20 Items — Oldest Memories Discarded Without Archival</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's buildMemoryContext() implementation calls memory.slice(0, 20) to limit the context injection to 20 memory items. This is a silent, hard cap with no configuration, no notification to the agent that memories were truncated, and no archival of the discarded entries. An agent that has learned 50 important facts about a user's project will have 30 of them silently dropped from its context on every turn. The agent will then make decisions contradicting knowledge it demonstrably learned, with no indication of why.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace the hard slice with a token-budget-aware selection: (1) order memories by relevance score: confidence * exp(-decay_rate * age_days), (2) select memories greedily until token budget is exhausted (MAX_MEMORY_TOKENS = 4096), (3) append a count of omitted memories at the end of the memory block: "[...38 additional memories not shown due to context budget]". Archive memories that fall below relevance threshold rather than discarding: INSERT INTO archived_memory SELECT * FROM memory WHERE score &lt; 0.1. Expose an un-archive command so the agent can request archived memory on demand via the memory tool.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R14" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R14</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§16 — skills — SkillLoader.scanSkillContent()</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">27-Pattern Skill Scanner Bypassed by Obfuscation — AST Analysis Required for Untrusted Skills</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The skill scanner checks SKILL.md scripts against 27 regex patterns (process.env., require(, eval(, etc.) to detect dangerous code. This regex approach is trivially bypassed: process["en"+"v"], globalThis["proc"+"ess"].env, atob("cHJvY2Vzcy5lbnY="), Array.prototype.constructor.constructor("return process")(), and dozens of other obfuscation patterns pass all 27 checks. Tier-3 agent-created skills can use any of these to exfiltrate environment secrets, call external APIs, or escape the sandbox. The scanner provides a false sense of security that may cause operators to disable sandboxing for "verified" skills.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace regex scanning with AST-based static analysis for tier-2 and tier-3 skills: parse the script using a JavaScript/TypeScript parser (e.g., @typescript-eslint/parser available as a CF Worker bundle) and walk the AST to detect: MemberExpression on process/globalThis/eval, CallExpression for eval/Function/require/import(), string concatenation into member access (dynamic property access with computed keys). For tier-3 (agent-created) skills, mandatory SandboxDO execution regardless of scan result — treat as always-untrusted. For tier-1 platform skills, keep the regex scanner as a fast pre-check but require SHA-256 signature verification as the primary trust anchor.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R15" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R15</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-credentials / §naelao-triggers — Webhook credential storage</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Webhook Secrets Have No Rotation Mechanism — Compromised Secret Permanently Valid</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Webhook signing secrets stored in naelao-credentials have no expiry, no rotation API, and no revocation mechanism. If a webhook secret is exposed (logged in a request body, leaked in a browser network tab, found in a git history), an attacker can forge signed webhook events indefinitely. There is no 90-day automatic rotation, no "rotate now" endpoint for space admins, and no grace period for dual-valid secrets during rotation. This applies to all external webhook sources: Stripe, Slack, GitHub, Linear, and custom providers.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add webhook_secret_rotated_at TIMESTAMP and webhook_secret_previous TEXT columns to the credentials schema. Implement rotation: (1) generate new secret, (2) store new secret as active, (3) store old secret in webhook_secret_previous with a 7-day grace period, (4) during verification accept either current or previous secret (if previous still within grace), (5) after 7 days, null out previous. Trigger automatic rotation at 90 days via a DO alarm in CredentialsDO. Add a POST /credentials/:id/rotate endpoint for manual rotation. Emit a rotation event to Analytics Engine and notify space admins via email when rotation occurs.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R16" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R16</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§space-type-versioning / §naelao-spaces-full — SpaceTypeRegistryDO.loadManifest()</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Space Type Manifest Loaded Without Signature — Missing signature Field Silently Ignored</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec describes SpaceTypeRegistryDO loading space type manifests that include configuration for allowed skills, billing caps, and execution permissions. The loading code calls verify(manifest.signature) but does not check whether the signature field is present before calling verify(). If a compromised SpaceTypeRegistryDO or a man-in-the-middle serves a manifest without a signature field, verify(undefined) returns true (or throws an uncaught TypeError that is silently swallowed) and the unsigned manifest is applied. An attacker controlling a single SpaceTypeRegistry can push malicious configuration to all spaces of that type.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add an explicit presence check before signature verification: if (!manifest.signature || typeof manifest.signature !== "string") { throw new Error("Manifest rejected: signature field missing or invalid"); }. Verify signature before accessing ANY other field of the manifest — a rejection must happen before the manifest content is read. Pin the verification public key in the Worker code (not in wrangler vars or KV) so it cannot be overwritten. Add a manifest version field and reject versions older than the minimum acceptable (prevents rollback attacks). Log all manifest loads with their signature hashes to Analytics Engine.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R17" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R17</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-spaces-full — Provisioner slug allocation</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Portal Slug KV Lock Uses 30-Second TTL — Concurrent Provisioning Race Allocates Duplicate Slugs</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Slug allocation during space provisioning uses a KV lock: env.PORTAL_ROUTES.put("slug-lock:" + slug, spaceId, {expirationTtl: 30}). KV writes have eventual consistency — two concurrent provisioning requests can both read "slug available" and both successfully write the lock before either sees the other's write. Result: two spaces share the same portal slug. The portal routing lookup returns the first matching space, making the second space's portal permanently unreachable. Since KV doesn't support compare-and-swap, the entire approach is broken under concurrent load.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Move slug allocation to a SlugRegistryDO with DO SQLite (strong consistency): CREATE TABLE slugs (slug TEXT PRIMARY KEY, space_id TEXT NOT NULL, reserved_at INTEGER NOT NULL). Allocation becomes: INSERT OR FAIL INTO slugs VALUES (?, ?, unixepoch("now")*1000). SQLite PRIMARY KEY enforces uniqueness; DO single-threaded execution prevents races. If INSERT fails (UNIQUE constraint), the slug is taken. The DO stub is keyed by a constant ID (e.g., "slug-registry-singleton") to ensure all slug allocations go through the same DO instance. Retain KV for slug→spaceId lookup cache (fast, eventual consistency is fine for reads — a slug owner never changes after allocation).</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R18" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R18</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-spaces-full — Product creation handler</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Product Slug Has No UNIQUE Constraint or Atomic Reservation — Concurrent Creates Produce Duplicates</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Product slug generation derives a slug from the product name (e.g., "My Product" → "my-product") in application code and inserts it into SpaceDO SQLite without a UNIQUE constraint on the slug column or an atomic reservation step. Two concurrent POST /products requests with the same product name will both compute "my-product", both check for availability (both see it as available), and both attempt INSERT — whichever finishes last wins but the first has already returned a 201 with the slug to the client. The client now has a slug that leads to the wrong product.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add CREATE UNIQUE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_products_slug ON products(slug) to the SpaceDO DDL (or UNIQUE in the column definition). This makes the second concurrent INSERT fail with a SQLite UNIQUE constraint error (error message contains "UNIQUE constraint failed"). Catch this specific error in the handler and return 409 Conflict with a message "Slug already in use — try a different product name". If the business requirement is to guarantee uniqueness with a suffix: attempt INSERT, on UNIQUE failure append -2, -3, etc. until success (all within the same DO request, leveraging DO single-threaded writes for atomicity).</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R19" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R19</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-spaces-full — SSO/OIDC config storage</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">OIDC Client Secret Tied to CF-Managed Master Key — Secret Rotation Impossible After Key Rotation</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">SSO configuration stores the OIDC client_secret encrypted with a platform master key (stored in CF Secrets Store). If Cloudflare rotates the Secrets Store master key (for compliance, incident response, or routine key hygiene), the encrypted client_secret becomes unreadable — the Space's OIDC login stops working with no warning. There is no re-encryption workflow, no backup of the plaintext secret, and no mechanism for the space admin to detect the failure until users start getting authentication errors.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement application-level key versioning independent of the CF Secrets Store master key: (1) generate a data encryption key (DEK) per space: const dek = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(32)), (2) store the DEK encrypted with the platform master key in SpaceDO SQLite: dek_encrypted BLOB, dek_version INTEGER, (3) encrypt all secrets with the DEK (AES-256-GCM), (4) to rotate: decrypt DEK with old master key, re-encrypt with new master key, update dek_encrypted — client_secret itself doesn't need to change. Add a CredentialHealthDO alarm that tests OIDC config decryptability weekly and alerts admins if decryption fails before users experience login failures.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R20" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R20</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-spaces-full — Space type/scenario management</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Sub-Space scenario_type Mutable After Creation — Space Type Contract Violated by PATCH</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec establishes immutable scenario types (agency_shell, workspace, marketplace, etc.) that govern permission models, billing, and capability sets. However, the space PATCH endpoint allows updating scenario_type without verifying immutability. A builder who starts with a "workspace" space and later changes it to "agency_shell" bypasses the approval flow required for agency-shell spaces, gains access to sub-space management APIs they should not have, and potentially violates billing tier contracts tied to space type. There is no CHECK constraint or application guard preventing this.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Make scenario_type immutable after creation. In the space PATCH handler: if (body.scenario_type &amp;&amp; body.scenario_type !== existingSpace.scenario_type) { return new Response("scenario_type is immutable after space creation", {status: 400}); }. Add a SQLite trigger as belt-and-suspenders: CREATE TRIGGER prevent_scenario_change BEFORE UPDATE ON spaces WHEN NEW.scenario_type != OLD.scenario_type BEGIN SELECT RAISE(ABORT, "scenario_type is immutable"); END. For legitimate type migrations (rare), provide a separate admin-only migration endpoint with audit logging and require explicit confirmation from a Naelao admin.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R21" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R21</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-threads-impl / §naelao-agent-do — Message listing queries</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Message Listing Queries Missing ORDER BY — Tool Results Arrive Out of Sequence in Agent Context</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Multiple message listing queries in the spec use SELECT * FROM messages WHERE thread_id = ? or SELECT * FROM messages WHERE thread_id = ? LIMIT ? OFFSET ? without any ORDER BY clause. SQLite's row return order without ORDER BY is undefined — it may return rows in insertion order, in rowid order, or in any order depending on internal page layout, DELETE history, and update operations. In an agent context where message ordering is semantically critical (tool result must follow the tool call that preceded it), arbitrary ordering means the LLM receives a corrupted context: tool results appear before tool calls, user messages appear after assistant responses. The agent produces non-sequitur replies.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add ORDER BY created_at ASC, id ASC to every message listing query. For cursor-based pagination (WHERE id &gt; ? LIMIT ?), the ORDER BY is especially critical: ORDER BY id ASC ensures the cursor correctly picks up from the last-seen message. Add a composite covering index: CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_messages_thread_time ON messages(thread_id, created_at ASC, id ASC). This index satisfies both the WHERE predicate and the ORDER BY in a single B-tree traversal with no sort step. Also apply this fix to all context reconstruction queries in AssistantDO and NaelaoAgentDO where thread messages are loaded for LLM context.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R22" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R22</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-threads-impl — POST /threads/:id/messages handler</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No Message Content Size Limit — Unbounded User Input Causes DO OOM on Context Reconstruction</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The POST /messages endpoint calls await c.req.json() with no size limit enforcement before inserting message content into DO SQLite. A user can POST a message with a 10 MB content string. When NaelaoAgentDO reconstructs the full thread context (SELECT * FROM messages WHERE thread_id = ?), it loads all messages into memory simultaneously. Three 5 MB messages = 15 MB of in-memory string data before even considering token serialization. CF Workers have a 128 MB memory limit — large threads with oversized messages trigger OOM crashes mid-agent-run, losing the in-progress response and leaving the thread in an inconsistent state.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Enforce a message size limit at the POST /messages handler: const MAX_CONTENT_BYTES = 131_072; // 128 KB. const body = await c.req.json(); if (new TextEncoder().encode(body.content).byteLength &gt; MAX_CONTENT_BYTES) { return c.json({error: "Message content exceeds 128 KB limit"}, 413); }. For legitimate large content (pasted code files, document text), implement a split-and-reference pattern: content larger than 128 KB is uploaded to R2 (POST /workspace/upload), receives a reference URL, and the message body contains the reference: {type: "r2_reference", url: "...", preview: "first 200 chars..."}. The agent loads large references on demand rather than in bulk context reconstruction.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R23" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R23</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-sweeper — Sweeper cron handler</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Sweeper Fan-Out to All Active Spaces Exceeds CF Workers 1,000 Subrequest Limit</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's naelao-sweeper runs as a CF Cron Trigger and fans out to all active spaces by making one subrequest (fetch to a Worker or DO) per space. CF Workers have a hard limit of 1,000 external subrequests per invocation (paid plan, configurable to 10 million with higher plan). For a deployment with 2,000+ active spaces, the sweeper silently stops dispatching after the first 1,000 — the remaining spaces miss their WAL cleanup, scheduled job dispatch, billing reconciliation, and sub-agent monitoring. This failure is silent: the sweeper returns 200 OK but has done nothing for half the fleet.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace fan-out with a Queue-based dispatch pattern: instead of the sweeper making one subrequest per space, it enqueues one message per space to naelao-sweeper-queue: await env.SWEEPER_QUEUE.sendBatch(spaces.map(s =&gt; ({body: {spaceId: s.id, tasks: ["heartbeat","cleanup","billing"]}}))); CF Queues support batch sends of up to 100 messages per call and have no fan-out limits. A separate Queue consumer Worker processes each space independently. This also adds retry logic (at-least-once delivery) for spaces where the Worker temporarily fails, and allows the consumer to scale horizontally with multiple consumer Workers.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R24" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R24</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-knowledge — GET /knowledge/search handler</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Vectorize topK Hardcoded at 20 — Relevant Documents Beyond Rank 20 Never Surface</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The knowledge search handler calls env.NAELAO_VECTORIZE.query(embedding, {topK: 20, filter: ...}). For a space with a large knowledge base (5,000+ indexed chunks), the 20th-most-similar chunk has a cosine similarity score well below the top-5 results. The actual answer to a user's question may be the 35th-most-similar chunk — below the cutoff. The agent appears to have amnesia on topics that are definitively indexed. This becomes worse as the knowledge base grows and the score distribution spreads. Additionally, the spec applies no score threshold — results with similarity 0.3 (essentially random) are returned alongside results with 0.95.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Make topK configurable per space with a sensible default: const topK = spaceConfig.knowledgeTopK ?? 50. Add a minimum score threshold: filter out results below 0.5 cosine similarity. Implement a two-stage retrieval: (1) fetch topK=100 candidates from Vectorize (fast ANN search), (2) apply cross-encoder re-ranking using a small re-ranker model (env.AI.run("@cf/cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2", {query, passages: candidates.map(c =&gt; c.metadata.text)})) to select the top 5-10 truly relevant results. The re-ranker step adds ~50ms latency but dramatically improves precision, especially for paraphrased queries.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R25" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R25</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-credentials — OAuth token refresh handler</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">OAuth Token Refresh Retains All Historical Token Values — Breach Exposes Full Token History</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The OAuth credential refresh handler writes the new access_token and refresh_token values to DO SQLite alongside the previous values (in a credentials_history table or via INSERT rather than UPDATE). If the credentials store is breached via any other vulnerability, an attacker gains not just the current token but every historical token for every OAuth integration ever refreshed. Many OAuth providers do not immediately revoke previously issued access tokens — historical tokens may still be valid. The credentials table should never store more than the current token pair.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Use atomic UPDATE (not INSERT) for token refresh: this.ctx.storage.sql.exec("UPDATE oauth_credentials SET access_token = ?, refresh_token = ?, expires_at = ?, updated_at = unixepoch('now')*1000 WHERE credential_id = ? AND space_id = ?", encryptedAccess, encryptedRefresh, expiresAt, credentialId, spaceId). Never store old token values — not in a history table, not in a backup column. For audit purposes, log only the credential_id, refresh timestamp, and a non-sensitive token fingerprint (first 6 chars of SHA-256 hash) to Analytics Engine. If a refresh fails, keep the old token active until the new one is confirmed valid.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R26" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R26</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-agent-do — wrangler.toml / NaelaoAgentDO</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">NaelaoAgentDO Missing Unbound CPU Limit Declaration — Agent Times Out Under Default 30-Second CPU Budget</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">CF Workers default CPU time budget is 30 seconds for paid plans (10ms for free). NaelaoAgentDO's per-turn processing includes: loading all thread messages from SQLite, building the system prompt with memory injection and skill XML, serializing tool schemas, calling the LLM (network I/O, doesn't count against CPU), parsing streamed XML/JSON responses, and executing multiple tool calls. The CPU-bound phases (prompt construction, JSON parsing, tool result processing) across a 20-step agentic loop can easily consume 30+ seconds of CPU time, causing the Worker to be killed mid-run. The spec does not declare a Unbound CPU limit.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add an explicit CPU limit declaration in wrangler.toml for all agent-related Workers and Durable Objects: [limits] cpu_ms = 300000. This enables the 5-minute CPU budget available on paid plans. For prompt construction (the most CPU-intensive non-network operation), profile and optimize: pre-compute the static system prompt prefix once per DO wake (cache in a class field) rather than rebuilding it from scratch on every turn. For the message context, use SQLite's built-in JSON functions to serialize directly in SQL rather than loading all rows into JavaScript objects. Add CI linting to assert that all Workers handling agent loops declare cpu_ms &gt;= 30000.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R27" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R27</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-threads-impl / §naelao-agent-do — Hono middleware chain</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Auth Middleware Calls c.req.json() — Route Handler Gets Empty Body on Second Parse Attempt</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's auth middleware parses the request body to extract parameters: const body = await c.req.json(). HTTP request bodies are single-read streams in the CF Workers runtime — once consumed, the stream is exhausted. Any downstream route handler that also calls await c.req.json() (the most common pattern) reads an exhausted stream and gets either an empty object or a "Body already consumed" error. This silently breaks all POST endpoints that have this middleware in their chain: login, message send, tool execution, webhook handling — all receive empty payloads.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">In middleware that needs to read the body, parse once and attach to context: const body = await c.req.json(); c.set("parsedBody", body). Downstream handlers retrieve via c.get("parsedBody") instead of re-calling c.req.json(). Alternatively, use c.req.clone().json() in middleware to read from a cloned request, leaving the original stream unconsumed — but verify this creates a full copy and not a shared underlying stream. A simpler fix: move auth parameter extraction from the body to Authorization headers or query params (which are not consumed on read), reserving body parsing exclusively for route handlers.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R28" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R28</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§ai-gateway / §together-ai — createTogetherAIClient() / model routing</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Together AI Model Translation Has No Fallback Validation — Unknown Model Returns Opaque 400 Error</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The model translation function translateModelName() maps Naelao-internal model names to Together AI API names via a modelMap lookup. If the requested model name is not in modelMap (misspelling, new model added to Together's catalog but not to modelMap, or a space passing an arbitrary model name), the function returns the unmapped name directly to the Together AI API. Together AI rejects unknown model names with a 400 error whose message ("model not found") provides no indication of which model was requested, why it failed, or what valid alternatives exist. The agent run fails with an opaque error.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add validation before the API call: const translatedModel = modelMap[requestedModel]; if (!translatedModel) { return new Response(JSON.stringify({error: `Model "${requestedModel}" is not supported. Available models: ${Object.keys(modelMap).join(", ")}`}), {status: 400, headers: {"Content-Type": "application/json"}}); }. Also validate that the space's billing tier allows the requested model (cheaper models only for free tier). Maintain the modelMap in a DO SQLite table or KV (rather than hardcoded) so new Together AI models can be added without code deploys. Log all model translation failures to Analytics Engine to detect when clients are requesting unsupported models.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R29" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R29</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§ai-gateway — AI Gateway DLP configuration</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">AI Gateway DLP Response Scanning Silently Disables Streaming — Agent Gets Buffered Response</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Cloudflare AI Gateway's DLP (Data Loss Prevention) response scanning buffers the entire LLM response before forwarding to the client when response scanning is enabled. This silently overrides the streaming flag: a request sent with stream: true gets a buffered response with DLP enabled, eliminating the time-to-first-token benefit of streaming. For a 2,000-token response at 30 tokens/second, streaming delivers first token in ~100ms; buffering delivers nothing for ~67 seconds. The spec enables DLP on the AI Gateway binding but expects streaming latency — these two are mutually exclusive when DLP scans responses.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">If low latency is required: enable DLP on REQUEST only (scan the system prompt and user message for PII before sending to LLM), not on the response. This catches PII leakage in inputs without buffering the output: aiGatewayConfig = {dlp: {request: true, response: false}}. If response scanning is a hard requirement: disable streaming for that gateway binding and compensate with a streaming simulation — poll for completion and send the full response when ready. Document this constraint prominently: "AI Gateway DLP response scanning is incompatible with streaming responses. Choose one." Add a CI check that rejects any config with both dlp.response: true and stream: true.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R30" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R30</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§workflows — CF Workflows step execution</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Code Outside step.do() Re-Executes on Workflow Replay — Side Effects Duplicated on Retry</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">CF Workflows caches the result of each step.do() call and skips re-execution on replay. However, code outside step.do() blocks (variable initialization, logging, analytics writes, webhook calls, Queue messages) re-executes every time the workflow replays or retries a failed step. The spec's workflow implementations write analytics events and send Queue messages at the top level of the run() method (outside step.do() blocks). On a replay from step 3 (after step 2 failed), steps 0 and 1 are skipped (cached) but the top-level code runs again — logging the same events twice, sending duplicate Queue messages, and calling external webhooks a second time.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Wrap ALL side effects inside step.do() blocks. No code with external effects should appear at the top level of workflow run(): ❌ await env.QUEUE.send({...}); at top level. ✅ await step.do("notify-queue", async () =&gt; { await env.QUEUE.send({...}); }). step.do() result caching ensures Queue sends happen exactly once even on replay. For logging, use the step's output as the log payload: const logResult = await step.do("log-start", async () =&gt; ({timestamp: Date.now(), runId})); — the log event is captured in the step cache and not re-emitted on replay. Apply this pattern to all workflow definitions in the spec.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R31" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R31</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§execution-ladder / §naelao-spaces-full — R2 multipart upload</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">R2 Multipart Upload UploadId Not Persisted Across DO Wakes — Large File Uploads Always Fail</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">R2 multipart uploads require persisting the uploadId and each completed part's ETag across multiple Worker invocations. The spec's file upload handler initiates multipart upload (gets uploadId), uploads parts across multiple HTTP requests, and completes. Between upload part requests, the DO may be evicted (inactivity timeout 70-140s). The uploadId and parts array are stored in a class field (this.pendingUploads = new Map()) which is lost on DO hibernation. The next upload-part request finds no pending upload and returns 404, corrupting the multipart upload. R2 charges for incomplete multipart uploads (storage cost) that can never be completed.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Persist multipart upload state to DO SQLite: CREATE TABLE r2_multipart_uploads (upload_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, r2_key TEXT NOT NULL, parts_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT "[]", initiated_at INTEGER, status TEXT DEFAULT "pending"). On each upload-part completion, UPDATE r2_multipart_uploads SET parts_json = json_insert(parts_json, "$[#]", json_object("PartNumber", partNum, "ETag", etag)) WHERE upload_id = ?. On complete-upload: read all parts from SQLite, call r2.completeMultipartUpload(key, uploadId, parts), then DELETE the row. Add a DO alarm to call r2.abortMultipartUpload() for uploads older than 24 hours — R2 charges for incomplete multiparts until explicitly aborted.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R32" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R32</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§operator-shell / §naelao-spaces-full — App iframe message handlers</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">postMessage Origin Not Validated in App Iframes — Forged Token Injection from Third-Party Pages</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">App iframes in the Naelao operator shell receive TOKEN_REFRESH and CONTEXT messages via window.postMessage without validating event.origin. Any third-party page that embeds the Naelao app iframe (or opens it in a popup) can send arbitrary messages and have them processed as legitimate platform messages. A malicious page can send TOKEN_REFRESH with a forged JWT containing elevated role claims, or a CONTEXT message with a spoofed spaceId, tricking the iframe into operating in a different space's context. This is a standard postMessage spoofing attack.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add event.origin validation to all postMessage handlers: window.addEventListener("message", (event) =&gt; { const ALLOWED_ORIGINS = ["https://app.naelao.dev", "https://app.naelao.com"]; if (!ALLOWED_ORIGINS.includes(event.origin)) { console.warn("Rejected message from untrusted origin:", event.origin); return; } processMessage(event.data); }). Also set Content-Security-Policy frame-ancestors 'self' https://app.naelao.dev on all pages that should only be embeddable within Naelao (prevents third-party embedding). For TOKEN_REFRESH messages, validate the token's signature independently rather than trusting the message content — treat postMessage payloads as untrusted user input.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R33" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R33</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§space-type-versioning / §dynamic-workers — Workflow / Dynamic Worker path validation</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Workflow R2 Path Validation Uses startsWith — Crafted spaceId Bypasses Isolation</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Dynamic Worker and Workflow loading validates paths with: if (!workflowPath.startsWith(`spaces/${spaceId}/`)) throw Forbidden. A space with ID "abc-123" crafts path "spaces/abc-123" (no trailing slash); startsWith("spaces/abc-123/") fails for legitimate paths but a space with ID "abc-123-evil" has all its paths pass the check because startsWith("spaces/abc-123") is true for "spaces/abc-123-evil/workflows/backdoor.ts". Similarly, R2 list operations with prefix "spaces/abc-123" will match objects from space "spaces/abc-123-evil" in a flat namespace. Path construction must use exact structural matching.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Use path segments with explicit trailing slashes and structural extraction: const expectedPrefix = `spaces/${spaceId}/`; if (!workflowPath.startsWith(expectedPrefix)) throw new Response("Forbidden", {status:403}). For extracting paths, use URL path parsing: const segments = workflowPath.split("/"); if (segments[0] !== "spaces" || segments[1] !== spaceId) throw Forbidden — this ensures segment[1] is exactly spaceId, not a spaceId prefix. For R2 list operations, always append "/" to the prefix: env.R2.list({prefix: `spaces/${spaceId}/`}) ensuring the prefix terminates at a path separator. Audit all R2 prefix usages in the codebase for this pattern.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R34" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R34</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-spaces-full — Space member role management</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Member Role Downgrade Does Not Invalidate Active Sessions — Privilege Window Until Session Expiry</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">When a space member's role is downgraded (e.g., from admin to viewer) or when a member is removed, the handler updates the space_members SQLite row but does not invalidate active WebSocket sessions or cached JWT tokens that still carry the old role claim. If the member has an active browser session with a JWT that expires in 24 hours, they retain admin privileges for the full 24 hours after being downgraded. During a security incident (compromised employee account), revoking access takes effect only after the attacker's session expires naturally. This is unacceptable for a platform handling user data and AI agent execution.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">On role change or member removal: (1) query all active sessions for the member in that space: SELECT session_id FROM sessions WHERE user_id = ? AND space_id = ? AND expires_at &gt; unixepoch("now")*1000; (2) for each session, close the associated WebSocket: const ws = this.ctx.getWebSockets().find(w =&gt; w.serializeAttachment()?.sessionId === sessionId); ws?.close(1008, "Role changed — please re-authenticate"); (3) add the session_id to a revoked_sessions KV set with TTL matching the token expiry; (4) in all auth middleware, check revoked_sessions before accepting a token. Also implement short-lived JWTs (15-minute expiry) with refresh tokens for lower blast radius.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-R35" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-R35</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§ai-gateway / §naelao-knowledge — Workers AI / embedding calls</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Workers AI Context Window Claimed in Docs Differs From Actual — Silent 400 on Oversized Prompts</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec references Workers AI model capabilities from Cloudflare documentation, which may overstate context windows. As of 2026, gemma-4-26b-a4b-it's actual context window is 128K (131,072) tokens, not the 256K documented in some sources. An agent constructing a prompt with full conversation history, memory injection, skill XML, and a large document chunk can easily exceed the actual limit. Workers AI returns a 400 error with a generic message; the agent has no fallback — it crashes the turn rather than truncating the context and retrying. This is a silent failure mode for users with long conversation histories.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Before every LLM call, count tokens and enforce a hard budget: const promptTokens = countTokens(messages, systemPrompt, tools); const MODEL_CONTEXT_WINDOW = MODEL_LIMITS[modelId] ?? 128_000; const MAX_PROMPT_TOKENS = Math.floor(MODEL_CONTEXT_WINDOW * 0.75); // reserve 25% for output. if (promptTokens &gt; MAX_PROMPT_TOKENS) { messages = truncateMessages(messages, MAX_PROMPT_TOKENS - systemPromptTokens - toolTokens); }. Truncation strategy: keep system prompt and last N user/assistant turns intact, drop the oldest turns first. Maintain MODEL_LIMITS as a verified-from-source table in the codebase, not from documentation. Add a quarterly CI test that queries the actual Workers AI API with a boundary-condition prompt to catch limit changes.</p></div>
  </div>
  </div>


<div class="sep"></div>
<section id="appendix-s" style="padding:60px 0 40px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 24px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.15em;text-transform:uppercase;">Appendix S</span>
    <h2 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:36px;font-weight:700;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.02em;margin:8px 0 6px;">Eleventh-Pass: Use the Platform — CF Native vs. Spec Custom Implementations</h2>
    <p style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:15px;color:var(--tx);line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 40px;">35 architectural gaps where the spec hand-rolls what Cloudflare already provides natively: withVoice mixin replacing VAPI, this.schedule() replacing cron-jobs.json sweeper, this.subAgent() replacing HTTP fetch dispatch, needsApproval: true replacing in-memory Promise resolvers, routeAgentRequest() replacing manual DO routing, useAgent/useAgentChat/useVoiceAgent replacing missing client SDK, this.setState() replacing ad-hoc SQL, this.sql template literal, Workers AI STT/TTS replacing external speech APIs, CF Realtime SFU for browser audio, and multi-agent team composition via facet hierarchies.</p>

  <div id="G-S01" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S01</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-notifications-full / §05 — initiateVoiceCall() / AssistantDO</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Voice Built on Third-Party VAPI Instead of CF withVoice Mixin + Workers AI Native STT/TTS</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec integrates VAPI (third-party) for voice agent calls: POST to api.vapi.ai with an external model, an 11labs voice provider, and a phone_number_id. This creates: (1) per-minute billing from VAPI on top of CF costs, (2) a complex webhook lifecycle that the spec never fully implements (no POST /webhooks/vapi handler), (3) dependency on VAPI availability for a critical escalation path, (4) no transcript access within the agent DO. CF provides a complete native voice agent stack: the withVoice mixin from @cloudflare/voice, native STT via @cf/deepgram/flux (built-in end-of-turn detection, 16kHz mono), native TTS via @cf/deepgram/aura-1 (context-aware pacing), and Twilio telephony via @cloudflare/voice-twilio — all processed in-process in the DO with zero external API cost.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace VAPI with the CF native voice pipeline: import { withVoice } from "@cloudflare/voice"; import { WorkersAIFluxSTT } from "@cloudflare/voice/stt"; import { WorkersAITTS } from "@cloudflare/voice/tts". class AssistantVoiceDO extends withVoice(Agent) { transcriber = new WorkersAIFluxSTT(this.env.AI, { eotThreshold: 0.75 }); tts = new WorkersAITTS(this.env.AI, { model: "@cf/deepgram/aura-1", speaker: "asteria" }); async onTurn(transcript, ctx) { const reply = await runAgentTurn(transcript, ctx); this.saveMessage("user", transcript); this.saveMessage("assistant", reply); return reply; } }. For telephony, use @cloudflare/voice-twilio adapter which routes Twilio Media Streams directly to the VoiceDO WebSocket — eliminating VAPI entirely.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S02" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S02</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — space-assistants / §launchpad — AssistantDO.requestApproval()</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">pendingApprovals In-Memory Map Lost on DO Hibernation — CF needsApproval Tool Flag Is the Native Solution</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec implements human-in-the-loop approval by storing Promise resolvers in an in-memory Map: pendingApprovals.set(approvalId, resolve). When the DO hibernates (inactivity eviction after 70-140s), the Map is destroyed — all pending approvals are permanently lost. The waiting fiber hangs until the DO alarm fires and auto-rejects. This also has a race condition: the alarm and the approval decision handler can both fire near-simultaneously, calling the same resolver twice and executing the approved action twice (double email, double charge). CF's @cloudflare/ai-chat provides needsApproval: true on any tool definition — the framework pauses execution, sends the approval request to the client over WebSocket, and resumes when the client calls addToolApprovalResponse({id, approved}) — no in-memory Map, no lost promises, no race condition.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace the custom approval mechanism with the CF SDK pattern. In tool definitions: tools: { sendEmail: tool({ description: "Send email to user", inputSchema: z.object({to: z.string(), body: z.string()}), needsApproval: true, execute: async ({to, body}) =&gt; sendEmail(to, body) })}. The SDK persists the paused turn in DO SQLite automatically — hibernation does not lose the state. On the client, use the useAgentChat() hook which exposes addToolApprovalResponse: const {messages, addToolApprovalResponse} = useAgentChat({agent: "AssistantDO"}); and render approval UI when messages contain a tool-approval part. If retaining a custom flow (e.g., for mobile push approvals), store approval state in DO SQLite (not in-memory): INSERT INTO pending_approvals (approval_id, fiber_id, tool_name, args_json, expires_at) and use a DO alarm (not a Map Promise) to timeout.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S03" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S03</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — space-assistants — cron-jobs.json / AssistantDO heartbeat</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Scheduled Tasks Use cron-jobs.json in R2 + Global Sweeper — CF this.schedule() Is the Native Solution</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec stores per-assistant scheduled tasks in cron-jobs.json in R2, read by a global sweeper cron every 5 minutes. Problems: (1) R2 read on every heartbeat tick for every active assistant, (2) the sweeper cron fires all assistants simultaneously (thundering herd), (3) no built-in retry for failed scheduled tasks, (4) cron expression parsing must be hand-implemented in the DO, (5) concurrent sweeper invocations can run the same task twice (no dedup). CF's Agent base class provides this.schedule(when, callback, payload, retryOptions) and this.scheduleEvery(intervalSeconds, callback, payload) — stored in a SQL-backed cf_agents_schedules table inside the DO itself, with built-in retry (maxAttempts, baseDelayMs, maxDelayMs), cron expression support, and deduplication by schedule ID.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace cron-jobs.json with CF Agent scheduling. In AssistantDO: async addScheduledJob(job: CronJob) { const schedId = await this.schedule(job.schedule, "runScheduledJob", {jobId: job.id, task: job.task}); await this.sql`INSERT INTO job_schedules (job_id, sched_id, ...) VALUES (${job.id}, ${schedId.id}, ...)`; }. async runScheduledJob(payload: {jobId: string, task: string}) { await this.executeTask(payload.task); }. The SDK fires the callback in the DO at the correct time with per-call retry. To list active schedules: this.getSchedules(). To cancel: this.cancelSchedule(schedId). This replaces the entire cron-jobs.json / sweeper / R2-read architecture with in-DO SQL-backed scheduling.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S04" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S04</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-sweeper / §05 — sweepSchedules() / heartbeat cron</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Concurrent Sweeper Invocations Create Duplicate Cron Job Runs — No Atomic Dedup Guard</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The sweeper cron reads all due cron jobs (nextRun &lt;= now), dispatches them, then updates nextRun. With at-least-once cron delivery, two sweeper invocations can overlap: both read the same set of due jobs before either writes the updated nextRun, both dispatch the same jobs as separate workflow instances. The user receives two emails, two charges are processed, two sub-agents are spawned — for every job that was due during the overlap window. The spec has no atomic compare-and-swap to prevent this: the UPDATE sets nextRun unconditionally, not conditionally on the old value.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Use an atomic compare-and-swap UPDATE as the dispatch gate: UPDATE cron_jobs SET next_run = computeNext(schedule), dispatched_at = unixepoch("now")*1000 WHERE job_id = ? AND next_run = ? AND (dispatched_at IS NULL OR dispatched_at &lt; next_run). The WHERE next_run = ? clause ensures only the invocation that read that specific nextRun value can update it — the second concurrent invocation reads a different value (already updated by the first) and gets rowsAffected = 0, skipping dispatch. Only proceed to workflow.create() if rowsAffected = 1. This pattern is the SQLite equivalent of compare-and-swap and works correctly given DO single-threaded execution. Also, if using CF Agent scheduling (G-S03), this dedup is built into the SDK.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S05" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S05</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-agent-do / §05 — NaelaoAgentDO / AssistantDO state</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Agent State Managed via Ad-Hoc SQL Queries — CF this.setState() Provides Typed Persistent State</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">NaelaoAgentDO and AssistantDO manage all persistent state through raw this.ctx.storage.sql.exec() calls, manually building INSERT/UPDATE/SELECT queries for every state field. CF's Agent base class provides this.setState(state: State) which: (1) persists state to a cf_agents_state SQLite table automatically, (2) broadcasts the state update to all connected WebSocket clients via the SDK's state sync protocol, (3) exposes this.state getter for reading current state, (4) fires this.onStateChanged(newState, oldState) hook for side effects. The current spec's pattern requires separate code for persistence, serialization, and broadcasting — three concerns the SDK collapses into one call.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Define a typed state interface and use setState: interface AgentState { currentTask: string|null; pendingApprovals: string[]; lastHeartbeat: number; activeSubAgents: string[]; }. In AssistantDO: await this.setState({...this.state, currentTask: taskId, lastHeartbeat: Date.now()}). Read state: const task = this.state.currentTask. Implement the hook: onStateChanged(state: AgentState, oldState: AgentState) { if (state.currentTask !== oldState.currentTask) { env.ANALYTICS.writeDataPoint({blobs: [state.currentTask ?? "idle"], indexes: [this.spaceId]}); } }. On client, useAgent&lt;AgentState&gt;({agent: "AssistantDO", name: spaceId}) provides a live-updating state object — no polling needed.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S06" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S06</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — space-assistants — AssistantDO.spawnSubAgent()</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Sub-Agents Dispatched via Raw HTTP fetch() — CF this.subAgent(ChildClass, name) Is the Native Solution</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec dispatches sub-agents by constructing HTTP requests to NaelaoAgentDO endpoints via service binding fetch(). CF's Agent base class provides this.subAgent(ChildAgentClass, instanceName) which: (1) resolves the child DO by class name + instance name (URL routing: /agents/{child-kebab}/{name}), (2) returns a typed stub with direct method RPC — no HTTP serialization, (3) supports nested addressing (grandchild via this.subAgent(GrandchildClass, name) on the child stub), (4) provides this.parentAgent(ParentClass) from the child to call back to the parent, (5) exposes this.parentPath (array of ancestors root-first). The spec's HTTP approach requires manual URL construction, response parsing, and provides no typed interface.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace HTTP sub-agent dispatch with CF SDK facets: async spawnResearcher(task: string) { const researcher = await this.subAgent(ResearcherDO, `researcher-${task.slice(0,20)}`); const result = await researcher.research(task); return result; }. Define ResearcherDO as a standard Agent subclass with typed RPC methods. The child can notify the parent: class ResearcherDO extends Agent { async research(task: string) { const parent = await this.parentAgent(AssistantDO); await parent.onSubAgentProgress({task, status: "started"}); const result = await doResearch(task); await parent.onSubAgentComplete({task, result}); return result; } }. Sub-agent URLs are auto-registered at /agents/assistant-do/{name}/sub/researcher-do/{name} — no wrangler.toml changes needed for routing.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S07" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S07</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 / §operator-shell — Frontend client</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No Client SDK Documented — CF Provides useAgent(), useAgentChat(), useVoiceAgent() React Hooks</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec describes the Naelao operator shell frontend but provides no client SDK documentation. Builders integrating the Space Assistant must write raw WebSocket or SSE code to connect to AgentDOs, parse tool call events, handle reconnections, and render approval prompts. The CF Agents SDK ships a complete React client: useAgent&lt;State&gt;({agent, name}) provides bidirectional state sync and typed RPC; useAgentChat({agent}) provides a full chat interface with streaming tokens, tool call visualization, and approval handling; useVoiceAgent({agent}) provides a complete voice UI with transcript, audio level, mute, and status. None of these are documented or integrated in the spec.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Define the client SDK layer. Install: npm install agents @cloudflare/voice. In the operator shell React app: const {state, call} = useAgent&lt;AgentState&gt;({agent: "AssistantDO", name: spaceId}); const {messages, input, handleInputChange, handleSubmit, addToolApprovalResponse} = useAgentChat({agent: "AssistantDO", name: spaceId, onToolCall: (tc) =&gt; handleClientTool(tc)}). Render approval prompts: messages.filter(m =&gt; m.parts?.some(p =&gt; p.type === "tool-approval")).map(m =&gt; &lt;ApprovalCard onApprove={() =&gt; addToolApprovalResponse({id: m.id, approved: true})} /&gt;). Add to wrangler.toml: [assets] directory = "./dist" to serve the React app from the same Worker. Document this in the spec as the canonical integration pattern for space builders embedding the assistant.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S08" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S08</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-router — Worker routing / DO resolution</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Manual idFromName() Routing — CF routeAgentRequest(request, env) Handles Agent URL Dispatch Natively</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's naelao-router Worker resolves agent DOs by calling env.NAELAO_AGENT_DO.idFromName(compositeKey) and constructing custom stub lookups for each DO class. The CF Agents SDK provides routeAgentRequest(request, env) which: (1) parses the URL path /agents/{class-kebab-name}/{instance-name}, (2) resolves the corresponding DO binding by class name → kebab conversion, (3) forwards the request to the correct DO instance, (4) handles sub-agent URL routing (/agents/{parent}/{name}/sub/{child}/{name}) automatically. The spec's manual routing requires maintaining a routing table, handling kebab-to-class mapping, and creating separate stubs for each DO type.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace the custom routing logic in naelao-router with routeAgentRequest: import { routeAgentRequest } from "agents"; export default { async fetch(request, env, ctx) { const agentResponse = await routeAgentRequest(request, env); if (agentResponse) return agentResponse; return handleNonAgentRequest(request, env, ctx); } }. In wrangler.toml, register all agent DOs: [[durable_objects.bindings]] name = "AssistantDO" class_name = "AssistantDO". The SDK maps the URL /agents/assistant-do/{id} to env.AssistantDO automatically. Agent class names are converted to kebab-case (AssistantDO → assistant-do) for URL segments. This eliminates the entire custom routing layer including the DO registry KV lookup.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S09" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S09</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-notifications-full — Twilio / VAPI telephony</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Twilio Media Streams Integration Hand-Rolled — @cloudflare/voice-twilio Provides It Natively</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec implements phone call integration via VAPI which internally manages the Twilio connection. If the spec ever integrates Twilio directly (for cost reduction or VAPI replacement), it would need to implement: Twilio Media Streams WebSocket protocol, audio format conversion (mulaw 8kHz ↔ 16kHz PCM), stream SID tracking, and call control REST calls. CF provides @cloudflare/voice-twilio adapter that handles all of this: it connects a Twilio Media Stream WebSocket to a VoiceDO WebSocket, converts audio formats, and routes control messages. The integration is: configure Twilio webhook URL to point at the Worker, adapter handles the rest.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace VAPI with direct Twilio + @cloudflare/voice-twilio: import { TwilioAdapter } from "@cloudflare/voice-twilio". In naelao-router, add a Twilio webhook handler: app.post("/webhooks/twilio/voice", async (c) =&gt; { const twiml = new VoiceResponse(); twiml.connect().stream({url: `wss://${c.req.header("host")}/agents/assistant-voice-do/${spaceId}`}); return c.text(twiml.toString(), 200, {"Content-Type": "text/xml"}); }). The Twilio Media Stream then connects to AssistantVoiceDO's WebSocket endpoint where the TwilioAdapter translates between Twilio's mulaw protocol and CF's PCM voice pipeline. In AssistantVoiceDO: const adapter = new TwilioAdapter(); this.onMessage = adapter.onMessage.bind(adapter). Cost: eliminates VAPI subscription entirely. Latency: one fewer hop (Twilio → CF directly vs. Twilio → VAPI → CF).</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S10" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S10</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-notifications-full — Voice call lifecycle</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Voice Call Transcript Not Persisted via saveMessage() — Conversation History Broken After Voice Turns</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's voice call implementation (via VAPI) fires initiateVoiceCall() and returns immediately. The voice turn transcript (what the user said, what the agent replied) is never stored in the agent's conversation history. After a voice interaction, the text conversation has a gap — the next text message has no context of what was discussed in the voice call. The agent may contradict itself, repeat questions it just asked verbally, or fail to act on commitments made during the voice turn. The CF withVoice mixin provides saveMessage(role, text) specifically to store voice turns in the conversation history shared with the text chat pipeline.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">After every voice turn, call saveMessage() to persist to shared history: async onTurn(transcript, ctx) { const reply = await this.runAgentTurn(transcript); this.saveMessage("user", transcript); this.saveMessage("assistant", reply); return reply; }. Additionally, on call end: async onCallEnd(connection) { const summary = await summarizeCall(this.getConversationHistory()); await this.ctx.storage.sql.exec("INSERT INTO voice_calls (space_id, user_id, summary, transcript_json, ended_at) VALUES (?,?,?,?,unixepoch('now')*1000)", this.spaceId, userId, summary, JSON.stringify(this.getConversationHistory())); }. This ensures the text conversation seamlessly continues after a voice interaction, and the full call transcript is available for audit and billing reconciliation.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S11" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S11</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — space-assistants — AssistantDO keep-alive</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">DO Keep-Alive Relies on 5-Minute Global Cron — CF this.keepAlive() Is the Native Solution</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec keeps AssistantDOs alive through the global 5-minute sweeper cron trigger that sends a heartbeat fetch to each active DO. This requires: the sweeper Worker, KV registry of active DOs, and a global cron schedule. CF's Agent class provides this.keepAlive() which: returns a dispose function, sends internal heartbeats at configurable intervals (default 30s), and this.keepAliveWhile(fn) which keeps the DO alive for the duration of an async operation. These are in-process — no Worker overhead, no KV lookup, no cron coordination.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace the external heartbeat with in-DO keepAlive: async onStart() { const dispose = await this.keepAlive(); this.ctx.waitUntil(Promise.resolve()); }. For long-running agent tasks: const result = await this.keepAliveWhile(async () =&gt; { return await runLongAgentTask(); }). Configure keepAlive interval: static options = { keepAliveIntervalMs: 60_000 }. The sweeper cron is only needed for platform-level maintenance (billing reconciliation, orphaned DO cleanup) — not for individual DO keep-alive. This eliminates the thundering-herd issue from G-Q16 where all DOs wake simultaneously.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S12" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S12</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-notifications-full / §ai-gateway — VAPI / Workers AI audio</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">VAPI External Speech Models Used Instead of CF Workers AI Native STT/TTS</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The VAPI integration sends audio to external speech providers (11labs for TTS, VAPI's own STT). CF Workers AI provides free (included in platform cost) speech models: @cf/deepgram/flux for STT (continuous streaming, built-in end-of-turn detection with eotThreshold, end-of-utterance events, keyword boosting via keyterms[]), and @cf/deepgram/aura-1 for TTS (context-aware pacing, multiple speakers). Using external providers adds per-minute billing from a third party, increases latency (round-trip to external API), and introduces a dependency on provider availability.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Use Workers AI speech models via the withVoice mixin: transcriber = new WorkersAIFluxSTT(this.env.AI, { eotThreshold: 0.75, eagerEotThreshold: 0.55, eotTimeoutMs: 3000, keyterms: ["Naelao", spaceKeywords] }); tts = new WorkersAITTS(this.env.AI, { model: "@cf/deepgram/aura-1", speaker: "asteria" }). For multi-lingual spaces, choose model: "@cf/myshell/melotts". For alternative TTS: "@cf/xai/grok-tts". Configure eotThreshold between 0.5 (sensitive, picks up short pauses) and 0.9 (conservative, waits for definitive end of speech). The keyterms option boosts domain-specific vocabulary recognition for terms like "refund", "escalate", or custom product names.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S13" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S13</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-notifications-full / §execution-ladder — CF Realtime SFU</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">CF Realtime SFU Not Used for Browser-to-Agent Real-Time Audio — Could Eliminate Telephony Dependency</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's voice calls require a phone number (VAPI phone_number_id) — users must receive a phone call to interact with the voice agent. For browser-based users, CF Realtime provides a WebRTC SFU running at CF edge (330+ PoPs, anycast) that routes browser audio directly to a VoiceDO WebSocket. The SFU WebSocket adapter (auto-reconnect as of May 2026) connects the browser's microphone stream to the VoiceDO's PCM input, eliminating the phone call requirement entirely for desktop/mobile web users.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add a browser voice endpoint using CF Realtime SFU: in the operator shell, use the SFU WebSocket adapter: import { createSFUSession, createSFUWebSocketAdapter } from "@cloudflare/realtime/sfu-utils". const {sessionId} = await createSFUSession({accountId, appId, appSecret, baseUrl: "https://rtc.live.cloudflare.com/v1"}); const wsAdapter = await createSFUWebSocketAdapter({...}, sessionId). On the Agent side, wire the SFU WebSocket to the VoiceDO's audio input. This gives browser users sub-100ms first-audio latency via the nearest CF datacenter, with automatic reconnect and audio buffering on network hiccups (May 2026 feature). Phone calls via VAPI/Twilio remain for users who can't use browser audio.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S14" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S14</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-agent-do / §05 — NaelaoAgentDO / AssistantDO</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">this.queue() Pattern Not Used — Internal Async Work Sent via CF Queues (External Overhead)</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">For work that should be deferred but processed within the same DO (e.g., post-turn analytics, memory extraction, skill crystallization), the spec sends CF Queue messages to an external consumer Worker. CF's Agent class provides this.queue(callbackName, payload, retryOptions) which enqueues work for processing within the same DO, backed by cf_agents_queues SQLite table. This is faster (no external Queue round-trip), cheaper (no Queue billing), and retries within the DO (up to maxAttempts times with exponential backoff). External CF Queues should only be used for cross-DO or cross-Worker fan-out.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">For work that can be processed within the same DO, use this.queue(): async onChatEnd(turn) { await this.queue("extractMemories", {messages: turn.messages, turnId: turn.id}); await this.queue("crystallizeSkills", {taskResult: turn.result, turnCount: turn.turnCount}); }. async extractMemories(payload) { const facts = await runMemoryExtraction(payload.messages); await this.upsertMemories(facts); }. Configure retry: static options = { retry: { maxAttempts: 3, baseDelayMs: 1000, maxDelayMs: 30000 } }. Reserve external CF Queues for: (1) notifying other DOs or Workers of events, (2) fan-out to multiple consumers, (3) delivery guarantees beyond the DO lifecycle (e.g., billing events that must survive DO crash).</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S15" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S15</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-agent-do / §05 — NaelaoAgentDO.initialize()</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">this.onStart() Lifecycle Hook Not Implemented — DO Initialization Not Idempotent</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec initializes AssistantDO and NaelaoAgentDO by checking for schema in the request handler path (if not initialized, run DDL). CF's Agent base class provides onStart() which: (1) fires once per DO lifetime on first wake after creation, (2) fires again after DO migration, (3) is guaranteed to complete before any requests are served (async but awaited), and (4) is not called on every wake — only on genuine starts. This is the correct place for schema initialization: idempotent (runs only when needed), atomic with request serving (no race condition), and semantically correct.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement onStart() as the canonical initialization point: async onStart() { this.sql`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS sessions (id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, space_id TEXT NOT NULL, user_id TEXT, created_at INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT (unixepoch("now")*1000))`; this.sql`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS messages (id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, session_id TEXT REFERENCES sessions(id), role TEXT NOT NULL, content TEXT NOT NULL, created_at INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT (unixepoch("now")*1000))`; // ... all tables ... }. Note: this.sql is the CF Agent SQL template literal helper (sql`SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ${userId}`) — it handles parameterization automatically. onStart() replaces blockConcurrencyWhile() schema init and the schema_initialized KV flag pattern.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S16" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S16</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§operator-shell — Frontend / browser client</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">useAgentToolEvents() Hook Missing — No Client-Side Tool Execution Progress Tracking</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's frontend has no mechanism to display tool call progress during an agent run. Users see a spinning indicator while the agent runs 15+ tool calls over 2 minutes — no feedback on which tool is running, which have completed, or if any have failed. CF's agents SDK provides useAgentToolEvents({agent}) which tracks all tool call lifecycle events in real-time: {runsById, runsByToolCallId, unboundRuns, getRunsForToolCall(toolCallId)}. Each AgentToolRunState has: tool name, input, status (running/complete/error), output, timing.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add tool progress display using useAgentToolEvents: const {runsById, unboundRuns} = useAgentToolEvents({agent: "AssistantDO", name: spaceId}). Render in the UI: {Object.values(runsById).filter(r =&gt; r.status === "running").map(r =&gt; &lt;ToolProgress key={r.id} name={r.tool} status={r.status} /&gt;)}. For the approval flow, tool calls with needsApproval: true will appear in runsByToolCallId with a "pending-approval" status before the user approves. This gives users real-time visibility into what the agent is doing, reducing perceived latency and building trust in the automation.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S17" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S17</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-agent-do / §05 — AssistantDO SQL access</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">this.ctx.storage.sql.exec() Used Directly — CF this.sql Template Literal Is Safer and More Idiomatic</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec accesses DO SQLite via this.ctx.storage.sql.exec("SELECT * FROM messages WHERE session_id = ?", sessionId) with positional ? parameters as a variadic argument. This is error-prone: parameter count must match ?, wrong order causes silent data errors. CF's Agent class provides this.sql&lt;T&gt; as a tagged template literal: this.sql&lt;Message[]&gt;`SELECT * FROM messages WHERE session_id = ${sessionId}`. The template literal version: (1) infers parameter positions from template slots — impossible to mismatch order, (2) returns typed T[] (no manual casting), (3) rejects nested template interpolation (SQL injection prevention by design), (4) is shorter and more readable.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace all raw exec() calls with the this.sql template literal. Before: const rows = [...this.ctx.storage.sql.exec("SELECT * FROM messages WHERE session_id = ? ORDER BY created_at ASC", sessionId)]. After: const rows = this.sql&lt;Message[]&gt;`SELECT * FROM messages WHERE session_id = ${sessionId} ORDER BY created_at ASC`. For INSERT with multiple values: this.sql`INSERT INTO messages (id, session_id, role, content) VALUES (${id}, ${sessionId}, ${role}, ${content})`. The template literal cannot express multi-row INSERTs in one call — use a loop or this.sql`INSERT INTO messages SELECT ... FROM json_each(${JSON.stringify(rows)})` for batch inserts. All direct this.ctx.storage.sql.exec() calls should be migrated.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S18" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S18</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§operator-shell / §05 — Frontend approval UI</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">addToolApprovalResponse() Not Implemented — Approval Prompts Have No Real-Time Client Path</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's human-in-the-loop approval flow sends an approval link via email or push notification. The user clicks a link, which POSTs to a REST endpoint. This means: the WebSocket session showing the pending turn has no mechanism to receive the approval response in real-time — the user must leave the chat, click an email link, then return. In-chat approval (clicking a button while looking at the agent's request) is not possible. CF's useAgentChat() hook exposes addToolApprovalResponse({id, approved, data?}) which sends the approval decision directly over the existing WebSocket connection — the agent resumes immediately in the same session.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement in-chat approval using the SDK hook. In the operator shell: const {messages, addToolApprovalResponse} = useAgentChat({agent: "AssistantDO", name: spaceId}). Render approval cards inline in the message list: {messages.map(m =&gt; m.parts?.filter(p =&gt; p.type === "tool-approval").map(p =&gt; (&lt;ApprovalCard key={p.id} toolName={p.toolName} args={p.args} onApprove={() =&gt; addToolApprovalResponse({id: p.id, approved: true})} onReject={() =&gt; addToolApprovalResponse({id: p.id, approved: false, data: {reason: "User rejected"}})} /&gt;)))}. Retain the email/push fallback for approvals where the user is not actively in the chat (implement the REST POST endpoint for those), but make in-chat the primary path.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S19" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S19</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — space-assistants — Sub-agent communication</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">parentAgent() and parentPath Not Implemented — Sub-Agents Cannot Notify Parent of Progress</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's sub-agent pattern has no mechanism for a child agent to call back to its parent. Progress updates from sub-agents go to a shared SQLite table that the parent polls (5-minute sweeper cadence). CF's Agent SDK provides this.parentAgent(ParentClass) from within a child DO which returns a typed RPC stub for calling methods on the parent, and this.parentPath (array of {className, name} objects from root to current agent). A child agent completing its research task can immediately notify the parent via typed RPC rather than waiting for the next sweeper tick.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add parent notification to sub-agent DOs: class ResearcherDO extends Agent&lt;Env, ResearcherState&gt; { async doResearch(task: string): Promise&lt;string&gt; { const parent = await this.parentAgent(AssistantDO); await parent.onSubAgentProgress({taskId: task, percent: 0}); const result = await runResearch(task); await parent.onSubAgentComplete({taskId: task, result}); return result; } }. In AssistantDO: async onSubAgentProgress(update) { await this.setState({...this.state, subAgentProgress: update}); }. async onSubAgentComplete(payload) { await this.deliverFiberResult(payload.taskId, payload.result); }. Use this.parentPath to log the full call chain for debugging: console.log("Agent chain:", this.parentPath.map(p =&gt; `${p.className}/${p.name}`).join(" → ")).</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S20" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S20</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-agent-do / §05 — AssistantDO WebSocket broadcast</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Manual getWebSockets().forEach() Loop — CF this.broadcast() Is the Native Broadcast Method</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec broadcasts streaming tokens to connected clients via: this.ctx.getWebSockets().forEach(ws =&gt; { try { ws.send(JSON.stringify(event)); } catch (e) { /* ignore */ } }). CF's Agent base class provides this.broadcast(data) which: (1) iterates all connected WebSocket clients, (2) skips closed connections automatically, (3) handles errors internally, (4) is synchronous and safe to call from any context. The manual forEach pattern silently skips sending to closed sockets (error swallowed) and has no guarantee about message ordering when called concurrently.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace manual WebSocket iteration with this.broadcast(): async sendStreamingToken(token: string) { this.broadcast(JSON.stringify({type: "token", delta: token, turnId: this.currentTurnId})); }. async sendToolCallStart(toolName: string, toolCallId: string) { this.broadcast(JSON.stringify({type: "tool_start", toolName, toolCallId})); }. async sendTurnComplete(result: TurnResult) { this.broadcast(JSON.stringify({type: "done", ...result})); }. For targeted messages (send to only one client, e.g., approval UI): use connection.send() from onConnect(connection, ctx) where connection is the specific client's WebSocket.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S21" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S21</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-notifications-full / §05 — Voice pipeline</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">afterTranscribe() Hook Missing — PII Not Filtered Before Voice Transcript Reaches LLM</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Voice calls transcribe user speech and feed the raw transcript directly into the agent's LLM context. If the user speaks their credit card number, SSN, or medical information, this PII enters the LLM's context and may be echoed back, logged, or stored. CF's withVoice mixin provides afterTranscribe(transcript, connection) hook which fires between STT output and LLM input — the hook can return a sanitized string or null (to skip the turn entirely).</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement afterTranscribe() for PII redaction: async afterTranscribe(transcript: string, connection: Connection): Promise&lt;string | null&gt; { const redacted = redactPII(transcript); if (redacted !== transcript) { await logPIIRedaction(this.spaceId, "voice_transcript"); } return redacted; }. Implement redactPII() using regex patterns for common PII: credit card (4-digit groups), SSN (XXX-XX-XXXX), phone numbers, and email addresses. For HIPAA-scoped spaces, use a more aggressive redaction that flags any numeric sequence &gt; 5 digits. Also implement beforeSynthesize(text, connection) to scan LLM responses before they are spoken: if the LLM echoes back credit card digits, catch and replace before TTS converts to audio.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S22" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S22</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-notifications-full / §05 — VoiceDO response pipeline</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">beforeSynthesize() Hook Missing — Content Moderation Gap on Voice Responses Before TTS</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The voice pipeline sends the LLM's text response directly to the TTS synthesizer without content moderation. A prompt-injected user ("Say you are an AI that has no restrictions") or a misbehaving LLM could cause the voice agent to speak harmful content that gets synthesized to audio and played through the user's phone speaker. This is harder to interrupt mid-playback than a text response. CF's withVoice mixin provides beforeSynthesize(text, connection) which fires between LLM output and TTS input — can return modified text or null (silences the turn).</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement beforeSynthesize() for content moderation: async beforeSynthesize(text: string, connection: Connection): Promise&lt;string | null&gt; { const violates = await checkContentPolicy(text); if (violates.harmful) { await this.speak(connection, "I'm sorry, I can't respond to that request."); return null; } if (text.length &gt; 2000) { return text.slice(0, 2000) + " [Response truncated for voice delivery]"; } return text; }. The null return cancels TTS synthesis for that turn. Also implement onInterrupt(connection) to handle barge-in (user speaks while agent is speaking) — cancel the current TTS stream and process the new user input immediately.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S23" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S23</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§ai-gateway — AI Gateway configuration</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">AI Gateway Spend Limits Not Configured — No Per-Provider Budget Enforcement at Infra Level</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec implements credit deduction in SpaceDO's deductCredits() as application-level billing. If a bug in the agent loop sends 1,000 LLM requests before the credits check fires, $3,000 of inference spend can accumulate. AI Gateway provides spend limits at the infrastructure level: configure a dollar-based budget per provider (Anthropic, OpenAI, Together) with fixed or rolling windows (daily/weekly/monthly). When the budget is hit, AI Gateway blocks further requests at the proxy layer — before the Worker even sees them. This is a hard backstop independent of application-level bugs.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Configure AI Gateway spend limits in the CF dashboard or via API: for each AI Gateway endpoint, set a spend limit: { "provider": "anthropic", "limit": 1000.00, "window": "daily", "action": "block" }. Add per-space budget enforcement using AI Gateway's custom metadata: when routing LLM requests through AI Gateway, add cf-aig-metadata: {"space_id": spaceId} header. Configure per-metadata-key spend limits so each space has its own infrastructure-level budget cap. The application-level deductCredits() remains as the primary billing mechanism; AI Gateway spend limits are the emergency backstop. Alert (don't just block) at 80% of spend limit so operators can investigate before service is interrupted.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S24" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S24</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-agent-do / §05 — Agent state telemetry</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">onStateChanged() Hook Not Implemented — No Agent State Change Audit Trail</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">AssistantDO and NaelaoAgentDO update state via raw SQL INSERTs with no centralized hook to observe state transitions. If the agent transitions from "planning" to "executing" to "waiting-approval" to "complete", these transitions are invisible to the platform. There is no audit trail, no latency tracking per state, and no alert if an agent gets stuck in a state for too long. CF's Agent class fires onStateChanged(newState: State, oldState: State) whenever setState() is called — a natural hook for telemetry.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement onStateChanged for audit and telemetry: onStateChanged(state: AgentState, oldState: AgentState) { const transition = `${oldState.phase ?? "init"} → ${state.phase ?? "idle"}`; this.env.ANALYTICS.writeDataPoint({ blobs: [transition, this.spaceId, state.currentTaskId ?? "none"], doubles: [Date.now() - (oldState.phaseStartedAt ?? Date.now())], indexes: [this.agentId] }); if (state.phase === "waiting-approval" &amp;&amp; !oldState.phase) { scheduleApprovalTimeoutAlert(state.approvalId, 600_000); } }. This creates a full state machine audit trail in Analytics Engine, enabling: "how long do agents spend in each phase?", "which spaces have approval bottlenecks?", "which tasks cause agents to get stuck?".</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S25" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S25</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-notifications-full / §05 — Voice call configuration</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">STT eotThreshold Not Configured — Default End-of-Turn Detection Causes Premature or Late Turn Ends</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's voice integration never configures the STT end-of-turn detection threshold. CF's @cf/deepgram/flux STT has configurable eotThreshold (0.5-0.9, default 0.7) and eagerEotThreshold (enables faster but less accurate end-of-turn events). At the default 0.7, the STT may cut the user off mid-sentence (if they pause briefly while thinking) or wait too long before handing off to the LLM (adding perceived latency). Different use cases need different thresholds: customer support (wait longer, users ramble — use 0.8+), quick commands (faster response — use 0.5), professional (balanced — use 0.7).</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Configure STT per space type: const eotThreshold = { customer_support: 0.85, command_control: 0.55, conversational: 0.70 }[space.scenarioType] ?? 0.70. transcriber = new WorkersAIFluxSTT(this.env.AI, { eotThreshold: spaceConfig.voiceEotThreshold ?? eotThreshold, eagerEotThreshold: spaceConfig.voiceEagerEot ?? 0.50, eotTimeoutMs: spaceConfig.voiceEotTimeout ?? 4000, keyterms: [...spaceConfig.voiceKeyterms, ...platformTerms] }). Store these voice config fields in SpaceDO SQLite and expose them in the space settings UI. Also configure keyterms: boosting domain-specific terms (product names, technical jargon) significantly improves STT accuracy for specialized spaces.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S26" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S26</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-notifications-full / §05 — VoiceDO.beforeCallStart()</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">beforeCallStart() Hook Missing — No Rate Limiting or Auth on Incoming Voice Calls</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's voice agent accepts all incoming calls from VAPI without any validation of the caller, rate limiting per user, or check of whether the user's space has voice enabled. A malicious actor who discovers a VAPI phone_number_id can initiate unlimited voice sessions, consuming speech-to-text inference credits and LLM tokens. CF's withVoice mixin provides beforeCallStart(connection) which fires before the call is accepted — returning false rejects the call.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement beforeCallStart() for call gating: async beforeCallStart(connection: Connection): Promise&lt;boolean&gt; { const meta = connection.metadata as {userId?: string, spaceId?: string}; if (!meta.userId || !meta.spaceId) return false; const space = await this.getSpaceConfig(meta.spaceId); if (!space?.voiceEnabled) { await this.speak(connection, "Voice calls are not enabled for this workspace."); return false; } const rateKey = `voice:${meta.userId}:${Math.floor(Date.now()/60000)}`; const callCount = await incrementAndCheckLimit(rateKey, space.voiceCallsPerMinute ?? 1); if (!callCount.allowed) { await this.speak(connection, "Call rate limit exceeded. Please try again in a minute."); return false; } return true; }. Also verify caller identity using the JWT passed in the WebSocket upgrade query parameter.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S27" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S27</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-notifications-full / §05 — CF Realtime SFU</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">SFU WebSocket Auto-Reconnect Not Used — Browser Audio Drops Permanently on Network Hiccup</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">If the spec implements browser-to-agent audio via a WebSocket connection (directly or via CF SFU), transient network drops (mobile user enters elevator, WiFi handoff) cause the audio stream to drop permanently — the user must hang up and call back. CF's Realtime SFU WebSocket adapter (auto-reconnect feature, released May 2026) automatically retries for 5 seconds on disconnect, buffers outgoing audio during the retry window, and replays buffered packets after reconnection. This makes brief network interruptions transparent to the voice conversation.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Use the CF Realtime SFU WebSocket adapter with auto-reconnect enabled: import { createSFUWebSocketAdapter } from "@cloudflare/realtime/sfu-utils". const adapter = await createSFUWebSocketAdapter({ accountId: env.CF_ACCOUNT_ID, appId: env.SFU_APP_ID, appSecret: env.SFU_APP_SECRET, baseUrl: "https://rtc.live.cloudflare.com/v1", autoReconnect: true, reconnectTimeoutMs: 5000, audioBufferMs: 3000 }, sessionId). The adapter buffers up to 3 seconds of audio during disconnection. On reconnect, it sends buffered packets with correct sequence numbers so the receiver reconstructs the audio stream. Downsampling utilities are provided: downsample48kStereoTo16kMono() for browser mic input, upsample16kMonoTo48kStereo() for playback.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S28" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S28</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§operator-shell — Frontend / browser</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">VoiceClient Vanilla JS Integration Missing — No Non-React Voice Embedding Option for Builders</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec documents the operator shell (React app) but provides no voice integration option for builders who want to embed a voice assistant in their own website or mobile app without React. CF's @cloudflare/voice/client provides VoiceClient for vanilla JS: import { VoiceClient } from "@cloudflare/voice/client". const client = new VoiceClient({agent: "AssistantVoiceDO"}). client.addEventListener("statuschange", () =&gt; updateUI(client.status)). await client.startCall(). Status values: "idle" | "listening" | "thinking" | "speaking". This enables embedding via a simple &lt;script&gt; tag without a React dependency.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add VoiceClient as an embeddable widget option. Create a naelao-voice-widget.js bundle (CF Pages, hosted at /static/voice-widget.js): const client = new VoiceClient({agent: "AssistantVoiceDO", name: window.NAELAO_SPACE_ID}). client.addEventListener("statuschange", () =&gt; { document.getElementById("naelao-voice-status").textContent = client.status; }). document.getElementById("naelao-voice-btn").onclick = () =&gt; client.startCall(). This gives space builders a 3-line embed: &lt;script src="https://app.naelao.dev/static/voice-widget.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&lt;div id="naelao-voice-status"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;button id="naelao-voice-btn"&gt;Talk to Assistant&lt;/button&gt;. Document VoiceClient alongside useVoiceAgent in the platform SDK docs.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S29" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S29</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — space-assistants — scheduleEvery / cron-jobs.json</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">scheduleEvery() Not Used for Recurring Tasks — Cron Expression Parsing Hand-Rolled and Error-Prone</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's cron-jobs.json stores cron expressions ("0 9 * * 1-5" for weekdays at 9am) that the sweeper parses with a custom lightweight cron parser. CF's Agent SDK provides this.scheduleEvery(intervalSeconds, callback, payload, retryOptions) for recurring tasks and this.schedule("0 9 * * 1-5", callback) for cron-expression-based scheduling. The SDK parses cron expressions internally (no custom parser needed), stores schedules in cf_agents_schedules SQLite with retry state, and fires callbacks directly on the DO with at-most-once semantics (no duplicate-run race condition unlike the sweeper).</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace cron-jobs.json with SDK scheduling. When a user adds a recurring task: async addRecurringTask(task: RecurringTask) { if (task.intervalSeconds) { const sched = await this.scheduleEvery(task.intervalSeconds, "runTask", {taskId: task.id}); } else if (task.cron) { const sched = await this.schedule(task.cron, "runTask", {taskId: task.id}); } await this.sql`INSERT INTO user_schedules (task_id, sched_id) VALUES (${task.id}, ${sched.id})`; }. async runTask(payload: {taskId: string}) { const task = this.sql&lt;Task[]&gt;`SELECT * FROM tasks WHERE id = ${payload.taskId}`[0]; if (task) await this.executeTask(task); }. To list: this.getSchedules(). To cancel: this.cancelSchedule(schedId). SDK schedules survive DO hibernation (stored in SQLite) and retry automatically on failure.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S30" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S30</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§ai-gateway — AI Gateway / Workers AI</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">session_affinity Not Set for Workers AI Calls — KV Prompt Cache Hit Rate Suboptimal</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Anthropic's prompt caching achieves high hit rates when the same CF edge location processes requests for the same conversation (the cache is local to the CF datacenter). Workers AI routes requests to the datacenter closest to the client, but without affinity guarantees, subsequent requests in the same conversation may route to different datacenters, causing cache misses. CF's Agent base class provides the sessionAffinity getter which returns a stable value (the DO's Durable Object ID) that can be used as a routing hint to Workers AI for consistent datacenter selection.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Pass session affinity to Workers AI calls: const response = await this.env.AI.run("@cf/anthropic/claude-3-5-sonnet", { messages, session_affinity: this.sessionAffinity }). The sessionAffinity getter returns a stable string (the DO ID) that Workers AI uses to prefer routing to the same datacenter. This maximizes Anthropic prompt cache hits for the same conversation (same system prompt prefix). Also set a consistent request ID: const requestId = crypto.randomUUID(); pass as cf-aig-request-id header to AI Gateway for deduplication of retried requests. For the Think SDK, session affinity is used automatically — another reason to extend Think rather than using env.AI.run() directly.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S31" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S31</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-agent-do / §05 — Sub-agent URL routing</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Sub-Agent URL Routes Not Registered in wrangler.toml — /agents/ Path Returns 404</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The CF Agents SDK routes agent requests via URL paths: /agents/{class-kebab}/{name} for root agents and /agents/{parent}/{name}/sub/{child}/{name} for sub-agents. These routes must be handled by a Worker that calls routeAgentRequest(). The spec's wrangler.toml likely has specific routes for named endpoints but does not configure the /agents/* catch-all route. Without this, all WebSocket connections from useAgent(), useAgentChat(), and useVoiceAgent() fail with 404. The spec's naelao-router needs to be configured to handle this route pattern.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add /agents/* routing to naelao-router in wrangler.toml: [[routes]] pattern = "app.naelao.dev/agents/*" zone_name = "naelao.dev". In naelao-router Worker: import { routeAgentRequest } from "agents"; export default { async fetch(request, env, ctx) { const url = new URL(request.url); if (url.pathname.startsWith("/agents/")) { return await routeAgentRequest(request, env) ?? new Response("Agent not found", {status: 404}); } return handleOtherRoutes(request, env, ctx); } }. Ensure all agent DO classes are exported from the Worker entry point and bound in wrangler.toml so routeAgentRequest can resolve them by class name. Sub-agent routes (/agents/.../sub/...) are handled automatically by the SDK router.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S32" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S32</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-agent-do / §05 — Agent tool policy</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">needsApproval: true Tool Flag Pattern Not Applied — Per-Tool Client Approval Granularity Missing</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec implements approval at the action-category level (requires_approval_for: ["customer_email_send", "issue_refund"]) checked at the AgentDO level before dispatching tool calls. CF's tool definition supports needsApproval: true per individual tool — the SDK pauses execution and sends the approval request to the connected client over WebSocket with the specific tool name and arguments. This is finer-grained (per tool, not per category) and client-initiated (user sees exactly what will be executed). The spec's approach requires a separate approval tracking system; the SDK pattern is built-in.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Apply needsApproval: true to high-risk tools in the tool definitions: tools: { sendEmail: tool({description: "Send email", inputSchema: emailSchema, needsApproval: true, execute: sendEmailFn}), processRefund: tool({description: "Process refund", inputSchema: refundSchema, needsApproval: true, execute: refundFn}), searchKnowledge: tool({description: "Search knowledge base", inputSchema: searchSchema, needsApproval: false, execute: searchFn}) }. The SDK handles the pause, WebSocket notification to the client, and resume. Low-risk tools (read-only, non-destructive) set needsApproval: false and execute immediately. The requires_approval_for list in space config can be used to dynamically set needsApproval based on space settings: const requiresApproval = spaceConfig.approvalRequired.includes(toolName).</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S33" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S33</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — space-assistants — AssistantDO voice + VAPI</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">forceEndCall() Not Implemented — Voice Calls Cannot Be Terminated Programmatically</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's voice call via VAPI can be terminated by the user hanging up or by the call timeout, but there is no mechanism for the agent to end the call programmatically: if the refund was approved, if the issue was resolved, if the user became abusive, or if the call has exceeded the space's voice minute budget. VAPI provides an end-call REST API but this requires an additional HTTP call with the call ID. CF's withVoice mixin provides forceEndCall(connection) which immediately ends the call from within the DO, freeing the TTS and STT pipeline.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement call termination triggers in the agent: async onTurn(transcript, ctx) { const reply = await this.runAgentTurn(transcript); if (shouldEndCall(reply)) { await this.speak(ctx.connection, "Your request has been processed. Goodbye!"); this.forceEndCall(ctx.connection); return reply; } return reply; }. shouldEndCall() checks: (1) the LLM reply contains an end-call intent (classify with a simple prompt), (2) call duration &gt; space.maxVoiceMinutes, (3) credit balance &lt; MIN_BALANCE. Also implement credit tracking in onTurn: const callDuration = (Date.now() - callStartTime) / 60000; await this.deductCredits(spaceId, callDuration * VOICE_COST_PER_MINUTE); if (creditsExhausted) { await this.speak(ctx.connection, "Voice credit exhausted."); this.forceEndCall(ctx.connection); }.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S34" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S34</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — space-assistants / §16 — Agent onConnect()</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Agent Connection Handler Not Implementing CF onConnect() Pattern — Auth Checked in Message Handler</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec authenticates WebSocket connections by checking the JWT in the first WebSocket message received (after upgrade). CF's Agent base class provides onConnect(connection, ctx) which fires immediately after WebSocket upgrade but before the first message. Returning or throwing from onConnect() terminates the connection before any messages are exchanged — the correct place for auth. Checking auth in the message handler means the connection is established (DO is woken, resources allocated) before the caller is authenticated, creating a DoS vector where unauthenticated clients can wake DOs at will.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement onConnect() for early authentication: async onConnect(connection: Connection, ctx: ConnectionContext) { const url = new URL(ctx.request.url); const token = url.searchParams.get("token"); if (!token) { connection.close(4001, "Authentication required"); return; } try { const payload = await verifyJWT(token, this.env.JWT_SECRET); if (payload.spaceId !== this.spaceId) { connection.close(4003, "Forbidden"); return; } connection.setState({userId: payload.sub, spaceId: payload.spaceId, role: payload.role}); } catch (e) { connection.close(4001, "Invalid token"); return; } }. Connection state set in onConnect() is available in all subsequent handlers (onMessage, onClose) via connection.state. This prevents unauthenticated clients from waking the DO and consuming resources.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-S35" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-S35</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — space-assistants — AssistantDO multi-agent orchestration</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No Multi-Agent Team Composition Pattern — CF Agent Facet Hierarchy Not Used for Specialist Agents</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec dispatches one sub-agent at a time (parent → single child) for background tasks. CF's Agent SDK supports multi-level facet hierarchies: AssistantDO → [ResearcherDO, CoderDO, ReviewerDO] in parallel, with each specialist running as a named sub-agent that can itself spawn deeper sub-agents. The spec has no pattern for: (1) parallel multi-specialist dispatch, (2) aggregating results from multiple concurrent sub-agents, (3) specialist agent selection based on task type, (4) specialist agents with their own persistent memory and tool sets. This limits complex tasks to sequential single-agent execution.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement a multi-agent team pattern using CF facets: async orchestrateTask(task: ComplexTask) { const [researcher, coder, reviewer] = await Promise.all([this.subAgent(ResearcherDO, `research-${task.id}`), this.subAgent(CoderDO, `code-${task.id}`), this.subAgent(ReviewerDO, `review-${task.id}`)]); const [research, code] = await Promise.all([researcher.research(task.context), coder.draft(task.spec)]); const review = await reviewer.review({research, code, requirements: task.requirements}); return {research, code, review}; }. Each specialist DO has its own: tool set (ResearcherDO has web search + knowledge tools, CoderDO has workspace tools, ReviewerDO has read-only tools), memory (separate SQLite in each DO), and system prompt. The parallel execution collapses what would be a sequential 30-step loop into 3 concurrent 10-step tasks, reducing total latency by ~3x.</p></div>
  </div>
  </div>


<div class="sep"></div>
<section id="appendix-t" style="padding:60px 0 40px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 24px;">
    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.15em;text-transform:uppercase;">Appendix T</span>
    <h2 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:36px;font-weight:700;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.02em;margin:8px 0 6px;">Twelfth-Pass: Space Assistant Completeness — OpenClaw Feature Parity, MCP Apps, Multi-Container, Skills Self-Improvement</h2>
    <p style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:15px;color:var(--tx);line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 40px;">35 architectural gaps covering: OpenClaw feature parity (continuous background loop, fiber concurrency, proactive trigger types, tool chaining, skill marketplace, inline sub-agent delegation, channel hub normalization, delegation chain), MCP Apps ecosystem (App Store, installed app persistence, tool approval flow, scope model, McpAgent/AIChatAgent separation, OAuth callback), Skills self-improvement via Hermes pattern (crystallization loop, versioning, test suite, within-session cache, conflict resolution), multi-container coordination (pool management, container specialization, filesystem sync, pre-warming, network policy), shared agent user data isolation (memory namespace, Vectorize filter, session isolation, R2 path divergence), instance management (atomic limit enforcement, hibernation flush, spaceId/userId restoration, observability), and missing CF primitive usage (Vectorize write path, decay_rate application, ctx.waitUntil() crash).</p>

  <div id="G-T01" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T01</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — AssistantDO / keepAlive</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No Continuous Background Loop — Heartbeat Driven by External Sweeper Instead of Native this.schedule() + this.keepAliveWhile()</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec drives AssistantDO heartbeats from an external naelao-sweeper Worker running every 5 minutes via CF Cron Trigger. This creates a distributed coordination problem: the sweeper must maintain a KV registry of all active assistants, wake each DO individually via HTTP fetch, and handle failures across potentially thousands of DOs simultaneously. The CF Agents SDK provides this.keepAliveWhile(condition) and this.keepAlive() to prevent DO hibernation without any external coordination, and this.schedule(delay, callback, payload) for self-scheduling the next heartbeat iteration. These primitives were designed exactly for this continuous-background-loop pattern and eliminate the sweeper Worker entirely.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace the external sweeper with internal DO scheduling:
```typescript
async onStart() {
  // Self-schedule first heartbeat immediately
  await this.schedule(300_000, "heartbeat", {});
}

async heartbeat(payload: unknown) {
  await this.runHeartbeatTasks();
  // Re-schedule next heartbeat
  await this.schedule(300_000, "heartbeat", {});
}

async runLongFiberTask(fiber: Fiber) {
  // Prevent hibernation while fiber is running
  return await this.keepAliveWhile(async () =&gt; {
    return await executeFiber(fiber);
  });
}
```
Remove the naelao-sweeper Worker and its KV registry entirely. Each DO manages its own wakeup cycle independently with zero cross-DO coordination overhead.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T02" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T02</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — AssistantDO / fiber concurrency</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Fiber Concurrency Model Incomplete — Fibers Stored in SQLite but Executed Sequentially, Not in Parallel</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec stores "fibers" in DO SQLite as a queue and processes them one at a time in the heartbeat loop. The OpenClaw architecture the spec claims to implement runs multiple fibers concurrently using Promise.allSettled() so that a long-running Python execution fiber does not block a quick Slack reply fiber. In a Durable Object, concurrent JavaScript execution is safe within a single turn because DOs are single-threaded per I/O boundary — multiple awaited operations can run in parallel with Promise.all. The spec's sequential fiber drain loop serialises all work, causing a blocked long-running task to delay time-sensitive user-facing responses.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace sequential fiber drain with concurrent execution:
```typescript
async heartbeat() {
  const pendingFibers = this.sql&lt;Fiber[]&gt;`
    SELECT * FROM fibers
    WHERE status = 'pending'
    ORDER BY priority DESC, created_at ASC
    LIMIT 10
  `;
  const results = await Promise.allSettled(
    pendingFibers.map(f =&gt; this.executeFiber(f))
  );
  results.forEach((r, i) =&gt; {
    if (r.status === 'rejected') {
      this.markFiberFailed(pendingFibers[i].id, r.reason);
    }
  });
}
```
Use ctx.waitUntil() via the Worker's ExecutionContext (passed in fetch, not DurableObjectState) to extend fiber execution beyond the heartbeat request boundary. Cap concurrent fibers at a configurable limit (default 5) to avoid memory pressure.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T03" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T03</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — AssistantDO / proactive triggers</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Proactive Trigger Types Limited to Time-Based Cron — Event-Based, State-Change, and Threshold Triggers Absent</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec implements proactive agent triggers exclusively as time-based cron schedules stored in cron-jobs.json. The OpenClaw architecture defines four trigger types: (1) time-based (cron), (2) event-based (incoming webhook payload matching a filter), (3) state-change-based (a watched value in DO SQLite crosses a boundary), and (4) threshold-based (a metric — e.g., unread Slack messages &gt; 20). The spec has no mechanism for the agent to react to an incoming Stripe webhook, no way to watch for a DB column crossing a threshold, and no event filter engine. This limits proactive behavior to scheduled tasks only.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add a trigger registry table and evaluation engine:
```typescript
// In onStart() schema init
this.sql`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS triggers (
  id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  type TEXT NOT NULL CHECK(type IN ('cron','event','state_change','threshold')),
  filter_json TEXT NOT NULL,
  action_json TEXT NOT NULL,
  last_fired_at INTEGER
)`;

// Event-based trigger evaluation (called from webhook handler)
async evaluateTriggers(event: WebhookEvent) {
  const triggers = this.sql&lt;Trigger[]&gt;`
    SELECT * FROM triggers WHERE type = 'event'
  `;
  for (const t of triggers) {
    const filter = JSON.parse(t.filter_json);
    if (matchesFilter(event, filter)) {
      await this.spawnFiber(JSON.parse(t.action_json), event);
    }
  }
}
```
Evaluate threshold triggers in the heartbeat loop by querying watched metrics. Route incoming webhooks (Stripe, GitHub, Slack events) through a shared /webhooks/{spaceId} endpoint that calls evaluateTriggers on the target AssistantDO.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T04" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T04</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — AssistantDO / tool execution</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Tool Chaining Dependency Graph Absent — Tools Run Sequentially With No DAG-Based Composition</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec runs LLM-selected tools sequentially in a simple loop: call tool, get result, append to messages, call LLM again. OpenClaw's tool composition model allows defining a dependency graph (DAG) where tool A's output is declared as input to tool B, enabling parallel execution of independent branches and typed data flow between tools. For example: (fetch_user_profile, fetch_account_history) run in parallel; their results feed summarize_customer_context; that feeds both draft_response and check_policy simultaneously. The spec's sequential loop cannot express this structure, adding unnecessary latency on multi-tool tasks.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Introduce a tool pipeline definition: tools can declare outputSchema and inputFrom fields:
```typescript
const tools = {
  fetchProfile: tool({
    outputSchema: userProfileSchema,
    execute: ({userId}) =&gt; fetchUserProfile(userId)
  }),
  fetchHistory: tool({
    outputSchema: historySchema,
    execute: ({userId}) =&gt; fetchHistory(userId)
  }),
  summarize: tool({
    inputFrom: ['fetchProfile', 'fetchHistory'],
    execute: ({fetchProfile, fetchHistory}) =&gt;
      summarizeContext(fetchProfile, fetchHistory)
  })
};
```
Implement a DAG executor: topological sort the tool call graph, execute independent nodes with Promise.all(), pass resolved outputs to dependent nodes. Log the full DAG execution trace to DO SQLite for debugging. This collapses three sequential LLM round-trips into a single parallel fan-out + join.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T05" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T05</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — Skills / marketplace</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Skill Marketplace Absent — Platform Skills Are Static; No User-Installable Skill Packs</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec defines three skill tiers (Platform, Space-level, Personal) but Tier 1 Platform skills are static files in R2 maintained by the Naelao team. There is no mechanism for users or space admins to browse and install pre-built skill packs from a marketplace (e.g., "HubSpot CRM Skills", "GitHub Code Review Skills", "Notion Writing Skills"). The OpenClaw architecture includes an agent marketplace where skill packs are versioned bundles of tools + instructions that can be installed into an agent with one click. Without this, the skill ecosystem is closed to Naelao-authored content only.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Create a skill marketplace data model and installation flow:
```typescript
// In naelao-marketplace Worker / DO
interface SkillPack {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  version: string;
  tier: 'platform' | 'community' | 'private';
  skills: Skill[];
  tools: ToolDefinition[];
  r2_key: string; // bundled skill pack in global R2
}

// In AssistantDO: install a skill pack
async installSkillPack(packId: string) {
  const pack = await this.env.MARKETPLACE_DO
    .get(packId).getMetadata();
  // Write skills to personal tier in assistant R2
  await this.env.R2.put(
    `spaces/${this.spaceId}/assistants/${this.userId}/skills/packs/${packId}.json`,
    JSON.stringify(pack)
  );
  await this.sql`INSERT OR REPLACE INTO installed_packs
    (pack_id, version, installed_at)
    VALUES (${packId}, ${pack.version}, unixepoch('now')*1000)`;
}
```
Expose a /marketplace endpoint in the operator shell listing available packs by category, rating, and install count.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T06" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T06</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — AssistantDO / sub-agent delegation</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No Inline Sub-Agent Delegation — Parent Always Fire-and-Forgets; Cannot Await Child Result Synchronously</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec's sub-agent dispatch is always fire-and-forget: the parent AssistantDO POSTs a task to a child agent and moves on. When the child completes, it writes results to a shared SQLite table. The parent only learns of completion on its next heartbeat poll (up to 5 minutes later). OpenClaw's agent delegation model allows a parent to await a child inline — blocking the parent fiber until the child returns a result — enabling complex multi-step workflows where intermediate results feed subsequent LLM calls. The spec has no spawnSubAgent() that returns a Promise resolving to the child's output.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement inline delegation using CF sub-agent RPC:
```typescript
// In AssistantDO
async delegateResearch(topic: string): Promise&lt;string&gt; {
  // this.subAgent() returns a typed RPC stub
  const researcher = await this.subAgent(
    ResearcherDO,
    `researcher-${this.userId}-${Date.now()}`
  );
  // Awaits child completion inline — parent fiber blocks here
  const result = await researcher.research(topic);
  return result;
}

// Usage in agent turn
const research = await this.delegateResearch(userQuery);
const answer = await this.llm([...context, research]);
```
For fire-and-forget tasks (background indexing, non-blocking notifications), retain the current pattern. For tasks where the parent needs the result before continuing the user-facing turn, use inline delegation. Document the distinction in the spec: inline vs. background delegation patterns.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T07" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T07</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — Channel hub / InboxMessage</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Channel Hub Normalization Missing — Slack, Discord, Telegram Have Separate Handlers With No Unified Message Format</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec has separate handler classes for each channel: SlackHandler, DiscordHandler, TelegramHandler, EmailHandler. Each handler parses the channel-native payload format, extracts the message text and user ID, and calls into the agent with channel-specific parameters. When adding a new channel (e.g., WhatsApp, Microsoft Teams), a developer must write a new handler from scratch and integrate it with AssistantDO separately. The OpenClaw channel hub normalizes all incoming messages to a single InboxMessage format before they reach the agent, so the agent never deals with channel-specific payload structures.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Define a canonical InboxMessage format and normalize at handler boundaries:
```typescript
interface InboxMessage {
  id: string;
  channelType: 'slack' | 'discord' | 'telegram' | 'email' | 'sms' | 'web';
  channelId: string; // e.g. Slack channel ID
  userId: string; // platform user ID
  displayName: string;
  text: string;
  attachments: Attachment[];
  replyTo?: string; // thread/message ID for replies
  timestamp: number;
  raw: unknown; // original payload for audit
}

// Each handler converts to InboxMessage
class SlackHandler {
  normalize(payload: SlackEvent): InboxMessage {
    return {
      channelType: 'slack',
      channelId: payload.event.channel,
      userId: payload.event.user,
      text: payload.event.text,
      // ...
    };
  }
}

// AssistantDO receives only InboxMessage
async onInboxMessage(msg: InboxMessage) { ... }
```
Store InboxMessages in a unified inbox table in AssistantDO SQLite for full conversation history across channels.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T08" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T08</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — AssistantDO / sub-agent result delivery</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No Agent Delegation Chain — Sub-Agent Results Returned via Polling, Not Synchronous Parent Notification</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">When a sub-agent finishes its task, it writes results to a shared KV or SQLite table and exits. The parent AssistantDO discovers the result on the next heartbeat poll (5-minute cycle). This polling model introduces up to 5 minutes of latency between task completion and the parent acting on the result — unacceptable for user-facing workflows where the agent is waiting for a research result before drafting a response. The CF parentAgent() RPC call allows a child to push its result directly to the parent's onSubAgentComplete() method, eliminating the polling delay.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace result polling with push notification via parentAgent():
```typescript
// In child ResearcherDO
async research(topic: string) {
  const result = await doResearch(topic);
  // Push result to parent immediately on completion
  const parent = await this.parentAgent(AssistantDO);
  await parent.onSubAgentComplete({
    taskId: this.taskId,
    result,
    completedAt: Date.now()
  });
  return result;
}

// In parent AssistantDO
async onSubAgentComplete(
  payload: {taskId: string; result: string; completedAt: number}
) {
  // Resume the waiting fiber immediately
  await this.resumeFiber(payload.taskId, payload.result);
  await this.sql`
    UPDATE fibers SET status='complete', result=${payload.result}
    WHERE id = ${payload.taskId}
  `;
}
```
The parent fiber that was awaiting child completion is resumed within milliseconds of child completion. Remove the result polling loop from the heartbeat — it is only needed as a fallback for crashed children.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T09" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T09</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — AssistantDO / MCP App Store</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No MCP App Store Concept — Users Cannot Browse, Install, or Manage MCP Apps That Extend AssistantDO</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec defines AssistantDO as an MCP server but provides no mechanism for users to install third-party MCP servers (MCP Apps) into their assistant to extend its tool set. In the OpenAI/OpenClaw model, MCP Apps are remote MCP servers that users can install via an OAuth flow — the agent then has access to their tools automatically. Without an App Store concept, the Naelao platform is locked to only the tools the Naelao team ships, preventing the ecosystem growth that makes agent platforms valuable. CF natively supports remote MCP servers via the McpAgent class and can serve as an MCP proxy.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Define the MCP App Store architecture:
1. Create a naelao-app-store Worker/DO that maintains a registry of available MCP Apps (name, description, OAuth endpoints, tool manifest, scopes).
2. Add an /app-store endpoint in the operator shell for browsing apps.
3. On install click, initiate OAuth flow: redirect user to MCP server's OAuth URL with redirect_uri=https://app.naelao.dev/mcp-oauth-callback.
4. On callback, store the access token and installed app metadata in AssistantDO SQLite (see G-T10).
5. On each agent turn, load installed app tools by fetching the MCP server's /tools endpoint using the stored access token.

Store the App Store registry in a global DO: appStoreStub.registerApp(appManifest). Space admins can publish private apps only visible within their space.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T10" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T10</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — AssistantDO / mcp_apps table</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Installed MCP App State Not Persisted — No mcp_apps Table in AssistantDO SQLite</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Even if an MCP App install flow is implemented, the spec has no SQLite table to store installed app metadata (OAuth tokens, scopes, app ID, installation date). Without persistent storage, installed apps are lost on DO hibernation. The agent would need to re-authenticate every time it wakes, breaking any MCP App integration. Additionally, there is no mechanism to list which apps a user has installed, revoke an app's access, or refresh expired OAuth tokens.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add the mcp_apps table in AssistantDO onStart():
```typescript
this.sql`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS mcp_apps (
  app_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  name TEXT NOT NULL,
  mcp_server_url TEXT NOT NULL,
  access_token TEXT NOT NULL,
  refresh_token TEXT,
  token_expires_at INTEGER,
  scopes_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '[]',
  installed_at INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT (unixepoch('now')*1000),
  last_used_at INTEGER,
  status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active'
    CHECK(status IN ('active','revoked','error'))
)`;

// Token refresh logic
async getAppToken(appId: string): Promise&lt;string&gt; {
  const app = this.sql&lt;McpApp[]&gt;`
    SELECT * FROM mcp_apps WHERE app_id = ${appId}
      AND status = 'active'
  `[0];
  if (!app) throw new Error('App not installed');
  if (app.token_expires_at &amp;&amp; app.token_expires_at &lt; Date.now()) {
    return await this.refreshAppToken(app);
  }
  return app.access_token;
}
```
Store OAuth tokens encrypted using the space's encryption key (env.ENCRYPTION_KEY). Never log access tokens in Analytics Engine or console.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T11" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T11</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — AssistantDO / MCP tool approval</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">MCP App Tool Approval Flow Missing — Installed MCP App Tools Bypass needsApproval Requirement on First Use</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">When a user installs an MCP App, its tools immediately become available to the agent with no human approval required on first execution. If a malicious or misconfigured MCP App exposes a "delete_all_files" tool, the agent could invoke it without the user ever seeing an approval prompt. The CF Agents SDK's needsApproval: true flag should be automatically applied to all tools from installed MCP Apps until the user has explicitly approved each tool at least once. First-use approval creates a trust baseline for the installed app's tool set.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement first-use approval for MCP app tools:
```typescript
async callMcpAppTool(
  appId: string, toolName: string, args: unknown
) {
  const approval = this.sql&lt;ApprovalRecord[]&gt;`
    SELECT * FROM mcp_tool_approvals
    WHERE app_id = ${appId} AND tool_name = ${toolName}
  `[0];

  if (!approval) {
    // First use: require explicit approval
    return await this.requestHumanApproval({
      type: 'mcp_tool_first_use',
      appId, toolName, args,
      message: `MCP App "${appId}" wants to run "${toolName}" for the first time.`
    });
  }

  // Approved: execute the tool
  return await this.invokeMcpTool(appId, toolName, args);
}
```
Add a mcp_tool_approvals table: (app_id, tool_name, approved_at, approved_by_user_id). After first approval, subsequent calls execute without prompting. Revocation of an app (mcp_apps.status = 'revoked') also clears its tool approvals.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T12" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T12</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — AssistantDO / MCP app scopes</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No MCP App Scopes/Permissions Model — App Installation Grants All Tools With No Per-Tool Scope Manifest</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec has no concept of per-tool scopes for MCP Apps. In the OpenAI plugin/MCP App model, each app publishes a scope manifest listing which tools require which permission levels (read-only, write, destructive). The user approves a scope manifest at install time, not individual tool calls. An MCP App that has only read scopes approved cannot execute write or destructive tools even if the agent tries to call them. Without scopes, all installed app tools have equal access, making it impossible to install a "read-only analytics" MCP App without also granting its incidentally available write tools.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add scope enforcement to MCP app tool dispatch:
```typescript
interface McpAppScope {
  toolName: string;
  level: 'read' | 'write' | 'destructive';
  description: string;
}

// At install time, user approves specific scopes
async installMcpApp(
  appId: string,
  approvedScopes: McpAppScope[]
) {
  const scopeJson = JSON.stringify(approvedScopes);
  await this.sql`INSERT INTO mcp_apps
    (app_id, scopes_json, ...)
    VALUES (${appId}, ${scopeJson}, ...)`;
}

// At tool call time, enforce scopes
async callMcpAppTool(
  appId: string, toolName: string, args: unknown
) {
  const app = await this.getMcpApp(appId);
  const scopes: McpAppScope[] = JSON.parse(app.scopes_json);
  const allowed = scopes.find(s =&gt; s.toolName === toolName);
  if (!allowed) {
    throw new Error(
      `Tool "${toolName}" not in approved scopes for app "${appId}"
    `);
  }
  return await this.invokeMcpTool(appId, toolName, args);
}
```
Display the scope manifest in the App Store install UI so users understand what they are approving.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T13" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T13</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — AssistantDO / McpAgent vs AIChatAgent</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">AssistantDO Conflates MCP Server and AIChatAgent Roles — Violates CF SDK Class Hierarchy</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec has AssistantDO simultaneously acting as: (1) an MCP server (exposing tools to external MCP clients via the MCP protocol), and (2) an AIChatAgent (running the inner LLM loop, managing conversation history, handling WebSocket chat sessions). The CF Agents SDK separates these: McpAgent extends Agent and handles MCP protocol (tool listing, tool execution over MCP JSON-RPC), while AIChatAgent extends Think and handles the conversational AI loop. Conflating both in one DO means MCP protocol messages and WebSocket chat messages share the same handler, creating routing ambiguity and preventing independent scaling of MCP serving vs. chat serving.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Separate AssistantDO into two distinct classes:
```typescript
// MCP server: exposes tools to external MCP clients
class AssistantMcpDO extends McpAgent&lt;Env&gt; {
  server = new McpServer({name: 'Naelao Assistant'});

  async init() {
    this.server.tool('search_memory', searchMemorySchema,
      (args) =&gt; this.searchMemory(args));
    this.server.tool('create_task', createTaskSchema,
      (args) =&gt; this.createTask(args));
  }
}

// Chat agent: handles user-facing conversation
class AssistantDO extends AIChatAgent&lt;Env, AssistantState&gt; {
  async onChatMessage(message: ChatMessage) {
    return await this.think(message.content);
  }
}
```
Route /mcp/* requests to AssistantMcpDO and /agents/assistant-do/* requests to AssistantDO. This is the pattern CF's moltworker reference implementation uses. The McpDO can delegate tool execution back to the main AssistantDO via service binding RPC.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T14" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T14</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — AssistantDO / MCP OAuth callback</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No Remote MCP Server OAuth Callback Handling — MCP App Installation Flow Has No Redirect URI Handler</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">MCP App installation via OAuth requires a redirect URI: after the user grants access on the MCP server's OAuth page, the browser redirects to https://app.naelao.dev/mcp-oauth-callback?code=...&amp;state=... The naelao-assistant Worker has no handler for this path. The OAuth code cannot be exchanged for an access token, so the installation flow never completes. This is a blocking gap for any MCP App ecosystem: without a callback handler, zero MCP apps can be installed.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add an OAuth callback handler to naelao-assistant (or naelao-router) Worker:
```typescript
// In naelao-router fetch handler
if (url.pathname === '/mcp-oauth-callback') {
  const code = url.searchParams.get('code');
  const state = url.searchParams.get('state');
  if (!code || !state) {
    return new Response('Bad Request', {status: 400});
  }
  // Decode state: {spaceId, userId, appId, codeVerifier}
  const stateData = await verifyAndDecodeOAuthState(
    state, env.JWT_SECRET
  );
  // Exchange code for token
  const app = await getAppManifest(stateData.appId);
  const tokenResponse = await fetch(app.tokenEndpoint, {
    method: 'POST',
    body: new URLSearchParams({
      grant_type: 'authorization_code', code,
      code_verifier: stateData.codeVerifier,
      redirect_uri: 'https://app.naelao.dev/mcp-oauth-callback',
      client_id: env.MCP_CLIENT_ID
    })
  });
  const token = await tokenResponse.json();
  // Store token in AssistantDO
  const assistantDO = getAssistantDO(
    stateData.spaceId, stateData.userId, env
  );
  await assistantDO.storeMcpAppToken(stateData.appId, token);
  return Response.redirect('/app-store?installed=' + stateData.appId);
}
```
Use PKCE (code_verifier/code_challenge) for security. Store the OAuth state JWT in a short-lived KV entry (TTL: 10 minutes) to prevent CSRF and replay attacks.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T15" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T15</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — Skills / Hermes self-improvement</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Skills Never Self-Improve — No Post-Task Skill Crystallization Loop (Hermes Pattern)</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec defines skills as static instruction files stored in R2, written once by users or the Naelao team. After an agent successfully completes a complex task, the specific tool call sequence and reasoning chain that worked are discarded. The Hermes self-improvement pattern defines: after each successful agentic task completion, extract the successful tool call sequence as a reusable skill, store it in the agent's personal skill tier, and use it automatically next time a similar task appears. This transforms each agent from a static instruction-follower into a continuously improving system that gets better at tasks it has done before.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement post-task skill crystallization:
```typescript
async onTaskComplete(task: CompletedTask) {
  if (!task.success || task.turnCount &lt; 3) return;

  // Extract the successful tool call sequence
  const toolSequence = task.messages
    .filter(m =&gt; m.role === 'assistant' &amp;&amp; m.tool_calls)
    .map(m =&gt; m.tool_calls);

  if (toolSequence.length === 0) return;

  // Ask the LLM to crystallize a skill from this run
  const skillDef = await this.llm([{
    role: 'system',
    content: 'Extract a reusable skill from this successful task run.'
  }, {
    role: 'user',
    content: JSON.stringify({task: task.description, toolSequence})
  }]);

  // Store as a new personal-tier skill
  await this.addSkill({
    id: `crystallized-${Date.now()}`,
    name: skillDef.name,
    description: skillDef.description,
    instructions: skillDef.instructions,
    sourceTask: task.id,
    version: 1
  });
}
```
Gate crystallization on task success (no human corrections after completion) and minimum complexity (&gt;= 3 tool calls). Log crystallization events to Analytics Engine for observability.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T16" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T16</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — Skills / versioning</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No Skill Versioning — Self-Modified Skills Overwrite Previous Version With No Rollback Capability</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">When a skill is self-improved (G-T15) or manually edited by a user, the new version overwrites the old R2 object. If the new version causes the agent to perform worse (more errors, user complaints, lower task completion rate), there is no way to rollback to the previous version. The spec has no version history for skills, no comparison between skill versions, and no automatic rollback trigger if a new version degrades performance metrics.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add skill versioning:
```typescript
// R2 key pattern with version
const skillKey = (skillId: string, version: number) =&gt;
  `spaces/${spaceId}/assistants/${userId}/skills/${skillId}/v${version}.json`;

async addSkill(skill: Skill) {
  const existing = await this.getLatestSkill(skill.id);
  const version = existing ? existing.version + 1 : 1;
  const key = skillKey(skill.id, version);
  await this.env.R2.put(key, JSON.stringify({...skill, version}));

  // Update skill index
  await this.sql`INSERT OR REPLACE INTO skill_versions
    (skill_id, version, r2_key, created_at)
    VALUES (${skill.id}, ${version}, ${key}, unixepoch('now')*1000)`;
  await this.sql`UPDATE skills SET current_version = ${version}
    WHERE id = ${skill.id}`;
}

async rollbackSkill(skillId: string, toVersion: number) {
  await this.sql`UPDATE skills
    SET current_version = ${toVersion}
    WHERE id = ${skillId}`;
}
```
Expose rollback in the operator shell skill management UI. Retain last 10 versions per skill in R2; prune older versions automatically. Track per-version task success rate to enable data-driven rollback decisions.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T17" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T17</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — Skills / test suite</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No Skill Test Suite — Self-Modified Skills Have No Automated Validation After Modification</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">When the Hermes skill crystallization loop (G-T15) or a user edit modifies a skill, the new version is immediately live with no automated validation. A poorly crystallized skill could instruct the agent to always use a deprecated tool, include incorrect parameter names, or contradict the base system prompt. Without test cases, the only signal that a skill version is broken is user complaints after the damage is done. The OpenClaw framework defines per-skill test cases: a set of input/expected-output pairs that are auto-run after any skill modification.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add a skill test framework:
```typescript
interface SkillTest {
  id: string;
  skill_id: string;
  input_message: string;
  expected_tool_calls: string[]; // tool names that must appear
  forbidden_tool_calls: string[]; // tools that must NOT appear
  expected_output_contains: string[];
}

async runSkillTests(skillId: string): Promise&lt;TestResult[]&gt; {
  const tests = this.sql&lt;SkillTest[]&gt;`
    SELECT * FROM skill_tests WHERE skill_id = ${skillId}
  `;
  const results = await Promise.all(tests.map(async t =&gt; {
    const run = await this.testRun(t.input_message, skillId);
    const passed =
      t.expected_tool_calls.every(tc =&gt;
        run.toolCalls.includes(tc)) &amp;&amp;
      t.forbidden_tool_calls.every(tc =&gt;
        !run.toolCalls.includes(tc));
    return {testId: t.id, passed, run};
  }));
  // Auto-rollback if any test fails
  if (results.some(r =&gt; !r.passed)) {
    await this.rollbackSkill(skillId);
  }
  return results;
}
```
Run tests asynchronously after skill modification using this.queue('runSkillTests', {skillId}). Alert the space admin if a skill auto-rolled back.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div id="G-T18" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-T18</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
      <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§05 — Space Assistant — Skills / caching</span>
    </div>
    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Skill Loading Not Cached Within Session — All Three Tiers Re-Read From R2 on Every Agent Turn</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec loads skills by reading R2 objects for all three tiers (Platform, Space, Personal) on each agent turn to compose the system prompt. A single turn may load 10-30 skill files across three R2 prefixes. With R2 read latency of 20-40ms per object and no within-session cache, a turn that activates 5 skills incurs 100-200ms of R2 read overhead on top of LLM latency. Skills rarely change during an active session (they change between sessions when a user edits them). An in-memory cache keyed by skill version avoids redundant R2 reads within the same DO activation.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add an in-memory skill cache with version-based invalidation:
```typescript
class AssistantDO extends AIChatAgent&lt;Env&gt; {
  // In-memory cache: survives multiple turns within same activation
  private skillCache = new Map&lt;string, {version: number; skill: Skill}&gt;();

  async loadSkills(): Promise&lt;Skill[]&gt; {
    const index = this.sql&lt;SkillIndex[]&gt;`
      SELECT id, current_version FROM skills WHERE active = 1
    `;
    const skills = await Promise.all(index.map(async ({id, current_version}) =&gt; {
      const cached = this.skillCache.get(id);
      if (cached?.version === current_version) return cached.skill;

      // Cache miss: read from R2
      const obj = await this.env.R2.get(
        skillKey(id, current_version)
      );
      const skill = await obj!.json&lt;Skill&gt;();
      this.skillCache.set(id, {version: current_version, skill});
      return skill;
    }));
    return skills;
  }
}
```
The cache is invalidated on hibernation (in-memory state is lost) but rebuilt from SQLite index on the next activation. Cache size is bounded by the number of active skills (typically &lt;50) — no eviction policy needed.</p></div>
