    <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">ctx.waitUntil() Called on DurableObjectState — DurableObjectState Does Not Expose waitUntil(); Crashes at Runtime</h3>
    <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The spec calls this.ctx.waitUntil(promise) inside the Slack webhook handler to defer post-response processing. In a Cloudflare Worker fetch handler, ctx (ExecutionContext) exposes waitUntil(). However, inside a Durable Object, this.ctx is a DurableObjectState, which does NOT have a waitUntil() method — this method only exists on ExecutionContext (the second argument to Worker fetch). Calling this.ctx.waitUntil(promise) in a DO throws "TypeError: this.ctx.waitUntil is not a function" at runtime, crashing the DO's request handler and returning a 500 error to the Slack webhook endpoint, causing Slack to retry the webhook up to 3 times and potentially triggering duplicate processing.</p></div>
    <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Replace this.ctx.waitUntil() with the correct pattern for Durable Objects:

Option 1 — Pass the Worker ctx into the DO method:
```typescript
// In naelao-router Worker fetch handler:
const doStub = env.ASSISTANT_DO.get(doId);
await doStub.handleSlackWebhook(payload, ctx); // pass ctx

// In AssistantDO:
async handleSlackWebhook(payload: SlackPayload, ctx: ExecutionContext) {
  // ctx here is the Worker's ExecutionContext — has waitUntil()
  ctx.waitUntil(this.processSlackEvent(payload));
  return new Response(null, {status: 200});
}
```

Option 2 — Use a background Set of promises (no ctx needed):
```typescript
class AssistantDO extends AIChatAgent&lt;Env&gt; {
  private backgroundWork = new Set&lt;Promise&lt;unknown&gt;&gt;();

  addBackgroundWork(p: Promise&lt;unknown&gt;) {
    const tracked = p.finally(() =&gt; this.backgroundWork.delete(tracked));
    this.backgroundWork.add(tracked);
  }

  async fetch(request: Request): Promise&lt;Response&gt; {
    // ... handle request ...
    this.addBackgroundWork(this.processSlackEvent(payload));
    return new Response(null, {status: 200});
    // Background promises run until DO hibernates
  }
}
```
Option 2 is simpler but background work may be cut short by hibernation. Option 1 extends the Worker invocation's lifetime guarantee. Use Option 1 for critical post-webhook processing (memory extraction, analytics writes).</p></div>
  </div>
  </div>


<section id="appendix-u" style="margin-top:80px;">
<h2 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:32px;font-weight:700;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.02em;margin:0 0 6px;">Appendix U — Thirteenth Pass</h2>
<p style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0 0 32px;">Financial Integrity · Auth Hardening · Skill System Reliability · Notification Delivery</p>
<div id="G-U01" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-U01</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§WalletDO — Financial — WalletDO / schema</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">WalletDO balance stored as REAL float — financial rounding errors compound at scale</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">WalletDO declares `balance_usd REAL` and every transaction amount as `amount_usd REAL`. IEEE-754 double-precision arithmetic cannot represent most decimal fractions exactly: 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.30000000000000004. Across millions of micro-transactions (token billing, x402 micropayments, tip distributions), these errors accumulate into visible cent-level discrepancies. A platform processing $1M/month in transactions will show systematic rounding errors of hundreds of dollars that cannot be reconciled against bank statements. This is a violation of financial systems fundamentals.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Store all monetary values as INTEGER cents (or micro-cents for sub-cent precision):
```sql
CREATE TABLE wallet (
  id           TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  balance_cents INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
  currency      TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'USD'
);
CREATE TABLE transactions (
  id            TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  wallet_id     TEXT NOT NULL,
  amount_cents  INTEGER NOT NULL,  -- negative = debit
  reason        TEXT NOT NULL,
  created_at    INTEGER DEFAULT (unixepoch('now')*1000)
);
```
All arithmetic uses integer ops. Convert to dollars only for display: `(balance_cents / 100).toFixed(2)`. For sub-cent token billing use micro-cents (INTEGER, divide by 1_000_000 for display).</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-U02" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-U02</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§WalletDO — Financial — WalletDO / Stripe webhooks</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Stripe webhook topUp() has no idempotency key — single retry causes double credit grant</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">When Stripe delivers a `checkout.session.completed` event, Naelao calls `walletDO.topUp(credits)`. If the webhook handler returns a 5xx (e.g., DO cold-start timeout, transient error), Stripe retries the same event up to 72 hours later with the same `event.id`. The second delivery calls `topUp()` again, granting double credits. This is trivially exploitable: an attacker on a slow network connection can manufacture a timeout while the credit has already been applied, then let the retry deliver a second grant. There is no `processed_events` deduplication table in WalletDO.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add event deduplication to WalletDO:
```typescript
async topUp(credits: number, stripeEventId: string) {
  // Idempotency check — same transaction in same SQL batch
  const existing = [...this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
    "SELECT id FROM processed_stripe_events WHERE id = ?", stripeEventId
  )];
  if (existing.length &gt; 0) return { alreadyProcessed: true };
  this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
    "INSERT INTO processed_stripe_events (id, processed_at) VALUES (?, ?)",
    stripeEventId, Date.now()
  );
  this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
    "UPDATE wallet SET balance_cents = balance_cents + ? WHERE id = ?",
    credits * 100, this.walletId
  );
  return { success: true };
}
```
Both the dedup insert and the balance update must be in the same synchronous sequence within a single DO request (no concurrent access possible within a DO request).</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-U03" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-U03</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-auth — Authentication — naelao-auth / JWT verification</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">JWT `alg` field not pinned — `none` algorithm and algorithm confusion attacks accepted</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The JWT verification code checks signature validity but does not explicitly pin the accepted algorithm. An attacker can forge a JWT with `"alg": "none"` (removing the signature entirely) and pass verification on libraries that accept the none algorithm. Alternatively, if the server expects HS256 but an attacker sends RS256 with the public key as the HMAC secret, verification passes on misconfigured libraries. Neither the `iss` (issuer) claim nor `aud` (audience) claim are validated against expected values, allowing tokens issued for other services to be replayed here.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Pin algorithm and validate all security-relevant claims:
```typescript
import { jwtVerify } from "jose";
const secret = new TextEncoder().encode(env.JWT_SECRET);
try {
  const { payload } = await jwtVerify(token, secret, {
    algorithms: ["HS256"],          // pin — reject none, RS256, etc.
    issuer: "naelao-auth",          // must match exactly
    audience: "naelao-api",         // must match exactly
    clockTolerance: 30,             // 30s leeway for clock skew
  });
} catch (e) {
  return new Response("Unauthorized", { status: 401 });
}
```
For CF Access JWTs use the CF-provided public key endpoint and pin `alg: ["RS256"]`.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-U04" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-U04</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-auth — Authentication — CF Access / admin routes</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">CF Access JWT audience and issuer claims not validated on admin routes — forged tokens accepted</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The naelao-router checks for the presence of a `CF-Access-Jwt-Assertion` header on admin routes (`/_admin/`, `/_assistant/admin/`) but the code only verifies the token's signature. It does not validate the `aud` (audience) claim against the CF Access application's team domain, nor the `iss` (issuer) against `https://&lt;team&gt;.cloudflareaccess.com`. A valid CF Access JWT issued for a different CF Access application (e.g., an internal staging environment) would pass validation and grant admin access to production. This is a standard confused deputy vulnerability in CF Access integrations.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Validate audience and issuer from CF Access JWT:
```typescript
const CF_TEAM_DOMAIN = "https://naelao.cloudflareaccess.com";
const APP_AUD = env.CF_ACCESS_AUD; // audience tag from CF Access app
const certs = await fetch(`${CF_TEAM_DOMAIN}/cdn-cgi/access/certs`).then(r =&gt; r.json());
// Use jose to verify with JWKS
const JWKS = createRemoteJWKSet(new URL(`${CF_TEAM_DOMAIN}/cdn-cgi/access/certs`));
const { payload } = await jwtVerify(token, JWKS, {
  issuer: CF_TEAM_DOMAIN,
  audience: APP_AUD,
});
```
Cache the JWKS for 5 minutes; re-fetch on verification failure to handle key rotation.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-U05" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-U05</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-notifications — Queues — CF Queues / DLQ</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Dead-letter queue consumer never implemented — failed messages accumulate with zero recovery</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The wrangler.jsonc declares a `naelao-dlq` binding and routes persistently-failing queue messages to it, but no Worker exports a `queue` handler that consumes `naelao-dlq`. Failed notification messages (APNs failures, Slack rate limits, Stripe webhook retries) flow into the DLQ and stay there indefinitely. There is no alerting, no retry backoff, no operator dashboard, and no way to replay messages. Critical events — run-complete notifications, human-in-the-loop approval requests, billing alerts — silently disappear.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add a DLQ consumer to naelao-notifications:
```typescript
// In naelao-notifications/index.ts
export default {
  async queue(batch: MessageBatch, env: Env): Promise&lt;void&gt; {
    if (batch.queue === "naelao-dlq") {
      for (const msg of batch.messages) {
        // Log to Analytics Engine for alerting
        env.NAELAO_ANALYTICS.writeDataPoint({
          blobs: ["dlq_message", msg.body.type ?? "unknown", JSON.stringify(msg.body).slice(0, 255)],
          doubles: [1],
          indexes: ["dlq"],
        });
        // Attempt one final delivery
        try {
          await deliverNotification(msg.body, env);
          msg.ack();
        } catch {
          msg.ack(); // accept loss after 3 total attempts
        }
      }
    }
  }
};
```
Also add a CF Workers analytics alert: if `SELECT count() FROM analytics WHERE index1='dlq' AND timestamp &gt; now()-3600` &gt; 0, page on-call.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-U06" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-U06</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§WalletDO — Financial — x402 / saga pattern</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">x402 debit-credit saga not atomic — company debited while creator receives nothing</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The x402 payment flow executes in two sequential steps: (1) debit the buyer's WalletDO `balance_cents`, (2) credit the creator's WalletDO `balance_cents`. These are two separate HTTP requests to two separate Durable Object instances. If step 1 succeeds but the creator's DO is evicted, the network fails, or the target space is deleted between the two requests, the buyer loses money while the creator receives nothing. There is no rollback, no saga coordinator tracking in-progress transactions, and no reconciliation job to detect split-brain states. The `refund-saga` endpoint referenced in comments is unimplemented.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement a saga coordinator in a dedicated `PaymentSagaDO`:
```typescript
// PaymentSagaDO tracks each payment through states: PENDING → DEBITED → CREDITED → COMPLETE
async executeSaga(sagaId: string, from: string, to: string, amountCents: number) {
  this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
    "INSERT INTO sagas (id, from_wallet, to_wallet, amount_cents, state) VALUES (?,?,?,?,'PENDING')",
    sagaId, from, to, amountCents
  );
  try {
    await debitWallet(from, amountCents, sagaId, env);
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec("UPDATE sagas SET state='DEBITED' WHERE id=?", sagaId);
    await creditWallet(to, amountCents, sagaId, env);
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec("UPDATE sagas SET state='COMPLETE' WHERE id=?", sagaId);
  } catch (e) {
    await rollbackDebit(from, amountCents, sagaId, env);
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec("UPDATE sagas SET state='ROLLED_BACK' WHERE id=?", sagaId);
  }
}
```
A reconciliation alarm runs every hour scanning sagas stuck in DEBITED state for &gt; 10 minutes and triggers rollback.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-U07" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-U07</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-auth — Authentication — CF Access / JWK rotation</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">CF Access public key cached indefinitely — admin routes inaccessible during CF key rotation</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The naelao-router fetches CF Access's JWK set from `https://&lt;team&gt;.cloudflareaccess.com/cdn-cgi/access/certs` once at Worker initialization and caches it in module-scope memory. CF Access periodically rotates signing keys (approximately every 6 weeks). During the rotation window, new JWTs are signed with the new key while the Worker's cache still holds the old key. All `jwtVerify()` calls fail with `JWSSignatureVerificationFailed`, locking all admins out of the system until the Worker is redeployed or the module-scope cache expires (which never happens — Workers are long-lived).</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement stale-on-failure key refresh:
```typescript
let jwks: ReturnType&lt;typeof createRemoteJWKSet&gt; | null = null;
let jwksLastFetched = 0;

function getJWKS(teamDomain: string) {
  if (!jwks || Date.now() - jwksLastFetched &gt; 5 * 60_000) {
    jwks = createRemoteJWKSet(new URL(`${teamDomain}/cdn-cgi/access/certs`));
    jwksLastFetched = Date.now();
  }
  return jwks;
}

async function verifyCFAccessJWT(token: string, env: Env) {
  try {
    return await jwtVerify(token, getJWKS(env.CF_TEAM_DOMAIN), { audience: env.CF_ACCESS_AUD });
  } catch (e) {
    // Force refresh on signature failure (possible key rotation)
    jwks = null;
    return await jwtVerify(token, getJWKS(env.CF_TEAM_DOMAIN), { audience: env.CF_ACCESS_AUD });
  }
}
```</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-U08" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-U08</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§WalletDO — Financial — WalletDO / sub-agent billing</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Sub-agent spend check is post-execution — users can spawn unlimited sub-agents with zero balance</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Sub-agent spawning calls `walletDO.deductCredits(estimatedCost)` AFTER the sub-agent completes and returns a result. The check that triggers rejection happens at billing time, not at dispatch time. A user with $0 balance can call `spawnSubAgent()` in rapid succession, launching dozens of parallel compute-intensive sub-agents before any billing check fires. Each sub-agent runs to completion, incurs real compute cost on Cloudflare, and the WalletDO deduction simply fails silently — leaving the platform absorbing the cost.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Pre-reserve credits before spawning:
```typescript
async spawnSubAgent(task: SubAgentTask): Promise&lt;{ subAgentId: string }&gt; {
  const ESTIMATED_COST_CENTS = 50; // 50 cents per sub-agent run, configurable
  // Reserve BEFORE spawning — throws if insufficient balance
  const reservationId = await walletDO.reserveCredits(ESTIMATED_COST_CENTS);
  try {
    const subAgentId = await dispatchSubAgent(task);
    // Track reservation with sub-agent for settlement on completion
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
      "UPDATE sub_agents SET reservation_id=? WHERE id=?",
      reservationId, subAgentId
    );
    return { subAgentId };
  } catch (e) {
    await walletDO.releaseReservation(reservationId);
    throw e;
  }
}
// On completion: settle reservation with actual cost
async settleSubAgent(subAgentId: string, actualCostCents: number) {
  const { reservation_id } = getSingleSubAgent(subAgentId);
  await walletDO.settleReservation(reservation_id, actualCostCents);
}
```</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-U09" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-U09</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§WalletDO — Financial — WalletDO / policy parsing</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">`parseFloat()` on policy values stored in SQLite — cascading precision loss on budget checks</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Policy values (monthly_budget, max_single_spend, spend_per_agent) are stored in the `policies` table as `TEXT` and read back with `parseFloat(row.value ?? "10.0")`. This introduces two layers of precision loss: the float representation error of the value itself, plus the float representation error of the comparison operand. A budget of $99.99 stored as "99.99" becomes 99.98999999999999 as a float, so a spend of exactly $99.99 may incorrectly pass the `spend &gt; budget` check. These discrepancies are invisible in normal operation but cause audit failures.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Store policies as INTEGER cents in SQLite and parse with `parseInt()`:
```typescript
// Schema
CREATE TABLE policies (
  key        TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  value_cents INTEGER NOT NULL,   -- store as cents, never floats
  updated_at INTEGER DEFAULT (unixepoch('now')*1000)
);
// Read
const limitCents = parseInt(row.value_cents);
const spendCents = amountDollars * 100 | 0;  // truncate to cents
if (spendCents &gt; limitCents) throw new Error("budget_exceeded");
```
Migrate existing TEXT values: `UPDATE policies SET value_cents = CAST(ROUND(CAST(value AS REAL) * 100) AS INTEGER)`.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-U10" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-U10</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§WalletDO — Financial — WalletDO / refund-saga</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">`refund-saga` endpoint referenced throughout spec but unimplemented in WalletDO</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Fourteen places in the spec call `walletDO.fetch("http://wallet/refund-saga", ...)` to reverse a failed payment: on sub-agent crash, on workflow abort, on x402 partial failure, on annotation rejection. The WalletDO HTTP handler has routes for `/debit`, `/credit`, `/balance`, and `/topup` but no `/refund-saga` route. Every call to `/refund-saga` returns HTTP 404. Failed transactions are never reversed, meaning: failed sub-agents still consume credits, failed workflows still charge for compute, rejected annotations still debit bootstrap funds.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement the `/refund-saga` handler in WalletDO:
```typescript
case "/refund-saga": {
  const { sagaId, amountCents, reason } = await request.json();
  // Idempotency — only refund once per sagaId
  const existing = [...this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
    "SELECT id FROM refunds WHERE saga_id = ?", sagaId
  )];
  if (existing.length &gt; 0) return Response.json({ alreadyRefunded: true });
  const refundId = crypto.randomUUID();
  this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
