<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">SKILL.md injected as prompt text with no executor — declared scripts and binaries never enforced</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Skills inject their SKILL.md (description + instructions) into the system prompt and rely entirely on the LLM to generate the right tool calls. There is no skill executor component that reads the skill's declared `scripts[]`, `required_bins`, `required_env`, and `resource_type` and validates that the model's tool calls actually match the skill's contract. A skill that declares it needs `ffmpeg` will silently produce garbage if `ffmpeg` is absent, because nothing checks the requirement before the model tries to use it.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add a skill executor that validates requirements and binds declared scripts as first-class tools:
```typescript
async function activateSkill(skill: Skill, env: Env): Promise&lt;Tool[]&gt; {
  // Validate environment before exposing the skill
  for (const bin of skill.required_bins ?? []) {
    if (!(await sandboxHasBinary(bin))) {
      throw new SkillUnavailable(`${skill.name} requires missing binary: ${bin}`);
    }
  }
  for (const key of skill.required_env ?? []) {
    if (!env[key]) throw new SkillUnavailable(`${skill.name} requires env: ${key}`);
  }
  // Bind declared scripts as callable tools with typed params
  return (skill.scripts ?? []).map(s =&gt; makeToolFromScript(skill, s));
}
```</p></div>
