<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Naive whitespace chunking splits mid-sentence and counts words, not tokens</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The chunker splits text on `\s+` to roughly 512 "tokens" by word count, with a 50-"token" overlap also measured in words. Word count is a poor proxy for token count (code and non-English text tokenize very differently), so chunks routinely overshoot or undershoot the model's expected window. Worse, splitting on raw whitespace cuts through the middle of sentences and code blocks, destroying the semantic cohesion that makes a chunk independently retrievable.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Chunk on semantic boundaries with a real tokenizer:
```typescript
import { encode } from "gpt-tokenizer"; // or a bge-compatible tokenizer
function chunkText(text: string, targetTokens = 512, overlapTokens = 64): string[] {
  // Split into sentences/paragraphs first
  const units = text.split(/(?&lt;=[.!?])\s+|\n\n+/);
  const chunks: string[] = [];
  let buf: string[] = [], bufTokens = 0;
  for (const unit of units) {
    const t = encode(unit).length;
    if (bufTokens + t &gt; targetTokens &amp;&amp; buf.length) {
      chunks.push(buf.join(" "));
      // carry overlap by tokens, not words
      buf = carryOverlap(buf, overlapTokens);
      bufTokens = encode(buf.join(" ")).length;
    }
    buf.push(unit); bufTokens += t;
  }
  if (buf.length) chunks.push(buf.join(" "));
  return chunks;
}
```</p></div>
</div>
