<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No automatic retention or expiry policy — messages, runs, and logs grow unbounded toward the 1GB DO limit</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">There is no specified retention/expiry policy for old messages, completed runs, tool-audit rows, or stream buffers. DO SQLite has a hard 1GB-per-DO limit; an active AssistantDO accumulating conversation history and per-token buffers will eventually approach it, at which point writes fail and the DO is bricked. There is also no enforcement of GDPR retention maxima (data must not be kept longer than necessary).</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Define and enforce per-table retention with a periodic prune:
```typescript
async pruneOldData() {
  const now = Date.now();
  const RETENTION = {
    messages:     180 * 86400_000, // 180 days
    runs:         90 * 86400_000,
    tool_audit:   30 * 86400_000,
    stream_buffer: 1 * 86400_000,
  };
  for (const [table, ttl] of Object.entries(RETENTION)) {
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(`DELETE FROM ${table} WHERE created_at &lt; ?`, now - ttl);
  }
}
// Schedule via this.scheduleEvery('24h', 'pruneOldData') (or alarm).
```
Archive pruned messages to R2 (cold storage) before deletion if the space tier includes long-term history.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-V38" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-V38</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Compliance — PII — platform / PII redaction</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No PII redaction before Analytics Engine, logs, or external LLM calls</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Conversation text, tool outputs, and API responses flow into Analytics Engine, console logs, and external LLM providers with no redaction step. These payloads routinely contain emails, phone numbers, API keys, and other secrets. Logs and Analytics Engine are queryable by operators and may be retained or forwarded to third-party log sinks, creating a PII exposure surface and a compliance liability. Analytics Engine cannot delete individual rows, so any PII written there is effectively permanent until the rolling window expires.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Run a redaction pass before any telemetry sink:
```typescript
const REDACTORS: [RegExp, string][] = [
  [/[\w.+-]+@[\w-]+\.[\w.-]+/g, "[email]"],
  [/\b(?:\d[ -]*?){13,16}\b/g, "[card]"],
  [/sk-[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}/g, "[api-key]"],
  [/\b\+?\d{1,3}[\s.-]?\(?\d{2,4}\)?[\s.-]?\d{3,4}[\s.-]?\d{3,4}\b/g, "[phone]"],
];
function redact(text: string): string {
  return REDACTORS.reduce((s, [re, rep]) =&gt; s.replace(re, rep), text);
}
// Apply to anything written to Analytics Engine / logs.
// For Analytics Engine, prefer storing a salted hash of identifiers, never raw PII.
```</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-V39" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-V39</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Compliance — Audit — SpaceDO / audit immutability</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">product_audit_log is a mutable SQLite table — audit records can be altered or deleted, defeating their purpose</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The audit log lives in a normal SpaceDO SQLite table that supports `UPDATE` and `DELETE` like any other. A compromised admin path, a buggy migration, or a malicious actor with write access can rewrite or erase audit history, so the log is not tamper-evident and cannot be relied upon for security forensics or compliance evidence. Append-only and integrity-chaining are not enforced.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Make the audit log append-only and hash-chained:
```typescript
// Each entry chains to the previous via a hash, making tampering detectable
async appendAudit(event: AuditEvent) {
  const prev = [...this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
    "SELECT hash FROM product_audit_log ORDER BY seq DESC LIMIT 1")][0];
  const prevHash = prev?.hash ?? "genesis";
  const payload = JSON.stringify(event);
  const hash = await sha256(prevHash + payload);
  this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
    "INSERT INTO product_audit_log (seq, payload, prev_hash, hash, at) VALUES ((SELECT COALESCE(MAX(seq),0)+1 FROM product_audit_log), ?, ?, ?, ?)",
    payload, prevHash, hash, Date.now()
  );
}
// Verification job recomputes the chain; any break flags tampering.
```
Mirror critical audit events to an external append-only sink (R2 with object-lock, or a Tail Worker) so even DO-level tampering is detectable.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-V40" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-V40</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§naelao-knowledge — Search — naelao-knowledge / FTS index</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Knowledge keyword search uses LIKE '%term%' with no index — full table scan that collapses at scale</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The keyword/lexical side of knowledge search uses `WHERE content LIKE '%term%'`, which cannot use any index and forces a full scan of every document row on every query. At a few hundred documents this is tolerable; at tens of thousands it becomes an O(n) scan that blows the DO CPU budget and serializes behind other queries, degrading the whole space. SQLite's FTS5 (already used for the Memory OS sessions layer) is the correct tool but is not applied to the knowledge corpus.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Back keyword search with an FTS5 virtual table:
```sql
CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE IF NOT EXISTS knowledge_fts USING fts5(
  chunk_id UNINDEXED, doc_id UNINDEXED, content,
  tokenize = 'porter unicode61'
);
-- Keep it in sync on ingest/delete
INSERT INTO knowledge_fts (chunk_id, doc_id, content) VALUES (?, ?, ?);
```
```typescript
// Query with BM25 ranking instead of LIKE scan
const rows = [...this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
  "SELECT chunk_id, doc_id, bm25(knowledge_fts) AS score FROM knowledge_fts WHERE knowledge_fts MATCH ? ORDER BY score LIMIT 50",
  ftsQuery
)];
```
Fuse these BM25 results with the vector results via reciprocal rank fusion for hybrid search.</p></div>
</div>



<section id="appendix-w" style="margin-top:80px;">
<h2 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:32px;font-weight:700;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.02em;margin:0 0 6px;">Appendix W — Fifteenth Pass</h2>
<p style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0 0 32px;">Workflows Determinism · Container Internals · Secrets &amp; Encryption · Email/File Inputs · Orchestration &amp; State · Migrations &amp; Deployment</p>
<div id="G-W01" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W01</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Workflows — Determinism — VerticalFinetuneWorkflow / replay</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Date.now() and randomUUID() called outside step.do() — replay creates duplicate files and inconsistent IDs</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The fine-tune workflow computes a training file path as `bootstrap/${vertical}/training-runs/${Date.now()}/train.jsonl` and a model description outside any `step.do()` callback. CF Workflows replays all code outside step boundaries on every continuation (after a failure, a sleep, or a hibernation). Because `Date.now()` and `crypto.randomUUID()` are non-deterministic, each replay produces a different path and ID, so the workflow writes duplicate training files to R2 and references a model version string that no longer matches the one persisted by an earlier step.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Move all non-deterministic values inside a step so they are computed once and memoized:
```typescript
const runId = await step.do("allocate-run-id", async () =&gt; {
  return { id: crypto.randomUUID(), at: Date.now() };
});
const trainingPath = `bootstrap/${vertical}/training-runs/${runId.id}/train.jsonl`;
// runId is now stable across all replays because step.do() persists its return value
```
Rule: any `Date.now()`, `crypto.randomUUID()`, `Math.random()`, or `fetch()` MUST live inside a `step.do()` so its result is captured in the workflow journal and replayed identically.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W02" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W02</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Workflows — Limits — VerticalFinetuneWorkflow / step return size</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Aggregation step returns the full training set inline — exceeds the step-result size limit and bloats the journal</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The `aggregate-training-data` step reads all labeled examples from R2 into an array, serializes them to JSONL, and returns `{ trainingPath, exampleCount }` — but the implementation collects the entire example set with no cap, and the formatted JSONL is held in the step closure. CF Workflows persists every step return value into the workflow journal; large returns blow past the per-step result size limit (single-digit MB) and bloat the journal, slowing every subsequent replay. A few thousand examples is enough to trip this.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Stream the data to R2 inside the step and return only a pointer:
```typescript
const result = await step.do("aggregate-training-data", async () =&gt; {
  const path = `bootstrap/${vertical}/training-runs/${runId}/train.jsonl`;
  // Write directly to R2; never return the payload itself
  await streamExamplesToR2(env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE, path, exampleSource);
  const count = await countLines(env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE, path);
  return { trainingPath: path, exampleCount: count }; // small, safe to journal
});
```
Step returns should always be small references (paths, IDs, counts), never bulk data.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W03" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W03</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Workflows — Limits — VerticalFinetuneWorkflow / step budget</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Polling loop has no max-iteration guard — a slow training job can exceed the 1000-step hard limit</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The job-monitoring loop is `while (jobStatus === "running") { step.sleep("30 minutes"); step.do("check-status"); }`. Each iteration consumes two steps. A fine-tune job that runs for 24+ hours produces ~96 iterations (~192 steps), and a stuck provider job that never transitions out of "running" loops indefinitely toward the 1000-step hard ceiling, at which point the workflow is force-terminated with no clean failure state.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Bound the poll loop with an explicit iteration cap and a wall-clock deadline:
```typescript
const MAX_POLLS = 96;          // 48 hours at 30-min intervals
const DEADLINE = startTime + 48 * 3600_000;
for (let i = 0; i &lt; MAX_POLLS; i++) {
  const status = await step.do(`check-status-${i}`, () =&gt; pollProvider(jobId));
  if (status !== "running") break;
  if (Date.now() &gt; DEADLINE) {
    await step.do("mark-timed-out", () =&gt; markJobFailed(jobId, "timeout"));
    break;
  }
  await step.sleep(`poll-${i}`, "30 minutes");
}
```
Use exponential backoff on the sleep interval to reduce total step count for long jobs.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W04" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W04</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Containers — Concurrency — AssistantSandboxDO / exec serialization</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Concurrent exec() calls hit the same container shell without serialization — interleaved output and state corruption</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The SandboxDO's `exec()` dispatches directly to `this.containerRef.exec()` with no command queue. When two requests run in parallel (e.g., a workspace tool and a sandbox tool in the same turn), both execute against the same container shell concurrently. Their stdout/stderr interleave, working-directory changes from one command bleed into the other, and any shared file (a temp file, a lock) races. The DO's single-threaded request model does NOT protect this, because `exec()` is a non-storage async call that does not hold the input gate.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Serialize commands through a per-DO queue:
```typescript
private execChain: Promise&lt;unknown&gt; = Promise.resolve();
async exec(cmd: string, args: string[], opts: ExecOpts): Promise&lt;ExecResult&gt; {
  // Chain each exec after the previous one completes
  const run = this.execChain.then(() =&gt; this.containerRef!.exec(cmd, args, opts));
  // Keep the chain alive even if one command throws
  this.execChain = run.catch(() =&gt; {});
  return run;
}
```
For commands that are genuinely independent and safe to parallelize, run them in separate working directories or separate containers (see multi-container, Appendix T).</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W05" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W05</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Containers — Lifecycle — AssistantSandboxDO / boot race</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">ensureRunning() is not atomic — two simultaneous calls boot two containers</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">`ensureRunning()` checks `if (status === "running" &amp;&amp; this.containerRef) return` and otherwise calls `getSandbox()`. The check and the boot are not atomic across the two non-storage async awaits, so two requests arriving on a cold DO both observe `stopped`, both call `getSandbox()`, and two container instances are provisioned. The second leaks (it is never tracked or terminated) and bills until its idle timeout.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Guard the boot with blockConcurrencyWhile and a single in-flight promise:
```typescript
private booting: Promise&lt;Sandbox&gt; | null = null;
async ensureRunning(): Promise&lt;Sandbox&gt; {
  if (this.containerRef &amp;&amp; await this.isAlive(this.containerRef)) return this.containerRef;
  if (this.booting) return this.booting; // coalesce concurrent boots
  this.booting = (async () =&gt; {
    const sb = getSandbox(this.env.SANDBOX, this.sandboxId);
    await sb.startup?.();
    this.containerRef = sb;
    return sb;
  })();
  try { return await this.booting; } finally { this.booting = null; }
}
```</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W06" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W06</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Containers — Lifecycle — AssistantSandboxDO / idle alarm</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Idle-termination alarm is reset only after exec() completes — a long command is killed mid-execution</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The idle-timeout alarm (e.g., 10 minutes) is refreshed by `resetIdleTimer()`, which is called only at the END of `exec()`. A single command that runs longer than the idle window — a 15-minute Python training script, a long build — does not refresh the timer while it runs, so the alarm fires and terminates the container while the command is still executing, killing the job with no result.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Refresh the deadline at command start and use a heartbeat for long commands:
```typescript
async exec(cmd: string, args: string[], opts: ExecOpts) {
  await this.ctx.storage.put("last_active", Date.now());
  await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + IDLE_MS); // arm before running
  // Heartbeat: while the command runs, keep pushing the deadline out
  const hb = setInterval(() =&gt; this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + IDLE_MS), IDLE_MS / 3);
  try {
    return await this.containerRef!.exec(cmd, args, opts);
  } finally {
    clearInterval(hb);
    await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + IDLE_MS);
  }
}
```
In the alarm handler, only terminate if `now - last_active &gt; IDLE_MS` AND no command is in flight.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W07" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W07</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Containers — Output — AssistantSandboxDO / output truncation</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">exec() truncates stdout/stderr silently — the agent proceeds on incomplete output with no truncated flag</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Command output is truncated with `.slice()` to ~10,000 bytes (stdout) and ~5,000 bytes (stderr) before being returned/logged. The truncation is silent: there is no `truncated: true` flag and no indication to the agent that the tail of the output was discarded. A command whose meaningful result is at the end (a build summary, a test report's final lines, a JSON payload) returns a head that looks complete, and the agent reasons over partial data.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Signal truncation and promote large output to R2:
```typescript
async function captureOutput(raw: string, kind: "stdout"|"stderr") {
  const LIMIT = kind === "stdout" ? 10_000 : 5_000;
  if (raw.length &lt;= LIMIT) return { text: raw, truncated: false };
  // Persist the full output, return head + tail + a reference
  const ref = `tmp/exec-output/${crypto.randomUUID()}.txt`;
  await env.NAELAO_WORKSPACE.put(ref, raw);
  return {
    text: raw.slice(0, LIMIT / 2) + "\n…[truncated]…\n" + raw.slice(-LIMIT / 2),
    truncated: true,
    fullOutputRef: ref,
  };
}
```
The agent can then fetch `fullOutputRef` if it needs the complete output.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W08" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W08</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Containers — Lifecycle — AssistantSandboxDO / command timeout</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">exec() has no per-command timeout — a hung process holds the container indefinitely</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The `exec()` call passes no timeout to the container runtime. A command that hangs — a Python script blocked on an unresponsive external API, a `read` waiting on stdin that never comes, an infinite loop — occupies the container forever (or until the idle alarm, which the in-flight command keeps resetting per G-W06). The agent turn that issued it blocks, and the container cannot service other commands.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Enforce a per-command timeout with a SIGTERM/SIGKILL escalation:
```typescript
async exec(cmd: string, args: string[], opts: ExecOpts &amp; { timeoutMs?: number }) {
  const timeoutMs = opts.timeoutMs ?? 120_000; // 2 min default
  const exec = this.containerRef!.exec(cmd, args, opts);
  const timeout = new Promise&lt;never&gt;((_, rej) =&gt;
    setTimeout(() =&gt; rej(new Error(`exec timeout after ${timeoutMs}ms`)), timeoutMs));
  try {
    return await Promise.race([exec, timeout]);
  } catch (e) {
    // Best-effort kill so the container is reusable
    await this.containerRef!.exec("pkill", ["-TERM", "-f", cmd], {}).catch(() =&gt; {});
    throw e;
  }
}
```
Expose `timeoutMs` per tool so long jobs can opt into a larger budget explicitly.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W09" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W09</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#10B981;background:#10B98118;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">LOW</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Containers — Lifecycle — AssistantSandboxDO / crash detection</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">A crashed container leaves a stale containerRef and DB status of running — next exec() fails opaquely</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">If the container process dies (OOM kill, SIGKILL, host reclaim), `this.containerRef` still holds a reference to the dead stub and the DB row still reads `status = "running"`. The next `exec()` call dispatches to the dead stub and fails with an opaque connection error rather than transparently rebooting. The DO never reconciles the discrepancy on its own.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Detect connection failures and self-heal:
```typescript
async exec(cmd: string, args: string[], opts: ExecOpts) {
  try {
    return await this.containerRef!.exec(cmd, args, opts);
  } catch (e) {
    if (isConnectionError(e)) {
      this.containerRef = null;
      this.ctx.storage.sql.exec("UPDATE sandbox SET status='stopped' WHERE id=?", this.sandboxId);
      await this.ensureRunning(); // reboot
      return await this.containerRef!.exec(cmd, args, opts); // retry once
    }
    throw e;
  }
}
```</p></div>
</div>
