<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Single platform key encrypts every space's credentials — no envelope encryption or per-tenant key</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The naelao-credentials Worker encrypts all stored MCP credentials, OAuth tokens, and BYOK API keys with one platform-level `NAELAO_MCP_ENCRYPTION_KEY`. There is no per-tenant data encryption key and no envelope encryption. A single compromise of that key (leaked secret, insider, misconfigured binding) instantly decrypts every customer's credentials across every space — there is no blast-radius containment.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Adopt envelope encryption with per-space data keys:
```typescript
// Per-space DEK, wrapped by the platform KEK
async function encryptForSpace(plaintext: string, spaceId: string, env: Env) {
  const dek = await deriveOrFetchDEK(spaceId, env);         // unique per space
  const iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12));
  const ct = await aesGcmEncrypt(plaintext, dek, iv);
  return { ciphertext: ct, iv, dekId: dek.id };             // store wrapped DEK separately
}
```
Alternatively derive per-space keys with HKDF(platform_key, spaceId). Rotating one space's DEK then re-encrypts only that space, and a leaked DEK exposes only one tenant.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W12" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W12</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Secrets — Encryption — naelao-credentials / AES-GCM IV</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">AES-GCM IV uniqueness not enforced or audited — nonce reuse under the same key is catastrophic</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Encryption uses `crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12))` for the GCM IV, which is correct in isolation, but nothing audits or guarantees IV uniqueness per key over time. A bug in key rotation, a replay of an old encrypt path, or restoring backed-up rows that reuse an IV under the same key collapses AES-GCM security entirely: an attacker who obtains two ciphertexts encrypted with the same (key, IV) recovers the XOR of the plaintexts and can forge tags. There is no logging of IVs to detect a collision.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Make IV reuse structurally impossible and detectable:
```typescript
// Prefer a deterministic, monotonic counter-based IV per (key) to guarantee uniqueness,
// or random IV + a uniqueness guard:
const iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12));
const ivHash = await sha256(concat(dekId, iv));
const clash = [...sql.exec("SELECT 1 FROM iv_log WHERE iv_hash=?", ivHash)];
if (clash.length) throw new Error("IV collision detected — aborting encrypt");
sql.exec("INSERT INTO iv_log (iv_hash, dek_id, at) VALUES (?,?,?)", ivHash, dekId, Date.now());
```
Rotate the DEK well before the birthday bound (2^32 messages for random 96-bit IVs).</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W13" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W13</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Secrets — OAuth — naelao-credentials / token lifecycle</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Stored OAuth tokens have no expiry tracking or refresh — silent tool failures and no rotation on use</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">OAuth tokens for third-party MCP integrations (GitHub, Stripe, Notion) are stored encrypted but the schema has no `token_expires_at`, `refresh_token`, or `last_refreshed_at`. When an access token expires, the agent's tool call to that MCP server silently fails with a 401 and no refresh is attempted. For providers that rotate refresh tokens on use (RFC 6749 §6), the rotated token is never captured, so the integration permanently breaks after the first refresh elsewhere.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Track expiry and refresh proactively:
```sql
ALTER TABLE credentials ADD COLUMN token_expires_at INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE credentials ADD COLUMN refresh_token_enc TEXT;  -- encrypted
ALTER TABLE credentials ADD COLUMN last_refreshed_at INTEGER;
```
```typescript
async function getValidToken(credId: string, env: Env) {
  const c = await loadCredential(credId, env);
  if (c.token_expires_at &amp;&amp; c.token_expires_at - Date.now() &lt; 24*3600_000) {
    const refreshed = await refreshOAuth(c.provider, decrypt(c.refresh_token_enc));
    await storeRefreshed(credId, refreshed); // capture rotated refresh token
    return refreshed.access_token;
  }
  return decrypt(c.access_token_enc);
}
```</p></div>
</div>
