<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W15</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Secrets — Logging — platform / secret scrubbing</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No guaranteed scrubbing of secrets from error messages and logs</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">When an MCP tool call or credential operation fails, the error message frequently includes the request URL, headers, or response body — any of which may carry an API key, bearer token, or OAuth secret. These errors are logged and sometimes surfaced to the client. There is no central scrubbing layer that redacts secret-shaped strings before logging or returning errors, so credentials leak into log sinks (which may be retained and forwarded to third parties).</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Run all log/error output through a redactor:
```typescript
const SECRET_PATTERNS: RegExp[] = [
  /[Bb]earer\s+[A-Za-z0-9._-]{20,}/g,
  /sk-[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}/g,
  /xox[baprs]-[A-Za-z0-9-]{10,}/g,        // Slack
  /ghp_[A-Za-z0-9]{36}/g,                 // GitHub PAT
  /(api[_-]?key|secret|token)["\s:=]+[A-Za-z0-9._-]{12,}/gi,
];
function scrub(s: string): string {
  return SECRET_PATTERNS.reduce((acc, re) =&gt; acc.replace(re, "[redacted]"), s);
}
// Wrap the logger and the error-to-client serializer with scrub().
```</p></div>
</div>
