<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No defined boundary between in-memory this.state and SQLite — transient fields vanish on eviction</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Agent state is split between persistent SQLite (fibers, sessions, messages) and in-memory TypeScript fields (`this.state` and ad-hoc instance variables). The spec never defines which data belongs where. Code that writes working state to an instance field expecting it to survive — across a tool call, across a turn, across eviction — loses it silently when the DO hibernates, because only the SQLite checkpoint is restored on wake. Recovery via `onFiberRecovered()` restores the SQLite snapshot, not the in-memory mutations.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Define and document a strict ownership rule, and persist anything that must survive:
```
// Ownership contract:
//  - this.state (synced):  small, client-visible UI state only (status, progress)
//  - SQLite (durable):     anything that must survive eviction or be queried
//  - instance fields:      request-scoped scratch ONLY; never relied on across awaits
```
```typescript
// Persist working state explicitly at each await boundary that could hibernate
await this.ctx.storage.put("loop_checkpoint", { step, partialResult });
// On wake, restore from storage, never from a remembered field
const cp = await this.ctx.storage.get("loop_checkpoint");
```</p></div>
</div>
