<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Two messages to the same thread interleave — no explicit queue guarantees ordered, non-overlapping turns</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">When two requests target the same thread concurrently (user double-sends, or a client retries), the DO serializes individual handler invocations but there is no explicit turn queue. A second message can begin a new LLM turn while the first turn's tool results are still being persisted, interleaving messages in the history and producing a corrupted, non-causal transcript. The single-threaded DO model protects individual storage ops, not the multi-await logical turn.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Serialize turns with an explicit per-thread lock/queue:
```typescript
private turnQueue: Promise&lt;void&gt; = Promise.resolve();
async handleMessage(msg: UserMessage) {
  // Each turn runs strictly after the previous one finishes
  const run = this.turnQueue.then(() =&gt; this.runTurn(msg));
  this.turnQueue = run.catch(() =&gt; {}); // keep the chain alive on error
  return run;
}
```
Alternatively reject a new message with `409 turn_in_progress` while a turn is active, and let the client decide to queue or cancel.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W30" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W30</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Orchestration — Reliability — AssistantDO / orphaned sub-agents</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Sub-agents have no parent-liveness link — if the parent dies, results are never delivered and work runs to waste</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">A spawned sub-agent runs in its own DO and reports back by writing to the parent's status table, which the parent polls on heartbeat. If the parent is deleted or permanently fails, the sub-agent keeps executing (consuming credits), finishes, writes a result no one reads, and is never cleaned up. There is no parent-liveness heartbeat, no result delivery via a durable channel, and no orphan timeout.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Give sub-agents a durable result sink and an orphan timeout:
```typescript
// Sub-agent delivers results to a queue keyed by parent, not by polling the parent DO
await env.SUBAGENT_RESULTS_QUEUE.send({
  parentRef, handoffId, subAgentId, result, completedAt: Date.now(),
});
// A consumer routes the result to the parent if alive, or to a dead-letter store if not.
// Sub-agent self-terminates if the parent is unreachable for N checks:
if (await parentUnreachable(parentRef, { checks: 3 })) {
  await this.saveResult(result);       // persist for later retrieval
  await this.terminate("orphaned");    // stop consuming credits
}
```</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W31" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W31</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Orchestration — Broadcast — NaelaoAgentDO / broadcast error isolation</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">broadcast loop has no per-socket error isolation — one closed socket aborts delivery to the rest</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The broadcast pattern `getWebSockets().forEach(ws =&gt; ws.send(...))` calls `send()` on each socket with no try/catch. A socket that closed between enumeration and send throws on `send()`, and because the throw is uncaught inside the synchronous `forEach`, delivery to all sockets later in the array is aborted. Clients enumerated after the dead one miss the message entirely.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Isolate each send and prune dead sockets:
```typescript
broadcast(data: string) {
  for (const ws of this.ctx.getWebSockets()) {
    try {
      ws.send(data);
    } catch {
      // Socket is dead/closing — prune it, keep delivering to the rest
      try { ws.close(1011, "send failed"); } catch {}
    }
  }
}
```
If using the SDK's native `this.broadcast()`, this isolation is handled — another reason to prefer it (see Appendix S).</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W32" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W32</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Orchestration — Lifecycle — NaelaoAgentDO / onConnect ordering</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">onConnect can fire before schema/state is initialized — handlers read undefined state</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">There is no guaranteed ordering that the DO's SQLite schema and initial state are fully loaded before `onConnect()` runs for an incoming WebSocket. If `onConnect()` (or the first message handler) reads `this.state.sessionId` or queries a table, it can hit undefined state or a not-yet-created table on a cold DO, causing intermittent errors that only appear under cold-start + immediate-connect timing.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Gate all entry points behind an init promise via blockConcurrencyWhile:
```typescript
constructor(ctx: DurableObjectState, env: Env) {
  super(ctx, env);
  ctx.blockConcurrencyWhile(async () =&gt; {
    await this.initSchema();        // CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS + migrations
    await this.loadState();         // hydrate this.state from SQLite
    this.ready = true;
  });
}
async onConnect(ws: WebSocket) {
  // blockConcurrencyWhile guarantees init finished before any handler runs,
  // but assert defensively for clarity:
  if (!this.ready) throw new Error("not initialized");
  // ... safe to read state
}
```</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W33" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W33</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Deployment — Migrations — platform / DO schema migration system</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No versioned migration system — schema changes cannot be applied to a live fleet of thousands of DOs</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">DO schema is created with `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` in the constructor. This is idempotent but not versionable: there is no `PRAGMA user_version` tracking, no migration runner, and no path to apply `ALTER TABLE`/backfill changes to DOs that already exist. When a new column or table is needed, existing DOs never receive it (queries return NULL or error), and there is no way to coordinate or observe migration progress across the fleet.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Add a versioned migration runner keyed on PRAGMA user_version, run on every wake:
```typescript
private migrations: ((sql: SqlStorage) =&gt; void)[] = [
  (sql) =&gt; sql.exec("CREATE TABLE messages (...)"),                 // v1
  (sql) =&gt; sql.exec("ALTER TABLE messages ADD COLUMN workspace_id TEXT"), // v2
  (sql) =&gt; { /* backfill */ },                                     // v3
];
private migrate() {
  const cur = Number([...this.ctx.storage.sql.exec("PRAGMA user_version")][0].user_version);
  for (let v = cur; v &lt; this.migrations.length; v++) {
    this.migrations[v](this.ctx.storage.sql);
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(`PRAGMA user_version = ${v + 1}`);
  }
}
// Call inside blockConcurrencyWhile in the constructor so it runs once per wake.
```</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W34" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W34</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#EF4444;background:#EF444418;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">CRITICAL</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Deployment — Migrations — platform / hibernated DO schema drift</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Hibernated DOs wake with stale schema after a schema-changing deploy — drift between dormant and active instances</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">A new deploy ships schema changes, but a DO that has been hibernated for days does not run any code (and thus no migration) until its next wake. Between deploy and wake, the fleet is split: recently-active DOs may have run the new init path while dormant ones still have the old schema. Without a migration runner (G-W33) that reconciles on wake, a dormant DO can wake and run queries against a schema that does not match the deployed code, corrupting state or failing silently. This interaction is not covered by the chaos test suite.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Ensure the migration runner (G-W33) runs unconditionally on every wake, and make all queries schema-version-tolerant:
```typescript
// In constructor, before serving any request:
this.ctx.blockConcurrencyWhile(async () =&gt; this.migrate());
```
Add tests that explicitly exercise the dormant→deploy→wake path:
```typescript
it("dormant DO migrates on wake after schema-change deploy", async () =&gt; {
  const id = newDOWithSchemaV1();
  deploySchemaV2();                 // bump migration list
  const woken = await wake(id);      // must run v1-&gt;v2 migration on wake
  expect(await woken.userVersion()).toBe(2);
});
```</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W35" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W35</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Deployment — Atomicity — platform / version skew</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Deploying 22 Workers is non-atomic — a skew window breaks Service Binding contracts between callers and callees</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The platform is ~22 Workers deployed via `wrangler deploy`. Deploys are per-Worker and not atomic across the fleet, so during a rollout there is a window where some Workers run the new version and others the old. If a new caller expects a v2 Service Binding contract (new field, renamed route) while the callee is still v1 (or vice versa), inter-Worker calls fail or behave incorrectly for the duration of the skew. No compatibility strategy is documented.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Make Service Binding contracts backward/forward compatible and deploy callees first:
```
// 1. Expand-then-contract: add new fields/routes as ADDITIVE first (callee tolerates
//    both old and new request shapes), deploy callees, then deploy callers, then
//    remove the old shape in a later release.
// 2. Version the internal contract explicitly:
```
```typescript
await env.NAELAO_BILLING.fetch(new Request("http://billing/v2/usage", {
  headers: { "x-contract-version": "2" }, /* ... */
}));
// Callee handles both /v1/ and /v2/ during the transition.
```
Deploy in dependency order (leaf services first) and never remove a contract in the same release that introduces its replacement.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W36" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W36</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#F97316;background:#F9731618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">HIGH</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Deployment — Rollback — platform / schema rollback</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No rollback strategy for schema-changing deploys — DO SQLite changes are not backward-compatible</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">There is no documented rollback procedure. Rolling back Worker code is straightforward, but if a deploy applied a DO SQLite migration (dropped a column, changed a constraint, backfilled data), rolling the code back to the previous version leaves it running against a schema it does not understand. Destructive migrations (DROP, type changes) are irreversible, so a bad deploy that includes one cannot be safely undone.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Make migrations rollback-safe by policy and keep code compatible across one version:
```
// Migration rules:
//  - Only ADDITIVE migrations in a normal deploy (add column/table/index).
//  - Never DROP or rename in the same release that stops using the old shape.
//  - Destructive cleanup happens N releases later, after rollback window closes.
//  - New code must read BOTH old and new schema for one release (tolerant reads).
```
For data backfills, write to new columns while still maintaining old ones until the rollback window passes; only then switch reads and, later, drop the old columns.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W37" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W37</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Deployment — Config — wrangler / DO migration tag</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">wrangler.jsonc has no migration tags — renaming or deleting a DO class will fail or break instances</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Cloudflare requires a `migrations` block with a tag (and `renamed_classes`/`deleted_classes` directives) in wrangler config whenever a Durable Object class is renamed or removed. None of the ~14 DO bindings declare migration tags. A future refactor that renames `NaelaoAgentDO` will either be rejected by wrangler or, worse, strand all existing instances of the old class with no way to address them.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Declare DO migrations with tags from the start:
```jsonc
{
  "durable_objects": { "bindings": [ /* ... */ ] },
  "migrations": [
    { "tag": "v1", "new_classes": ["NaelaoAgentDO", "AssistantDO", "WalletDO", "SpaceDO"] },
    // Future rename:
    { "tag": "v2", "renamed_classes": [{ "from": "NaelaoAgentDO", "to": "AgentDO" }] }
    // Future delete (only after instances drained):
    // { "tag": "v3", "deleted_classes": ["LegacyDO"] }
  ]
}
```
Never rename/delete a DO class without a corresponding migration tag.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W38" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W38</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Testing — Isolation — test harness / Service Binding isolation</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">Per-test DO isolation via random IDs does not isolate Service Bindings — tests cross-contaminate via inter-Worker calls</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Tests isolate DO instances by using fresh `crypto.randomUUID()` IDs per test, but Service Bindings between test Workers are not isolated: a test exercising naelao-router that triggers a Service Binding call into naelao-spaces hits the shared test instance of that Worker, whose DO namespace and any module-scope state are common across tests. Tests marked `concurrent` can therefore pollute each other through the binding layer even though their DO IDs differ.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Isolate cross-Worker state per test and avoid module-scope mutable state:
```typescript
// Namespace all DO IDs created via Service Bindings with a per-test prefix
const testPrefix = `test-${crypto.randomUUID()}`;
// Pass it through so downstream Workers derive DO names within the test namespace
// e.g. idFromName(`${testPrefix}:space:${id}`)

// Run cross-binding integration tests serially, not concurrently:
describe.sequential("router -&gt; spaces integration", () =&gt; { /* ... */ });
```
Forbid module-scope mutable state in Workers; keep all state in DO storage keyed by the test-prefixed names.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W39" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W39</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Deployment — CI/CD — pipeline / CI and secret handling</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">No CI/CD pipeline, deploy ordering, or secret-handling documented for the 22-Worker fleet</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">There is no `.github/workflows` or equivalent CI/CD definition. The spec does not specify how the 22 Workers are linted, tested, and deployed; in what order (which matters for version skew, G-W35); whether deploys are automated or manual; or how deployment secrets (`NAELAO_JWT_SECRET`, the CF API token, signing keys) are stored and injected. This leaves deployment ad hoc and secret handling undefined — a significant operational and security gap.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Define a CI/CD pipeline with ordered deploys and secret hygiene:
```yaml
# .github/workflows/deploy.yml (sketch)
jobs:
  test:    { steps: [ checkout, "npm ci", "npm run test" ] }   # vitest-pool-workers
  deploy:
    needs: test
    steps:
      # Deploy in dependency order: leaf services first, edge router last
      - run: wrangler deploy --config workers/naelao-credentials/wrangler.jsonc
      - run: wrangler deploy --config workers/naelao-billing/wrangler.jsonc
      # ... then dependent services ...
      - run: wrangler deploy --config workers/naelao-router/wrangler.jsonc
    env:
      CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.CF_API_TOKEN }}   # GitHub Secrets, never in repo
```
Use `wrangler secret put` for runtime secrets; never bake them into wrangler.jsonc or env vars in the repo.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-W40" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
<div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:6px;">
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:var(--or);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;">G-W40</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#3B82F6;background:#3B82F618;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:4px;letter-spacing:.08em;">MEDIUM</span>
<span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Deployment — Config — wrangler / compatibility_date consistency</span>
</div>
<h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">compatibility_date not validated for consistency across dev/test/prod environments</h3>
<div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The vitest config and the root wrangler.jsonc both pin `compatibility_date` to the same value, but there is no check that every per-Worker and per-environment wrangler config (especially production) sets a consistent, intentional date. A missing or mismatched `compatibility_date` across environments changes runtime semantics (which APIs and behaviors are active), so a Worker can behave differently in prod than in test for reasons unrelated to code — a class of bug that is very hard to diagnose.</p></div>
<div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div>
<p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Centralize and assert the compatibility date:
```typescript
// scripts/check-compat-date.ts — run in CI before deploy
import { readFileSync, globSync } from "node:fs";
const EXPECTED = "2026-05-01";
for (const cfg of globSync("workers/**/wrangler.jsonc")) {
  const d = JSON.parse(stripJsonc(readFileSync(cfg, "utf8"))).compatibility_date;
  if (d !== EXPECTED) throw new Error(`${cfg}: compatibility_date ${d} != ${EXPECTED}`);
}
```
When bumping the date, bump it everywhere in one change and re-run the full test suite against the new date.</p></div>
</div>



