    <span style="font-family:'DM Mono',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#64748B;letter-spacing:.06em;">§Security / CORS — CORS Configuration Hardening</span>
  </div>
  <h3 style="font-family:'Fraunces',serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;color:var(--hd);letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 18px;">CORS May Be Configured Permissively — Wildcard or Over-Broad Origins Allow Cross-Site Authenticated Requests</h3>
  <div class="box box-rd"><div class="box-lbl">The Gap</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">The API Gateway Worker sets CORS headers but the configuration is not validated against a strict allowlist. If Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * is set on any authenticated endpoint, any website can make cross-origin requests on behalf of a logged-in user (CSRF-equivalent risk for simple requests, and CORS is entirely bypassed for credentialless fetch). Builder dashboard APIs and the management API are the highest-risk targets. The spec acknowledges JWT authentication but CORS misconfiguration would allow an attacker to call APIs using the victim's cookies.</p></div>
  <div class="box box-gr"><div class="box-lbl">Required Fix</div><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;">Implement a strict CORS allowlist: define ALLOWED_ORIGINS as a set { "https://naelao.com", "https://app.naelao.com", "https://*.naelao.com" }. In the CORS middleware: check if request Origin is in the allowlist; set Access-Control-Allow-Origin: {exact_origin} if matched; if not matched, return 403 (do not reflect arbitrary origins). Never set Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * on authenticated endpoints. For public APIs (marketplace, public space data), allow *, but ensure these endpoints never return user-specific data or accept authentication credentials (no cookies, no Authorization headers). Run CORS bypass tests in the security test suite.</p></div>
</div>
<div id="G-AI41" style="margin:40px 0 0;padding:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--brd);">
