  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT28" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT28 [HIGH] — Space-in-Space Embedding (Nested Spaces)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No mechanism exists for a builder to embed another Space as a panel or widget within their own Space. Each Space is an isolated product; cross-Space composition requires custom frontend code outside the UI Builder, breaking the no-code guarantee.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement SpaceEmbed component in UI library: &lt;SpaceEmbed spaceId="xyz" height="400px" /&gt;. On render, fetch Space shell for embedded Space via /embed/{space_id}?parent_space={current_space_id}. Embedded Space runs in sandboxed iframe with CSP child-src 'self'. Establish cross-iframe postMessage protocol for parent ↔ embedded Space communication (typed message schema). Require embedded Space owner to whitelist parent Space domain. Track cross-Space embed analytics. Enforce max 3 levels of nesting.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT29" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT29 [HIGH] — Cross-Space Data Sharing Consent Model</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Space isolation enforces strict space_id filtering on all queries, but no consent model exists for Space A to legitimately access KB documents or user profile data from Space B (e.g., enterprise with multiple Spaces sharing a user directory).
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement DataSharingGrant: Space A owner requests read access to specific data_types (kb_documents, user_profiles) from Space B owner. Space B owner approves via signed consent record stored in AccountDO SQLite. DataAccessProxy checks grant before cross-Space query. Grant scoped by: grantor_space_id, grantee_space_id, data_type, expires_at, purpose_description. Audit all cross-Space data accesses in immutable log. GDPR: include cross-Space grants in end-user data export.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT30" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT30 [MEDIUM] — Space Forking — Clone Existing Space</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders wanting to use an existing Space as a starting point for a new product must manually recreate all configuration. No Space fork/clone mechanism exists to copy a Space's config, SpaceType binding, component layouts, and theme to a new Space instance.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement POST /api/spaces/{source_id}/fork endpoint. ForkWorkflow: (1) copy SpaceConfigDO SQLite snapshot to new SpaceDO, (2) duplicate SpaceType binding with new space_id, (3) copy component_configs, theme_config, prompt_versions tables with new PKs, (4) deep-copy R2 assets with new path prefix, (5) clear end-user data (users, sessions, message history). Return new space_id. Surface "Fork this Space" in marketplace for published SpaceType templates. Fork inherits source's SpaceType version at fork time.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT31" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT31 [MEDIUM] — Space Hibernation Policy for Inactive Spaces</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Idle Spaces continue consuming DO resources (memory while warm, storage fees) indefinitely. On the free plan with millions of Spaces, persistent resource consumption from inactive Spaces is unsustainable with no hibernation or archival lifecycle.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define hibernation tiers: WARM (active last 7 days — full DO access), COOL (8-30 days — DO hibernated, instant wake), COLD (31-90 days — SQLite snapshot to R2, DO deleted, 5s cold start), ARCHIVED (&gt;90 days — snapshot only, no wake without explicit builder action). Implement HibernationScheduler as daily CF Workflow scanning last_activity_at. Wake-on-demand: first request to COLD Space triggers restore from R2 snapshot. Notify builder before COLD transition. Always-warm option on paid plans.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT32" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT32 [HIGH] — Space Migration Between Subscription Plans</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> When a builder upgrades from free to paid plan (or downgrades), no Space migration procedure exists for quota changes: KV limits, Vectorize index limits, max active sessions, and DO storage are reconfigured without any migration path or rollback for quota violations.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement PlanMigrationWorkflow: on Stripe subscription upgrade event, (1) update plan_tier in AccountDO, (2) provision new resources matching new plan (larger Vectorize index if tier increases), (3) for downgrades: check current usage vs new limits, block downgrade if over-quota with usage report showing what must be reduced, (4) migrate data to new resource tiers (re-index to new Vectorize if changing index size), (5) emit plan_migrated event to Analytics Engine. Show migration status in builder dashboard.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT33" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT33 [HIGH] — Space Federation for Enterprise Multi-Space Topology</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Large enterprise customers may need multiple Spaces (one per department/product line) acting as a cohesive product with shared user directory, unified billing, and cross-Space agent collaboration. No federation model exists; each Space is fully isolated.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement SpaceFederation: enterprise account creates federation with federation_id. Member Spaces join federation with explicit consent. FederationDO manages: shared user directory (federated identity), unified credit pool (shared billing bucket), cross-Space agent invocation protocol. FederatedSessionToken carries space_id list for cross-Space API access. Federation admin can set cross-Space data sharing policies. Audit all cross-Space actions in FederationDO immutable log. GDPR: single data export covers all federation member Spaces.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT34" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT34 [MEDIUM] — Space Dependency Graph and Version Locking</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Spaces depend on SpaceType versions but have no explicit dependency graph or version lock file. When a SpaceType publisher releases a breaking change, dependent Spaces auto-update silently (or are broken by deprecation) with no resolution path.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement space.lock file concept: SpaceDO stores spacetype_id, pinned_version, last_compatible_version per dependency. SpaceType publisher must increment major version on breaking changes and keep prior major version available for 90 days. DependencyResolver on Space load: check pinned_version still available; if deprecated, surface builder alert with migration guide. Builder can pin to specific version or set semver range (^1.2.0). Platform enforces no Space can run on EOL SpaceType version after 90-day grace.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT35" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT35 [HIGH] — Service Mesh and Internal mTLS Between Workers</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao internal Worker-to-Worker calls use CF Service Bindings which bypass the public internet but carry no mutual authentication. A compromised internal Worker could impersonate any other service with no cryptographic identity verification.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement internal service identity via CF Service Tokens (CF Access service auth tokens). Each internal Worker is assigned a service identity with a scoped CF Service Token. Service-to-service calls include CF-Access-Client-Id / CF-Access-Client-Secret headers validated by the callee's CF Access policy. For high-security paths (billing, auth), require mTLS client certificates. Rotate service tokens quarterly via automated secret rotation. Log all internal service calls with caller identity in Analytics Engine.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT36" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT36 [HIGH] — Just-in-Time Privileged Access Management</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao platform engineers have standing production access with no time-bounding or session recording. A compromised engineer account has persistent access to all customer data. No break-glass procedure with approval workflow exists for emergency production access.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement JIT PAM via CF Access: production access requires Okta MFA + manager approval workflow. Access grants expire in 4 hours; extend requires re-approval. All privileged sessions recorded via session recording (CF Access feature). Break-glass: emergency access requires 2-person approval stored in immutable log. Post-access: automated anomaly detection compares accessed resources vs declared purpose. Quarterly access reviews: all standing production permissions reviewed and reaffirmed or revoked.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT37" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT37 [HIGH] — Zero-Trust Internal Network Architecture</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Internal Naelao services implicitly trust co-located Workers. No zero-trust boundary enforcement, lateral movement prevention, or service-to-service authentication matrix defines which services may call which. A single compromised Worker could reach all internal APIs.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define service communication matrix: explicit allow-list of which Worker may call which (e.g., space-worker → billing-do ALLOWED; space-worker → admin-api DENIED). Enforce via CF Access service token validation at each internal service boundary. Implement network segmentation via CF Teams tunnel policies. Deploy zero-trust policy: default-deny all internal service calls, explicit allows in policy engine. Audit matrix changes via git-controlled policy-as-code with CF Access API sync. Alert on policy violations in real-time.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT38" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT38 [HIGH] — Unit Economics Measurement (CAC / LTV / Payback)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No system tracks builder-level unit economics: customer acquisition cost (CAC) per marketing channel, lifetime value (LTV) per builder cohort, or payback period. Naelao cannot identify which acquisition channels are profitable or when to reduce CAC spend.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement CohortAnalyticsDO: on builder signup, record acquisition_channel (UTM params), plan_tier, signup_date. Monthly CF Workflow computes per-cohort MRR, churn, LTV (MRR × avg_lifetime_months). CAC from marketing spend imported via admin API (Google Ads, Meta spend). Payback = CAC / (MRR × gross_margin). Store cohort analytics in D1 for cross-cohort SQL queries. Build internal dashboard showing LTV:CAC ratio by channel, plan tier. Alert finance team when LTV:CAC &lt; 3:1.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT39" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT39 [HIGH] — Freemium Conversion Funnel Analytics</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No instrumentation tracks builder conversion from free → paid plan. Upgrade trigger events (which features builders tried before upgrading), conversion rate by cohort, and time-to-conversion are invisible. Freemium funnel optimization is guesswork.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Instrument upgrade trigger events in Analytics Engine: feature_gate_hit (builder tried paid feature), upgrade_modal_shown, upgrade_clicked, plan_selected, payment_completed. Store builder_id, plan_from, plan_to, trigger_feature, session_duration. Build conversion funnel in builder analytics dashboard. Compute cohort conversion rates (free → paid within 30/60/90 days). A/B test upgrade modal copy/pricing display. Alert growth team when 30-day cohort conversion drops &gt;20% vs prior month.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT40" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT40 [MEDIUM] — Pricing Elasticity A/B Testing</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao has no mechanism to test different price points for subscription plans or add-ons. Without pricing A/B tests, the team cannot measure demand sensitivity, identify optimal price points, or quantify the revenue impact of pricing changes.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement PricingExperimentDO: assign new builders to pricing variants (e.g., 20% see $29/mo Starter vs $39/mo control). Track conversion_rate and ARPU per variant. Statistical significance via two-proportion z-test; auto-promote winner at p&lt;0.05 with 95% confidence. Integrate with Stripe Products API to dynamically assign price_id per variant. Ensure experiment integrity: once assigned variant, builder always sees same price. GDPR: pricing variants count as automated decision-making — disclose in privacy notice.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT41" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT41 [HIGH] — Builder Space Edit Changelog (Who/What/When)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Every change a builder makes to their Space config (component additions, prompt edits, SpaceType upgrades) is applied immediately with no audit trail of who changed what and when. A team account with 5 members has no history of which member made a breaking change.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ChangelogDO per Space: every mutating API call (PUT /spaces/{id}/config, POST /spaces/{id}/prompts, etc.) appends a changelog entry: { change_id, user_id, action_type, diff_json, timestamp, ip_address }. Store last 1000 entries in DO SQLite; archive older entries to R2. Surface Space changelog in builder dashboard with diff view (before/after JSON). Implement one-click revert to any changelog entry. Export changelog as audit CSV. Include changelog in builder data export.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT42" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT42 [HIGH] — End-User Segmentation Engine</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders cannot define segments of their end-users (e.g., "paid subscribers", "trial users", "enterprise accounts") to customize agent behavior, feature access, or pricing per segment. All end-users receive identical Space behavior regardless of their attributes.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement SegmentationEngine in SpaceDO: builder defines segments via UI (no-code rule builder): {name: "paid_users", rules: [{field: "user.metadata.plan", operator: "eq", value: "paid"}]}. Segments stored as segment_rules JSON in SpaceDO SQLite. On each end-user session init, evaluate segments against user metadata; store matched segment_ids in session token. Agent dispatch and feature gates check session segment_ids. Builder can configure per-segment prompt variations, KB access scopes, and usage limits.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT43" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT43 [HIGH] — Builder Collaboration with Conflict Resolution</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Multi-member builder teams editing a Space configuration concurrently will silently overwrite each other's changes. The last writer wins with no merge conflict detection, no locking, and no change attribution. This causes data loss in team environments.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement optimistic concurrency via config_version counter in SpaceDO SQLite. Every config read returns current version_number. Writes include If-Match: version_number header; server rejects with 409 Conflict if version mismatch. Conflict response includes server-side version for client-side merge UI. For concurrent UI Builder sessions, implement presence system (WebSocket broadcasting who is editing which component). Implement per-section locking (builder A owns Prompt section, builder B owns Layout section) to reduce conflicts.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT44" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT44 [MEDIUM] — Space Health Composite Score</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No composite health metric exists combining uptime, p99 latency, error rate, and end-user satisfaction into a single Space health score. Builders and Naelao support teams cannot triage degraded Spaces without cross-referencing multiple disparate metrics.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement HealthScorer: daily CF Workflow queries Analytics Engine for each Space and computes weighted composite score: uptime_weight×0.3 + latency_score×0.3 + error_rate_score×0.2 + csat_score×0.2. Normalize each dimension to 0-100. Store daily health_score in SpaceDO SQLite health_history table (30 days). Surface traffic-light indicator (green/yellow/red) in builder dashboard. Naelao support dashboard shows bottom-10% health Spaces for proactive outreach. Alert builder when health score drops &gt;20 points in 24h.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT45" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT45 [MEDIUM] — Event Streaming to External Systems (Kafka / Pub/Sub)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders wanting to stream Space events (agent_run_completed, user_message, kb_doc_retrieved) to their own data infrastructure (Kafka, Google Pub/Sub, AWS EventBridge) have no supported integration. Events are only available via Naelao's Analytics Engine, creating data lock-in.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build EventStreamConnector: builder configures outbound stream destination (Kafka topic + bootstrap servers, Pub/Sub topic, EventBridge event bus ARN, webhook URL). CF Queue consumer workers fan out each event to configured destinations. Event schema: { event_type, space_id, session_id, timestamp, payload }. Delivery: at-least-once with exponential retry. Builder-configurable event filter (only forward agent_run_completed events, not all). Encrypt stream credentials at rest in SpaceDO SQLite. Track stream lag and errors in builder dashboard.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT46" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT46 [HIGH] — ETL Pipeline for Cross-Space Analytics</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao has no data warehouse or ETL pipeline for cross-Space analytical queries. Platform-level metrics (total platform DAU, aggregate token usage, platform MRR) require ad-hoc queries across all SpaceDOs and cannot be efficiently computed at query time.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement NaelaoWarehouseETL: hourly CF Workflow aggregates Analytics Engine data per dimension into D1 summary tables: platform_daily_stats(date, total_dau, total_token_usage, total_revenue), space_daily_stats(space_id, date, dau, token_usage, revenue). Monthly, export to BigQuery (Naelao internal analytics). Build internal analytics dashboard on D1 queries. Ensure ETL is idempotent (upsert by date key). Separate pipeline for per-builder billing ETL (feeds billing reconciliation). Data retention in D1: 2 years; archive to BigQuery beyond.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT47" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT47 [MEDIUM] — R2 Cold Storage Tiering and Lifecycle Policies</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> All R2 objects are stored in the same hot tier regardless of access frequency. Old KB document versions, archived session transcripts, and historical backups accumulate indefinitely with no cost-optimizing lifecycle policies or Glacier-equivalent cold tier.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement R2 object lifecycle rules via R2 API: objects with prefix backups/ transition to Infrequent Access after 30 days; objects with prefix archive/ deleted after 90 days. For Space-level assets: keep last 3 component versions, delete older. Implement LifecycleWorker (daily scheduled CF Worker) that queries R2 list with last-modified filter and applies policies via r2.delete() / r2.copy(). Track storage cost savings in Analytics Engine. Expose retention policy configuration UI in builder Space settings (GDPR-compliant data retention).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT48" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d4edda;border-left:4px solid #27ae60;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT48 [LOW] — Global Space Registry for Cross-Space Discovery</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No catalog of publicly discoverable Spaces exists beyond the SpaceType marketplace. Builders and end-users cannot discover public Spaces by category, use case, or SpaceType. Cross-Space linking requires knowing the exact Space URL.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build SpaceRegistry: builders can opt-in to list their Space in the public registry. RegistryDO maintains catalog of opted-in Spaces with: space_id, name, description, category, spaceType_id, star_count, active_users_count. Search index via Vectorize embedding of Space descriptions. REST API: GET /api/registry/spaces?q=&amp;category=&amp;page=. Render registry at naelao.com/explore. Implement Space rating/review system. Builder can un-list at any time (GDPR right to erasure for Space metadata). Moderate listings for policy violations.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT49" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT49 [HIGH] — Space Capacity Runtime Enforcement</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Plan-level quotas (max end-users, max KB docs, max agent runs/day) are defined conceptually but enforcement at runtime is not fully implemented. A free-plan Space can exceed limits without hard stops, creating fairness and cost issues at scale.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement CapacityGuard in SpaceDO: on each session_init, check active_users_count vs plan_max_users; reject with 429 + upgrade prompt if over. On KB doc upload, check kb_doc_count vs plan_max_kb_docs; reject with 400 + upgrade prompt. On agent_run, check daily_runs_count (reset at UTC midnight) vs plan_max_daily_runs; return { error: "daily_limit_reached", limit: N, resets_at: epoch }. All checks against plan_limits fetched from AccountDO (cached 60s in SpaceDO memory). Surface quota gauges in builder dashboard.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AT50" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AT50 [MEDIUM] — R2 Event Notification Dead-Letter Queue Handling</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> The R2 event notification pipeline (Worker receiving object create/delete events) has no dead-letter queue for failed event processing. If the downstream consumer Worker fails (e.g., Vectorize timeout during KB doc indexing), the event is silently lost and the KB doc is never indexed.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Configure naelao-r2-dlq Queue with maxRetries=3 and deadLetterQueue binding. R2 consumer Worker: on processing failure, throws RetryableError to trigger Queue retry with exponential backoff (30s, 90s, 270s). After 3 failures, message routes to DLQ. DLQ monitor Worker: alerts on-call via PagerDuty webhook when DLQ depth &gt; 10. Provides /admin/dlq endpoint to list, inspect, and replay DLQ messages. Track retry counts and failure reasons in Analytics Engine per event_type.
  </div>
</div>

<section id="appendix-au">
<h2>Appendix AU — CF Platform Limits, Mobile/PWA, Builder Auth, Marketplace, Billing, AI Patterns &amp; Operations</h2>

<div id="G-AU01" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU01 [HIGH] — CF Workflows Step Timeout Recovery</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Each CF Workflow step has a 30-second execution timeout. Long-running steps (LLM inference calls, large embedding jobs) may exceed this limit, causing the Workflow step to fail with no documented retry or recovery path, abandoning in-flight work.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> For steps that may exceed 30s, decompose into: (1) submit step — POST request to async LLM provider returning job_id, (2) poll step — loop via Workflow sleep(5s) + status check (max 12 iterations = 60s), (3) retrieve step — fetch result. Use CF Workflow's built-in step.sleep() for polling. Cap total retries at 5; after 5 failures emit workflow_step_failed event to Analytics Engine and notify builder. Document max per-step timeout in builder platform docs.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU02" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU02 [HIGH] — DO Storage Quota Alerting (Approaching 10GB Limit)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Durable Object SQLite storage is capped at 10GB per DO instance. No monitoring or alerting exists when a SpaceDO approaches this limit. A Space with large KB, many sessions, and extensive analytics data will hit the cap and throw StorageError without warning.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement StorageMonitor: weekly scheduled Worker queries each SpaceDO's storage.sql.exec("SELECT page_count * page_size AS db_size FROM pragma_page_count(), pragma_page_size()"). If db_size &gt; 8GB (80% threshold), emit storage_quota_warning event and alert builder via email + in-dashboard banner. At 9.5GB, also alert Naelao ops. Remediation: archive old message history to R2, purge old analytics, compact SQLite. Expose storage usage gauge in builder Space settings.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU03" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU03 [HIGH] — CF KV Namespace Key Quota and Sharding Strategy</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> CF KV allows at most 100 million keys per namespace on paid plans. With millions of Spaces, each storing per-user sessions, feature flags, and cached content in a single KV namespace, the 100M key limit will be reached. No sharding strategy or namespace quota monitoring exists.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement KV namespace sharding: partition keys by space_id prefix hash into 10 namespaces (namespace = kv_buckets[hash(space_id) % 10]). Each namespace handles ~10M keys at scale. Monitor key count per namespace via CF API daily; alert at 80M keys per namespace. Define key naming convention: {space_id}:{key_type}:{key}. Document migration procedure for adding a new shard. Consider KV only for ephemeral/cache data; prefer DO SQLite for durable per-Space data.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU04" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU04 [HIGH] — Analytics Engine Write Limits and Event Sampling</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> CF Analytics Engine has a default write limit of 25 million data points per day. High-traffic Spaces generating per-message analytics events can exhaust this limit, causing silent event drops and incomplete billing/usage data at platform scale.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement adaptive sampling in AnalyticsEmitter: track daily_event_count in KV (reset at UTC midnight). When daily_event_count &gt; 20M, enable 50% sampling for low-priority events (page_view, ui_click); always write high-priority events (agent_run, token_deduction, error). Log sampling decisions for billing reconciliation. Request Analytics Engine write limit increase (paid plan: 25M/day default; enterprise: custom). Alert Naelao ops when daily count exceeds 22M so limit increase can be pre-requested.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU05" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU05 [HIGH] — CF Queues Throughput Limit (5,000 msgs/sec) at Platform Scale</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> CF Queues support up to 5,000 messages per second per queue. At platform scale with millions of Spaces generating events simultaneously, a single naelao-events queue will hit this throughput ceiling, causing message backpressure and delayed event processing.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Shard event queues by space_id: route Space events to naelao-events-{hash(space_id) % 8} (8 queues × 5,000 = 40,000 msgs/sec capacity). Implement QueueRouter Worker that selects queue by consistent hash. Monitor per-queue depth and message lag via CF API; alert at &gt;50,000 messages backed up. For bursty workloads, implement producer-side batching: buffer up to 10 events per space_id over 100ms and send as single batch message. Expand shard count without code change by incrementing modulus and rebalancing.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU06" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#f8d7da;border-left:4px solid #c0392b;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU06 [CRITICAL] — DO SQLite Schema Migration Safety</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> DO SQLite schema evolves as Naelao features are added (new tables, new columns). No migration version tracking, idempotency guarantees, or rollback mechanism exists. A failed migration mid-execution leaves the DO in an inconsistent schema state that causes all subsequent requests to fail.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement MigrationRunner on every DO initialization: CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS __migrations(version INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, applied_at TEXT). On DO startup, run pending migrations in version order as SQLite transactions. Each migration: BEGIN TRANSACTION; [ALTER TABLE / CREATE TABLE]; INSERT INTO __migrations VALUES(N, datetime('now')); COMMIT. On failure, ROLLBACK — DO returns to prior schema. Never use destructive migrations in production (no DROP COLUMN). Test all migrations in staging with representative data. Version migrations as sequential integers; never reorder or delete.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU07" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU07 [HIGH] — Progressive Web App (PWA) Support for Spaces</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Spaces have no web app manifest or Service Worker, preventing end-users from installing Spaces as standalone PWAs on their device. Without PWA support, Spaces cannot appear in mobile home screens, lack native-app-like UX, and cannot work offline.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Generate per-Space web app manifest at /spaces/{space_id}/manifest.json: { name, short_name, start_url, display: "standalone", theme_color (from ThemeConfig), icons: [192, 512 px] }. Register Service Worker sw.js: precache Space shell (HTML/CSS/JS); implement stale-while-revalidate for component assets; network-first for API calls with offline fallback page. Builder can configure PWA settings (app name, splash screen icon, theme color) in UI Builder. PWA install eligibility requires HTTPS (already CF-native).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU08" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU08 [HIGH] — Web Push Notifications for End-Users via VAPID</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No Web Push API integration exists for delivering push notifications to end-users when their Space tab is closed. Builders cannot notify end-users of async events (AI response ready, new message, appointment reminder) outside an active session.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement Web Push service: generate VAPID keypair stored in PlatformSecrets KV. Client-side: request Notification permission; call pushManager.subscribe({applicationServerKey: vapid_public_key}); POST subscription object to /api/spaces/{id}/push/subscribe stored in SpaceDO SQLite. Server-side: NotificationDO holds VAPID sender; on trigger event, calls web-push library to send payload to stored subscription. Browser Service Worker handles push event: shows notification via self.registration.showNotification(). Handle PushSubscriptionChange (subscription refresh).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU09" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU09 [HIGH] — Mobile-Responsive UI Builder Components</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> The UI Builder operates on a desktop layout canvas only. No mobile breakpoint preview, touch event support, or responsive layout configuration exists. Spaces built without mobile consideration render poorly on phones, with overlapping components and non-touch-friendly controls.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Add breakpoint mode switcher to UI Builder canvas: Desktop (1280px) / Tablet (768px) / Mobile (375px). Each component stores per-breakpoint overrides in ComponentConfig: { desktop: {cols: 12}, tablet: {cols: 6}, mobile: {cols: 12} }. ComponentRenderer reads viewport and applies appropriate config. Add touch-event handling to interactive components (tap for click, swipe for carousel). Test all pre-built components for touch usability on iOS Safari and Android Chrome. Surface mobile preview in builder dashboard.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU10" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU10 [HIGH] — Native Mobile App SDK for Space Embedding</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders wanting to embed their Space in a React Native or Flutter app have no supported SDK. Without a native SDK, mobile developers must implement the full Space protocol (auth, WebSocket, streaming) from scratch, creating fragmented integrations.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build @naelao/react-native-sdk: SpaceProvider component wrapping WebView with injected JavaScript bridge. Bridge protocol: postMessage for agent_run requests, streaming SSE via injected JS EventSource polyfill, auth token refresh via native secure storage (Keychain/Keystore). For Flutter: naelao_flutter package using webview_flutter with same bridge. SDK handles: session token refresh, push notification registration (links to AU08), biometric auth trigger (AU38), deep link handling. Publish to npm and pub.dev with example apps.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU11" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU11 [MEDIUM] — iOS and Android Deep Links for Space Content</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No universal links (iOS) or app links (Android) configuration exists for Spaces. Sharing a link to specific Space content (a conversation, a KB article) from a native app context fails to deep-link into the app and falls back to the browser, breaking native app UX.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Configure Apple App Site Association file (/.well-known/apple-app-site-association) served from Space domain with CF Worker: { "applinks": { "details": [{"appIDs": ["{team_id}.{bundle_id}"], "components": [{"/" : "/spaces/*"}]}] } }. Analogously configure assetlinks.json for Android. Builder provides team ID and bundle ID in Space Settings. CF Worker serves both files at well-known paths. Native SDK (AU10) registers handleDeepLink() to parse space_id + content_id from URL and navigate to correct screen.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU12" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU12 [MEDIUM] — PWA Install Prompt in Space UI</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No beforeinstallprompt handling exists in Space shells. The browser's native PWA install prompt appears randomly (if at all) with no coordination with the builder's desired install CTA placement, timing, or A/B testing of install prompts.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Space shell script: window.addEventListener("beforeinstallprompt", e =&gt; { e.preventDefault(); store deferred prompt in ref }). Builder can place InstallPromptButton component in UI Builder; clicking it calls deferredPrompt.prompt(). Track install_prompt_shown, install_prompt_accepted, install_prompt_dismissed in Analytics Engine. Builder configures prompt trigger rule: after N sessions, after first successful interaction, or on explicit button. Show install prompt only if app not already installed (check window.matchMedia("(display-mode: standalone)")).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU13" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU13 [MEDIUM] — Space Offline Mode via Service Worker Cache</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> When an end-user loses network connectivity, their Space shows a generic browser offline error. No Service Worker caches recent conversations or KB answers, and no synchronization strategy exists for messages sent offline.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Service Worker strategy: on install, cache Space shell and last 50 agent responses. On fetch: for API calls, try network; if offline, serve last cached response with offline indicator. For new messages sent offline: queue in IndexedDB outbox (message_text, timestamp, session_id). On network restore (sync event or online event), replay outbox to Space API. Display offline banner in Space UI with "You are offline — messages will send when reconnected." Sync status tracked in Space shell state.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU14" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU14 [HIGH] — WebAuthn / Passkeys for Builder Platform Authentication</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builder platform login uses password + TOTP only. No WebAuthn/FIDO2 passkey support exists for the builder dashboard. Passkeys are phishing-resistant and more secure than passwords; their absence leaves builder accounts vulnerable to credential phishing.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement WebAuthn registration in builder settings: navigator.credentials.create({publicKey: challenge}) for passkey registration. Store credential_id + public_key in AccountDO SQLite. Authentication flow: challenge from server → navigator.credentials.get() → verify signature server-side. Support platform authenticators (TouchID, FaceID, Windows Hello) and roaming authenticators (YubiKey). Allow multiple passkeys per account. Show passkey management UI (view, delete registered passkeys). Fall back to password if passkey assertion fails.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU15" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU15 [HIGH] — Hardware Security Key (YubiKey / FIDO2) as Builder 2FA</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builder two-factor authentication supports only TOTP authenticator apps. Hardware security keys (YubiKey, Google Titan) using FIDO2/U2F protocol are not supported. Enterprise builders requiring hardware-bound second factors have no supported path.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Extend WebAuthn implementation (AU14) to explicitly support roaming authenticators (cross-platform, not just platform). During registration, set authenticatorSelection.authenticatorAttachment = "cross-platform" to accept USB/NFC/BLE security keys. Store attestation data and AAGUID to identify key type. UI: show "Add Security Key" option alongside "Add Passkey" in MFA settings. For enterprise plans, allow enforcement policy: "Require hardware security key for all team members." Integrate with existing MFA enrollment flow without breaking TOTP.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU16" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU16 [HIGH] — Session Hijacking and Cookie Theft Protection</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builder session tokens are stored as httpOnly cookies but have no binding to client fingerprint or IP. An attacker who steals a session cookie via XSS or network interception can use it from any location/device. Anomalous session reuse is not detected.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement session binding: on login, compute session_fingerprint = HMAC(user_agent + cf_ip_continent + device_id_header). Store in SessionDO alongside session_token. On each request, recompute fingerprint; if mismatch exceeds threshold (new continent + new device), flag as suspicious: require re-authentication or MFA challenge. Log anomalous_session_reuse events to immutable audit log. Implement concurrent session detection: builder can see active sessions and revoke any. Send email alert when new session login detected from unrecognized device.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU17" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU17 [HIGH] — Platform Support Ticketing System with SLA Tiers</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No platform support ticketing system exists. Builders have no structured way to report issues, and Naelao has no SLA-tracked response system. Support requests go through generic email or community channels with no priority routing, SLA tracking, or escalation path.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build SupportTicketDO: ticket = { ticket_id, builder_id, plan_tier, subject, body, status, priority, created_at, first_response_at, resolved_at }. SLA targets: Free 48h first response; Pro 8h; Enterprise 1h. Builder submits via in-dashboard "Get Help" form or API. SLA timer starts on submission; if first_response_at &gt; SLA, auto-escalate to tier-2 queue and notify support lead. Integration: sync tickets to Zendesk/Linear via webhook. Surface ticket status in builder dashboard. After resolution, send CSAT survey (links to G-AR34).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU18" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU18 [MEDIUM] — Support SLA Breach Alerting</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Even if a ticketing system exists, no automated alerting notifies the support team when a ticket is approaching or has breached its SLA response window. SLA breaches go undetected until a builder escalates externally.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement SLAMonitor: scheduled CF Worker runs every 15 minutes, queries SupportTicketDO for tickets with status=open AND first_response_at IS NULL AND created_at &lt; NOW() - sla_window(plan_tier). For each at-risk ticket, send PagerDuty alert with ticket_id, builder_id, time_over_sla, plan_tier. For breached tickets, send automated apology email to builder with next-response commitment. Track SLA adherence rate in support metrics dashboard. Report monthly SLA performance to leadership. Enterprise SLA: contractual penalty credits on breach.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU19" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
