<div id="G-AU49" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#f8d7da;border-left:4px solid #c0392b;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU49 [CRITICAL] — DO Concurrent Request Serialization and Write Ordering</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Durable Objects process one request at a time (serialized), but this guarantee only holds within a single DO instance. When SpaceDO is partitioned by session_id and multiple sessions for the same space_id write to a shared AccountDO concurrently (e.g., deducting credits), write ordering and atomicity across the concurrent credit deductions are not formally guaranteed.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Enforce write serialization within AccountDO: all credit deductions are processed by a single AccountDO per account_id — this is already implicit in DO architecture (one DO per account). Ensure SpaceDO never directly mutates AccountDO state — always route via AccountDO RPC. Add sequence numbers to all AccountDO mutations to detect out-of-order delivery. Implement AccountDO idempotency layer: each deduction carries deduction_request_id; duplicate deductions return cached result without double-deduction. Document DO concurrency model clearly in internal architecture docs to prevent future mis-implementations.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AU50" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AU50 [MEDIUM] — Builder Email Notification Preference Management</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao sends builders various email notifications (billing, platform alerts, Space health, feature announcements) but no preference management center exists. Builders cannot unsubscribe from specific categories, change frequency, or manage notification channels (email vs Slack vs webhook), leading to notification fatigue and unsubscribes.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build NotificationPreferencesDO per builder: stores { category: string, channels: email|slack|webhook, frequency: immediate|daily_digest|weekly, enabled: boolean }. Categories: billing_alerts, space_health_alerts, platform_updates, marketing, security_alerts (cannot disable). Builder manages preferences at /settings/notifications. Slack integration: builder provides Slack webhook URL; notifications fan-out to email + Slack. Webhook option: POST to builder-specified URL with notification payload. Daily digest: aggregate notifications into single email (BuildDigestWorker runs at 9am builder timezone). CAN-SPAM compliance: all marketing emails include unsubscribe link updating preference DO.
  </div>
</div>

<section id="appendix-av">
<h2>Appendix AV — SpaceType Composition, Billing Analytics, Multi-Tenancy, Webhooks, RAG Quality &amp; CF Platform</h2>

<div id="G-AV01" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV01 [HIGH] — SpaceType Composition and Inheritance</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> SpaceTypes are monolithic — a publisher cannot build a specialized SpaceType on top of a base SpaceType, inheriting its tools, prompts, and KB structure while overriding specific properties. Every SpaceType must be built from scratch, causing massive duplication across similar products.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement SpaceType extends field: { "extends": "base-customer-support-v1.0" }. On install, RegistryDO resolves parent chain and merges config: child properties override parent, tool arrays are merged (child additions + parent tools), system prompt uses parent as template with child overrides. Circular inheritance detection via graph traversal. Version pinning on parent reference. Child SpaceType publisher must own or have explicit license to extend parent. Surface inheritance chain in marketplace listing. Limit inheritance depth to 3 levels.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV02" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#f8d7da;border-left:4px solid #c0392b;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV02 [CRITICAL] — SpaceType Config Schema Validation</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> When a builder installs a SpaceType and fills in the configuration form, the submitted config JSON is not validated against the SpaceType's declared config schema. Malformed configs (missing required fields, wrong types, invalid enum values) are stored and silently break the agent at runtime.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> SpaceType publisher declares config_schema as JSON Schema (draft-07). On builder config submission, SpaceConfigDO runs JSON Schema validation via ajv library compiled at Worker startup. Return structured validation errors: { field: "api_key", error: "required field missing", schema_path: "$.properties.api_key" }. Surface field-level errors in builder UI config form. Prevent Space activation if config fails validation. On SpaceType version upgrade: validate existing config against new schema; surface migration warnings if new required fields added.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV03" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV03 [HIGH] — SpaceType Hot-Reload Without DO Eviction</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Updating a Space's SpaceType configuration (e.g., changing system prompt, adding a KB, modifying a tool definition) currently requires the Durable Object to process a full re-initialization. During high-traffic periods, this causes a brief service interruption for active end-user sessions.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement hot-reload protocol: POST /api/spaces/{id}/config with If-Match: version header triggers ConfigReloadRPC on SpaceDO. SpaceDO atomically updates in-memory config cache and increments config_version without evicting existing WebSocket sessions. Active sessions continue with prior config through their current conversation; new sessions start with new config. Large changes (tool additions requiring re-embedding) queued as async tasks. Surface reload success/failure in builder dashboard with affected session count.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV04" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV04 [HIGH] — Agent Episodic Memory System</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Agents can only access KB documents and the current conversation context. No episodic memory exists for storing and retrieving specific past events (e.g., "User X mentioned they're a developer on March 5th"; "User Y had an issue with feature Z last week"). Agents greet returning users as strangers.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement EpisodicMemoryDO per Space per end-user: on each conversation completion, extract key facts/events via LLM summary ("user_role: developer", "past_issue: feature_z", "preference: detailed explanations"). Store as memory_entries(user_id, fact_text, fact_embedding, created_at, relevance_score). On new session: query top-5 relevant memories by embedding similarity to opening message, inject as "What you know about this user:" block in system prompt. Memory entry TTL configurable by builder (default 90 days). GDPR: include in user data export; purge on erasure request.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV05" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV05 [MEDIUM] — Agent Procedural Memory (Learned How-To Workflows)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Agents must re-derive solutions to recurring workflow challenges on each session. No procedural memory stores successful multi-step solutions (e.g., "to resolve billing issue: 1. check subscription status 2. verify payment method 3. offer extension"). Agents reinvent the wheel every session.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ProceduralMemoryStore in SpaceDO SQLite: procedures(procedure_id, trigger_pattern, steps_json, success_rate, usage_count). Populate via two paths: (1) builder-defined playbooks uploaded in dashboard, (2) agent-learned: when a multi-step resolution receives positive end-user feedback, LLM extracts the procedure and stores it. On session: match query against procedure trigger patterns (embedding similarity); if match &gt; 0.85, inject procedure steps into context. Track procedure usage and success rate; prune procedures with success_rate &lt; 0.3 after 20 uses.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV06" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV06 [HIGH] — Real-Time Context Injection into Agent System Prompt</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> System prompts are static strings stored in SpaceDO. Agents have no access to real-time dynamic data that should inform their behavior: current datetime, user's local timezone, session origin (web/mobile/embedded), user's geographic location, or live Space metadata. Agents give temporally incorrect responses (e.g., "tomorrow is Monday" when it is not).
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ContextInjector middleware: before every LLM call, enrich system prompt with dynamic block: "Current UTC time: {ISO8601}. User timezone: {tz from Accept-Language or user profile}. User country: {CF-IPCountry}. Session channel: {web|mobile|embed}. Space name: {space_name}. User plan: {user.metadata.plan}." Builder marks fields to include/exclude in Space settings. Timezone detected from CF request headers or user profile preference. Injected block appended after static system prompt and before conversation history.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV07" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV07 [HIGH] — Tool Definition Schema Versioning and Backwards Compatibility</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Agent tool definitions (function calling schemas) are deployed as atomic updates with no version tracking. When a tool's input schema changes (field renamed, new required parameter), active conversations that have already emitted tool_call tokens with the old schema produce malformed tool calls that fail at dispatch.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement tool schema versioning: each tool has version field incremented on any schema change. SpaceDO stores all tool versions in tool_versions table. Tool dispatcher resolves version by checking the schema_version field embedded in each tool_call. For major version bumps (breaking): run parallel for 1 deploy cycle (dispatch old schema to old handler, new schema to new handler). Define semver policy: PATCH (add optional field) is always backwards-compatible; MINOR (add required field) requires migration; MAJOR (remove/rename field) requires parallel run period.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV08" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV08 [MEDIUM] — Dynamic Agent Capability Discovery at Runtime</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> An agent's available tools are fixed at Space configuration time. There is no mechanism for an agent to dynamically discover which tools are currently available (e.g., a connector is temporarily unavailable, a Space plan was downgraded removing premium tools) or adjust its behavior based on runtime capability state.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement CapabilityRegistry in SpaceDO: on each session init, compute available_tools list by filtering configured tools against: (1) connector health checks (is external API reachable?), (2) plan entitlements (does current plan include this tool?), (3) feature flags (is this tool in beta for this Space?). Inject available_tools list into LLM tool_choice parameter. If a tool becomes unavailable mid-session (connector goes down), SpaceDO pushes tool_unavailable WebSocket event to session; agent receives "Tool X is currently unavailable, use alternative Y."
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV09" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV09 [HIGH] — MRR Decomposition Analytics (New / Expansion / Churn)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao tracks total MRR as a single number but does not decompose it into: new MRR (first-time subscriptions), expansion MRR (upgrades), contraction MRR (downgrades), churned MRR (cancellations), and reactivation MRR (returning customers). Without decomposition, growth vs churn signals are invisible.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build MRRDecompositionDO: on each Stripe subscription event (created/updated/deleted), classify MRR delta as: new (first subscription), expansion (plan upgrade), contraction (plan downgrade), churn (cancellation), reactivation (churned customer re-subscribing). Store daily_mrr_movements(date, type, amount_usd, subscription_id). Aggregate weekly/monthly in D1. Build internal finance dashboard showing MRR waterfall chart. Alert on churn MRR exceeding new+expansion MRR for 2 consecutive weeks (net negative growth signal).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV10" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV10 [HIGH] — Net Revenue Retention (NRR) Measurement</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Net Revenue Retention (NRR = (beginning_MRR + expansion - contraction - churn) / beginning_MRR × 100%) is not calculated. NRR above 100% indicates that existing customers are growing faster than they are churning — the primary SaaS health metric — but it is not tracked anywhere in the platform.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Compute monthly NRR in CohortAnalyticsDO (AT38): for each monthly cohort, track beginning_MRR (cohort's MRR at month 0) and current_MRR (cohort's MRR at month N). NRR = current_MRR / beginning_MRR. Store as cohort_nrr(cohort_month, measurement_month, nrr_pct). Build NRR trend chart in finance dashboard. Alert leadership when platform NRR drops below 100% (absolute revenue shrinkage). Segment NRR by plan tier (free→paid NRR, pro NRR, enterprise NRR) to identify where expansion is strongest.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV11" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV11 [HIGH] — Builder Cohort Retention Curves</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No cohort-based retention analysis exists for Naelao's builder base. Without retention curves (what % of Jan-2026 signups are still active in Feb, March, April...), the team cannot identify when in the builder journey churn spikes, nor validate whether product improvements increase retention.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement CohortRetentionDO: define "active" as: logged into dashboard OR made API call within the period. On each builder activity event, update last_active_at in AccountDO. Monthly CF Workflow computes: for each cohort (by signup_month), what % of builders had any activity in subsequent month N. Store cohort_retention(cohort_month, months_since_signup, active_count, original_cohort_size, retention_pct). Visualize as retention heatmap table in internal analytics. Alert when any cohort's month-1 retention drops below platform average — early signal of onboarding regression.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV12" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV12 [HIGH] — Billing Dispute Resolution Workflow</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders who dispute a charge (unexpected overage, billing error, double charge) have no formal dispute workflow. Without a structured process backed by usage audit data, disputes devolve into ad-hoc email exchanges with no SLA and no data-driven resolution path.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement DisputeWorkflow: builder opens dispute via /settings/billing/disputes with reason (incorrect_charge, service_outage, unauthorized_charge) and date range. System auto-queries Analytics Engine for builder's usage data matching disputed period, generates usage report PDF. Support agent reviews: if usage supports charge, provide evidence to builder; if not, issue Stripe refund/credit via Stripe Credit API. Track dispute_opened, dispute_evidence_generated, dispute_resolved, refund_issued events. SLA: initial response within 2 business days. Integrate dispute data with dunning logic to pause retries during open dispute.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV13" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV13 [HIGH] — Prorated Billing on Mid-Cycle Plan Changes</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> When a builder upgrades or downgrades their subscription mid-billing-period, Stripe proration behavior is not explicitly configured. Default Stripe behavior may charge the full new plan price without crediting unused days on the old plan, or defer the change to the next cycle — neither behavior is intentionally specified.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Explicitly configure Stripe proration: on upgrade mid-cycle, use proration_behavior: "create_prorations" — builder charged prorated difference immediately. On downgrade, use proration_behavior: "always_invoice" with credit for remaining period applied to next invoice. Preview prorated amount before confirming via Stripe.subscriptions.retrieveUpcomingInvoice(). Show preview in upgrade/downgrade confirmation UI: "You will be charged $X now for the remainder of this billing period." For enterprise annual plans: handle mid-year upgrade with single proration invoice.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV14" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV14 [HIGH] — End-User Subscription Lifecycle Within Space</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders who charge their end-users for Space access (monthly subscription tiers, pay-per-session) have no native UI for end-users to manage their own subscription within the Space. End-users cannot upgrade, downgrade, view invoices, or cancel their subscription without leaving the Space.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build SubscriptionPortal component for Space UI library: shows current plan, next billing date, usage this cycle, and action buttons (Upgrade, Downgrade, Cancel, View Invoices). On action, calls Naelao API which proxies to Stripe Customer Portal (or Stripe Billing Portal session). Builder configures which plans end-users can switch between. Cancel flow: show retention offer (1-month discount) before confirming cancellation. Invoice history: list of past invoices with download link (Stripe-hosted). Accessible at Space URL /billing or via builder-placed SubscriptionPortal component in Space UI.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV15" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#f8d7da;border-left:4px solid #c0392b;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV15 [CRITICAL] — Noisy Neighbor Isolation Between Spaces</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Multiple Spaces share CF Worker instances (V8 isolates on the same machine). A CPU-intensive Space (heavy LLM post-processing, large context manipulation) can consume isolate CPU budget, causing other Spaces' requests to be queued. No per-Space CPU budget or priority scheduling exists.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement per-Space CPU isolation via CF Workers for Platforms (AT10): each Space runs in its own WfP tenant Worker script with isolated CPU time budget. Heavy compute tasks (embedding, bulk export) routed to separate Unbound Workers with dedicated CPU time. Implement request priority queue in DispatcherWorker: classify request priority (interactive session = high, batch export = low). Monitor per-Space CPU time via Analytics Engine; alert on Spaces consuming &gt;5× average CPU per request. Rate-limit CPU-intensive operations (bulk processing) to off-peak hours with configurable scheduling.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV16" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV16 [HIGH] — Per-Space CPU Time Limit Enforcement</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> CF Workers have a 30ms CPU time limit (free tier) or 30s (paid Unbound Workers). Heavy operations in a Space Worker (complex in-memory data processing, large JSON serialization) can hit CPU time limit and throw CPUTimeExceeded errors with no per-Space enforcement policy or graceful degradation.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define per-Space CPU budget policy: interactive requests (chat, config fetch) use Standard Workers (30ms CPU, fast); background tasks (export, re-indexing, eval) use Unbound Workers (up to 30s CPU). For standard requests approaching CPU limit: implement work-chunking — split large operations (context assembly, response post-processing) into sub-operations that each complete within 10ms. Instrument CPU usage via performance.now() boundaries. Monitor CPU_time_used per space_id in Analytics Engine. Alert when a Space consistently uses &gt;80% of CPU budget per request.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV17" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV17 [HIGH] — Builder Guided Onboarding Wizard</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> New builders face a blank dashboard with no guided onboarding flow. They must discover: how to choose a SpaceType, configure it, add KB content, customize the UI, test the agent, and publish the Space — all without guided context. High early drop-off reflects this friction.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build OnboardingWizard component in builder dashboard: 6-step checklist (1. Choose SpaceType, 2. Configure Space, 3. Add Knowledge, 4. Preview &amp; Test, 5. Customize Look, 6. Publish &amp; Share). Each step has inline help text and a 30-second demo video. Persist completion state in AccountDO onboarding_progress JSON. Step completion auto-detected (KB docs &gt; 0 = step 3 done). Show progress bar and step count. "Skip tour" option but re-accessible from /onboarding. Track step_completion_rate per step in Analytics Engine for onboarding funnel analysis. Trigger celebration animation on final publish step.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV18" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV18 [HIGH] — Builder Configuration Draft Save and Resume</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builder Space configurations are all-or-nothing publishes. If a builder starts configuring a complex SpaceType (long system prompt, multiple tool configurations) and navigates away, their work is lost. No draft save mechanism exists.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ConfigDraftDO per builder per Space: auto-save draft every 30 seconds of UI inactivity. Draft stored as draft_config JSON in SpaceDO SQLite draft_configs table. On return to config editor: detect pending draft and offer "Resume draft" or "Discard." Draft includes full editor state (open panels, cursor position, active tab). Published config and draft are independent; publishing creates new entry in changelog (AU41). Expire drafts after 30 days of no editing. Show "Unsaved changes" indicator in editor. Allow multiple named draft versions for A/B testing different configs.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV19" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV19 [HIGH] — Proactive Platform Changelog Notifications to Builders</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> When Naelao changes platform behavior (deprecates an API endpoint, changes rate limits, requires config schema updates), builders are not proactively notified. They discover changes when their Space breaks in production, not in advance.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ChangelogNotificationDO: each platform change classified as: breaking (immediate email + in-dashboard banner + 90-day migration window), deprecation (email + banner + sunset date), enhancement (optional email digest). Builder configures notification preferences (AU50). Breaking changes trigger: (1) email to all affected builders (those using deprecated endpoint), (2) red banner in dashboard until acknowledged, (3) API response header X-Deprecation-Warning on affected endpoints, (4) SDK console.warn() for deprecated methods. Track notification_delivered, notification_acknowledged per builder per change.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV20" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV20 [HIGH] — Builder Actionable Error Message Quality</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builder-facing API and dashboard error messages are often raw internal errors (HTTP 500, "Internal Server Error", CF Worker unhandled exception messages). These provide no actionable guidance and expose internal implementation details, creating poor DX and security concerns.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ErrorMapper layer in all public-facing Workers: catch all unhandled errors before they reach the response. Map to user-facing error schema: { error_code: "SPACE_CONFIG_INVALID", message: "Your Space config is missing a required field: api_key", docs_url: "docs.naelao.com/errors/SPACE_CONFIG_INVALID", support_ticket_url, request_id }. Error codes are enumerated and documented. Never expose stack traces or internal error messages to builders. For 500 errors: log full error to Logpush + PagerDuty; return generic "An unexpected error occurred. Request ID: {req_id}" to builder. Surface request_id in builder dashboard error history for support reference.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV21" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV21 [HIGH] — Pre-Limit Usage Warning Alerts (80%/90% Thresholds)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders have no advance warning before they hit plan limits (token quota, storage, active sessions). They discover the limit when operations start failing (HTTP 429), creating bad UX for their end-users with no time to upgrade beforehand.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement UsageAlertDO: track resource utilization ratios (used/limit) for all quota dimensions. Thresholds: at 80% send in-dashboard yellow warning banner + email. At 90% send red banner + urgent email with upgrade CTA. At 100% (hit) send immediate email + automatic upgrade prompt modal in dashboard. Alert preferences configurable (AU50). Compute utilization daily via scheduled Worker querying SpaceDO and AccountDO. For token quota: real-time check via token_deductions_this_month / monthly_token_limit. Surface utilization gauges on builder dashboard home page (mini progress bars per resource).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV22" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#f8d7da;border-left:4px solid #c0392b;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV22 [CRITICAL] — Outbound Webhook HMAC-SHA256 Signing with Replay Prevention</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao sends outbound webhooks (agent run completed, Space event) but does not sign the webhook payload with HMAC-SHA256. Webhook receivers cannot verify that the request originates from Naelao and has not been tampered with. No timestamp-based replay attack prevention exists.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement webhook signing: for each outbound webhook, compute signature = HMAC-SHA256(secret, "v1:" + unix_timestamp + "." + request_body). Send headers: X-Naelao-Signature: v1={hex_signature}, X-Naelao-Timestamp: {unix_epoch}. Builder retrieves webhook_secret from dashboard (stored encrypted in SpaceDO). Receiver validates: recompute signature, compare; reject if timestamp &gt; 5 minutes old (replay prevention). Provide verification code snippets for Node, Python, Go in docs. Rotate webhook secret in dashboard (zero-downtime: accept both old and new secret for 60s transition).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV23" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV23 [HIGH] — Webhook Delivery Log and Self-Serve Replay UI</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> There is no log of outbound webhook deliveries. When a webhook-consuming service was down (e.g., 2am outage), builders cannot see which webhooks failed, inspect their payloads, or replay them. Failed events are permanently lost with no recovery path.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement WebhookDeliveryLog in SpaceDO SQLite: webhook_deliveries(delivery_id, event_type, payload_hash, attempt_count, last_status_code, response_body_preview, delivered_at, created_at). Retain last 1000 deliveries per Space. Builder dashboard shows webhook log with status (success/failed/pending), HTTP status code, and response preview. "Replay" button re-submits specific delivery with original payload. Bulk replay: select date range and re-send all failures. Export log as CSV. Alert builder via notification (AU50) when &gt;10 consecutive deliveries fail to the same endpoint.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV24" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV24 [HIGH] — Per-Endpoint API Rate Limit Configuration</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> All Naelao API endpoints share the same global rate limit bucket per API key. High-frequency low-cost endpoints (GET /api/spaces/{id}/config) and low-frequency high-cost endpoints (POST /api/spaces/{id}/run) compete for the same bucket. Builders hitting limits on cheap endpoints are blocked from expensive ones.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define endpoint cost tiers: Tier 1 (read operations: 1 unit), Tier 2 (config writes: 5 units), Tier 3 (agent runs: 20 units), Tier 4 (bulk operations: 100 units). Rate limit bucket: 1000 units/minute. Each request deducts its tier cost from bucket. Return X-RateLimit-Cost: {units} header showing cost of each request. Builder API keys can configure per-endpoint overrides: raise Tier 1 limit for high-frequency read cases. Enterprise: custom tier costs and bucket sizes. Surface endpoint-level rate limit consumption in builder analytics (AU21).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV25" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV25 [HIGH] — X-Request-Id Correlation ID Propagation</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Internal Naelao requests across Workers (SpaceWorker → SpaceDO → BillingDO → VectorizeDO) do not carry a shared correlation ID. Debugging a failed agent run requires manually correlating logs across multiple systems with no shared identifier, making distributed tracing nearly impossible.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Generate X-Request-Id (UUID v4) at entry Worker for every inbound request. Propagate via: (1) DO RPC calls: pass requestId as first parameter to all DO methods, (2) service-to-service calls: X-Request-Id header on all fetch() calls, (3) CF Queue messages: requestId field in message JSON, (4) Analytics Engine writes: request_id field on all events. Entry Worker includes Request-Id in response header. Builder API responses always include X-Naelao-Request-Id. Logpush output includes requestId for Splunk correlation. Implement opentelemetry-compatible trace ID format for future tracing integration.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV26" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV26 [HIGH] — SSE Keepalive for Proxy and Load Balancer Timeout Prevention</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Server-Sent Events (SSE) connections for streaming agent responses are silently terminated by intermediate proxies and load balancers that have 60-90 second idle timeouts. If an LLM takes &gt;60s to produce the first token, the SSE connection closes and the response is lost.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement SSE keepalive: every 15 seconds of no data, send SSE comment line ": keepalive\n\n" over the stream. SSE spec defines comments (lines starting with ": ") as valid but ignored by clients. This resets proxy idle timeout counters. Additionally, send structured keepalive events: "data: {type:\"keepalive\", timestamp: epoch}\n\n" so client-side code can detect connection liveness. Client-side: implement reconnection logic (EventSource auto-reconnects; set Last-Event-ID header from last received event ID for resumption). Document maximum expected TTFF (Time To First Byte) so builders can set appropriate client timeouts.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV27" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV27 [HIGH] — Streaming Response Abort and LLM Connection Cleanup</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> When a client disconnects mid-stream (user navigates away, browser tab closes), the Worker continues consuming LLM API tokens and streaming to a dead connection. No abort signal handler or LLM API connection cancellation exists, wasting compute and incurring unnecessary token costs.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement stream abort handling: in SSE Worker, attach AbortController to LLM API fetch call. On client disconnect (detected via request.signal.aborted or write error on response stream), call abortController.abort() to cancel the LLM API request. For OpenAI: streaming fetch abort automatically closes the connection. For other providers: use provider-specific cancel API if available. Log stream_aborted events with tokens_consumed_before_abort in Analytics Engine for cost attribution. Deduct only actually consumed tokens from credit balance (not estimated). SpaceDO cleans up partial message records on abort.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV28" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV28 [MEDIUM] — Builder-Configurable Custom Error Pages for Spaces</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> When a Space is unavailable (over quota, maintenance mode, agent error), end-users see generic browser error pages or raw JSON error responses. Builders cannot customize these error experiences with branded, helpful messaging.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Add error page configuration to Space settings: builder provides HTML/text template for error scenarios: quota_exceeded, maintenance, service_error, not_found. Templates support merge tags: {{space_name}}, {{support_email}}, {{retry_after}}. Templates stored in SpaceDO SQLite. SpaceEntryWorker intercepts error conditions before responding: if error matches configured template, render template as HTML response with appropriate HTTP status code. Default templates provided (styled to Space theme). Builder can also configure redirect URLs for each error type. Track custom_error_shown events to monitor error frequency.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV29" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV29 [HIGH] — Space Maintenance Mode with Custom Message</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders have no way to put their Space in a controlled maintenance state while making breaking configuration changes. Any config change is applied live to active end-users, potentially causing degraded experience during the change window.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Add maintenance_mode toggle to Space config: { enabled: true, message: "We're updating our AI — back in 5 minutes!", ends_at: "2026-06-22T14:00:00Z" }. When maintenance_mode is enabled, SpaceEntryWorker returns 503 with custom message to all end-user requests. Builder dashboard shows maintenance mode indicator. Active WebSocket sessions receive maintenance_started event and should disconnect gracefully. Builder can schedule maintenance windows in advance (recurring or one-time). Maintenance mode bypass: builder and admin tokens bypass maintenance check (to test during maintenance). Auto-disable maintenance_mode when ends_at passes via scheduled Worker.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV30" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV30 [HIGH] — RAG Retrieval Quality Metrics (Precision@K / Recall@K / NDCG)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No metrics exist for evaluating the quality of KB retrieval. When an agent gives a wrong answer citing a KB source, it is unknown whether the problem was retrieval (wrong chunks returned) or generation (right chunks, wrong reasoning). Without retrieval metrics, optimization is directionless.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement RetrievalEvalFramework: builder uploads golden_retrieval_dataset.jsonl (query, expected_chunk_ids). RetrievalEvalWorker runs each query through Vectorize, computes Precision@K (fraction of top-K results that are relevant), Recall@K (fraction of relevant results in top-K), and NDCG@K (normalized discounted cumulative gain accounting for ranking). Run eval on KB changes and embedding model changes. Store results in SpaceDO evals table alongside model evals (AT07). Surface retrieval quality dashboard in builder analytics showing metrics trend over time. Alert when Precision@5 drops below 0.6.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV31" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV31 [MEDIUM] — Per-Query Retrieval Relevance Scoring at Runtime</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Retrieved KB chunks are injected into context without evaluating whether they are actually relevant to the current query. Irrelevant chunks waste context tokens, degrade response quality, and increase costs. No relevance gating exists between Vectorize retrieval and context injection.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement relevance gating: after Vectorize returns top-K chunks (with similarity scores), filter out chunks with similarity &lt; relevance_threshold (default 0.65, builder-configurable). For remaining chunks, optionally run lightweight cross-encoder relevance check (fast bi-encoder model via CF AI). Only inject chunks passing both filters. If zero chunks pass: do not inject KB context; agent responds from training knowledge with "I don't have specific documentation on this." Track filter_retention_rate (% of retrieved chunks that pass filter) and no_relevant_chunks_rate per query in Analytics Engine.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV32" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV32 [HIGH] — KB Chunk Quality Assessment and Deduplication</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Knowledge base documents are chunked and indexed without quality assessment. Low-quality chunks (too short to be meaningful, near-duplicate of another chunk, garbled OCR text, off-topic content) pollute the vector index, degrading retrieval quality for all queries.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ChunkQualityFilter on KB upload: (1) Length filter: reject chunks &lt; 50 tokens or &gt; 2000 tokens; (2) Duplicate detection: compute MinHash fingerprint per chunk, reject if Jaccard similarity &gt; 0.85 vs existing chunks; (3) Language coherence: detect garbled text via perplexity score (high perplexity = low quality text); (4) Relevance-to-Space filter: compare chunk embedding to Space's topic description embedding, warn if similarity &lt; 0.5. Surface quality report to builder after upload: "8 chunks rejected (3 too short, 5 near-duplicates)." Allow builder to override rejections. Track kb_chunk_quality_score per Space in Analytics Engine.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV33" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV33 [HIGH] — Multi-Vector Hybrid Retrieval (Dense + Sparse / BM25)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Current retrieval uses only dense vector (semantic) search via Vectorize. Dense-only retrieval misses exact keyword matches that are critical for precise queries (product codes, error messages, proper nouns). Hybrid retrieval combining dense + sparse (BM25/keyword) significantly outperforms either alone.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement hybrid retrieval: (1) Dense path: Vectorize similarity search (existing), (2) Sparse path: BM25 keyword index stored in SpaceDO SQLite as inverted index (term → [chunk_id, tf_idf_score]). For each query: run both paths in parallel via Promise.all(), collect top-K from each. Merge results via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF): RRF_score = 1/(k + dense_rank) + 1/(k + sparse_rank). Return top-K merged results. Builder configures dense_weight vs sparse_weight (default: 60/40). Sparse index updated incrementally on KB changes. Track hybrid vs dense-only retrieval quality improvement in evals.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV34" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV34 [MEDIUM] — Query Expansion for Improved Semantic Search</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Short or ambiguous end-user queries (e.g., "pricing") produce poor semantic search results because the query embedding doesn't capture related concepts (cost, plan, subscription, billing). No query expansion mechanism generates alternative phrasings to improve retrieval coverage.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement QueryExpander: for queries &lt; 5 tokens, use LLM to generate 3 alternative phrasings ("how much does it cost", "what are the pricing plans", "subscription tiers"). Embed original query + alternatives. Retrieve top-K/4 results for each embedding, merge via Reciprocal Rank Fusion. Cost control: use fast model (Haiku) for expansion to minimize latency impact. Cache expansion results for identical queries (SpaceDO KV cache, 1-hour TTL). Track query_expanded events and compare retrieval quality (expansion-on vs expansion-off A/B experiment). Default: on for queries &lt; 5 tokens; builder can disable.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV35" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV35 [MEDIUM] — HyDE (Hypothetical Document Embeddings) for Retrieval</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Standard retrieval embeds the user's question and searches for similar document chunks. HyDE generates a hypothetical ideal answer to the question, embeds that hypothetical answer, and uses it for search — this typically outperforms question-based retrieval by 15-30% on BEIR benchmarks.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement HyDERetriever: for agent queries, call fast LLM (Haiku) with "Write a 3-sentence answer to: {question}" to generate a hypothetical document. Embed the hypothetical document. Use its embedding as the Vectorize query vector instead of the question embedding. Return top-K results. Blend with standard retrieval (question embedding): merge via RRF for robustness. Add 100-150ms latency; trade-off: run HyDE only for queries where standard retrieval returned 0 high-confidence results (as fallback). A/B test HyDE-on vs HyDE-off via ExperimentDO. Builder can enable in Space settings.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV36" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV36 [MEDIUM] — Contextual Chunk Compression Before LLM Injection</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Retrieved KB chunks are injected verbatim into context, including surrounding text irrelevant to the specific query. A 500-token chunk may contain only 50 tokens relevant to the question, wasting 450 tokens of context window and inflating costs.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ContextualCompressor: after retrieval, for each chunk run LLM prompt "Given this question: {query}. Extract only the sentences from the following passage that are directly relevant: {chunk_text}. If no sentences are relevant, output: IRRELEVANT." Inject compressed excerpt instead of full chunk. Skip compression if chunk &lt; 100 tokens (already short). Use cheap model (Haiku) for compression to keep cost low. Track compression_ratio (tokens_after/tokens_before) in Analytics Engine; target &gt; 40% reduction. A/B test: measure faithfulness scores (AT09) with vs without compression. Builder configures compression threshold.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV37" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV37 [HIGH] — Cross-Encoder Re-Ranking of Retrieved KB Chunks</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Vectorize bi-encoder retrieval has high recall but mediocre precision — it returns roughly relevant chunks but cannot precisely rank them by relevance to the specific query. A cross-encoder model scores query-chunk pairs jointly, significantly improving ranking quality at the cost of additional compute.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement CrossEncoderReranker: after Vectorize returns top-20 chunks, call CF AI cross-encoder model (cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2 or equivalent) with {query, chunk} pairs in parallel (max 20 calls). Cross-encoder returns relevance score 0-1 for each pair. Sort chunks by cross-encoder score; inject top-K (default K=5) into context. Cache cross-encoder results for 5 minutes (identical query+chunk pairs). Track reranker_latency_ms and precision_improvement (eval comparison) in Analytics Engine. Default enabled for RAG-primary Spaces; builder can disable for latency-sensitive use cases.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV38" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV38 [HIGH] — CF Workers CPU Time Limit Mitigation Strategy</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> CF Standard Workers have a 30ms CPU time limit. Complex operations in Space Workers (large JSON serialization, markdown parsing, context assembly for long conversations) can approach or exceed this limit, causing CPUTimeExceededError and dropped requests with no fallback.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Audit all Worker code paths for CPU-expensive operations. Mitigation strategies: (1) Move heavy computation to Unbound Workers (up to 30s CPU, higher cost): batch processing, eval runs, bulk exports. (2) Precompute expensive artifacts: serialize component configs at deploy time rather than per-request. (3) Lazy loading: use dynamic import() to defer large module initialization. (4) Stream processing: process responses in TransformStream chunks rather than buffering entire response. (5) Monitor CPU time via performance.now() instrumentation. (6) Track cpu_time_ms per operation type in Analytics Engine; alert on P99 &gt; 20ms for standard Workers.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV39" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#f8d7da;border-left:4px solid #c0392b;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV39 [CRITICAL] — CF Durable Object Class Migration Without Data Loss</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> When Naelao needs to migrate a Durable Object class to a new implementation (rename class, change serialization format, restructure SQLite schema), CF requires the new class to share the same ID namespace. No migration path exists for transforming existing DO state to the new format without data loss or service interruption.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Follow CF DO class migration protocol: (1) Create new DO class (e.g., SpaceDOv2) alongside old class in the same Worker. (2) Deploy dual-write phase: requests routed to new class; on first access, SpaceDOv2 reads from old SpaceDO via RPC, migrates data to new schema, marks migration_complete flag. (3) Monitor migration_complete coverage (all active Spaces migrated = target 99.9%). (4) After full coverage, remove old DO class. (5) Each step deployed and validated separately. Test migration in staging with production data snapshot. Never rename DO class without migration protocol — CF cannot automatically map old IDs to new class names.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV40" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV40 [HIGH] — CF Workers Subrequest Limit (1000/Request) Batching</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> CF Workers are limited to 1000 subrequests per invocation (fetch() calls, DO RPC calls, service bindings). Complex agent orchestration (tool calls to multiple external APIs + DO storage + Vectorize + Analytics Engine writes) can approach or exceed this limit in a single request, causing OperationExceededLimit errors.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Audit high-subrequest paths in Space Workers. Mitigation: (1) Batch Analytics Engine writes: accumulate multiple events and write in a single multi-row batch call. (2) Batch external tool calls: if agent invokes multiple external APIs, fan out via single fetch with parallel sub-streams rather than N sequential fetches. (3) Cache DO reads: cache SpaceDO config reads in Worker memory for request duration to avoid repeated DO RPC calls. (4) Instrument subrequest counting via proxy wrapper on fetch(). (5) Alert when any request path exceeds 500 subrequests (50% threshold). Track max_subrequests_per_request in Analytics Engine.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV41" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV41 [HIGH] — CF Workers Bundle Size and Cold Start Optimization</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Large Space Worker bundles (importing heavy libraries for markdown parsing, schema validation, crypto) increase V8 isolate cold start time from ~5ms to &gt;50ms. Cold starts impact the first request after a Worker idle period, creating latency spikes that degrade end-user experience.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement bundle size optimization strategy: (1) Tree-shake all imports — use named imports not wildcard (import {ajv} not import * as ajv). (2) Move heavy libraries (pdfjs, puppeteer) to separate Unbound Workers invoked only when needed. (3) Use dynamic import() for conditional features (only load crypto library if BYOK feature enabled for this Space). (4) Set up esbuild bundle analyzer in CI — alert if bundle grows &gt; 10% vs baseline. (5) Target bundle size &lt; 1MB compressed. Track cold_start_latency_ms in Analytics Engine with worker_was_cold flag. (6) Use CF Workers warmup strategy: scheduled Worker pings to keep isolates warm during business hours.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV42" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV42 [HIGH] — Space Uptime and Latency SLA Definition</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao does not publish formal uptime or latency SLAs to builders. Builders cannot negotiate contracts requiring uptime guarantees, and Naelao has no contractual obligation to maintain any specific reliability level. Enterprise builders cannot get SLA commitments for their end-users.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define and publish tiered SLAs: Free (best-effort, no SLA), Pro (99.5% uptime/month, P99 &lt; 2s agent response), Enterprise (99.9% uptime/month, P99 &lt; 1s agent response, dedicated support). SLA measurement: monthly uptime = (minutes - downtime_minutes) / minutes. Downtime = any period where &gt;1% of requests return 5xx for &gt;5 consecutive minutes. Track via synthetic monitoring (AU39). SLA breach: Pro receives 1-week credit; Enterprise receives 1-month credit. Publish SLA metrics at status.naelao.com (AA43). Enterprise SLA contractually binding via Naelao Master Service Agreement.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV43" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV43 [HIGH] — WebSocket Client Reconnection with Session Continuity</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> The WebSocket client SDK reconnects on disconnect but has no exponential backoff, no session continuity (messages sent during disconnect are queued but their delivery is not guaranteed to the reconnected client), and no cap on reconnection attempts.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement client-side ReconnectionManager: on WebSocket close event, attempt reconnection with exponential backoff: delays = [1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, 16s, 30s, 30s...] capped at 30s. Max attempts: 20 (10-minute window). On reconnect, send reconnect message: { type: "reconnect", session_id, last_received_message_id }. Server-side: SpaceDO holds message queue per session (last 50 messages, 5-minute TTL). On reconnect RPC: return messages after last_received_message_id. Client replays them. Show "Reconnecting..." indicator in Space UI. After 20 failed attempts: show "Connection lost — please refresh" with manual reconnect button.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV44" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV44 [HIGH] — DO Hibernation Cold Start Latency Mitigation</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Cold-starting a hibernated SpaceDO (loading SQLite from storage, re-initializing in-memory state, re-establishing connections) adds 200-400ms to the first request after a period of inactivity. For Spaces used sporadically, every user interaction may incur this penalty.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement cold start mitigation: (1) Lazy initialization: load only the minimum required state synchronously (space_id, plan_tier, config_version); defer full config deserialization to first actual operation. (2) State caching: pre-warm critical DOs via scheduled Worker pings every 60s during business hours (builder-configurable: always-warm option on paid plans). (3) Progressive hydration: return immediately with cached config from KV (5s TTL) while DO warms up in background. (4) DO locality: ensure SpaceDO is co-located with its most common callers via Smart Placement (AT14). Track cold_start_latency_ms and cold_start_rate in Analytics Engine.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV45" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV45 [MEDIUM] — Parent-Document Retrieval Pattern for KB Chunks</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> KB documents are chunked into small segments (200-500 tokens) for indexing precision, but these small chunks often lack the surrounding context needed for a coherent answer. Injecting only the matched chunk misses the broader document context that makes the chunk meaningful.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement parent-document retrieval: store two representations per KB document: (1) child chunks (200-500 tokens) in Vectorize for fine-grained retrieval, (2) parent documents (full doc or 2000-token parent sections) in SpaceDO SQLite. Each child chunk stores parent_id. Retrieval flow: Vectorize returns child chunks → look up parent_ids → fetch parent documents from SQLite → inject parent text into context (deduped if multiple children from same parent). Net effect: precise retrieval (via small chunks) with rich context (via large parents). Track context_length_with_vs_without_parent in Analytics Engine to measure token cost trade-off.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV46" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV46 [HIGH] — API Response Pagination Standards (Cursor-Based)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao list APIs (GET /api/spaces, GET /api/spaces/{id}/sessions, GET /api/spaces/{id}/kb/docs) return all results without pagination or return offset-based pagination. At scale (millions of Spaces, thousands of sessions), offset-based pagination causes full table scans and returns inconsistent results during concurrent writes.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Standardize all list APIs on cursor-based pagination: response = { data: [...], next_cursor: "opaque_base64_cursor", has_more: bool }. Cursor encodes: last_seen_id + sort_direction. Next request: GET /api/spaces?cursor={next_cursor}&amp;limit=100 (max 100). Server decodes cursor to: SELECT * FROM spaces WHERE id &gt; last_seen_id ORDER BY id ASC LIMIT 101. Return has_more: true if 101 results found (omit 101st from response). Cursor is opaque to clients. Document pagination in OpenAPI spec (AU18). SDK auto-pagination helpers auto-follow cursors. Ensure consistent ordering for stable cursors (use immutable ID, not updated_at).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV47" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV47 [MEDIUM] — Trial Extension Policy and Manual Override</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao trials expire after a fixed period with no mechanism for the sales team to manually extend a trial for a promising prospect, A/B test different trial lengths, or grant permanent free access to select accounts (investors, design partners, press).
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement TrialExtensionDO: admin API POST /admin/accounts/{id}/trial with { extend_days: 14, reason: "enterprise_prospect", authorized_by: "sales_rep_id" }. AccountDO updates trial_ends_at and logs extension in audit trail. Support special account types: INVESTOR (no expiry, free plan), DESIGN_PARTNER (Pro plan features, no charge), PRESS (Pro plan, watermark-free). Trial length A/B test: assign 30% of new signups to 21-day trial vs 14-day control; measure conversion rate delta. Admin dashboard: list of accounts with extended trials, authorization details, conversion outcome tracking.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV48" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV48 [HIGH] — Builder API Key Zero-Downtime Rotation</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builder API key rotation requires deleting the current key and creating a new one, creating a gap where neither key is valid and any automation using the old key breaks. No overlap/transition period or zero-downtime rotation flow exists.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement API key rotation: builder clicks "Rotate Key" in dashboard. System issues new_key immediately (both old_key and new_key valid simultaneously). Old key marked as rotating (state: ROTATING) in AccountDO. ROTATING keys: remain valid for 24 hours with X-Key-Expiry response header warning. After 24h: old key transitions to REVOKED state. Builder dashboard shows countdown to old key expiry. Automated rotation option: builder provides webhook; Naelao calls webhook with new key before revoking old. Track key_rotation events in immutable audit log. Alert if ROTATING key is still being used within 1 hour of expiry.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV49" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV49 [HIGH] — Negative Feedback Loop for KB Retrieval Improvement</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> End-user thumbs-down on an agent response contains valuable signal that the retrieval was poor (wrong sources) or the generation was wrong (right sources, bad answer). Neither signal is captured for KB improvement. Builder cannot identify which KB documents are causing poor responses.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement FeedbackCapture: on thumbs-down, capture: session_id, message_id, retrieved_chunk_ids, user_feedback_type (wrong_info/not_helpful/off_topic). Store in SpaceDO SQLite feedback table. Aggregate: per chunk_id, count negative_feedback_count / total_retrievals = retrieval_failure_rate. Surface to builder: KB document quality report showing "Document X has been retrieved 50 times but received thumbs-down 30 times (60% failure rate)." Suggest actions: "Consider revising or removing this document." Optional: use feedback to fine-tune embedding model (AT01) for retrieval improvement. Track feedback_to_action_rate as builder engagement metric.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AV50" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AV50 [HIGH] — Space Configuration Version History and Point-in-Time Restore</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Space configuration changes (AU41 captures who/what/when) but does not support point-in-time restore of the entire Space configuration to a past state. A builder who makes 5 bad changes cannot restore to "the working config from yesterday" — they must manually undo each change.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ConfigVersionHistory: each published Space config snapshot stored in SpaceDO SQLite config_snapshots table with snapshot_id, config_json, published_at, published_by, changelog_id. Keep last 90 snapshots. Builder dashboard: Config History tab shows timeline of snapshots with diff summary. "Restore to this version" button: sets current config to selected snapshot's config_json, creates new changelog entry (restored_from: snapshot_id). Compare any two snapshots side-by-side (unified diff view). Export any historical config snapshot as JSON. Weekly config backup to R2 as additional safety net beyond DO storage.
  </div>
</div>

<section id="appendix-aw">
<h2>Appendix AW — Compliance Certifications, Observability, Content Processing, Access Control, Privacy, Notifications, Dev Tools &amp; Scaling</h2>

<div id="G-AW01" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#f8d7da;border-left:4px solid #c0392b;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW01 [CRITICAL] — SOC 2 Type II Certification Readiness</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao has no documented SOC 2 Type II audit readiness program. Enterprise customers require SOC 2 Type II reports as a procurement prerequisite. Without it, Naelao cannot enter procurement processes at Fortune 500 companies regardless of technical capability.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Initiate SOC 2 Type II readiness program: (1) Define scope: Trust Service Criteria — Security (CC), Availability (A), Confidentiality (C), Privacy (P). (2) Implement evidence collection automation: connect CF Logpush, GitHub audit log, and Stripe events to Drata or Vanta compliance automation platform. (3) Close control gaps: access reviews (AT36), vendor risk assessments, change management (AV50), incident response procedures (AU37). (4) Engage qualified auditor for 12-month observation window. (5) Publish SOC 2 report to enterprise customers under NDA via trust center. Annual renewal required.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW02" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW02 [HIGH] — ISO 27001 Information Security Management System</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No ISO 27001 ISMS exists. European enterprise customers (especially in financial services and healthcare) require ISO 27001 certification as a mandatory procurement criterion. CF infrastructure alone does not provide certification — the organizational ISMS must be certified.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ISO 27001 ISMS: (1) Define scope statement and information security policy. (2) Complete Statement of Applicability (SoA) mapping 114 Annex A controls to Naelao processes. (3) Conduct risk assessment (asset register, threat modeling, risk treatment plan). (4) Implement controls: access management (AU14-16), cryptographic key management (AP19), supplier relationships (AT27 connectors), business continuity (AP48). (5) Internal audit and management review cycle. (6) Certification audit via accredited body (BSI, Bureau Veritas). Maintain via annual surveillance audits.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW03" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW03 [HIGH] — NIST Cybersecurity Framework Alignment</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No NIST CSF alignment documentation exists. US Federal, defense, and critical infrastructure customers require NIST CSF adherence. Without a documented NIST CSF implementation, these customer segments are inaccessible.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Map Naelao security controls to NIST CSF v2.0 five functions: Identify (asset inventory, risk assessment, supply chain risk via AT27), Protect (access control via AU14-16, data security via AP19, awareness training), Detect (continuous monitoring via AJ series, intrusion detection), Respond (incident response plan, communications via AA43), Recover (recovery planning via AP48, improvements tracking). Publish NIST CSF implementation tier rating. Create customer-facing NIST CSF summary document for security questionnaires. Review quarterly against framework updates.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW04" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW04 [HIGH] — PCI DSS Level 1 Compliance for Payments</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao handles cardholder data on behalf of builders who charge end-users. While Stripe Elements reduces PCI scope, Naelao's role as a platform that orchestrates payments for millions of transactions requires formal PCI DSS compliance documentation and potentially a Level 1 merchant/service provider assessment.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Conduct PCI DSS scoping exercise: determine if Naelao is a Level 1 service provider (&gt;300K transactions/year) or merchant. Use Stripe as PCI-compliant processor (Stripe handles cardholder data; Naelao never stores card numbers). Complete SAQ-A (simplest) or SAQ-D as applicable. Network segmentation: ensure payment-processing Workers are isolated from non-payment services. Penetration testing of payment flows (quarterly). Maintain payment security awareness training for engineering. Publish PCI compliance status on trust center. File annual ROC (Report on Compliance) with card brands if Level 1.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW05" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW05 [MEDIUM] — Cloud Security Alliance (CSA) STAR Certification</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Enterprise cloud security teams use the CSA STAR registry (Cloud Controls Matrix) to assess cloud providers. Without a CSA STAR entry, security teams must perform bespoke assessments instead of relying on standardized documentation, slowing enterprise procurement.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Complete CSA Consensus Assessment Initiative Questionnaire (CAIQ-Lite for Level 1, full CAIQ for Level 2). Map Naelao controls to CCM v4.0 domains: Application Security, Audit Assurance, Business Continuity, Data Security, Encryption, Identity &amp; Access Management, Infrastructure Security, Privacy. Publish CAIQ response on CSA STAR registry (free for Level 1 self-assessment). Pursue STAR Certification (Level 2) via ISO 27001 audit (AW02) plus STAR Certification assessment. Update annually. Reference STAR registry in enterprise trust center.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW06" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW06 [HIGH] — Ethical AI Guidelines and AI Policy Review Board</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao has no published ethical AI guidelines or internal AI policy review board. As builders deploy AI products to millions of end-users, absence of ethical guardrails exposes Naelao to reputational and regulatory risk from AI harms (bias, discrimination, manipulation, autonomous harm).
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Establish AI Ethics program: (1) Publish Naelao AI Principles (transparency, fairness, safety, privacy, accountability). (2) Create AI Review Board: engineering lead, legal, product, external AI ethicist. Board reviews new AI capabilities before launch. (3) Implement bias testing for AI responses across demographic groups. (4) Require builders to declare AI use in Space description ("This Space uses AI"). (5) Publish model cards for AI models used in platform. (6) Incident process for AI harm reports. (7) EU AI Act compliance (high-risk AI system classification, transparency obligations). Annual ethics audit.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW07" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW07 [HIGH] — EU AI Act Compliance Framework</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> The EU AI Act (effective August 2026) imposes obligations on AI systems deployed to EU users. Naelao and its builders may be deploying "high-risk" or "general purpose AI" systems subject to transparency, logging, human oversight, and registration requirements. No EU AI Act compliance framework exists.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Complete EU AI Act mapping: classify each Naelao AI feature by risk tier (minimal, limited, high, unacceptable). Most Spaces = "limited risk" (transparency obligation: disclose AI). High-risk criteria check: does any Space system make automated decisions affecting EU individuals' rights (credit, employment, healthcare)? If yes: conformity assessment, technical documentation, human oversight mechanism, bias testing, registration in EU database. GPAI provisions: Naelao (as model provider integrator) must maintain technical documentation and EU-standard model policies. Publish EU AI Act conformity documentation by effective date.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW08" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW08 [HIGH] — OpenTelemetry Distributed Tracing Integration</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao has no distributed tracing system. When a builder reports "agent responses are slow," engineers cannot trace the latency through: entry Worker → SpaceDO → VectorizeDO → LLM API → response. Each hop is a black box with no timing visibility.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement OpenTelemetry in all Workers: use @opentelemetry/sdk-node with OTLP exporter sending traces to CF Workers Trace API (or Honeycomb/Jaeger endpoint). Each Worker creates trace spans: space-worker.receive_request, space-do.fetch_config, vectorize.query, llm.inference, space-do.write_message. Propagate W3C Trace Context header (traceparent) across all fetch() and DO RPC calls. Sampling: 100% for errors, 1% for successful requests (adjustable). Surface builder-visible latency breakdown in builder observability dashboard: "Time to first token: 340ms (vectorize: 50ms, llm_queue: 10ms, llm_inference: 280ms)."
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW09" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW09 [HIGH] — Performance Regression Detection in CI</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No automated performance regression detection exists in Naelao's CI pipeline. A code change that increases p99 latency by 50% ships to production undetected. Only after builders report slowness does an investigation begin.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Integrate performance benchmarking into CI: on each PR, run k6 load test (AJ38) against staging environment. Record p50/p95/p99 latency per key operation (session_init, agent_run, kb_query). Compare against baseline (last 7 days' average from Analytics Engine). If p99 regresses &gt; 20% vs baseline: mark CI check as failed, block merge, post regression report as PR comment. Store benchmark history in D1. Alert on-call when production p99 exceeds SLA thresholds (AV42). Track benchmark_regression_detected, benchmark_regression_blocked_merge counts monthly.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW10" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW10 [HIGH] — Real-Time Error Rate Dashboard for Builder Spaces</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders have no real-time view of their Space's error rate. When a deployment causes a sudden increase in agent failures, builders discover it via end-user complaints rather than proactive platform alerting. The gap between incident start and builder awareness is measured in hours.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build real-time ErrorRateDashboard: in builder dashboard home, show live error rate sparkline (last 30 minutes, 1-minute buckets) queried from Analytics Engine. Error types tracked: agent_run_failed, tool_call_failed, kb_query_timeout, session_auth_failed. Alert thresholds: warn at &gt;1% error rate, critical at &gt;5%. Browser push notification (AU08) when error rate exceeds critical threshold. Error breakdown by type so builder can identify root cause (e.g., 90% are tool_call_failed → connector is down). Link each error type to relevant docs and debug checklist. Auto-create support ticket (AU17) when error rate exceeds 10%.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW11" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW11 [HIGH] — Structured Log Aggregation with Log Levels</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao Workers emit unstructured console.log() output mixed with console.error() to CF Worker logs. There is no structured log schema, no log levels (DEBUG/INFO/WARN/ERROR), no log correlation (request_id), and no log aggregation pipeline delivering logs to builders.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement structured logging library for all Workers: logger.info/warn/error({ requestId, spaceId, event, ...fields }). Log schema: { timestamp, level, event, request_id, space_id, duration_ms, ...event_fields }. Output as JSON to CF Workers logs. CF Logpush ships JSON logs to Naelao SIEM (Splunk/Datadog). Builder-facing logs: filtered by space_id, exposed via builder log viewer (last 1000 entries, 24h retention, searchable). Log levels: DEBUG (disabled in production by default), INFO (normal operations), WARN (degraded but functional), ERROR (request failed). Error logs trigger PagerDuty on frequency threshold.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW12" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW12 [MEDIUM] — Alerting Runbook Library for On-Call Engineers</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> When PagerDuty fires an alert for a Naelao platform issue, on-call engineers have no runbooks describing the expected investigation procedure, known causes, and remediation steps for each alert type. Incident response time is longer than necessary due to knowledge loss.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Create runbook library at runbooks.naelao.internal: one runbook per alert type (DO storage near 10GB, KV throughput exceeded, Vectorize latency spike, LLM provider failure, payment webhook failure, etc). Each runbook: alert description, severity, expected symptoms, step-by-step investigation checklist, common causes and fixes, rollback procedure, escalation path. Runbooks stored in git (version-controlled, peer-reviewed). Link each PagerDuty alert to its runbook URL. Track runbook_used and resolution_time per incident. Update runbooks after every post-incident review. On-call rotation includes runbook review as onboarding step.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW13" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW13 [HIGH] — PDF Document Processing Pipeline with Layout Awareness</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> PDF KB documents are processed as plain text extraction, losing table structure, column layouts, headers, and footnotes. Complex PDFs (financial reports, technical specifications with tables) produce garbled text chunks that mislead the agent with out-of-order content.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement PDFLayoutExtractor using pdfjs-dist in an Unbound Worker: extract text with position metadata (bounding boxes). Reconstruct reading order: sort text blocks by page → column → vertical position. Table detection: identify grid patterns in bounding boxes, serialize tables as markdown (| header | header |\n|---|---|\n| cell | cell |). Header/footer detection: filter repeating elements. Process PDFs asynchronously via CF Workflow; store extracted structured text in R2; update SpaceDO SQLite with processing status. Alert builder if PDF extraction confidence &lt; 70% (complex scanned PDFs may need OCR — AS14).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW14" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW14 [HIGH] — Spreadsheet and CSV Knowledge Base Ingestion</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders frequently have knowledge in Excel/CSV format (product catalogs, pricing tables, FAQ spreadsheets). Uploading these as KB documents produces raw CSV text that the agent struggles to reason over. No table-aware chunking or structured query support exists for tabular data.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement SpreadsheetIngester: on .xlsx/.csv upload, use SheetJS to parse. For each sheet: (1) identify header row, (2) serialize each row as a natural-language description "Product: iPhone 15, Price: $999, Category: Phone, Stock: Available" to create semantically rich text for embedding. (3) For large sheets (&gt;1000 rows), chunk into 50-row segments with header repeated in each chunk. (4) Store raw table in SpaceDO SQLite as structured_tables(sheet_name, data_json) for SQL-like tool queries. Expose sql_query tool that executes SELECT statements against structured_tables. Handle merged cells and multi-level headers.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW15" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW15 [HIGH] — Web Page Crawling for Live KB Updates</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders who want their Space to have up-to-date knowledge from a website (product docs, help center, blog) must manually upload documents. No web crawling capability exists to automatically keep the KB synchronized with a website, causing stale knowledge.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement WebCrawler: builder configures seed URLs and crawl depth (max 3). CrawlWorkflow: fetch URL via CF fetch() → parse HTML with cheerio → extract main content (remove nav/footer/ads via content heuristics) → extract links → queue new URLs within same domain. Rate limit: 1 req/sec per domain. Store fetched pages in R2. Differential update: on re-crawl, compare content hash vs stored; only re-embed changed pages. Schedule: builder configures crawl frequency (daily/weekly/monthly). Robots.txt compliance: respect Disallow directives. Track crawl_pages_fetched, crawl_errors, kb_docs_updated per crawl run.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW16" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW16 [HIGH] — Knowledge Base Automatic Expiry and Freshness Tracking</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> KB documents have no expiry mechanism. A document uploaded 2 years ago with outdated pricing information continues to be retrieved and used by the agent as authoritative. Builders have no visibility into document age or staleness.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Add freshness metadata to KB docs: uploaded_at, last_updated_at, expires_at (optional), source_url (for crawled docs). On retrieval, include doc_age in retrieved chunk metadata. Agent system prompt injection: "Note: the following sources are older than 180 days and may be outdated: [doc names]." Builder dashboard: KB freshness report — list of docs not updated in &gt;90/180/365 days, sorted by retrieval frequency (stale but heavily used = highest risk). Builder configures auto-archive: docs not updated in X days automatically marked as archived (still queryable but flagged). Auto-expire: docs past expires_at automatically deleted.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW17" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW17 [MEDIUM] — Knowledge Graph Construction from KB Documents</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> The KB is a flat collection of document chunks indexed by semantic similarity. Complex reasoning questions that require traversing relationships (e.g., "What products are compatible with Product X and available in the EU?") cannot be answered by pure vector retrieval — they need a structured knowledge graph.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement KnowledgeGraphExtractor: on KB upload, use LLM to extract entity-relationship triples (subject, predicate, object) from document: ("iPhone 15", "compatible_with", "Lightning Cable"), ("Lightning Cable", "available_in", "EU"). Store triples in SpaceDO SQLite knowledge_graph(subject, predicate, object, confidence, source_doc_id). Expose graph_query tool: given question, LLM formulates SPARQL-like query, system executes against SQLite using graph traversal. For complex multi-hop queries: combine graph traversal with vector retrieval (hybrid RAG). Surface knowledge graph visualization in builder dashboard.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW18" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW18 [MEDIUM] — Automatic KB Document Summarization for Large Documents</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Large documents (50+ pages) are chunked but have no summary representation. When an end-user asks a general question about a large document ("What is this document about?"), the agent must retrieve specific chunks that may not capture the document's overall purpose and structure.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement DocSummaryGenerator: on KB doc upload (after chunking), generate multi-level summaries via LLM: (1) executive summary (150 words, top-level overview), (2) section summaries (one per major header), (3) entity extraction (key terms, people, products, dates). Store summaries in SpaceDO SQLite doc_summaries table. Retrieval strategy: on query, first check if query matches summary tier (general questions match executive summary, specific questions match section summaries, very specific match chunk-level). Inject appropriate tier into context. Cache summaries; regenerate when source doc is updated.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW19" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW19 [HIGH] — Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC) for Spaces</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Current access control is role-based (RBAC): a user is an admin, member, or viewer. Enterprise use cases require attribute-based policies: "Only users in the Sales department can access financial KB documents" or "Only users in EU can see pricing for EU products." ABAC cannot be expressed in RBAC.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ABAC policy engine in SpaceDO: builder defines policies as JSON rules: { resource: "kb", action: "read", condition: { user.department: "sales", user.region: "EU" } }. Policies stored in SpaceDO SQLite abac_policies table. On each resource access, PolicyEngine.evaluate(user_attributes, resource_type, action) resolves matching policies. User attributes sourced from session JWT custom claims (builder-defined at identity provider). Return PERMIT/DENY. Provide policy testing sandbox in builder dashboard: test a hypothetical user against all policies to verify access decisions.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW20" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW20 [HIGH] — Resource-Level Permission Grants for KB Documents</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> KB permissions are all-or-nothing per Space: either a user can access all KB documents or none. Builders cannot restrict specific sensitive KB documents (e.g., internal pricing, executive strategy docs) to specific user roles while making other KB docs publicly accessible.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement KB document-level access control: each KB doc has access_policy: "public" | "authenticated" | { roles: ["admin", "premium"] } | ABAC expression. On KB retrieval, filter retrieved chunks: only return chunks from docs where current user satisfies doc's access_policy. Session JWT includes user roles for policy evaluation. Builder configures per-document access in KB management UI (select doc → set access policy). Audit all access denials in SpaceDO SQLite. For GDPR: document access policies included in data processing records (AS16 GDPR Art.30).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW21" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW21 [MEDIUM] — Team Member Permission Inheritance and Override</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builder team members inherit permissions from their assigned role with no mechanism for per-person overrides (e.g., elevate one team member to admin for a specific Space, restrict another from seeing billing data while keeping other admin rights). Role permissions are all-or-nothing.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement permission override system: AccountDO stores permission_overrides(account_id, user_id, permission_key, granted: bool). On permission check: evaluate base_role permissions → apply overrides (grants add permissions, denials remove). Builder admin can add/remove specific permissions for specific team members without changing their role. Permissions are atomic: can_view_billing, can_edit_config, can_deploy, can_view_analytics, can_manage_kb, can_manage_members. Override changes logged in immutable audit trail. Enterprise: manager approval workflow for sensitive permission overrides.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW22" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW22 [HIGH] — IP Allowlist / Denylist for Builder Dashboard Access</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Enterprise builder accounts with strict security policies need to restrict dashboard access to corporate network IPs only. No IP allowlisting exists for the builder dashboard. An attacker with stolen credentials from outside the corporate network can access the dashboard without restriction.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement IP allowlist for builder accounts: builder admin configures ip_allowlist: ["203.0.113.0/24", "198.51.100.50"] in security settings stored in AccountDO. Entry Worker for dashboard routes checks CF-Connecting-IP against account's allowlist on every authenticated request. Allowlist enforcement: if enabled and IP not in list, return 403 with "Access restricted to approved networks" — even with valid session token. Bypass via emergency access code (time-limited TOTP code for off-network emergencies). IP change audit: log each new IP that accesses dashboard. CF Access also provides this as a native capability (AW01 scope).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW23" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW23 [HIGH] — Cross-Space Data Access Audit Trail</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Cross-Space data sharing grants (AT29) allow Space A to read data from Space B. When Space A reads Space B's user data, the access event is not audited. Space B's owner cannot see which data was accessed, when, or by whom, violating the transparency requirements of both GDPR and enterprise data governance.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Extend DataSharingGrant (AT29) with mandatory audit logging: every cross-Space data access writes audit_cross_space_access(granter_space_id, grantee_space_id, data_type, records_accessed_count, accessed_at, session_id, purpose) to GrantorDO immutable audit log. Grantor Space owner can query audit log via builder dashboard: "Space X accessed 15 user profile records from your Space on 2026-06-22 at 14:32." Include cross-Space access events in GDPR Art.30 records (AS16). Rate limit cross-Space accesses: max 1000 records/hour per grant to prevent data exfiltration. Alert grantor on unusual access patterns (3× daily average).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW24" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW24 [HIGH] — End-User Consent Management for Data Collection</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Spaces collecting end-user data (profile, messages, behavior) require explicit consent under GDPR, CCPA, and other privacy laws. No consent management system exists within Spaces. Builders cannot collect, store, or prove consent from their end-users for data processing.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build ConsentManager component for Space UI library: on first session, show configurable consent modal with: purpose description, data types collected, retention period, sharing with third parties. End-user can accept all, customize (select purposes), or decline. Consent decisions stored in SpaceDO SQLite end_user_consents(user_id, purpose, granted, timestamp, consent_version). Agent and KB access gated by consent: KB retrieval requires consent_granted for "AI personalization." Builder configures consent modal text and required/optional purposes in dashboard. Provide consent history export for GDPR. Re-consent trigger when purposes change.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW25" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW25 [HIGH] — PII Detection and Redaction in Agent Conversations</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> End-users routinely share PII in conversations (credit card numbers, SSNs, phone numbers, email addresses). Agents echo this PII in responses and it is stored verbatim in conversation history. No PII detection or redaction pipeline exists, creating GDPR, CCPA, and PCI liability.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement PIIDetector middleware: before storing any message (user or agent), scan for PII patterns: credit card numbers (Luhn-validated regex), SSNs (XXX-XX-XXXX), phone numbers (E.164), email addresses. For each PII type: (1) MASK in stored conversation: "My card is [CREDIT_CARD_REDACTED]", (2) ALERT builder if PII volume exceeds threshold. For agent responses: detect before sending to end-user; if agent inadvertently repeats PII, redact from response. Builder configures PII policy: strict (block storage), mask (store redacted), or warn (log and store unredacted for compliance use). Powered by CF AI regex + LLM classification.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW26" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW26 [HIGH] — Conversation History Encryption at Rest</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Conversation history stored in SpaceDO SQLite is encrypted by CF infrastructure at the storage level, but application-level encryption is absent. If CF infrastructure encryption is insufficient for a compliance requirement (e.g., healthcare, finance), there is no field-level or row-level encryption option.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement application-level encryption for message_history table: encrypt message_content field using AES-256-GCM with per-Space encryption key derived from builder's BYOK key (AP19) or Naelao-managed key. Key derivation: HKDF(master_key, space_id + "messages") → 256-bit symmetric key. Encrypt on write, decrypt on read in SpaceDO. Key rotation: re-encrypt messages when BYOK is rotated (AT01 pattern). For Spaces with HIPAA mode (AP39): mandatory application-level encryption. Surface encryption status in Space settings: "Messages encrypted with AES-256-GCM using your key."
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW27" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW27 [HIGH] — Data Anonymization Pipeline for Analytics</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Analytics data in CF Analytics Engine contains raw end-user identifiers (user_id, IP hashes, session IDs). For privacy compliance (GDPR minimization principle) and CCPA, analytics data should be anonymized or pseudonymized to the minimum necessary precision.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement AnalyticsAnonymizer: before writing to Analytics Engine, apply: (1) user_id → HMAC(user_id + date_bucket + salt) — k-anonymity via daily key rotation, (2) IP address → never stored (only CF-IPCountry used), (3) session_id → truncated to first 16 chars (reduces uniqueness), (4) message content → never stored in analytics (only length/type metadata). Store anonymization salt rotation schedule in KV (rotated daily for behavioral analytics, monthly for cohort analytics). Provide differential privacy noise for low-count events (add Laplace noise when event_count &lt; 10). Document anonymization guarantees in privacy policy.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW28" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW28 [HIGH] — Naelao Red Team and Penetration Testing Program</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No formal red team or penetration testing program exists for Naelao. Security vulnerabilities in the platform are discovered reactively via bug bounty (AS01) rather than proactively through systematic adversarial testing of critical paths (auth, billing, agent injection).
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Establish annual penetration testing program: (1) Quarterly automated DAST scans using OWASP ZAP or Burp Suite against staging environment. (2) Annual external penetration test by qualified security firm covering: API auth bypass, privilege escalation, prompt injection (agent), payment tampering, data exfiltration. (3) Internal red team exercises: engineer rotations testing production security controls. (4) Purple team exercises: red team (attack) + blue team (detection) working together to improve detection coverage. Remediate all Critical findings within 48h, High within 7 days. Publish penetration test scope (not results) in trust center. Track mean-time-to-remediate (MTTR) by severity.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW29" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW29 [HIGH] — Supply Chain Security (SLSA / Sigstore)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao's build and deployment pipeline has no software supply chain attestation. Compromised build tooling (malicious npm package, hijacked CI pipeline) could inject malicious code into production Workers without detection. SLSA (Supply-chain Levels for Software Artifacts) attestation is absent.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Achieve SLSA Level 2: (1) Version all build inputs (npm lockfile, Dockerfile FROM pinned to digest). (2) Generate SLSA provenance attestation in CI: GitHub Actions generates signed provenance (workflow_id, commit_sha, build_timestamp). (3) Sign Worker bundles with Sigstore cosign before deployment. (4) Verify signatures in deployment gate: deployment blocked if signature is absent or verification fails. (5) Maintain Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) per deployment (AS04 existing gap extends to include provenance). (6) Monitor Sigstore TL transparency log. Advance to SLSA Level 3 (hermetic builds) annually.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW30" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW30 [HIGH] — Transactional Email Templates with Builder Branding</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao sends transactional emails to end-users on behalf of Spaces (session summaries, notification digests, export ready) using generic Naelao branding. Builders who want white-labeled transactional emails sent to their end-users from their own domain cannot configure custom email templates.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build EmailTemplateEngine: builder creates transactional email templates in dashboard with handlebars-style merge tags ({{user_name}}, {{space_name}}, {{action_url}}). Templates stored in SpaceDO SQLite. Builder configures custom From domain (DNS verification via DKIM/SPF records shown in dashboard). Emails sent via Resend or Sendgrid with builder's verified From domain. Naelao manages sending infrastructure; builder provides template + domain. Template editor with live preview, SPAM score check, mobile preview. Built-in templates for common triggers: welcome_email, session_summary, magic_link. Rate limit: max 1000 emails/Space/hour.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW31" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW31 [MEDIUM] — SMS Notification Channel for Agent-Triggered Messages</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Agent-triggered notifications can only reach end-users via email, browser push, and in-app notification. Many end-users have higher engagement with SMS, and time-sensitive notifications (appointment reminders, OTP codes, alerts) are more effective via SMS.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Integrate Twilio (or Vonage) as SMS provider: builder configures Twilio Account SID + Auth Token (encrypted in SpaceDO) and sender phone number. Notification system (AP22) gains SMS channel. Agent can trigger SMS via send_sms tool: { phone: "+14155551234", message: "Your report is ready: {url}" }. Rate limits: max 100 SMS/Space/day on Pro, 1000 on Enterprise. End-user opts in to SMS via explicit consent form in Space (AW24). Opt-out: reply STOP to any message automatically unsubscribes via Twilio webhook. Track sms_sent, sms_delivered, sms_opted_out per Space in Analytics Engine.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW32" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW32 [HIGH] — Agent-Triggered Email Sending from Space</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Agents in Spaces cannot send emails to end-users or third parties as part of their workflow. Common use cases (send a summary after a support call, email a generated report, notify a team member of an action item) require builder-developed workarounds.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Add send_email to agent standard tool library: { to: "user@example.com", subject: "Your report", body_template_id: "report_ready", merge_vars: { report_url: "..." } }. Email sending routed through EmailTemplateEngine (AW30) using builder's configured email domain. Outbound email rate limit: 100/Space/hour. Require end-user email to be in Space's known user emails (prevent spam). Builder can configure allowed recipient patterns (e.g., only @company.com domains). Track emails sent with per-recipient deduplication (max 1 email per user per 5 minutes for same template). Log all sent emails in SpaceDO with message_id for bounce tracking.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW33" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW33 [HIGH] — Slack and Teams Integration for Agent Notifications</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders using Slack or Microsoft Teams for internal communication cannot receive Naelao Space alerts or agent outputs in their preferred channel. All platform notifications are email-only with no native Slack/Teams integration for real-time operational awareness.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build Slack integration: builder installs Naelao Slack App via OAuth; app stores Slack team_id + bot_token encrypted in AccountDO. NotificationDO routes builder alerts to Slack channel based on notification preferences (AU50). Slack message format: structured blocks with alert type, Space affected, severity, action buttons (View Space, Acknowledge). Microsoft Teams: build Teams bot using Bot Framework; identical notification routing. Agent-triggered Slack messages: agent can use send_slack_message tool to post to builder-configured channels (sales updates, support escalations). Interactive buttons in Slack/Teams messages trigger Space API callbacks.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW34" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW34 [MEDIUM] — Voice Call Notification for Critical Alerts</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Critical platform alerts (Space down, billing payment failed, security breach detected) currently reach builders only via email and in-app banner. For builders running business-critical Spaces, a phone call for critical alerts provides significantly faster response time (especially outside business hours).
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Integrate Twilio Voice (or PagerDuty phone call) for CRITICAL-severity alerts: builder configures on-call phone number in notification preferences. On CRITICAL alert trigger: PagerDutyDO initiates Twilio voice call, reads alert summary via TTS ("Critical: Space customer-support is down. Error rate: 48%. Please acknowledge."). Call recipient presses 1 to acknowledge (silences further calls), 2 to escalate (calls backup number), 3 for runbook. Track voice_call_sent, voice_call_acknowledged, mean_time_to_acknowledge in on-call metrics. Builder can configure business hours → email only, after-hours → voice call. Enterprise: integrate with existing PagerDuty/OpsGenie instead of Twilio.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW35" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW35 [MEDIUM] — VS Code Extension for Space Development</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders who prefer code-based Space configuration have no VS Code extension for schema validation, IntelliSense, and local development. They edit Space config JSON blindly without autocomplete, schema validation, or test preview in their IDE.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build naelao VS Code extension: (1) JSON schema validation for Space config files (SpaceType config schema pulled from registry, validates on save). (2) IntelliSense for merge tags in prompt templates ({{ hints for available variables). (3) Local preview: run naelao dev command to start local Space server (mocked CF Worker) for testing. (4) Deploy command: naelao deploy pushes config to staging/production. (5) KB upload command: naelao kb push ./docs/ for bulk KB management. (6) Log tail: naelao logs --space=xyz streams live logs in VS Code terminal. Publish to VS Code Marketplace. JetBrains plugin as follow-on (AU10 parallel).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW36" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW36 [HIGH] — GitHub Action for Space CI/CD Integration</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders who want to version-control their Space configuration in git and deploy via CI/CD have no official GitHub Action. They must use the API manually with custom scripts, creating fragile bespoke pipelines with inconsistent behavior.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Publish naelao/deploy-space GitHub Action: inputs: space_id, config_path, api_key (from GitHub Secrets). Steps: (1) Validate config against SpaceType schema, (2) Run E2E tests via naelao-test SDK (AU39), (3) Deploy to staging Space and run smoke tests, (4) Deploy to production Space if tests pass, (5) Post deployment summary to PR as comment. Support matrix deployments (multiple Spaces in one workflow). Supports canary strategy: deploy to 10% traffic, wait 10 minutes, promote to 100%. Available in GitHub Marketplace. Example workflows in documentation (AU23). Companion GitLab CI template for non-GitHub users.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW37" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW37 [HIGH] — Local Development Environment for Spaces (naelao dev)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders cannot develop and test Space configurations locally before deploying. The only development path is deploying to a staging Space and testing against it — which requires API keys, network access, and cloud costs even for simple config changes.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build naelao CLI command naelao dev: starts local Space development server mimicking CF Worker environment. Uses Wrangler (CF's local dev tool) under the hood with Naelao-specific fixtures. Mocks: VectorizeDO (in-memory vector store), SpaceDO (local SQLite), LLM provider (configurable: real API or mock responses from ./fixtures/). Hot reload: config file changes trigger instant Space restart. Local HTTPS tunnel via cloudflared for webhook testing. naelao dev runs the UI Builder preview panel locally against local Space. Output: same SSE streaming format as production. Documented at docs.naelao.com/cli/dev.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW38" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW38 [MEDIUM] — Space Template Repository (GitHub-Based)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No official repository of Space configuration templates exists for common use cases (customer support bot, FAQ bot, coding assistant, document Q&amp;A, sales bot). Builders must build from scratch, reinventing common patterns.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Create github.com/naelao/space-templates repository: curated Space configuration templates for 20+ use cases. Each template: complete config.json (SpaceType config, system prompt, tool definitions), sample_kb/ folder with example documents, tests/ folder with E2E test cases (AU39), README with deployment instructions. Templates deployable in one command: naelao deploy --template customer-support. Templates versioned with SpaceType compatibility matrix. Community contributions accepted via PR. Featured on builder dashboard onboarding wizard (AV17). Template download count tracked for popularity signals.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW39" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW39 [MEDIUM] — Postman / Bruno Collection for Naelao API</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders wanting to explore the Naelao API with tools like Postman or Bruno must manually construct requests. No official Postman collection or Bruno collection exists. Without it, API exploration is slow and error-prone.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Generate Postman collection from OpenAPI spec (AT18): use openapi-to-postman converter in CI pipeline. Collection includes: all endpoints, pre-request scripts setting auth headers, example request bodies, tests validating response schema. Export as Postman Collection v2.1 JSON and Bruno collection format. Publish at docs.naelao.com/api/collections. Auto-update on each API spec change. Include environment files for staging and production with variable placeholders. Also generate Insomnia export. Link "Run in Postman" button on each API reference page. Track collection download counts to measure developer engagement.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW40" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#f8d7da;border-left:4px solid #c0392b;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW40 [CRITICAL] — CF Account Limits and Platform Quota Planning</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> CF accounts have hard limits: 100 Durable Object namespaces, 100 Workers scripts (unless Workers for Platforms), 100 Vectorize indexes, 500 KV namespaces. Naelao's growth plan may hit these account-level limits, blocking new Space creation with no mitigation path.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Conduct CF account quota audit: document current usage vs limits for all quota dimensions. At 70% utilization: (1) Request quota increase from CF Enterprise sales, (2) Implement sharding strategy across multiple CF accounts for Vectorize (one account per 80 indexes = ~800K Spaces per account). Multi-account orchestration: AccountRouterDO maps space_id → cf_account_id for resource routing. Vectorize sharding (AT44 partial — extend for multi-account). Workers for Platforms eliminates Workers script limit. Monitor quota utilization daily via CF API polling. Alert ops team at 80% of any quota dimension.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW41" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW41 [HIGH] — Database Connection Pool Exhaustion Under Load</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> SpaceDO SQLite connections are serialized (one request at a time), but high-concurrency Spaces with many simultaneous requests queue behind each other. Queue depth has no cap — a thundering herd (all users hitting Space simultaneously) can cause unbounded queue depth and timeout cascades.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement admission control in SpaceDO: maintain active_request_count counter. If active_request_count &gt; max_concurrent (configurable per plan: Free=5, Pro=20, Enterprise=100), return 429 with Retry-After: 1 immediately rather than queuing. This converts unbounded queue into shed load — client retries after 1s vs indefinite wait. Track admission_rejected_count in Analytics Engine. For high-traffic Spaces: scale out via horizontal Space partition (each partition handles subset of users). SpaceDO input queue (CF DO built-in) handles sequential processing; admission control ensures queue remains bounded.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW42" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW42 [HIGH] — Global Traffic Routing and Latency Optimization</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> End-users in Asia-Pacific connecting to a SpaceDO pinned in US-East experience 200-300ms round-trip latency on every DO RPC call. For real-time chat applications, this adds perceptible lag to every message. Global DO distribution strategy is missing.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement global routing strategy: (1) Smart Placement (AT14) auto-locates DO near end-users for existing traffic patterns. (2) For new Spaces: create DO in region closest to builder (detected from builder's IP country at Space creation). (3) Read replicas: for read-heavy operations (config fetch, KB queries), maintain regional read replicas in KV (5-minute TTL) to serve from nearest CF PoP without transatlantic DO RPC. (4) CDN for static assets: Space shells, component libraries served from CF edge cache (200+ PoPs). Target: &lt;100ms P99 for config reads globally. Track p99_latency_by_region in Analytics Engine.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW43" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW43 [HIGH] — Horizontal Space Partition for High-Traffic Spaces</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> A single SpaceDO handles all requests for a Space. High-traffic Spaces (millions of end-user sessions/month) bottleneck on DO throughput. DO concurrency is bounded by the request queue; no horizontal scaling path exists within a single Space.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ShardedSpaceDO: for Spaces exceeding traffic threshold, partition sessions across N SpaceDO shards by session_id % shard_count. SpaceRouterDO maintains shard_count per space_id; routes session requests to correct shard. Shard config stored in KV (cache layer). Config/KB reads: broadcast to all shards (or serve from single config-DO, separate from session-DOs). Write coordination: global operations (credit deduction, Space config update) go to primary shard which then broadcasts. Auto-shard promotion: when a Space exceeds 100 concurrent sessions, SpaceRouterDO adds a shard. Transparent to end-users.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW44" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW44 [HIGH] — Platform Quota Self-Service Increase Requests</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> When builders need quota increases (more token budget, larger Vectorize index, more concurrent sessions), there is no self-service path. They must contact support and wait for manual increases. This creates friction for fast-growing builders and unnecessary support load.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build QuotaRequestDO: builder submits self-service quota increase request via dashboard. Request includes: resource_type, current_limit, requested_limit, justification (dropdown: rapid_growth, enterprise_requirement, event_spike). Auto-approve if: requested increase &lt;= 2× current limit AND builder in good standing (no payment failures, no abuse flags). Manual review if: requested increase &gt; 2× OR resource has waiting period (Vectorize index takes 24h to provision). Track request_auto_approved, request_manually_approved, request_denied. Email builder confirmation with new limits and effective date. Log all quota changes in immutable audit trail.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW45" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#f8d7da;border-left:4px solid #c0392b;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW45 [CRITICAL] — Disaster Recovery Runbook and RTO/RPO Targets</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao has no documented disaster recovery plan with defined RTO (Recovery Time Objective) and RPO (Recovery Point Objective) targets. In a CF region outage or catastrophic data loss event, the recovery procedure is undefined, extending outage duration and potentially losing customer data.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define and document DR plan: RTO target: &lt;4 hours (Pro), &lt;1 hour (Enterprise). RPO target: &lt;1 hour (Pro), &lt;15 minutes (Enterprise). DR procedures: (1) CF Region Failure — Smart Placement auto-migrates DOs to available region; manual DO namespace migration if Smart Placement insufficient. (2) R2 Data Loss — restore from previous-day R2 versioned backup; rebuild Vectorize indexes from KB documents in R2. (3) DO Data Loss — restore from SQLite snapshots taken to R2 every 4 hours. (4) CF Account Compromise — secondary CF account warm standby for critical Workers (entry, auth, billing). Annual DR drill: tabletop exercise + partial failover test. Publish RTO/RPO targets in SLA (AV42).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW46" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW46 [MEDIUM] — Naelao Platform Internal Security Training Program</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No formal security awareness training program exists for Naelao engineering and operations staff. Employees may be unaware of social engineering tactics, phishing attacks, or secure coding practices, creating human-layer vulnerabilities that technical controls cannot fully mitigate.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement annual security training program: (1) Secure coding training for engineers (OWASP Top 10, injection prevention, cryptography best practices). (2) Phishing simulation: quarterly phishing emails to all staff; click-throughs trigger mandatory training module. (3) Social engineering awareness: recognize pretexting, vishing, baiting. (4) Incident response role-play: tabletop exercise simulating data breach scenario. (5) New employee security onboarding: complete training before production access granted. Track training_completion_rate by team. Integrate with HR system for tracking. Evidence of training collected for SOC 2 (AW01) and ISO 27001 (AW02) audits.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW47" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW47 [HIGH] — Content Delivery Network (CDN) Architecture for Space Assets</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Space static assets (component library JavaScript, CSS, fonts, images) are served from CF Workers on every request. While CF Workers are globally distributed, they do not leverage CF's CDN caching for static assets, causing unnecessary computation and higher latency vs pure cache hit.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement CDN strategy for Space assets: (1) Immutable assets (component JS with content hash in filename): Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable. Served from CF R2 via CF Pages (CDN-backed). (2) Semi-static assets (Space config, theme CSS): Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, stale-while-revalidate=60, s-maxage=600. Cache-Tag: space-{space_id} for purging (AT22). (3) Dynamic API responses: Cache-Control: no-store. (4) Measure cache hit ratio via CF Analytics; target &gt;90% for static, &gt;60% for semi-static. CDN architecture reduces Worker CPU usage and latency for asset-heavy Spaces.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW48" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW48 [HIGH] — Multi-Region Active-Active Deployment Architecture</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> CF Workers deploy globally by default, but SpaceDOs have a single home region. True active-active architecture where multiple regions can serve writes for the same Space requires multi-region DO coordination, which has no specified design.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define multi-region architecture: CF Workers already active-active (all PoPs). SpaceDO active-active via: (1) For read-heavy operations: regional KV cache (AT22 pattern extended). (2) For write operations: CF Smart Placement (AT14) ensures DO is in region of most writes. (3) True active-active writes (rare): CF Multi-Region Durable Objects (CF upcoming feature — track CF roadmap). (4) Near-term: geographic sharding of Spaces — EU Spaces → EU DO namespace, US Spaces → US namespace, APAC Spaces → APAC namespace. Enforce sharding at Space creation via jurisdiction selection. Data residency: EU sharding satisfies GDPR Art.44 (AN41).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW49" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d4edda;border-left:4px solid #27ae60;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW49 [LOW] — Builder Account Merger and Acquisition Support</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> When a builder company is acquired or merges with another, their Naelao account (all Spaces, billing, team members) needs to be transferred to a new parent account. No account merger/transfer process exists. The acquired company's Spaces are stranded in the original account.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement AccountTransferWorkflow: initiated by Naelao support (not self-service, to prevent fraudulent transfers). Steps: (1) verify ownership of both source and target accounts (email confirmation from both), (2) transfer all Spaces (space ownership update in SpaceDO), (3) transfer billing (Stripe customer merge or subscription transfer), (4) transfer team members (invite to new account, optional), (5) freeze source account (read-only, 30-day grace for DNS/integration updates), (6) delete source account on confirmation. Audit log captures full transfer history. Enterprise: handled via customer success team with custom contract amendment.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AW50" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AW50 [HIGH] — Space Blueprint Export and Import (Portable Format)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> There is no portable Space export format that captures all Space configuration, content, and metadata in a vendor-neutral way. Builders cannot migrate their Space to a different host, create full backups, or share Space blueprints with other builders in a structured format.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define SpaceBlueprint format: ZIP archive containing: manifest.json (Space metadata, SpaceType reference, schema version), config.json (full Space configuration), prompts/ (all prompt versions as .txt), kb/ (all KB documents as .md/.pdf), theme.json (ThemeConfig), components/ (all ComponentConfig JSON files), README.md (builder notes). Export via POST /api/spaces/{id}/export/blueprint → async job → download link. Import via POST /api/spaces/import/blueprint (validates manifest schema, checks SpaceType availability). Blueprint is portable: import into any Naelao account. GDPR: blueprint export excludes end-user data (conversations, profiles) — separate conversation export (AU30).
  </div>
</div>

<section id="appendix-ax">
<h2>Appendix AX — Agent Orchestration, Feature Flags, Rate Limiting, KB Advanced, API Security, Multimodal, i18n, Monetization</h2>

<div id="G-AX01" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX01 [HIGH] — DAG-Based Multi-Step Agent Workflow Engine</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Complex agent tasks requiring multiple dependent steps (research → draft → review → publish) must be hard-coded as sequential LLM calls. No directed acyclic graph (DAG) workflow engine exists for builders to visually design multi-step agent pipelines with branching and parallel execution.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build WorkflowEngine in SpaceDO: builder defines DAG in JSON: nodes (step_id, type: llm|tool|condition|parallel), edges (input_from, output_to). Execution engine uses CF Workflows for durability. Parallel nodes: Promise.all() on independent branches. Condition nodes: LLM-evaluated or rule-based routing. Max DAG depth: 10 levels. Max parallel branches: 5. Visual DAG editor in UI Builder (canvas-based, drag-and-drop nodes). Export/import workflow JSON. Pre-built workflow templates: "Research + Draft + Review", "Extract + Validate + Store". Track workflow_execution_time, workflow_step_failures per workflow_id.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX02" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX02 [HIGH] — Agent Subagent Spawning with Result Aggregation</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> A primary agent cannot spawn specialized subagents and aggregate their results. For tasks requiring parallel research across multiple domains (e.g., competitive analysis: spawn one agent per competitor), the architecture has no built-in fan-out/fan-in pattern.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement SubagentDO: primary agent invokes spawn_subagent(task, subagent_config) tool. SubagentDO processes task independently (own LLM calls, own KB queries). Result returned to primary agent via DO RPC. Fan-out: spawn up to 5 subagents in parallel. Fan-in: primary agent receives array of results, aggregates into final response. Subagent isolation: cannot access primary session context (prevents context pollution). Resource accounting: subagent token usage debited from same Space budget. Track subagent_spawn_count, subagent_completion_time, subagent_failure_rate. Max recursion: subagents cannot spawn further subagents (depth limit = 1).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX03" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX03 [HIGH] — Agent Feedback Loop with Self-Correction</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Agents produce an initial response and stop. No feedback loop exists where the agent evaluates its own output, identifies shortcomings, and self-corrects before responding to the end-user. A single pass produces lower-quality results than an iterative refinement loop.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement SelfCorrectionLoop: after initial agent response, run critique step: "Evaluate this response for: completeness, accuracy, actionability. List specific improvements needed." If critique identifies issues, run revision step with critique as input. Max iterations: 3. Convergence: stop when critique scores response 8+/10 on all dimensions. Cost guard: only run self-correction for agent types where builder enables it (costs 3× tokens). Track correction_iterations_count and quality_improvement_score (pre vs post correction, using faithfulness scorer from AT09). A/B test: correction-enabled vs disabled on same queries.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX04" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX04 [HIGH] — Agent Long-Running Task Status and Polling</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Long-running agent tasks (document analysis, batch processing, deep research) execute synchronously. End-users must wait with the connection open for potentially minutes. No asynchronous task system with status polling or callback notification exists.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement AsyncAgentTask: client sends POST /api/spaces/{id}/run with prefer: respond-async header. Server: creates TaskDO with task_id, runs agent via CF Workflow (AT01 pattern). Returns 202 Accepted + { task_id, status_url }. Client polls GET /api/spaces/{id}/tasks/{task_id} → { status: queued|processing|complete|failed, result: ... }. Long-poll option: GET with timeout=30s blocks up to 30s if task not complete. Webhook callback: client provides callback_url in initial request; TaskDO calls URL on completion. Task expiry: results retained 24 hours. WebSocket push: if client has active session, push task_complete event directly.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX05" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX05 [HIGH] — Agent Memory Context Window Management Strategy</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> As multi-turn conversations grow, injecting all message history into the context window eventually hits the model's context limit. No strategy exists for selective memory compression or history summarization that preserves the most relevant conversation history without losing critical details.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ContextWindowManager: track running token count of conversation history. When history exceeds 60% of model's context window: (1) Compress oldest N messages via LLM summary: "Summarize this conversation segment in 100 words preserving key facts, decisions, and open items." (2) Replace original messages with compressed summary. (3) Keep last 5 messages verbatim (recency bias). (4) Bookmark critical messages (user explicitly marks as important) — always include verbatim. Compression quality validation: after compression, check that pinned facts from summary are still accurate. Track compression_ratio and summary_quality_score per compression event.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX06" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX06 [MEDIUM] — Agent Conversation Branching and Rollback</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Once an agent conversation takes a wrong path (e.g., misunderstood the user's intent and went down wrong solution branch), there is no way to branch from an earlier point. End-users must start a new conversation and re-provide all context.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ConversationBranching: each message in conversation_history has message_id and parent_message_id (tree structure). End-user can click "Branch from here" on any previous message to start a new branch from that point. BranchDO creates new conversation rooted at the selected message_id, inheriting full context up to that point. End-user can switch between branches (like git branches). Branch list shown in conversation sidebar. Max 5 active branches per session. Builder can disable branching feature per Space. Track branch_created_count and branch_vs_linear_satisfaction (compare CSAT scores).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX07" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX07 [HIGH] — Agent Interrupt and Pause Mid-Execution</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Once an agent starts executing a multi-step workflow, there is no mechanism for the end-user to interrupt it mid-execution (e.g., "stop, I changed my mind") or for the agent to pause at a decision point and request clarification before proceeding with an irreversible action.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement agent interrupt via WebSocket: client sends { type: "interrupt", session_id } message. SpaceDO sets interrupt_requested flag on active workflow. Workflow checks flag between steps; on interrupt: gracefully complete current step, then return partial results with status: interrupted. Pause-for-approval: agent can emit { type: "requires_approval", action_description: "Send email to {user}?", action_id } event. Client must respond { type: "approve" | "reject", action_id } before agent proceeds. Timeout on pending approval: 30 seconds → auto-reject. Builder configures which tool calls require approval.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX08" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX08 [HIGH] — Platform Feature Flag System for Gradual Rollouts</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao has no feature flag system. New features ship to all builders simultaneously on deploy, making it impossible to do: percentage rollouts, A/B tests of new features, per-account feature gating, or instant kill-switch if a new feature causes issues.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement FeatureFlagDO: flags stored as { flag_id, enabled_pct, targeting_rules: [{attribute, operator, value}], state: on|off|rollout }. Targeting rules: per-account (account_id in list), plan-based (plan_tier = enterprise), percentage (HMAC(account_id + flag_id) % 100 &lt; enabled_pct). Flag evaluation: O(1) per request, cached in Worker memory for 30s. Flag management UI in admin dashboard. Instant kill-switch: set flag to 0% without deploy. New features wrapped in if (flags.isEnabled("new_feature", accountId)). Track feature_flag_evaluation per flag per account. Emergency disable: CLI command to disable any flag globally within 10s.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX09" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX09 [HIGH] — Per-Space Feature Flag Gating</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders cannot control which features are available within their Space via feature flags. A builder testing a beta feature (new component type, experimental agent mode) must enable it for all end-users of their Space simultaneously with no gradual rollout or A/B testing capability.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Extend FeatureFlagDO to Space-level: builder creates Space-level flags in dashboard: { flag_name, rollout_pct, targeting: by_user_segment }. Space flags evaluated per session based on session user attributes (user.plan, user.created_at, random sampling). Inject active flags into session context: agent and UI can conditionally show features based on flag state. Builder flag dashboard: shows flag state, % of sessions seeing feature, conversion metrics. Pre-built flag types: percentage rollout (10% users get new UI), user segment (only premium users see feature), date-gated (feature visible after 2026-07-01).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX10" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX10 [HIGH] — A/B Testing Framework for Space Features</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders want to A/B test different agent behaviors, prompt variations, or UI layouts to find what drives best end-user outcomes. No A/B testing framework exists integrated with the Space platform — the AT40 pricing A/B was platform-level only.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build SpaceExperimentDO: builder creates experiment { experiment_id, variants: [{variant_id, config_delta, traffic_split}], metric: conversion_rate|session_length|csat_score, min_sample_size }. Traffic split: session assigned to variant via hash(user_id + experiment_id). Config delta applied on top of base Space config (e.g., variant B uses different system_prompt). Metric collection: track metric events per variant. Statistical significance: two-proportion z-test, auto-declare winner at p &lt; 0.05 with min_sample_size reached. Dashboard: show experiment progress, confidence intervals. Auto-promote winning variant to 100% traffic. Only one experiment per Space at a time.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX11" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#f8d7da;border-left:4px solid #c0392b;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX11 [CRITICAL] — Kill Switch for Emergency Feature Disablement</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> If a newly deployed feature causes a critical incident (data corruption, security vulnerability, platform instability), there is no fast kill switch to disable just that feature without rolling back the entire deployment. Full rollback takes 5-10 minutes; a kill switch would take seconds.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement KillSwitchDO: every new feature wrapped in feature flag (AX08). Kill switch UI in admin dashboard: one-click disable for any live feature. Kill switch propagation: updates flag state in KV with 10s TTL, pushes WebSocket event to all active Workers via CF Durable Object broadcast. Workers receive kill switch event and immediately stop serving feature within 10s (KV cache expiry). Kill switch audit log: who activated, when, reason (required field), duration. Auto-expire kill switch after 24h (requires explicit re-activation). Runbook: kill switch activation procedure documented in runbook library (AW12).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX12" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX12 [HIGH] — Token Bucket Rate Limiting with Burst Allowance</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Current rate limiting uses a fixed window counter (N requests per minute). Fixed window allows burst at window boundary (double the limit by sending N at end of window N and N at start of window N+1). Token bucket algorithm with burst allowance provides smoother rate limiting without boundary bursts.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Replace fixed-window rate limiting with token bucket: RateLimitDO maintains tokens balance per API key. Tokens replenish at rate = limit/60 per second. Max tokens = burst_size (e.g., 2× per-second rate for short bursts). On each request: if tokens &gt;= cost, deduct cost; else 429. Replenishment: add tokens since last_request_at × replenishment_rate. Caps at burst_size. This allows short bursts (up to burst_size requests instantly) while enforcing long-term average rate. Token bucket state persisted in DO SQLite (last_tokens, last_refill_at). Update X-RateLimit-Remaining header (AV24) to reflect current token balance.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX13" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX13 [HIGH] — Per-User Rate Limits Within a Space</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Rate limits are enforced per API key (builder-level) but not per end-user within a Space. A single abusive end-user sending 1000 rapid-fire messages consumes the entire Space's rate limit, degrading service for all other end-users. No per-user rate limiting within a Space exists.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement per-user rate limiting in SpaceDO: maintain per-user token bucket in SpaceDO SQLite user_rate_limits(user_id, tokens, last_refill_at). Default limits: 10 messages/minute per user, 100 messages/hour. Configurable per Space by builder. On each session message: deduct from user's bucket; if empty, return 429 with Retry-After. Admin users (builders testing their own Space) exempt from end-user rate limits. Track per-user rate limit hits in Analytics Engine; alert builder if single user exceeds 50% of Space's daily message budget (potential abuse). Combine with IP-based rate limiting for unauthenticated Spaces.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX14" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX14 [MEDIUM] — Adaptive Rate Limiting Based on System Load</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Rate limits are static thresholds independent of system load. During a CF incident, LLM provider slowdown, or DO throughput reduction, static rate limits allow the same traffic volume that exceeds degraded system capacity, causing cascading failures.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement AdaptiveRateLimiter: SystemLoadDO monitors platform health metrics (p99 latency, error rate, queue depth) via Analytics Engine polling every 30s. Derives load_factor: 0.0 (nominal) to 1.0 (critically degraded). Rate limits dynamically reduced: effective_limit = base_limit × (1 - 0.7 × load_factor). At load_factor 0.5: 35% rate reduction. At load_factor 1.0: 70% reduction (emergency). Load factor computed from: (latency_percentile_ratio + error_rate + queue_depth_ratio) / 3. Changes propagated to all RateLimitDOs via KV update. Builders informed of temporary limit reduction via X-RateLimit-Reason: system_load_shedding header.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX15" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX15 [HIGH] — Rate Limit Bypass Tokens for Trusted Internal Services</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Internal Naelao services (billing processor, export worker, eval runner) must call Space APIs but should not be subject to builder-facing rate limits. Currently internal services compete with external clients for the same rate limit bucket, causing internal job failures during high traffic periods.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement rate limit bypass for internal service accounts: generate INTERNAL_SERVICE_TOKEN stored in Secrets (different from builder API keys). Rate limit middleware checks for service token: if present + valid HMAC signature, bypass rate limit entirely. Service tokens scoped to specific operations (billing_service: only /billing endpoints, export_service: only /export endpoints). Audit all internal service calls with service_identity in Analytics Engine. Rotate service tokens quarterly (AS05 automation). Track internal vs external request ratio per endpoint. Rate limit does apply to internal services for DoS protection at 100× the external limit.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX16" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX16 [HIGH] — KB Version Snapshots and Full Rollback</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Individual KB document versions are not tracked holistically. If a bulk update corrupts the entire KB (all documents accidentally deleted, wrong content uploaded), there is no KB snapshot to roll back to. Only individual document-level operations, not KB-state snapshots, are possible.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement KBSnapshotDO: before any bulk KB operation (bulk upload, bulk delete, crawler run), take a snapshot of current KB state: list of (doc_id, content_hash, chunk_ids) stored in SpaceDO SQLite kb_snapshots table. Keep last 10 snapshots. Builder can roll back to any snapshot: (1) identify doc_ids in snapshot not in current KB → re-fetch from R2 backup and re-index, (2) identify doc_ids in current KB not in snapshot → delete from Vectorize and SpaceDO. Rollback via CF Workflow for durability. Surface snapshot history in builder dashboard: "KB State on June 20 — 150 documents" with restore button.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX17" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX17 [HIGH] — Knowledge Base Access Logging for GDPR Compliance</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> No comprehensive log exists of which KB documents were retrieved in response to which end-user queries. For GDPR compliance (Art. 22 — automated decision-making transparency) and for debugging wrong answers, builders need a trace of the knowledge used in each response.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Log KB retrievals in SpaceDO SQLite kb_access_log(session_id, message_id, query_text_hash, retrieved_doc_ids, similarity_scores, context_used_yn). Store last 1000 entries per Space. Builder dashboard: "What knowledge was used?" panel for any conversation — shows source documents with relevance scores. GDPR: on end-user Art.15 data access request (AO30), include KB retrieval log showing what automated knowledge retrieval influenced their conversation. Retention: kb_access_log purged per analytics retention policy (AU29). Export included in conversation bulk export (AU30). Track kb_access_log_row_count per Space to manage SQLite space.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX18" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX18 [MEDIUM] — Proactive Knowledge Surfacing During Chat</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Agents reactively answer questions based on what end-users ask. No mechanism exists for agents to proactively surface relevant KB articles or resources during a conversation when they detect the end-user is struggling or when there is directly applicable knowledge the user hasn't asked about.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ProactiveKnowledgeSurface: after every end-user message, SpaceDO runs a background retrieval query (async, does not block response). If retrieved_chunk similarity &gt; 0.85 AND chunk not already used in conversation AND relates to predicted next user need: inject proactive suggestion: "You might also find this relevant: [Article Title] — [preview]. View full article?" Builder configures: proactive suggestions on/off, max per session (default 3), similarity threshold. Track proactive_suggestion_shown, proactive_suggestion_clicked, proactive_suggestion_engagement_rate per Space. A/B test: sessions with proactive surfacing vs without, measuring resolution rate and session length.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX19" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX19 [MEDIUM] — KB Document Relationship Graph for Navigation</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> KB documents are stored as independent chunks with no explicit relationship structure. End-users asking a question related to Doc A cannot be easily guided to related Doc B (prerequisite, follow-up, alternative). No cross-document navigation layer exists.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build DocRelationshipEngine: on KB upload, LLM extracts relationships between documents: (parent_doc, EXPLAINS, child_doc), (doc_a, REQUIRES, doc_b), (doc_a, RELATED_TO, doc_b), (doc_a, SUPERSEDES, doc_b). Store in SpaceDO SQLite doc_relationships table. On retrieval: when Doc A is cited in a response, also surface related documents: "See also: [Doc B] (prerequisite), [Doc C] (related)." Builder can manually add/edit relationships in KB manager. Surface relationship graph visualization in builder KB dashboard. Track related_doc_clicked vs primary_doc_clicked to measure navigation value.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX20" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX20 [MEDIUM] — Federated KB Search Across Multiple Spaces</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> A builder with 10 Spaces (one per product line) cannot perform a unified knowledge search across all their Spaces' KBs simultaneously. End-users experience fragmented knowledge silos — a support agent in Space A cannot access knowledge from Space B even when relevant.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement FederatedKBSearch: builder creates KB federation by selecting multiple Spaces to include. FederationDO performs parallel Vectorize queries across all federated Spaces' indexes, collects top-K per Space, merges via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (AV33 pattern). Requires cross-Space data sharing grant (AT29) for non-owned Spaces. Result attribution: each chunk labeled with source Space. Agent system prompt clarifies which Space's knowledge is being used. Cache federated search results by query hash (5-minute TTL). Enforce data residency (AW48): all federated Spaces must be in same jurisdiction for EU compliance.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX21" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX21 [HIGH] — KB Analytics: Most-Retrieved Documents and Knowledge Gaps</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders have no analytics on which KB documents are retrieved most frequently, which queries return zero relevant results (knowledge gaps), or which documents are retrieved but not cited in final responses (potentially irrelevant documents consuming tokens).
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build KBAnalyticsDO: track per-doc retrieval counts, citation counts (doc retrieved AND included in final response), zero-result query count. Weekly CF Workflow aggregates into SpaceDO SQLite kb_analytics table. Builder dashboard KB Analytics panel shows: Top 10 most retrieved docs (confirms valuable content), Top 10 never-retrieved docs (dead content to remove or improve), Top 10 queries with zero relevant results (knowledge gaps to fill), citation_rate per doc (retrieved but not cited = low relevance). Alert builder: "20 queries this week returned zero results — consider adding documentation for: {common topics}." Extracted from retrieved_query patterns via LLM clustering.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX22" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX22 [HIGH] — OAuth 2.0 Scopes for Builder API Keys</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builder API keys grant full access to all Naelao APIs. There is no scope system allowing a builder to issue a read-only key (for analytics dashboard), a Space-specific key (for a specific Space only), or a webhook-only key. Principle of least privilege cannot be applied to API keys.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement API key scopes: builder creates key with selected scopes from: spaces:read, spaces:write, spaces:run, kb:read, kb:write, analytics:read, billing:read, webhooks:manage. Each API key stored with scopes JSON in AccountDO SQLite api_keys table. Entry Worker validates: requested operation in allowed scopes; else 403 Forbidden with X-Required-Scope header. Scope definitions documented in OpenAPI spec. Builder can create multiple keys with different scopes: one for CI/CD (spaces:write), one for monitoring (analytics:read), one for agent run integration (spaces:run). Key scope shown in API key management UI.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX23" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX23 [HIGH] — API Key Abuse Detection and Automatic Suspension</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Compromised builder API keys can be used indefinitely until manually revoked. No automated anomaly detection exists for unusual API key usage patterns (sudden spike in requests, requests from new geographies, unusual endpoint access patterns) that would indicate key compromise.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ApiKeyAnomalyDetector: for each API key, maintain usage baseline (rolling 7-day average: requests/hour, unique IPs, endpoint distribution). On each request, check against baseline: if requests/hour &gt; 3× baseline OR new country/continent detected OR sudden endpoint distribution shift, flag as SUSPICIOUS. SUSPICIOUS state: send email to builder "Unusual activity detected on your API key" + require MFA re-confirmation to continue. CRITICAL anomaly (requests/hour &gt; 10× baseline): auto-suspend key immediately, email builder + require account owner confirmation to reinstate. Track key_anomaly_detected, key_auto_suspended events in immutable audit log.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX24" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX24 [HIGH] — Google / GitHub / Apple SSO for Builder Platform</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builder platform authentication supports only email+password and TOTP. Social login (Google, GitHub, Apple) is not supported. Most builders have Google or GitHub accounts and expect one-click signup/login without creating a new password, reducing onboarding friction.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement OAuth 2.0 social login: integrate Google Identity Platform, GitHub OAuth App, and Apple Sign In. On first social login: check if email matches existing account (link accounts), else create new account with social identity. Store social_connections(provider, provider_user_id, email, linked_at) in AccountDO. Multiple providers can be linked to one account. Security: social login still requires MFA if builder has MFA enabled (prompt post-OAuth). Delink provider option in account settings. Track signup_source (email vs google vs github vs apple) in Analytics Engine for acquisition attribution. Builder can require specific provider (enterprise: require Google Workspace SSO).
