</div>
<div id="G-AX27" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-AX27 [HIGH] — API Request Replay Attack Prevention</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Naelao API requests lack a replay prevention mechanism. An attacker who captures a valid signed request (e.g., via MITM) can re-send it indefinitely. Without timestamp validation or nonce-based replay prevention, captured requests are replayable.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement request replay prevention for API key authentication: require X-Naelao-Timestamp (unix epoch) header on all authenticated requests. Reject requests where |current_time - request_timestamp| &gt; 300s (5-minute tolerance). Require X-Naelao-Nonce (unique random value per request) for state-modifying requests. NonceDO tracks recent nonces (5-minute window) in KV with TTL=300s; reject duplicate nonces. Return 400 with X-Naelao-Error: replay_detected if duplicate. Document in API reference that clients must include fresh timestamps and unique nonces. SDK auto-generates timestamp and nonce headers. Only applies to API key auth; JWT sessions use their own exp claim for replay prevention.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-AX28" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
