</div>

<section id="appendix-ba">
<h2>Appendix BA — Multi-Tenancy Hardening, Real-Time Collaboration, SpaceType Patterns, Operational Excellence, End-User Advanced, Security Hardening, Extensibility, Analytics, Networking, Observability</h2>

<div id="G-BA01" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#f8d7da;border-left:4px solid #c0392b;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA01 [CRITICAL] — Cross-Tenant Data Leak Prevention at DO Layer</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> DO naming scheme must guarantee per-tenant isolation. If DO names are predictable or based on guessable identifiers, a tenant could craft requests that resolve to another tenant's DO, accessing their SQLite data. No formal namespace isolation guarantee is specified.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Enforce DO naming convention: {tenant_hash}:{resource_type}:{resource_id} where tenant_hash = HMAC-SHA256(tenant_id, platform_secret). Route all DO stubs through a routing Worker that validates the requesting JWT tenant_id matches the DO namespace prefix before forwarding. Log cross-tenant probe attempts to security Analytics Engine table. Automated alert on &gt;3 cross-tenant probes in 5min.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA02" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA02 [HIGH] — Tenant Resource Quota Enforcement at Ingress</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Resource quotas (DO storage, KV keys, Vectorize vectors, R2 storage) are monitored but not enforced at request ingress. A tenant that exceeds their quota continues to accumulate resources until the next monitoring cycle detects the breach.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement quota enforcement middleware in routing Worker: before processing, check tenant quota cache (CF KV, refreshed every 60s). If any quota exceeded: reject request with 429 + quota_exceeded detail. Quota check KV key: quota:{tenant_id}:{resource}, value: {used, limit, last_updated}. Quota usage incremented atomically in DO SQLite, synced to KV on writes. Grace: 5% overage allowed before hard block.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA03" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#f8d7da;border-left:4px solid #c0392b;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA03 [CRITICAL] — Tenant Secret and Credential Isolation</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builder API keys, LLM provider credentials, CRM tokens, and webhook secrets must be stored with per-tenant encryption keys. A single platform-wide encryption key means a key compromise exposes all tenant credentials.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement per-tenant credential vault: each tenant has derived encryption key = HKDF(platform_master_key, tenant_id). All credentials encrypted with tenant key before storage in D1/DO SQLite. Key rotation: platform_master_key rotation re-encrypts all tenant keys without changing tenant key derivation inputs. Tenant credential access requires both auth JWT and correct tenant_id for key derivation. Audit log every credential read.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA04" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA04 [HIGH] — Space-to-Space Isolation in Embedded Contexts</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> When multiple Spaces are embedded on the same page (Space-in-Space, AT36), they share the same browser origin. Without isolation, Space A's JavaScript can read Space B's DOM, intercept events, or exfiltrate conversation data.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Mandate iframe sandbox for all embedded Spaces: sandbox="allow-scripts allow-forms allow-same-origin" with allow-same-origin restricted to Space's own subdomain only. Use postMessage with strict origin validation for Space-to-parent communication. CSP on Space shell: frame-ancestors 'none' except whitelisted builder domains. Report CSP violations to analytics endpoint. No shared localStorage keys across Spaces.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA05" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA05 [HIGH] — Noisy Neighbor CPU Isolation</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> CF Workers have per-request CPU limits but no tenant-level CPU quota across multiple requests. A noisy tenant making 10,000 concurrent requests consumes disproportionate CF compute budget, degrading latency for other tenants on the same CF account.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement tenant-level request throttling: CF Durable Object maintains per-tenant request_rate counter (token bucket, 1000 req/min default). Requests over limit queued in CF Queue (max queue depth 10,000; older requests dropped). Priority queuing: paid tiers get higher priority. Track tenant_throttle_events in Analytics Engine. Ops alert if any tenant consistently hits throttle (&gt;10% of requests) — trigger capacity review.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA06" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA06 [HIGH] — Collaborative Space Builder (Multi-User Config Editing)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builder teams cannot collaborate on SpaceType configuration simultaneously. No multi-user editing support means one person works at a time, with no conflict detection if two people edit the same config concurrently.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement OT (Operational Transform) or CRDT-based collaborative config editor: SpaceType config stored as CRDT document in DO SQLite. Each builder edit is an operation (op) that applies to the document. Ops broadcast via WebSocket to co-editors. Presence cursors show who is editing what field. Conflict-free merge for simple field edits; flag conflicts for complex structural changes. Track collaboration_session_count.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA07" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA07 [HIGH] — Real-Time Space Analytics Dashboard (Live View)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builder analytics dashboard shows historical data only. No live view shows currently active sessions, messages per minute, error rate in real-time. Builders launching a new Space have no visibility into real-time performance.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Builder dashboard "Live" tab: WebSocket connection from browser to analytics DO (one per builder). Analytics DO subscribes to Space activity events via internal pub/sub. Pushes live metrics: active_sessions_count, messages_per_minute, error_rate_1min, p50_latency_1min, top_queries_live. Refresh every 2s. Connection limit: 100 concurrent live dashboard connections per builder. Graceful degradation to 30s polling if WebSocket unavailable.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA08" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA08 [HIGH] — Team Workspace and Permission Management</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders operate as individual accounts with no team workspace concept. Organizations cannot add multiple team members, assign roles (admin/editor/viewer), or share SpaceType access — blocking enterprise adoption.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Add Organization entity: {id, name, owner_id, plan, members:[{user_id, role, invited_at, joined_at}]}. Roles: OWNER (full access), ADMIN (all except billing), EDITOR (create/edit SpaceTypes, cannot publish), VIEWER (read-only). SpaceTypes owned by Organization. Member invitation via email. SSO provisioning auto-joins org. Audit log for all org actions. Billing consolidated at org level. Track team_size distribution.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA09" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA09 [MEDIUM] — Shared Builder Asset Library</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders in the same organization re-create identical KB documents, prompt templates, tool configurations, and system prompts across multiple SpaceTypes. No shared asset library enables reuse across SpaceTypes within an org.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Add Asset Library: organization-scoped store of reusable assets: {prompt_templates, kb_document_sets, tool_configs, persona_definitions, system_prompt_snippets}. SpaceType config references assets by ID. Asset updated once → all SpaceTypes using it pick up change at next deploy. Asset versioning: SpaceType pins asset_version or tracks latest. Track asset_reuse_rate (assets with &gt;1 SpaceType reference).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA10" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA10 [MEDIUM] — Builder Activity Feed and Notifications</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builders have no activity feed showing what happened to their Spaces: new user sessions, error spikes, KB updates, billing events, teammate actions. No notification system surfaces time-sensitive events.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Builder notification center: activity_feed table (builder_id, event_type, title, detail, created_at, read_at). Event sources: session_milestone (100th user), error_spike, billing_threshold, teammate_action, KB_update_needed, SpaceType_review_approved. Delivery: in-app bell icon + badge, email digest (configurable: instant/daily/weekly), Slack webhook (optional). Mark-as-read tracking. Track notification_open_rate by event_type.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA11" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA11 [HIGH] — SpaceType Runtime Version Pinning</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Platform runtime (agent framework version, tool schema version, model defaults) evolves over time. SpaceTypes deployed against one runtime version may break when platform runtime upgrades. No version pinning mechanism protects stable SpaceTypes.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define runtime_version as platform-level semver (e.g., "2.3.0"). Each SpaceType pins minimum_runtime_version in manifest. On platform upgrade: run compatibility check against all published SpaceTypes. Breaking changes increment major version. SpaceTypes pinned to old major version continue running on that runtime. Builder receives deprecation notice: "Upgrade to runtime 3.0 by [date] to access new features." Track runtime_version_distribution.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA12" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA12 [HIGH] — SpaceType Input Validation Schema (Zod/JSONSchema)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> SpaceType configurations accept arbitrary user inputs (form fields, config values). Without schema validation, malformed configurations reach agent execution layer, causing cryptic errors rather than helpful validation messages.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define SpaceType input schema using JSON Schema (Draft 7): each configurable field has type, format, minLength, maxLength, enum, pattern constraints. Validated at config save time with detailed error messages: "API Key must be 32-64 characters." Runtime validation guards tool inputs. Use AJV (compiled validators) for &lt;1ms validation overhead. Schema auto-generates form UI in builder dashboard. Schema exported in SpaceType manifest for SDK consumers.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA13" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA13 [HIGH] — SpaceType Dependency Resolution and Conflict Detection</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> SpaceTypes that depend on other SpaceTypes (composition, AV01) or external services may have incompatible version requirements. No dependency resolution system detects conflicts before deployment.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define SpaceType dependency manifest: {depends_on: [{spacetype_id, min_version, max_version}], external_services: [{name, required_capabilities}]}. At deploy time: resolve dependency graph, check version constraints, detect conflicts (A requires tool_schema &gt;= 2.0, B requires tool_schema &lt; 2.0). Report conflicts with resolution suggestions. Lockfile pattern: spacetype.lock.json records exact resolved versions for reproducible deploys.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA14" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA14 [MEDIUM] — SpaceType Configuration Inheritance and Overrides</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Builder organizations use the same base SpaceType across multiple Spaces with slight variations (different personas, different KB subsets, different tone). No inheritance mechanism allows defining a base config and overriding specific fields per Space.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement SpaceType config inheritance: Space config has base_spacetype_id + overrides: {field: value}. At runtime, config = merge(base_spacetype_config, overrides). Override precedence: Space-level &gt; SpaceType-level &gt; platform defaults. Inheritance depth max 3 levels. Builder UI shows "inherited from [SpaceType]" labels for non-overridden fields. Track override_depth distribution.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA15" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA15 [HIGH] — SpaceType Rollback and Blue-Green Deployment</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> When a SpaceType config update causes degradation (error spike, user complaints), builders have no instant rollback path. Manual rollback requires re-entering previous config values.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement SpaceType version history: every deploy creates immutable version record (version_id, config_snapshot, deployed_at, deployed_by). Builder can rollback to any previous version with one click. Blue-green: new version deployed to 10% of traffic for 15min before full rollout (configurable). Auto-rollback: if error_rate increases &gt;3x during canary, auto-revert to previous version, alert builder. Track rollback_frequency per SpaceType.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA16" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA16 [HIGH] — SLO Error Budget Tracking and Burn Rate Alerts</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> SLOs are defined (AW45 Space SLA definition) but error budget consumption is not tracked in real-time. Teams discover SLO breaches at end-of-month reviews rather than when burn rate indicates an imminent breach.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement error budget tracking: for each SLO (e.g., p99_ttft &lt; 3s, 99.9% availability), compute: error_budget_total = (1 - SLO_target) * window_minutes. error_budget_consumed = actual_bad_minutes. burn_rate = consumed / (elapsed_time / total_window). Alert thresholds: burn_rate &gt; 14.4x = page (budget exhausted in 1h), burn_rate &gt; 6x = ticket (exhausted in 1 day). Dashboard shows error budget gauge per SLO.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA17" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA17 [HIGH] — On-Call Rotation and Escalation Policy</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Platform alerts exist but no on-call rotation or escalation policy is defined. When critical alerts fire, there is no defined responder, paging mechanism, or escalation path — incidents go unacknowledged.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define on-call structure: primary + secondary + escalation (manager). PagerDuty/OpsGenie integration: CF Worker sends alert payload to paging service. Escalation: if no ack within 5min, page secondary; 15min no ack, page manager. On-call schedule: weekly rotation. Runbook link in every alert. Post-incident: 5-why root cause within 48h. Track MTTR (Mean Time To Resolve) and MTTA (Mean Time To Acknowledge) per alert type.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA18" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA18 [HIGH] — Capacity Planning and Scaling Signals</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Platform operates on CF serverless (auto-scales) but CF account limits (script count, DO count, KV namespace count, Vectorize index count) are finite. No capacity planning process monitors approach to these limits.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Monthly capacity review CF Workflow: query CF API for account-level usage vs limits (scripts, DOs, KV namespaces, R2 buckets, Vectorize indexes). Compute utilization_pct per resource. Alert: WARNING at 70%, CRITICAL at 90%. Projected: at current growth rate, which limits will be hit in 30/90 days. Actions: pre-request limit increases at 70%, activate namespace sharding at 90%. Publish capacity headroom dashboard for ops team.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA19" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA19 [HIGH] — Graceful Degradation Under Partial Outage</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> When a subsystem fails (LLM provider down, Vectorize unavailable, R2 unreachable), agents fail completely rather than degrading gracefully. No degraded-mode operation serves partial responses.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define degradation tiers: FULL (all subsystems OK), DEGRADED_KB (Vectorize/R2 down — agent responds without KB context, discloses limitation), DEGRADED_LLM (fallback to simpler model or cached responses), DEGRADED_AUTH (serve cached session tokens, block new auth), EMERGENCY (static maintenance page). Each tier has automatic trigger conditions and recovery checks. Space shell displays "Some features temporarily limited" banner in DEGRADED mode.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA20" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA20 [HIGH] — Synthetic Transaction Monitoring</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Platform health is measured reactively (real user errors). No synthetic monitoring runs representative user journeys on a schedule to proactively detect degradation before users experience it.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement synthetic monitor: CF Cron every 5min runs synthetic transaction: (1) auth as test user, (2) POST /sessions to test Space, (3) send test message, (4) validate response latency &lt; 5s and response not empty, (5) verify KB retrieval returned result. On failure: alert on-call, mark synthetic_check_failed in status page. Success: record latency in Analytics Engine. Track synthetic_success_rate as primary availability signal.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA21" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA21 [HIGH] — End-User Conversation Continuity Across Devices</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> End-users who start a conversation on mobile and switch to desktop encounter a new session with no history. Cross-device conversation continuity is not specified — breaking the user experience for multi-device users.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement cross-device session sync: end-user sessions keyed by user_id (not device_id). On login, fetch active_sessions for user. If existing session found with recent activity (&lt;7 days), offer "Continue your conversation" prompt. Session state (messages, context) stored in SpaceDO keyed by user_id. WebSocket reconnect by session_id from any device. Track cross_device_continuation_rate.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA22" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA22 [MEDIUM] — End-User Saved Responses and Knowledge Snippets</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> End-users receive valuable information from agents but have no way to save or bookmark individual responses for later reference. Valuable answers are lost when the conversation window is closed or the history expires.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Add "Save response" button to each agent message in Space shell. Saved responses stored in user_saved_responses DO SQLite table: {id, session_id, message_id, content_snapshot, title, tags, saved_at}. Accessible via "My Saved Items" panel in Space shell. Export as PDF or copy to clipboard. Builder can disable this feature per SpaceType. Track save_rate (saved responses / total agent responses).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA23" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA23 [MEDIUM] — End-User Conversation Search</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> End-users with long conversation histories (AY19) cannot search through past interactions. Finding a previously discussed topic requires manual scrolling through hundreds of messages.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement client-side conversation search for active session and server-side search for archived sessions. Client-side: debounced filter on rendered messages. Server-side: full-text search over user_conversation_history using DO SQLite FTS5 extension. Search API: GET /users/{id}/conversations/search?q=term&amp;space_id=. Returns: [{session_id, message_id, excerpt, timestamp}]. Highlight matches in results. Track search_usage_rate and search_success_rate.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA24" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA24 [HIGH] — End-User Feedback Loop to Builder</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> End-users can rate individual responses (AX49 streaming quality metrics) but no mechanism aggregates and surfaces this feedback to builders in actionable form. Builders cannot identify which types of questions the Space handles poorly.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build feedback aggregation pipeline: end-user thumbs up/down on responses → CF Queue → Analytics Engine. Builder dashboard "Feedback" tab: feedback_rate (% of responses rated), positive_rate, negative_rate_by_topic (clustered by query embedding). Weekly digest email to builder: "Your Space received 23 negative ratings this week — common theme: [cluster summary]." Flag sessions with &gt;3 consecutive negative ratings for manual review.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA25" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA25 [HIGH] — End-User Data Export (GDPR / Portability)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> GDPR Article 20 grants right to data portability. End-users must be able to export all their data (conversation history, saved items, profile) in machine-readable format. No export mechanism is specified.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Add "Export my data" in Space shell user settings. On request: CF Workflow collects all user data across Space: conversation history (JSON), saved responses (JSON), profile (JSON), analytics events (anonymized). Packages into ZIP in R2. Sends secure download link via email (expires 24h). Process within 72h (GDPR requirement). Log export_request in audit trail. Track export_request_rate.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA26" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA26 [HIGH] — JWT Token Binding and Device Fingerprinting</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> JWTs are bearer tokens — stolen tokens work from any device. No token binding ties a JWT to the issuing device, allowing credential theft attacks to succeed silently.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement token binding: at JWT issuance, compute device_fingerprint = hash(user_agent + CF-IPCountry + CF-Device-Type + DPoP_public_key). Embed fingerprint in JWT claims. At each request, recompute fingerprint and compare. Mismatch: reject token with 401, log token_binding_mismatch event, alert user via email. DPoP (RFC 9449) support for cryptographic token binding in OAuth flows. Track token_binding_failure_rate.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA27" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA27 [HIGH] — Supply Chain Security for CF Worker Scripts</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> CF Worker scripts may import npm packages (via bundler). Compromised npm packages are a primary supply chain attack vector. No software supply chain controls are specified beyond basic CVE scanning (AW29).
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement supply chain controls: (1) Lock all dependencies in package-lock.json, pin exact versions, (2) npm audit in CI — block deploy on CRITICAL CVEs, (3) Generate SBOM (Software Bill of Materials) as CycloneDX JSON on every build, store in R2, (4) Sigstore Cosign: sign every Worker bundle artifact, verify signature at deploy time, (5) Dependency review: weekly automated PR for patch-level updates via Dependabot, (6) Approved dependency list — new deps require security review.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA28" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA28 [HIGH] — Runtime Secret Scanning in Worker Code</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Developers may accidentally hardcode secrets (API keys, connection strings) in CF Worker source code. No runtime secret scanning detects hardcoded secrets in deployed bundles.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> CI pipeline step: run trufflesecurity/trufflehog on every git push, scanning for 700+ credential patterns. Block deploy if secrets found. Worker bundle analysis: post-build, scan compiled JS bundle with custom regex patterns for common secret formats. CF Wrangler secrets-check hook: prevents wrangler deploy if known-bad patterns detected in source. Alert security team on any detection. Track secret_detection_count (should always be 0 in prod).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA29" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#f8d7da;border-left:4px solid #c0392b;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA29 [CRITICAL] — API Key Scope Enforcement at Runtime</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> API keys have defined scopes (AX22) but scope enforcement is only checked at the routing layer. If the routing layer is bypassed or has a bug, scoped keys could access resources outside their intended scope.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement defense-in-depth scope enforcement: (1) Routing layer checks scopes (first line), (2) Each DO verifies requestor scope before executing any method — DO-level scope check is independent and cannot be bypassed, (3) Resource ownership validation: DO confirms resource belongs to the authenticated tenant_id before responding, (4) Scope enforcement audit: weekly automated test verifies that scoped keys cannot access out-of-scope resources. Log scope_violation_attempt.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BA30" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BA30 [HIGH] — Automated Penetration Testing Schedule</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Manual red team exercises (AW28) are periodic but no automated pentest schedule ensures continuous security validation. New features deployed between manual pentests may introduce vulnerabilities that go undetected for months.
