</div>
<div id="G-BD43" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BD43 [MEDIUM] — API Economy and Monetization of Platform Capabilities</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Platform capabilities (KB retrieval, agent orchestration, SpaceType runtime) could be exposed as APIs for developers to build custom applications outside of the Space shell paradigm. No API economy strategy monetizes platform capabilities beyond the Space product.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define API economy tier: PLATFORM_API plan: developers access raw platform capabilities: (1) KB API: store and retrieve knowledge vectors, (2) Agent API: start agent sessions with custom prompts, (3) Orchestration API: run SpaceType workflows programmatically. Pricing: per API call (token-based for agent, per query for KB). Use case: developers building mobile apps, voice assistants, Slack bots powered by platform. Track API_economy_revenue and developer_count in API program.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BD44" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d4edda;border-left:4px solid #27ae60;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BD44 [LOW] — Platform Benchmarks and Public Performance Claims</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Platform makes no public performance claims. No benchmark suite measures platform capabilities (response latency, retrieval accuracy, agent quality) that could serve as marketing evidence and competitive differentiation.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define public benchmark suite: (1) Latency: median time-to-first-token vs competitor platforms (measure monthly), (2) Retrieval accuracy: precision@5 on domain-specific benchmark datasets, (3) Agent task completion: success rate on standardized agent tasks (like GAIA benchmark), (4) Uptime: 12-month trailing availability pct. Publish quarterly benchmark report. Commit: if benchmarks fall below published thresholds, investigate and improve before next report. Track benchmark_trend and press_coverage_from_benchmarks.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BD45" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#f8d7da;border-left:4px solid #c0392b;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BD45 [CRITICAL] — Disaster Recovery for Builder Data</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> General DR runbook (AW44, BC48 backup testing) covers platform recovery, but no builder-specific data recovery procedure ensures a builder can recover their SpaceType configs, KB documents, and conversation history after accidental deletion.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define builder data recovery: (1) Soft-delete all builder resources (SpaceTypes, KB docs, Spaces) — retain 30 days before hard delete, (2) Builder self-service recovery: dashboard "Recently Deleted" panel → restore with 1 click within 30 days, (3) Support-assisted recovery: for older deletions (30-90 days), support team can restore from R2 lifecycle snapshots, (4) Conversation history recovery: DO SQLite backups restored to staging for data extraction, (5) Bulk export before deletion: automatic data export triggered on account cancellation request. Track recovery_request_rate and recovery_success_rate.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BD46" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BD46 [HIGH] — Network Perimeter Defense for Platform APIs</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Platform APIs are publicly accessible. No network perimeter defense (WAF rules, DDoS protection beyond CF default, geo-blocking options, bot detection) beyond default CF protections is configured at application level.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Configure application-level perimeter defense: (1) CF WAF: custom rules for API abuse patterns (no user-agent header, excessive header size, known attack signatures), (2) CF Bot Fight Mode: challenge non-browser clients accessing Space shell, (3) Rate-based rules: &gt;1000 req/min from single IP → challenge page (not block), (4) Geo-blocking: optional per builder (block countries they do not operate in), (5) CF Turnstile: invisible CAPTCHA on auth endpoints. Track WAF_block_rate, false_positive_rate, and DDoS_mitigation_events.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BD47" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BD47 [HIGH] — API Versioning Sunset Tracking</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> API versioning policy (AZ48) defines deprecation timelines but no automated tracking system monitors which builders are still using deprecated APIs and sends targeted reminders as sunset dates approach.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Build API version tracking: log api_version from every API request to Analytics Engine (extract from URL path or Accept-Version header). Per-builder report: GET /builders/{id}/api-usage shows version distribution. Pre-sunset campaign: 90 days before: email all builders using deprecated version with migration guide. 30 days before: in-dashboard warning banner. 7 days before: email + API response header sunset_warning. Day 0: deprecated version returns 410 Gone with migration URL. Track migration_completion_rate before each sunset date.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BD48" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BD48 [HIGH] — Platform API Documentation Quality</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> API documentation portal (AU22 API docs portal) exists but no quality standards ensure documentation is accurate, complete, and up-to-date with actual API behaviour. Stale docs cause builder frustration and support tickets.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define doc quality standards: every API endpoint requires: (1) Description (what it does, when to use it), (2) All parameters documented with type, required/optional, valid values, example, (3) Response schema with all fields documented, (4) Error codes with descriptions and resolution steps, (5) Working code examples in TypeScript and Python, (6) Changelog section. Automation: CI test compares OpenAPI spec against live API behaviour — fail if undocumented endpoints or schema mismatches. Track doc_coverage_pct and support_tickets_from_doc_issues.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BD49" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BD49 [MEDIUM] — Global Support Coverage and Follow-the-Sun</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Support operations with no specification for geographic coverage leave APAC and EMEA builders without timely support during their business hours. A San Francisco-hours support team provides 8-hour response gaps to international builders.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define follow-the-sun support model: three support hubs (Americas / EMEA / APAC) covering 24x6 enterprise support (8h each). On-call rotation covers remaining hours. Builder timezone detected from account profile — assigned to nearest hub. Tickets routed to hub with current active shift. Handoff protocol: end-of-shift summary written for each open P1/P2 ticket. Track support_response_time by builder_timezone and hub_utilization. Target: no &gt;4h gap in enterprise support coverage.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BD50" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d4edda;border-left:4px solid #27ae60;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BD50 [LOW] — Platform Founding Principles and Values Documentation</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Platform technical architecture is extensively documented but no statement of founding principles and values guides decision-making when trade-offs arise (e.g., privacy vs features, openness vs safety, builder autonomy vs end-user protection). Without this, decisions are made inconsistently.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Write Platform Principles document: (1) Builder empowerment without coding — every configuration must be doable via UI, no exceptions, (2) End-user safety over feature velocity — ship safety measures before adjacent features, (3) Scale from day one — no temporary architectures, (4) Privacy by design — collect minimum data, default to privacy, (5) Transparency with builders — publish uptime, pricing changes, API deprecations with ample notice. Reference in all major technical decision ADRs (BC50). Review principles annually with leadership. Track decisions_aligned_to_principles_pct in PIRs.
  </div>
</div>

<section id="appendix-be">
<h2>Appendix BE — CF Platform Limits, Emerging Tech, Builder Experience, QA Infrastructure, Security Final Layer, End-User Engagement, Cross-Cutting Architecture</h2>

<div id="G-BE01" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BE01 [HIGH] — CF Workers Script Size Limit (10MB Compressed)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> CF Workers scripts have a 10MB compressed size limit. As the platform routing Worker grows with more middleware, routing logic, and bundled dependencies, this limit becomes a real constraint. Exceeding it causes deploy failures.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Monitor Worker script size on every build: CI step runs wrangler deploy --dry-run and checks bundle size. Alert if &gt; 7MB (70% threshold). Optimization strategies: (1) Code split via ES module imports (CF supports multi-module Workers), (2) Move large static data (pricing tables, regex libraries) to CF KV instead of bundle, (3) Lazy-load rarely-used middleware via dynamic import(), (4) Audit dependencies monthly — remove unused packages. Target: keep routing Worker &lt; 5MB compressed.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BE02" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BE02 [HIGH] — CF KV Value Size Limit (25MB) and Key Count (100M)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> CF KV values have a 25MB maximum size. Cached LLM responses, session token payloads, or large config snapshots that exceed this limit cause silent write failures if not validated. The 100M key per namespace limit also needs headroom monitoring.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Add KV write validation: before any KV.put(), measure value byte length. If &gt; 23MB (92% threshold): log warning, split value into chunked keys ({key}_chunk_{n}) and store chunk manifest at base key. For large cached responses: truncate to 23MB with truncated=true flag. Key count monitoring: monthly CF Workflow queries key count via KV list (paginated), alerts at 80M keys (80%), suggests namespace sharding at 90M.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BE03" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BE03 [HIGH] — CF Queues Message Retention and DLQ Lifecycle</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> CF Queues retain messages up to 4 days in DLQ (covered in R2 event DLQ, AT50). However, the primary queue also has a 4-day maximum retention. Messages that fail processing for &gt;4 days (e.g., consumer Worker consistently down) are silently dropped — never entering DLQ.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Monitor queue health: CF Workflow every 30min: check queue_backlog_depth and oldest_message_age via CF API. If oldest_message_age &gt; 3 days: CRITICAL alert — investigate consumer health immediately. If DLQ depth &gt; 1000: alert — bulk replay or investigate root cause. DLQ processing: separate consumer Worker processes DLQ messages with manual review gate. Track messages_silently_dropped_count (should always be 0). Consumer circuit breaker: pause consumption if error_rate &gt; 50% to allow investigation.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BE04" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BE04 [HIGH] — CF Workers Sub-Request Limit (1000 per Request)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> CF Workers can make maximum 1000 subrequests per incoming request invocation. Complex agent pipelines making many tool calls (web searches, API calls, KB retrievals) across a single conversation turn can approach this limit, causing unexpected failures.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Audit subrequest consumption per agent turn: instrument subrequest counter in routing Worker. Log subrequest_count per request. Alert if any request exceeds 800 subrequests (80% of limit). Optimization: (1) Batch multiple KV reads into single KV.getAll() call, (2) Consolidate Vectorize queries, (3) Fan-out tool calls via CF Queue (avoids subrequest counting), (4) Cache frequent subrequests in memory within request scope. Track p99_subrequest_count and limit_approach_incidents.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BE05" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BE05 [HIGH] — CF Analytics Engine Row Limit (25M/day) Per Account</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Analytics Engine (covered in G-AU04) allows 25M rows/day per account — shared across all Workers in the account. At 1M active sessions/day with 10 events per session, that is 10M events/day — 40% of quota consumed by traffic alone, leaving little headroom for error logs, audit events, and system metrics.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement row budget management: daily Analytics Engine write budget: 25M rows. Allocate by priority: system_metrics (5M), session_events (10M), audit_logs (5M), error_logs (3M), debug_events (2M). Rate limiter per event category in routing Worker. When category budget exhausted: drop events (for debug), or sample at 10% (for session events). Emergency: switch low-priority event types to R2 batch export instead of Analytics Engine. Track daily_row_consumption_pct per category.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BE06" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BE06 [MEDIUM] — CF Vectorize Index Limit (100 Indexes Per Account)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Platform creates one Vectorize index per Space KB (AY27 covers warm-up, BC18 covers partitioning). With 100 indexes per CF account limit, a platform with 100+ active Spaces hits the limit and cannot create new indexes. No index multiplexing strategy is specified.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement Vectorize index multiplexing: instead of one index per Space, group multiple Spaces into shared indexes with Space_id metadata filter. Strategy: (1) Tier 1 (high-traffic Spaces, &gt;1000 sessions/day): dedicated index, (2) Tier 2 (medium-traffic, 100-1000/day): group 5 Spaces per index, (3) Tier 3 (low-traffic, &lt;100/day): group 20 Spaces per index. All queries include metadata filter {space_id: {$eq: id}}. Track index_utilization_pct and query_latency by tier.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BE07" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d4edda;border-left:4px solid #27ae60;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BE07 [LOW] — CF D1 Database Limit (50,000 Databases Per Account)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Per-tenant D1 databases (AT11) for tenant SQL isolation would hit the 50,000 D1 database limit at scale. At 50,000 builders, each with one D1 database, the account limit is reached. No sharding or pooling strategy handles this.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Use shared D1 databases with tenant isolation: rather than one D1 database per tenant, shard by: {tenant_id MOD num_shards} into a fixed number of D1 shards (e.g., 100 shards). All tables include tenant_id column with index. Row-level security enforced in query layer (WHERE tenant_id = ?). D1 migration: apply to all shards. Monitoring: per-shard row count and query latency. Alert if any shard &gt; 80% of D1 500MB limit. Shard rebalancing: move high-volume tenant to dedicated D1.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BE08" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BE08 [MEDIUM] — CF R2 Bucket Limit and Naming Convention</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> R2 bucket count limits per CF account. Platform using multiple R2 buckets (KB storage, conversation exports, analytics exports, fine-tune datasets, backups) may approach limits at scale. No bucket naming convention or lifecycle management prevents bucket sprawl.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Define R2 bucket architecture: fixed bucket set (not per-tenant): kb-storage (all KB documents), conversation-exports, analytics-exports, model-datasets, backups, temp-uploads. All objects prefixed by tenant_id: kb-storage/{tenant_id}/{space_id}/{doc_id}. Lifecycle rules per bucket: temp-uploads: 7-day deletion, backups: 90-day transition to Infrequent Access. Track bucket count and object count per bucket. Avoid dynamic bucket creation.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BE09" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d4edda;border-left:4px solid #27ae60;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BE09 [LOW] — CF Workers Free Tier Request Limit Impact on Development</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Development and staging environments on CF Workers consume request quota from the same account. During load testing (BC46) or heavy development, dev/staging Workers may consume quota intended for production.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Separate CF accounts for environments: production CF account, staging CF account, development CF account. Each account has independent quotas. IaC manages all three accounts. Account isolation: production Secrets cannot be accessed from staging/dev workers by design. Cross-account cost tracking: total CF spend aggregated monthly. CI/CD deploys to staging account first, promote artifacts to production account. Track environment_cost_distribution (prod/staging/dev should be ~90/8/2%).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BE10" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BE10 [MEDIUM] — CF Durable Objects Alarm API for Scheduled Tasks</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> DOs support an Alarm API (this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date)) for scheduling future callbacks. No use of the Alarm API is specified for time-triggered DO tasks: session expiry, scheduled analytics flush, memory consolidation, reminder dispatch. Without alarms, these require external Cron Workers instead of co-located DO logic.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Use DO Alarm API for co-located scheduling: SpaceDO.setAlarm() for: (1) Session timeout: setAlarm(Date.now() + session_ttl_ms) — alarm fires → close idle session, (2) Analytics flush: setAlarm(Date.now() + 30000) — flush in-memory event buffer to Analytics Engine, (3) Memory consolidation: setAlarm for next 3am — trigger nightly consolidation. alarm() method handles all alarm types based on stored alarm_type in SQLite. Track alarm_fire_latency (time between scheduled and actual firing).
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BE11" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BE11 [HIGH] — AI Agent Communication Protocol (A2A)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Multiple AI agents need to communicate in complex pipelines (AX02 subagent spawning, AY02 agent-to-agent handoff). No standard agent communication protocol is used — all agent coordination is bespoke. Google's A2A (Agent-to-Agent) protocol is emerging as a standard.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement A2A-compatible agent interfaces: each SpaceDO exposes /.well-known/agent.json (A2A Agent Card) describing: name, description, capabilities, supported_protocols, authentication. A2A task endpoint: POST /a2a/tasks → {task_id, status, result}. Task streaming via SSE. Inter-agent calls use A2A protocol over HTTPS with JWT auth. Benefits: cross-platform agent interoperability. Track a2a_inter_agent_call_count and external_a2a_client_count.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BE12" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BE12 [HIGH] — Agentic RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Current RAG retrieves once per turn. Agentic RAG allows the agent to iteratively refine retrieval: retrieve → assess sufficiency → retrieve again with refined query if needed. No iterative retrieval pattern is specified.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement agentic RAG loop: (1) Initial retrieval: standard vector search, (2) Agent assesses: "Do I have enough context to answer?" (classification step), (3) If INSUFFICIENT: generate refined_query based on retrieved gaps, retrieve again (max 3 iterations), (4) If SUFFICIENT: generate answer. Each retrieval iteration logged as retrieval_round in trace. Track mean_retrieval_rounds per query, and answer_quality comparison: agentic vs single-shot RAG on eval set. Cost: agentic RAG uses more tokens — gate by query complexity score.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BE13" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#fff3cd;border-left:4px solid #e67e22;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BE13 [HIGH] — Structured Output with Tool-Augmented Reasoning (ReAct)</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Agent reasoning is implicit (standard chat completion). No ReAct (Reasoning + Acting) pattern structures agent thought-action cycles explicitly, making reasoning harder to audit and less reliable on complex tasks.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement ReAct prompting pattern: system prompt structures agent response format: Thought: [reasoning step] → Action: [tool call or final answer] → Observation: [tool result]. Parser extracts Thought/Action/Observation from LLM output. Multiple cycles until Action=FINAL_ANSWER. Benefits: explicit reasoning trace, easier to detect errors, better on complex multi-step tasks. Store full ReAct trace in DO SQLite for audit. Toggle: builder configures reasoning_mode: "react" | "standard" | "chain-of-thought". Track ReAct_task_completion_rate vs standard.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BE14" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d1ecf1;border-left:4px solid #2980b9;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BE14 [MEDIUM] — Constitutional AI Principles Integration</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Agent safety relies on model training and prompt-level instructions. No Constitutional AI (CAI) principles layer provides an additional safety check that critiques and revises agent responses against a defined set of principles before delivery.
  </div>
  <div class="box-gr" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #2e7d32;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;">
    <em>Required Fix:</em> Implement CAI review step (optional, configurable per SpaceType): after initial LLM response generation, send to CAI reviewer: "Review this response against these principles: [builder-defined principles]. Identify any violations and provide a revised response." On violation detected: use revised response, log cai_revision_event. Builder-defined principles examples: "Never claim certainty about medical facts", "Always recommend consulting a professional for legal matters", "Do not disclose pricing of competitors." Track cai_revision_rate and revision_categories.
  </div>
</div>
<div id="G-BE15" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <div class="box-rd" style="background:#d4edda;border-left:4px solid #27ae60;padding:1rem;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
    <strong>G-BE15 [LOW] — Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) Model Routing</strong><br>
    <em>The Gap:</em> Platform routes all requests to the same configured LLM. Emerging MoE architectures (Mixtral, GPT-4o) activate specialist expert networks for different query types. No query-type-aware model routing leverages MoE capabilities.
  </div>
